window.BANK_I=[{"id":"I|L1|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Mode of infection","s":"A stem asks for the MODE OF INFECTION, not the mode of transmission. Which answer has the right shape?","lec":"L1","sl":82,"c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Soil-transmitted","E":"Food-borne","D":"Skin penetration by the filariform (L3) larva","A":"Faecal–oral","C":"Person-to-person contact"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Also a transmission channel, and it describes a group of worms rather than an act.","E":"'Food-borne' is a mode of TRANSMISSION. Examiners set one stem for each in the same paper, with overlapping options, so writing this when asked for infection scores nothing.","D":"Correct. Mode of INFECTION names the precise act plus the NAMED STAGE. Everything else here names a channel.","A":"A route, not an act plus a stage.","C":"A channel. It is the right transmission answer for Enterobius but still not a mode of infection."},"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Which intestinal nematode is the classic cause of autoinfection, and what makes it possible?","lec":"L1","sl":53,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Trichuris trichiura — its eggs are infective as soon as they are laid","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides — its eggs embryonate in the bowel lumen","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis — rhabditiform larvae can moult to infective filariform larvae inside the gut","A":"Hookworm — filariform larvae re-penetrate the perianal skin","B":"Necator americanus — adults migrate upward from the caecum"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Trichuris eggs also need a soil period; they are not infective when passed.","D":"Ascaris eggs must embryonate in SOIL for two to three weeks; an egg swallowed straight from the perianal region cannot infect.","E":"Correct, and it is why strongyloidiasis can persist for decades and can hyperinfect under corticosteroids.","A":"Hookworm larvae must develop in soil first — this is the plausible-sounding but wrong route.","B":"Necator is a hookworm and behaves as in D; adult migration is not an infection route."},"img":"slides_i/L1_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Egg morphology","s":"A stool contains a barrel-shaped egg with a transparent plug at each end. Which worm, and what clinical sign accompanies a heavy infection?","lec":"L1","sl":29,"c":"<b>Egg morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Enterobius vermicularis — perianal itching","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides — intestinal obstruction","A":"Trichuris trichiura — rectal prolapse","C":"Hookworm — iron-deficiency anaemia","E":"Capillaria philippinensis — protein-losing enteropathy"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Enterobius eggs are asymmetrically flattened on one side and are collected by perianal SCOTCH TAPE, not from stool.","B":"Ascaris eggs are round with a thick mamillated albuminous coat.","A":"Correct. The barrel shape with bipolar mucoid plugs is unmistakable, and heavy whipworm infection in a child classically causes rectal prolapse.","C":"Hookworm eggs are thin-walled, transparent ovals containing 4–8 cells.","E":"Capillaria eggs are described as PEANUT-shaped with flattened bipolar plugs — similar to Trichuris but with a striated shell and flatter plugs, and this is the deliberate near-miss."},"img":"slides_i/L1_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Diagnosis","s":"A mother brings two children with anal itching that is worse at night. What specimen should be collected, and how?","lec":"L1","sl":77,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"A perianal skin scraping in 10% KOH","A":"A formed stool sample for concentration technique","E":"Three stool samples on alternate days","B":"A perianal scotch-tape (cellophane) swab taken in the early morning before washing","D":"A duodenal aspirate"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"KOH is for fungal elements and for Paragonimus sputum, not for pinworm eggs.","A":"Stool examination is insensitive for Enterobius precisely because the eggs are not deposited in the gut lumen.","E":"Repeat stools raise sensitivity for other nematodes but not for this one.","B":"Correct. The female Enterobius migrates out at night and lays eggs on perianal skin, so the eggs are on the SKIN and not usually in stool. Take the tape before bathing or defaecation.","D":"A duodenal aspirate is used for Strongyloides and Giardia."},"img":"slides_i/L1_77.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Life cycle","s":"Which group of intestinal nematodes migrates through the lungs, and what symptom can that produce?","lec":"L1","sl":15,"c":"<b>Life cycle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Only hookworm — pleural effusion","D":"Trichuris, Enterobius and Capillaria — cough and wheeze","A":"Only Ascaris — haemoptysis","E":"Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides — cough and wheeze (Löffler-type pneumonitis)","C":"None of the intestinal nematodes enters the lung"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Hookworm is in the club, but the symptom is cough/wheeze, and it is not alone.","D":"None of these three migrates through the lung; they stay in the gut.","A":"Ascaris is in the club but is not alone.","E":"Correct. These three make the classic heart–lung migration, which is why an intestinal worm can present with respiratory symptoms. Remembering the trio prevents you leaping to a lung fluke.","C":"The opposite of the truth, and the reason respiratory symptoms are such a productive distractor."},"img":"slides_i/L1_15.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Hookworm","s":"Which statement about hookworm is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":43,"c":"<b>Hookworm</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Both infective and diagnostic stages are the adult worm","A":"The infective stage is the egg, swallowed from contaminated vegetables","D":"The infective stage is the rhabditiform larva, which penetrates skin","C":"The diagnostic stage is the filariform larva in stool","B":"The infective stage is the filariform (L3) larva, which penetrates skin; the diagnostic stage is the egg in stool"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Adults are never an infective or diagnostic stage here.","A":"Hookworm eggs are not infective; they must hatch and develop in soil.","D":"The RHABDITIFORM larva is the feeding, non-infective stage; the FILARIFORM is the infective one.","C":"Finding larvae rather than eggs in fresh stool points to Strongyloides, which is exactly the discrimination the papers set.","B":"Correct, and the pairing is the template for the whole lecture: infective stage and diagnostic stage are different objects, in different places."},"img":"slides_i/L1_43.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Strongyloides","s":"A 45-year-old man has abdominal pain and chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool examination shows LARVAE, not eggs. What is the most likely agent, and why is the finding decisive?","lec":"L1","sl":56,"c":"<b>Strongyloides</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Enterobius vermicularis — larvae hatch on perianal skin","E":"Hookworm — its eggs hatch rapidly once passed","A":"Trichuris trichiura — its larvae emerge in the rectum","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides — larvae return to the gut after lung migration","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis — its eggs hatch in the bowel, so larvae rather than eggs appear in fresh stool"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Enterobius eggs are on perianal skin and larvae are not the stool finding.","E":"Hookworm eggs hatch only after some hours outside the body — in a fresh specimen you see eggs.","A":"Trichuris passes eggs, and its larvae are not found in stool.","C":"Ascaris passes eggs.","D":"Correct. This is the single most useful discriminator in L1: EGGS in fresh stool = hookworm; LARVAE in fresh stool = Strongyloides."},"img":"slides_i/L1_56.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Capillaria","s":"A patient from the north-east has months of diarrhoea, weight loss and protein-losing enteropathy. Stool shows peanut-shaped eggs with flattened bipolar plugs and a striated shell. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L1","sl":63,"c":"<b>Capillaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","E":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from contaminated vegetables","A":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing third-stage larvae","C":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing encysted larvae"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"That is hookworm or Strongyloides.","E":"That is Ascaris, Trichuris or Enterobius.","A":"Correct. Capillaria philippinensis is acquired from small raw freshwater fish, and the stage named is the THIRD-STAGE LARVA. It also autoinfects, which is why the illness is protracted and severe.","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis viverrini (L4) — same meal, different stage, different organ. Read the stage, not the fish.","D":"Encysted larvae in pork is Trichinella (L2)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_63.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Control","s":"Which pair of measures controls SOIL-TRANSMITTED helminths specifically?","lec":"L1","sl":34,"c":"<b>Control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Using a sanitary toilet and wearing shoes","C":"Cooking fish thoroughly and avoiding pickled crab","A":"Treating the patient and all household contacts","B":"Boiling drinking water and chlorinating pools","E":"Controlling the snail intermediate host"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and it is the answer key's own pairing. A toilet keeps eggs out of the soil; shoes keep filariform larvae out of your feet.","C":"That prevents food-borne trematodes and Capillaria, not soil-transmitted nematodes.","A":"That is the Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana answer — person-to-person spread, not soil.","B":"Water treatment addresses protozoa, not soil-transmitted helminths.","E":"Snail control belongs to the trematodes in L4."},"img":"slides_i/L1_34.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Ascaris","s":"What would happen if a person accidentally swallowed an Ascaris lumbricoides egg taken directly from the perianal area, without a soil period?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"<b>Ascaris</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Visceral larva migrans, because the larva hatches immediately","C":"Intestinal infection, because Ascaris eggs are infective as soon as they are passed","B":"Nothing — the egg is not yet embryonated and therefore not infective","E":"Cutaneous larva migrans at the site of contact","D":"Autoinfection with adult worms in the colon"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Hatching requires an embryonated egg.","C":"That is Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana, whose eggs are infective when laid.","B":"Correct. Ascaris eggs need two to three weeks in SOIL to embryonate. Freshly passed eggs are harmless, which is exactly why Ascaris is NOT on the autoinfection list.","E":"Cutaneous larva migrans is caused by animal hookworm larvae penetrating skin.","D":"Ascaris cannot autoinfect, for exactly the reason in A."},"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Diagnosis","s":"What is the best general method for diagnosing an intestinal nematode infection?","lec":"L1","sl":22,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Serology","D":"Blood film","C":"Stool examination","B":"Muscle biopsy","E":"Skin snip"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Serology is a fall-back where no egg can be found — for example in extrapulmonary paragonimiasis or trichinellosis.","D":"Blood films are for the blood and tissue nematodes (microfilariae) and for malaria.","C":"Correct, and it is the defining property of the whole lecture — these worms live in the gut lumen, so their eggs or larvae come out in the stool. L2's worms invert exactly this.","B":"Muscle biopsy is the Trichinella method (L2).","E":"Skin snips are used for Onchocerca, not for gut nematodes."},"img":"slides_i/L1_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q8","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Which of the following parasites can cause autoinfection?","lec":"L1","sl":82,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q8 · BM28 Q37 — 'which parasite can manifest autoinfection?'</span>","o":{"E":"Paragonimus spp.","D":"Taenia saginata","C":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides","A":"Opisthorchis viverrini"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Paragonimus needs two intermediate hosts outside the body.","D":"Only T. SOLIUM autoinfects, and it does so as cysticercosis; saginata does not.","C":"Correct, and the usual key. The full autoinfection list the BM28 answer key gives is: Strongyloides stercoralis, Enterobius vermicularis, Taenia SOLIUM (as cysticercosis), Hymenolepis nana, Capillaria philippinensis — and the BM30 key adds Cryptosporidium.","B":"Ascaris eggs must embryonate in soil first, so they cannot re-infect the host who passed them.","A":"Opisthorchis needs a snail and then a fish."},"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q37"]},{"id":"I|L1|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q3","t":"Strongyloides","s":"A 45-year-old man with abdominal pain and chronic watery diarrhoea is found to have LARVAE in his faeces. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L1","sl":56,"c":"<b>Strongyloides</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q3 — larvae in faeces with chronic watery diarrhoea</span>","o":{"E":"Trichuris trichiura","B":"Hookworm","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides","A":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"Trichuris passes barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs.","B":"Hookworm passes eggs; larvae appear only if the specimen has been left standing for hours, which the question does not say.","D":"Correct. Strongyloides eggs hatch inside the bowel, so a FRESH stool contains rhabditiform LARVAE rather than eggs.","C":"Ascaris passes round mamillated eggs.","A":"Enterobius eggs are on perianal skin and are collected by tape."},"img":"slides_i/L1_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q20","t":"Hookworm","s":"A 40-year-old man presents with dyspnoea, cough and abdominal pain. Stool examination reveals an oval, thin-walled, transparent egg containing 4–8 cell stages. What is the mode of infection of this parasite?","lec":"L1","sl":40,"c":"<b>Hookworm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q20 · BM30 — 4–8 cell egg with respiratory symptoms</span>","o":{"C":"Ingestion of vegetables contaminated with unembryonated eggs","B":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing metacercariae","D":"Ingestion of mountain crabs containing metacercariae","E":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","A":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysticerci"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Unembryonated eggs are never infective — this option is wrong on the stage alone.","B":"A fish/metacercaria option points to Opisthorchis (L4).","D":"The mountain-crab option is the designed trap: respiratory symptoms tempt you towards Paragonimus. But Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides ALL migrate through the lung, so cough does not mean lung fluke.","E":"Correct. The egg described is a HOOKWORM egg, and hookworm is acquired by filariform larvae penetrating the skin.","A":"That is Taenia solium (L3)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_40.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-hook"]},{"id":"I|L1|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q5","t":"Capillaria","s":"A patient has had chronic diarrhoea for three months. Stool examination shows a peanut-shaped egg. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L1","sl":63,"c":"<b>Capillaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q5 · BM28 · BM30 formative — 'peanut-like egg', chronic diarrhoea</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of raw beef containing cysticerci","D":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil-contaminated vegetables","B":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","C":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","E":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing third-stage larvae"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Cysticercus bovis in beef is Taenia saginata.","D":"That route belongs to Ascaris, Trichuris and Enterobius.","B":"That is hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis viverrini — the same meal with a different stage, which is how this distractor works.","E":"Correct. The 'peanut-like' egg is Capillaria philippinensis, and the BM28 answer key states the mode of infection in exactly these words."},"img":"slides_i/L1_63.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q-cap","BM30 Q-cap"]},{"id":"I|L1|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-trich","t":"Trichuris","s":"A child presents with rectal prolapse. Stool examination shows barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar mucoid plugs. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L1","sl":29,"c":"<b>Trichuris</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 · BM30 formative — barrel egg, rectal prolapse</span>","o":{"A":"Trichuris trichiura","C":"Capillaria philippinensis","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides","B":"Trichinella spiralis","E":"Taenia solium"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Barrel-shaped with bipolar plugs, yellow-brown, plus rectal prolapse in a child is the whipworm.","C":"Capillaria is the deliberate near-miss — also bipolar, but PEANUT-shaped with FLATTENED plugs and a striated shell, and it gives protein-losing enteropathy rather than prolapse.","D":"Ascaris eggs are round with a thick mamillated coat.","B":"Trichinella is diagnosed from muscle, not stool, and gives myositis rather than prolapse.","E":"Taenia eggs are spherical with radial striation."},"img":"slides_i/L1_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-trich"]},{"id":"I|L1|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q1","t":"Control","s":"How are soil-transmitted helminths controlled?","lec":"L1","sl":34,"c":"<b>Control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q1 — control of soil-transmitted helminths</span>","o":{"C":"Using sanitary toilets and wearing shoes","B":"Treating the patient and the whole family","E":"Boiling drinking water","D":"Eliminating the snail intermediate host","A":"Cooking freshwater fish thoroughly"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. A toilet stops eggs reaching the soil, and shoes stop filariform larvae reaching skin. The two measures cover both halves of the transmission route.","B":"That is the correct answer for Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana, whose route is person-to-person.","E":"Water treatment matters for protozoa.","D":"Snail control belongs to the trematodes in L4.","A":"Cooking fish prevents Capillaria and Opisthorchis, but those are not soil-transmitted."},"img":"slides_i/L1_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q7","t":"Enterobius","s":"A mother brings her two children to hospital with itchy anal symptoms. How should this infection be controlled?","lec":"L1","sl":78,"c":"<b>Enterobius</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q7 — mother with two itchy-bottomed children</span>","o":{"D":"Cook food thoroughly","E":"Use a sanitary toilet","B":"Boil water before bathing","A":"Wear shoes","C":"Treat the infected patient and the whole family"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Pinworm is not food-borne.","E":"Sanitation matters for soil-transmitted worms, whose eggs need a soil period. Pinworm does not use the soil.","B":"Bathing water is not the route.","A":"Shoes prevent skin penetration by hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Correct. Enterobius eggs are infective the moment they are laid and spread by hand, bedding and dust, so a household or institution must be treated together — otherwise reinfection is immediate."},"img":"slides_i/L1_78.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q19","t":"Diagnosis","s":"Which is the best method for diagnosing an intestinal nematode infection?","lec":"L1","sl":22,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q19 — best method for diagnosing intestinal nematodes</span>","o":{"A":"Muscle biopsy","B":"Blood examination","D":"Stool examination","E":"Urine filtration","C":"Sputum examination"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Muscle biopsy is for Trichinella.","B":"Blood is the specimen for the filarial worms and for malaria.","D":"Correct — and this is the one-line definition of the lecture. These worms live in the gut lumen, so eggs or larvae leave in the stool.","E":"Urine filtration is for Schistosoma haematobium.","C":"Sputum is for Paragonimus."},"img":"slides_i/L1_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q1","t":"Ascaris egg","s":"A stool examination shows an egg whose outermost layer is a coarse, bumpy ALBUMINOUS coat. Which parasite is this?","lec":"L1","sl":17,"c":"<b>Ascaris egg</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q2 · BM31 SUM1 Q1 — 'albuminous coated egg' (asked in three cohorts running)</span>","o":{"E":"Capillaria philippinensis","C":"Trichuris trichiura","A":"Ascaris lumbricoides","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Trichinella spiralis"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Capillaria is peanut-shaped with FLATTENED bipolar plugs and a striated shell.","C":"Trichuris is barrel-shaped with a clear mucoid plug at each pole. No albuminous coat.","A":"Correct. The fertilised Ascaris egg has THREE shell layers and the outermost is the mammillated albuminous coat, which is what makes it look lumpy and bile-stained yellow-brown. One word — albuminous — settles this item.","D":"Strongyloides is not diagnosed on eggs at all — you find rhabditiform LARVAE in stool.","B":"Trichinella passes no egg in stool; the diagnostic stage is encysted larva in muscle."},"img":"slides_i/L1_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q2"]},{"id":"I|L1|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q2","t":"Larvae in stool","s":"A stool examination shows LARVAE (no eggs). Which infection does this indicate?","lec":"L1","sl":52,"c":"<b>Larvae in stool</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q3 · BM31 SUM1 Q2 — 'larva found in stool'. NOTE: BM31's key records that the lecturer allowed two answers on this item and said it might be dropped.</span>","o":{"D":"Necator americanus","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","C":"Trichuris trichiura","A":"Hymenolepis nana","B":"Taenia saginata"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Hookworm passes unembryonated eggs. Larvae appear only if the specimen has stood for a day — which is the near-miss the examiner plants, and is why BM31's lecturer conceded two answers.","E":"Correct. Strongyloides eggs hatch INSIDE the bowel wall, so a freshly passed stool already contains rhabditiform larvae. This is the single most repeated one-line fact in the block.","C":"Trichuris passes barrel-shaped eggs.","A":"Hymenolepis nana passes eggs with polar filaments.","B":"Taenia passes eggs with radial striation, or proglottids."},"img":"slides_i/L1_52.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q3"]},{"id":"I|L1|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 1","t":"Ascaris — obstruction","s":"A 5-year-old girl has severe abdominal pain and constipation. An X-ray shows PARTIAL INTESTINAL OBSTRUCTION of the small bowel, and multiple large worms are seen. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"<b>Ascaris — obstruction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Lect 1 & Lect 2 — the 5-year-old with partial intestinal obstruction, asked twice in the same paper</span>","o":{"B":"Ingestion of raw vegetables contaminated with embryonated eggs","A":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","C":"Ingestion of water contaminated with unembryonated eggs","E":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing third-stage larvae","D":"Autoinfection by embryonated eggs"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. A bolus of large worms obstructing a child's small bowel is Ascaris, and Ascaris is acquired by swallowing the EMBRYONATED egg off soil-contaminated food.","A":"Skin penetration is hookworm and Strongyloides — neither forms a worm bolus.","C":"The stage is the trap. An UNembryonated egg is not infective; Ascaris eggs need 2–3 weeks in soil to embryonate.","E":"Cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis (L4).","D":"Ascaris cannot autoinfect, precisely because the egg must embryonate in soil first."},"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM32 Lect 2"]},{"id":"I|L1|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q28","t":"Ascaris — why soil matters","s":"What would happen if a person accidentally swallowed an Ascaris lumbricoides egg taken directly from a patient's perianal skin, without the egg having spent time in soil?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"<b>Ascaris — why soil matters</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q28 — 'what happens if you swallow an Ascaris egg straight from the perianal area, without it passing through soil?'</span>","o":{"E":"Cysticercosis in muscle and brain","B":"Ascariasis, because all helminth eggs are infective when passed","A":"Nothing — the egg is unembryonated and therefore not infective","D":"Cutaneous larva migrans at the site of contact","C":"Autoinfection with hyperinfection syndrome"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Cysticercosis follows ingesting Taenia solium eggs, which are infective immediately.","B":"False for Ascaris and Trichuris; true for Enterobius, whose eggs ARE infective within hours — which is the contrast the examiner is testing.","A":"Correct, and this is the whole point of the question: Ascaris eggs leave the body UNEMBRYONATED and need 2–3 weeks in warm moist soil to become the infective embryonated egg. This is exactly why Ascaris is a soil-transmitted helminth and why it cannot autoinfect.","D":"Creeping eruption follows skin penetration by animal hookworm larvae.","C":"Autoinfection belongs to Strongyloides, Enterobius, Capillaria and T. solium."},"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q32","t":"Host terminology","s":"What is the role of a RESERVOIR host?","lec":"L1","sl":69,"c":"<b>Host terminology</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q32 — 'what is the function of a reservoir host?'</span>","o":{"B":"The host that harbours the larval or asexual stages of the parasite","E":"The host in which the parasite reaches sexual maturity and reproduces","C":"An animal that harbours the same parasite as humans and maintains it in nature as a source of human infection","A":"A host in which the parasite cannot complete its life cycle and is a dead end","D":"An arthropod that carries the parasite on its body surface without biological development"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"That is the INTERMEDIATE host.","E":"That is the DEFINITIVE host.","C":"Correct. For Capillaria philippinensis the reservoir is fish-eating birds; for Trichinella it is pigs and wild boar.","A":"That is the ACCIDENTAL (dead-end) host — humans for Angiostrongylus and Toxocara.","D":"That is a MECHANICAL transmitter, e.g. the cockroach."},"img":"slides_i/L1_69.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q26","t":"Strongyloides — three phases","s":"A patient has creeping eruption of the skin, dyspnoea, and abdominal pain — all at once. What would stool examination show?","lec":"L1","sl":54,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — three phases</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q26 — creeping eruption + dyspnoea + abdominal pain; what is found in faeces?</span>","o":{"D":"Encysted larvae","E":"Unembryonated eggs with a thin transparent shell","A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs","C":"Embryonated eggs with polar filaments","B":"Rhabditiform larvae"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Encysted larvae are found in MUSCLE in trichinellosis, never in stool.","E":"Thin-shelled unembryonated eggs are hookworm — and hookworm gives creeping eruption and cough too, but it cannot autoinfect, so the three phases do not run together.","A":"Trichuris — no skin or lung phase at all.","C":"Hymenolepis nana (L3).","B":"Correct. Skin + lung + gut simultaneously is the signature of Strongyloides autoinfection, and Strongyloides is the one intestinal nematode whose stool stage is a LARVA."},"img":"slides_i/L1_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Larva discriminator — buccal cavity","s":"A fresh stool sample examined within an hour of passing contains actively moving rhabditiform larvae. You must decide between hookworm and Strongyloides stercoralis. Which single pair of features settles it?","lec":"L1","sl":41,"c":"<b>Larva discriminator — buccal cavity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Strongyloides has a SHORT buccal cavity and a LARGE genital primordium; hookworm has a LONG buccal cavity and a SMALL genital primordium","C":"Strongyloides has a LONG buccal cavity and a SMALL genital primordium; hookworm is the reverse","B":"Strongyloides has a filariform oesophagus at this stage; hookworm has a rhabditiform one","E":"Strongyloides has a notched tail at this stage; hookworm has a pointed tail","D":"Strongyloides larvae are motile and hookworm larvae are not"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — and the mnemonic that survives exam pressure is that Strongyloides is the one in a hurry: short mouth, big gonad, ready to become infective inside you (autoinfection).","C":"This is the deliberate inversion. It is the single most common way to lose this mark.","B":"Both are RHABDITIFORM larvae here, so both have the bulbous rhabditiform oesophagus — that feature cannot discriminate.","E":"Pointed vs notched tail is real, but it is the FILARIFORM discriminator, not the rhabditiform one. The stem says rhabditiform.","D":"Both are motile in a fresh sample. Motility tells you the sample is fresh, not which worm it is."},"img":"slides_i/L1_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Larva discriminator — tail","s":"A Harada–Mori culture set up from a patient's stool is read on day 7 and grows long slender larvae with an elongated oesophagus. The tail tip is notched. What has the culture recovered, and what does that imply for treatment?","lec":"L1","sl":42,"c":"<b>Larva discriminator — tail</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Strongyloides filariform larvae — ivermectin is the drug of choice, not albendazole alone","B":"Hookworm filariform larvae — albendazole or mebendazole will clear it","D":"Strongyloides rhabditiform larvae — the notch is a rhabditiform feature","E":"Hookworm rhabditiform larvae — culture converts filariform back to rhabditiform","A":"Trichuris larvae — Trichuris is the only nematode cultured by Harada–Mori"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. NOTCHED tail = Strongyloides; pointed tail = hookworm. Strongyloides matters more because of autoinfection, and the deck gives ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day.","B":"Hookworm filariform larvae have a POINTED tail. The drug half would be right, which is exactly why this is the planted near-miss.","D":"Long and slender with an elongated oesophagus is filariform by definition; rhabditiform larvae are short with a bulb.","E":"Culture drives development FORWARD (rhabditiform → filariform), never backwards.","A":"Trichuris is diagnosed from unembryonated eggs in stool; it has no larval culture step in this deck."},"img":"slides_i/L1_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Loeffler's syndrome — which worms","s":"A patient has fever, dry cough, wheeze and marked eosinophilia. The physician suspects a migrating helminth larva in the lung. Which group of intestinal nematodes can produce this picture?","lec":"L1","sl":20,"c":"<b>Loeffler's syndrome — which worms</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Ascaris lumbricoides, hookworm and Strongyloides stercoralis — all three send larvae through the lung","E":"Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura and Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Hookworm, Trichuris trichiura and Capillaria philippinensis","C":"Strongyloides stercoralis and Enterobius vermicularis only","A":"Every intestinal nematode in this lecture, because all nematode larvae migrate"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Loeffler's syndrome needs a lung-migrating larva, and only these three do it — Ascaris after the egg hatches, hookworm and Strongyloides after skin penetration.","E":"Trichuris and Enterobius have NO migratory phase at all; their larvae develop in the gut lumen.","B":"Trichuris and Capillaria both stay in the bowel. Capillaria's damage is villous destruction, not pneumonitis.","C":"Enterobius does not migrate through the lung. Strongyloides alone is an incomplete answer.","A":"The whole point of the wrap-up slide is that they do NOT all migrate — this option is the trap for anyone pattern-matching 'nematode = migration'."},"img":"slides_i/L1_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Severe strongyloidiasis","s":"A man on high-dose prednisolone for lupus develops abdominal pain, cough and then Gram-negative bacteraemia with meningitis. Stool shows abundant rhabditiform larvae. What has happened, and what is the correct course of ivermectin?","lec":"L1","sl":53,"c":"<b>Severe strongyloidiasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis — ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day continued until stool examination stays negative for 2 weeks","B":"Hyperinfection strongyloidiasis — a single 2-day course of ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day","C":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis — albendazole for 3 days, because ivermectin does not cross into the CNS","A":"Loeffler's syndrome from Ascaris — albendazole single dose","E":"Hookworm autoinfection — mebendazole for 3 days"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on both halves. Spread BEYOND the normal migration route (here to the meninges, with larvae carrying enteric bacteria) is DISSEMINATED disease, and the deck's regimen is ivermectin continued until stool is negative for two weeks.","B":"Hyperinfection is massive larval proliferation confined to bowel and lung. Once there is bacterial meningitis the disease has disseminated, and the 2-day uncomplicated course is far too short.","C":"The species and the mechanism are right but the drug is wrong — the deck gives ivermectin for severe disease, and the sepsis is caused by bacteria the larvae carry, not by larvae in the CSF.","A":"Ascaris produces Loeffler's syndrome but cannot autoinfect and does not cause Gram-negative sepsis.","E":"Hookworm does NOT autoinfect — its filariform larvae must develop in soil first. This is the classic hookworm/Strongyloides swap."},"img":"slides_i/L1_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Ascaris egg types","s":"A stool report describes an Ascaris egg that is oval with an irregular outline, has a thin chitinous shell with an albuminous coat, is colourless-to-pale, and contains refractile granules rather than a single cell mass. Which egg is this, and can it infect the next person?","lec":"L1","sl":17,"c":"<b>Ascaris egg types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"An unfertilised egg — it can never become infective, because there is no embryo to develop","C":"A fertilised egg — it becomes infective after 2–3 weeks in soil","E":"A decorticated egg — it is infective as soon as it is passed","D":"An embryonated egg — it is the infective stage and is found in stool","B":"A fertilised egg that has already embryonated in the bowel lumen"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Refractile granules and the irregular, longer (78–105 µm) shape mark the UNFERTILISED egg. It is a diagnostic finding only — nothing develops inside it.","C":"Fertilised eggs are rounder (45–84 µm), yellow-brown, and carry a single unsegmented cell, not granules.","E":"Decorticated simply means the albuminous layer has been LOST. This stem explicitly says the albuminous coat is present, and decorticated eggs are not infective either.","D":"The embryonated egg is the infective stage but it is found in SOIL, not in stool — the deck states this on both p.18 and p.23.","B":"Ascaris eggs cannot embryonate in the bowel. That requirement for a soil period is exactly why Ascaris cannot autoinfect."},"img":"slides_i/L1_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Hookworm species pair","s":"An adult worm recovered at endoscopy has a buccal capsule bearing TWO PAIRS OF TEETH. A colleague asks whether the stool egg count could have told you the same thing. What is the correct pair of statements?","lec":"L1","sl":38,"c":"<b>Hookworm species pair</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The worm is Ancylostoma duodenale; the eggs of the two human hookworms are indistinguishable, so stool is reported simply as 'hookworm egg'","A":"The worm is Necator americanus; the eggs are indistinguishable, so stool is reported as 'hookworm egg'","E":"The worm is Ancylostoma duodenale; its eggs are larger (60–80 × 35–40 µm) and can be told apart from Necator on size","D":"The worm is Necator americanus; its cutting plates correspond to a thin-shelled 4–8 cell egg unique to the species","B":"The worm is Ancylostoma caninum, an animal hookworm, so no eggs will ever appear in human stool"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on both halves. TEETH = Ancylostoma, cutting PLATES = Necator, and the deck states plainly that the eggs are *indistinguishable.","A":"Necator has a pair of cutting PLATES, not teeth. The second half is right, which is what makes this the near-miss.","E":"The size ranges do overlap and the deck marks them *Indistinguishable — quoting the numbers as if they discriminated is the trap.","D":"Wrong species for teeth, and the 4–8 cell thin-shelled egg is shared by both human hookworms.","B":"A. caninum is an animal hookworm causing creeping eruption, but the stem describes a worm recovered from the human gut, and 'no eggs ever' overstates the point being tested."},"img":"slides_i/L1_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Capillaria — autoinfection","s":"A man from north-east Thailand has had watery diarrhoea and borborygmi for three months, has lost 10 kg, and now has pitting oedema. Stool shows peanut-shaped eggs with striated shells, and also larvae and adult worms. Why are larvae and adults appearing in the stool, and what explains the oedema?","lec":"L1","sl":62,"c":"<b>Capillaria — autoinfection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The female is larviparous, so autoinfection multiplies the worm burden in the small intestine; villous destruction causes a protein-losing enteropathy with hypoalbuminaemia","D":"The female is oviparous only, so the larvae come from eggs that hatched in the bowel; the oedema is from iron-deficiency anaemia","B":"Adults and larvae in stool mean the patient has Strongyloides co-infection; the oedema is renal","E":"The worms have migrated ectopically from the bile duct; the oedema is from portal hypertension","A":"The larvae are third-stage larvae from undercooked fish passing through unchanged; the oedema is nutritional only"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Capillaria philippinensis females are both oviparous AND larviparous — the larviparous ones drive internal autoinfection, so the burden escalates and villi are flattened or destroyed, giving hypoalbuminaemia and pitting oedema.","D":"The deck lists both reproductive modes; restricting it to oviparous removes the mechanism the question is testing. Iron-deficiency anaemia is the HOOKWORM story.","B":"Strongyloides gives rhabditiform larvae, not peanut-shaped striated eggs, and does not cause protein-losing enteropathy.","E":"Ectopic migration into the biliary tree is ASCARIS. Capillaria stays in the small bowel.","A":"Third-stage larvae are what you SWALLOW in the fish; they do not simply pass through, and calling the oedema purely nutritional misses the hypoalbuminaemia mechanism."},"img":"slides_i/L1_62.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Enterobius — three routes","s":"A 6-year-old has perianal itching worst at night. Three routine stool concentrations are negative. Which single statement about this infection is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":75,"c":"<b>Enterobius — three routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"The diagnostic stage is collected from PERIANAL SKIN by Scotch tape, because the embryonated egg is both the diagnostic and the infective stage and is deposited there","B":"Stool concentration is the correct test and three negatives exclude the diagnosis","D":"The infective stage is an unembryonated egg that must spend 2–3 weeks in soil","A":"Retroinfection means the gravid female crawls back up the bowel to lay eggs in the caecum","C":"Treatment is a single dose for the child alone; the family need not be treated"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is why the stool was negative — eggs are RARE in faeces. Enterobius is unusual in that one stage is both diagnostic and infective.","B":"This is the reasoning error the stem sets up: negative stool does not exclude enterobiasis, it is the expected result.","D":"Enterobius eggs contain a fully developed larva when laid — infective almost immediately. The soil period belongs to Ascaris and Trichuris.","A":"Retroinfection is the hatched LARVAE re-entering through the anus from the perianal skin, not the adult female reversing direction.","C":"The deck is explicit: treat the whole family and retreat after two weeks."},"img":"slides_i/L1_75.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Trichuris — anatomy to sign","s":"A 5-year-old presents with bloody mucous diarrhoea and tenesmus, and a prolapsed rectum studded with small whip-like worms. Which statement links this worm's anatomy to what you are seeing?","lec":"L1","sl":28,"c":"<b>Trichuris — anatomy to sign</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The narrow anterior three-fifths is threaded into the caecal and colonic mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage — hence dysentery and, with a heavy burden, prolapse","E":"The thick posterior three-fifths anchors in the mucosa while the thin anterior end hangs free in the lumen","B":"The worm sucks blood from submucosal vessels through a buccal capsule, which is why the stool is bloody","A":"The adult lives in the small intestine and the blood comes from villous destruction","D":"Prolapse occurs because the worms obstruct the lumen as an aggregated mass"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The deck specifies the narrow portion is 3/5 of the body and that it is EMBEDDED in large-bowel mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage.","E":"The proportions are inverted. The thin end is the anterior, burrowing end — that is what 'whipworm' describes: thin lash, thick handle.","B":"Trichuris has no blood-sucking buccal capsule; that is hookworm. The bleeding here is mucosal petechiae.","A":"Trichuris lives in the LARGE intestine, mainly caecal mucosa. Villous destruction in the small bowel is Capillaria.","D":"Aggregated worm masses causing obstruction is ASCARIS. Trichuris prolapse is from straining with heavy mucosal infestation."},"img":"slides_i/L1_28.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Culture methods","s":"Which statement about the culture methods in this lecture is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":45,"c":"<b>Culture methods</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Harada–Mori culture is used for both hookworm and Strongyloides and grows filariform larvae; the agar plate method is listed only for Strongyloides","D":"Harada–Mori is used only for hookworm; Strongyloides is cultured only on agar plates","C":"Both methods recover adult worms, which is how the two species are told apart","E":"Culture is used to embryonate Trichuris and Ascaris eggs so their infectivity can be confirmed","B":"Culture replaces stool microscopy because larvae are never seen directly in fresh stool"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The deck lists Harada–Mori under BOTH worms and adds the agar plate method under Strongyloides only.","D":"Half right. Harada–Mori appears under Strongyloides too, so restricting it to hookworm loses the mark.","C":"Both methods recover LARVAE. Species is then called on tail shape (pointed vs notched).","E":"No egg-embryonation culture appears anywhere in this lecture; Trichuris and Ascaris are diagnosed on eggs seen directly.","B":"Strongyloides rhabditiform larvae are exactly what you look for in direct stool examination — culture supplements it, it does not replace it."},"img":"slides_i/L1_45.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Diagnostic stage — the grid","s":"Which worm is correctly paired with the stage you would REPORT from the specimen (its diagnostic stage)?","lec":"L1","sl":82,"c":"<b>Diagnostic stage — the grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Strongyloides stercoralis — rhabditiform larva","B":"Hookworm — filariform larva","A":"Ascaris lumbricoides — embryonated egg","C":"Trichuris trichiura — embryonated egg","D":"Enterobius vermicularis — unembryonated egg"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Strongyloides is the one worm whose LARVA, not egg, appears in stool — the eggs hatch inside the bowel.","B":"Hookworm's diagnostic stage is the unembryonated 4–8 cell EGG. The filariform larva is its INFECTIVE stage, found in soil.","A":"Ascaris eggs are passed UNembryonated (fertilised, unfertilised or decorticated). The embryonated egg is the infective stage in soil.","C":"Trichuris is the same trap: unembryonated egg in stool, embryonated egg in soil.","D":"Enterobius is the reverse trap — its egg is EMBRYONATED when laid, which is why it is both diagnostic and infective."},"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"Host terminology — Capillaria","s":"For Capillaria philippinensis, which assignment of host roles is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":64,"c":"<b>Host terminology — Capillaria</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Definitive host humans; intermediate host small freshwater fish; reservoir host fish-eating birds","C":"Definitive host fish-eating birds; intermediate host humans; reservoir host small freshwater fish","D":"Definitive host humans; intermediate host fish-eating birds; reservoir host small freshwater fish","E":"Definitive host small freshwater fish; humans are an accidental host only","A":"There is no intermediate host — the infection is soil-transmitted like the other intestinal nematodes"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The fish carries the third-stage larva (intermediate), the bird maintains the parasite in nature without harm (reservoir), and the adult worm reproduces sexually in the human gut (definitive).","C":"Humans harbour the sexually reproducing adult, so humans cannot be the intermediate host.","D":"The roles of bird and fish are swapped. The larva develops in the FISH — that is what you eat.","E":"Humans are the definitive host here; calling them accidental is the Angiostrongylus/Gnathostoma pattern, not this one.","A":"Capillaria is the one FOOD-BORNE worm in this lecture — the deliberate exception to the soil-transmitted group."},"img":"slides_i/L1_64.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Prevention — matched to route","s":"Five patients each need ONE piece of prevention advice. Which pairing is right?","lec":"L1","sl":25,"c":"<b>Prevention — matched to route</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"A rice farmer with iron-deficiency anaemia and hookworm eggs in stool — wear shoes and gloves when working the soil","E":"A child with pruritus ani and Enterobius — wear shoes outdoors","C":"A man with chronic watery diarrhoea and Capillaria — wash vegetables before eating","D":"A woman vomiting a large cylindrical worm (Ascaris) — cook small freshwater fish thoroughly","A":"A child with rectal prolapse and Trichuris — avoid undercooked pork"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Hookworm enters by SKIN PENETRATION from soil, so the advice must block skin–soil contact.","E":"Enterobius is person-to-person, air-borne and retroinfection — the advice is short fingernails and handwashing, plus treating the whole family.","C":"Capillaria is food-borne from raw small freshwater FISH; washing vegetables is the advice for the egg-ingested worms.","D":"Ascaris is acquired by swallowing embryonated eggs from soil — wash hands and vegetables. The fish advice belongs to Capillaria.","A":"Pork appears nowhere in this lecture; Trichuris is acquired by ingesting embryonated eggs from soil."},"img":"slides_i/L1_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Ectopic ascariasis","s":"A child with a heavy Ascaris burden develops right upper quadrant pain and jaundice. Which statement best fits?","lec":"L1","sl":20,"c":"<b>Ectopic ascariasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Adult worms have migrated ectopically into the common bile duct; ultrasound or CT is the imaging that demonstrates it","B":"Larvae migrating through the liver have caused Loeffler's syndrome; chest radiography is the investigation","A":"An aggregated mass of worms is obstructing the small bowel; stool examination will be negative","C":"The eggs have embolised to the liver, as in schistosomiasis; a liver biopsy is needed","E":"This is impossible — Ascaris adults remain in the small intestinal lumen throughout"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The deck names ectopic ascariasis to nose, mouth and common bile duct, and lists endoscopy, CT, ultrasound and MRI as diagnostic methods alongside stool.","B":"Loeffler's syndrome is the LUNG phase and presents with cough and wheeze, not obstructive jaundice.","A":"Obstruction is real in heavy infection but presents as small-bowel obstruction, not jaundice — and stool would still show eggs.","C":"Egg embolisation to the liver is the SCHISTOSOMA mechanism, from a different lecture.","E":"Ectopic migration of adults is explicitly listed, so 'impossible' is false — this is the option for anyone who only memorised 'lumen of small intestine'."},"img":"slides_i/L1_20.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Orientation","s":"What single property separates every worm in this lecture from every worm in L1, and what does it change about diagnosis?","lec":"L2","sl":2,"c":"<b>Orientation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"They live in tissue, blood or the nervous system rather than the gut lumen, so stool examination is useless","B":"They are all transmitted by mosquitoes rather than by soil, so blood films replace stool","C":"They are all acquired from raw food, so a dietary history replaces the laboratory","E":"They are all zoonotic, so animal serology replaces human testing","D":"They are all treated with ivermectin, so the diagnosis rarely matters"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — and the BM33 deck says so by re-showing L1's chart with 'diagnosed by stool examination' under one column and this lecture under the other. No adult lays eggs into the bowel lumen, so every diagnostic method has to change: blood film, muscle biopsy, serology, CSF.","B":"Only the filarial worms are mosquito-borne; Gnathostoma, Angiostrongylus and Trichinella are food-borne.","C":"Three of the five are food-borne, but the filarial worms are not.","E":"Several are zoonotic, but that is not what unites them and it does not change the specimen.","D":"Treatment differs between them, and it is not the organising idea."},"img":"slides_i/L2_2.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Angiostrongylus","s":"A patient develops severe headache, neck stiffness and paraesthesiae after eating raw Pila snails. CSF shows eosinophilic pleocytosis. What is the parasite, and what is the human's host status?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Wuchereria bancrofti — the human is the definitive host","B":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis — the human is the definitive host and adults develop in the lung","E":"Gnathostoma spinigerum — the human is the definitive host","A":"Trichinella spiralis — the human is an accidental host","D":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis — the human is an accidental host in which the larva can never mature"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Wuchereria is mosquito-borne and causes lymphatic obstruction.","B":"The lung stage belongs to the RAT's cycle, not the human's.","E":"Gnathostoma also makes humans accidental hosts, but its signature is migratory swelling, and the snail history points to Angiostrongylus.","A":"Trichinella comes from raw pork or wild boar and lodges in striated muscle.","D":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva migrates to the CNS, cannot complete development and dies there, producing eosinophilic meningitis."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Trichinella","s":"A man develops diarrhoea, then muscle swelling and tenderness in the limbs with periorbital and facial oedema, two weeks after a wedding feast of raw wild boar. What is the diagnostic stage, and in what specimen?","lec":"L2","sl":26,"c":"<b>Trichinella</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Larvae in cerebrospinal fluid","A":"Adult worms in stool","C":"Microfilariae on a night blood film","D":"Eggs in stool by concentration technique","B":"Encysted larvae in striated muscle, found on muscle biopsy"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"CSF larvae would point to Angiostrongylus.","A":"Adults are not recovered from stool in practice, and the gut phase is brief.","C":"Microfilariae belong to the filarial worms.","D":"Trichinella females are viviparous — they release larvae, not eggs, and nothing appears in stool.","B":"Correct. Trichinella's adults are transient in the gut; the larvae encyst in striated muscle inside a nurse cell, and that is what a biopsy shows. The sequence — enteric phase, then myositis with periorbital oedema — is the classic story."},"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Filariasis","s":"A 50-year-old man in Narathiwat has fever and enlarged lymph nodes. What is the mode of transmission of the most likely parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Ingestion of undercooked freshwater fish","A":"The bite of an infected Mansonia mosquito, which deposits L3 larvae that penetrate the bite wound","D":"Skin penetration by cercariae in water","C":"Injection of sporozoites by a female Anopheles","E":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Fish-borne infections give hepatobiliary or intestinal disease, not lymphadenopathy.","A":"Correct. Southern Thailand plus fever with lymphadenopathy points to Brugia malayi, whose vector is Mansonia. Note the verb: filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED on the skin and penetrate the bite wound — they are not injected.","D":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma (L4).","C":"Sporozoite INJECTION is Plasmodium — and malaria characteristically has neither rash nor lymphadenopathy, which is what separates it here.","E":"Soil-transmitted eggs give intestinal nematodes."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Gnathostoma","s":"Which clinical picture is characteristic of Gnathostoma spinigerum, and how is it acquired?","lec":"L2","sl":7,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Migratory subcutaneous swelling, from third-stage larvae in raw freshwater fish, eel or frog","E":"Fixed subcutaneous nodules, from filariform larvae penetrating skin","A":"Eosinophilic meningitis, from metacercariae in raw crab","D":"Lymphoedema of the leg, from L3 larvae deposited by a mosquito","C":"Periorbital oedema with myositis, from encysted larvae in pork"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The larva wanders, so the swelling MOVES — the single most useful clinical clue. Cyclops is the first intermediate host and a fish, eel or frog the second.","E":"A fixed nodule is not the pattern, and skin penetration is not the route.","A":"Eosinophilic meningitis is Angiostrongylus, and the meal is a snail, not a crab.","D":"Lymphoedema is filarial.","C":"That is Trichinella."},"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Filariasis","s":"Which mosquito genus transmits which filarial species in Thailand?","lec":"L2","sl":33,"c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Mansonia transmits Brugia malayi; Culex, Aedes and Anopheles transmit Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Anopheles transmits Brugia malayi; Mansonia transmits Wuchereria bancrofti","C":"Aedes transmits Brugia malayi; Culex transmits Wuchereria bancrofti","A":"All four genera transmit both species equally","B":"Only Culex transmits either species"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and this single split is what every vector-matching question in the archive is built on. Brugia malayi is also concentrated in southern Thailand, matching Mansonia's distribution.","D":"Reversed — and this is the commonest wrong pairing offered.","C":"Aedes carries W. bancrofti, not B. malayi.","A":"The species split is the examinable point.","B":"Culex is one of three W. bancrofti vectors."},"img":"slides_i/L2_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Diagnosis","s":"When should blood be drawn to look for microfilariae of Wuchereria bancrofti, and why?","lec":"L2","sl":40,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Immediately after exercise, which mobilises microfilariae from the lungs","D":"At midday, because the microfilariae accumulate in peripheral blood during activity","A":"At any time, because the microfilariae are present continuously","C":"At night, because the microfilariae show nocturnal periodicity matching the vector's biting time","B":"Only after treatment, which releases microfilariae into the circulation"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Exercise is not the trigger.","D":"The opposite of nocturnal periodicity.","A":"If that were true the timing question would not exist.","C":"Correct. The microfilariae appear in peripheral blood when the vector feeds, so a daytime film can be falsely negative in a genuinely infected patient. This is the one timing fact the papers set.","B":"Treatment kills microfilariae rather than releasing them, and diagnosis precedes treatment."},"img":"slides_i/L2_40.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Verbs","s":"Three parasites enter through the skin by three different mechanisms. Which set is correctly matched?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Verbs</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"All three are injected by their arthropod vector","A":"Plasmodium sporozoites penetrate intact skin; filarial larvae are injected; Schistosoma cercariae are deposited","B":"Plasmodium sporozoites are INJECTED; filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED and penetrate the bite wound; Schistosoma cercariae PENETRATE INTACT SKIN","D":"All three penetrate intact skin from water","E":"Plasmodium is deposited; filarial larvae penetrate intact skin; Schistosoma is injected"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Schistosoma has no arthropod vector at all.","A":"Every verb is misassigned.","B":"Correct, and the papers use these three verbs as distractors for each other — BM31's Leishmania question offered 'injection of sporozoite during mosquito's blood meals' as a wrong option in a non-malaria stem.","D":"Only Schistosoma does this.","E":"All three are wrong."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Angiostrongylus","s":"Which parasite has the human as an ACCIDENTAL host?","lec":"L2","sl":23,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Brugia malayi","B":"Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides","E":"Trichuris trichiura"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Same for B. malayi.","B":"Humans are the definitive host of W. bancrofti — the adults live in human lymphatics and reproduce there.","D":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva reaches the CNS and dies without maturing. Gnathostoma is the other accidental-host parasite in this lecture.","C":"Humans are the definitive host of Ascaris.","E":"Humans are the definitive host of Trichuris."},"img":"slides_i/L2_23.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q23","t":"Trichinella","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with diarrhoea, muscular hypertrophy, tenderness in the extremities and facial oedema. He ate raw wild boar two weeks ago at a relative's wedding. What is the diagnostic stage?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>Trichinella</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q23 — raw wild boar, myositis, facial oedema</span>","o":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae in stool","B":"Adult worms in stool","C":"Microfilariae in blood","E":"Embryonated eggs in stool","D":"Encysted larvae in muscle"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae in stool is Strongyloides (L1).","B":"The intestinal phase is brief and adults are not the diagnostic finding.","C":"Microfilariae belong to the filarial worms.","E":"Trichinella females release LARVAE, not eggs, and nothing reaches the stool.","D":"Correct. Trichinella larvae encyst inside a nurse cell in striated muscle; muscle biopsy is the diagnostic specimen, and the answer key describes the nurse cell as oval with pink calcification."},"img":"slides_i/L2_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q12","t":"Filariasis","s":"A 50-year-old man from Narathiwat has fever and enlarged lymph nodes. What is the mode of transmission of the most likely parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q12 — Narathiwat, fever with lymphadenopathy</span>","o":{"D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork","E":"Injection of sporozoites during an Anopheles blood meal","A":"Ingestion of raw freshwater fish","B":"Skin penetration by cercariae in fresh water","C":"The bite of an infected Mansonia mosquito"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Pork gives Taenia solium or Trichinella.","E":"Malaria is the designed near-miss for any 'fever in an endemic area' stem — but malaria characteristically has NO rash and NO lymphadenopathy, which is exactly why this stem mentions nodes.","A":"Fish-borne parasites give hepatobiliary or intestinal disease.","B":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma.","C":"Correct. Southern Thailand plus fever and lymphadenopathy is Brugia malayi, whose vector is Mansonia."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-angio","t":"Angiostrongylus","s":"A patient has a history of eating Pila spp. (apple snail). What infection is most likely?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 เฉลย SUM1 — history of eating Pila spp. (หอยเชอรี่)</span>","o":{"B":"Fasciolopsis buski","A":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Gnathostoma spinigerum","E":"Opisthorchis viverrini","C":"Paragonimus spp."},"a":"A","r":{"B":"F. buski comes from raw aquatic PLANTS such as water chestnut.","A":"Correct. The apple snail carries the third-stage larva of A. cantonensis, and the human is an accidental host in whom the larva reaches the CNS and causes eosinophilic meningitis.","D":"Gnathostoma comes from raw fish, eel or frog and gives migratory swelling.","E":"O. viverrini comes from raw cyprinoid fish and goes to the bile ducts.","C":"Paragonimus comes from raw or pickled crab and goes to the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-accidental","t":"Accidental host","s":"Which of the following parasites has the human as an ACCIDENTAL host?","lec":"L2","sl":23,"c":"<b>Accidental host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — 'which parasite has a human as an accidental host?'</span>","o":{"D":"Brugia malayi","A":"Necator americanus","C":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","B":"Taenia saginata","E":"Paragonimus spp."},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Humans are the definitive host of B. malayi.","A":"Humans are the definitive host of hookworm — adults live and reproduce in the human gut.","C":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva cannot mature and dies in the CNS.","B":"Humans are the definitive host of T. saginata.","E":"Humans are the definitive host of Paragonimus — the adults live in the human lung."},"img":"slides_i/L2_23.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-infstage","t":"Infective stage","s":"Which statement about the infective stage is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Infective stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — Wuchereria vs skin-penetrating larvae</span>","o":{"A":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by skin penetration of filariform larvae from soil","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects when the mosquito injects microfilariae directly into the blood","E":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects when the mosquito deposits third-stage larvae that enter through the bite wound","B":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by ingestion of embryonated eggs","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by ingestion of larvae in undercooked fish"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"That is hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Microfilariae are what the mosquito takes UP from the human, not what it puts back.","E":"Correct, and the formative's own key makes the contrast explicit: Schistosoma, hookworm and Strongyloides have larvae that PENETRATE SKIN, whereas Wuchereria has larvae DEPOSITED BY THE VECTOR at the bite site.","B":"Filarial worms have no egg stage outside the female.","C":"No fish stage exists in this cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-larva","t":"Larval stages","s":"Which larval stage is the INFECTIVE stage of hookworm?","lec":"L2","sl":16,"c":"<b>Larval stages</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — filariform vs rhabditiform larva</span>","o":{"B":"Rhabditiform larva","C":"Filariform larva","D":"Third-stage larva in a fish","E":"Encysted larva in muscle","A":"Microfilaria"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"The rhabditiform larva is the feeding, non-infective stage — it is what hatches from the egg in the soil, and what you find in fresh stool in strongyloidiasis.","C":"Correct. The filariform (L3) larva is the slender, non-feeding, skin-penetrating stage.","D":"That is Capillaria or Gnathostoma.","E":"That is Trichinella.","A":"That is the filarial worms' blood stage, which infects the MOSQUITO rather than a human."},"img":"slides_i/L2_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q25","t":"Gnathostoma","s":"A patient has recurrent, INTERMITTENT MIGRATORY SWELLING of the face and limbs. He regularly eats improperly cooked eel. What is the most likely parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q25 (improperly cooked eel + recurrent facial swelling) · BM28 SUM1 Q35 (intermittent migratory swelling)</span>","o":{"C":"Paragonimus spp.","E":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Trichinella spiralis","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Gnathostoma spinigerum"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Paragonimus goes to the lung.","E":"Angiostrongylus also comes from undercooked food but goes to the CNS — eosinophilic meningitis, not skin swellings.","D":"Trichinella causes FIXED periorbital oedema and myalgia after raw pork or wild boar; it does not migrate from place to place. That fixed-versus-migratory contrast is the point of the item.","B":"Opisthorchis stays in the bile ducts.","A":"Correct. 'Migratory swelling that comes and goes' = cutaneous gnathostomiasis; the third-stage larva wanders through subcutaneous tissue. Eel, snakehead fish, frog and chicken are the classic sources."},"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q35"]},{"id":"I|L2|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q9","t":"Angiostrongylus — CSF","s":"A 30-year-old man has severe headache and neck stiffness. He ate Pila spp. (apple snail). CSF analysis shows a raised EOSINOPHIL count. Which parasite is responsible?","lec":"L2","sl":18,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — CSF</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q9 — Pila spp. snail + severe headache + neck stiffness + eosinophils in CSF</span>","o":{"D":"Capillaria philippinensis","E":"Trichinella spiralis","C":"Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","A":"Gnathostoma spinigerum"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Capillaria is an intestinal worm causing chronic watery diarrhoea.","E":"Trichinella affects striated muscle, not meninges.","C":"Enterobius stays in the large bowel.","B":"Correct. Eosinophilic meningitis + apple snail is Angiostrongylus, the rat lungworm. Humans are an ACCIDENTAL host, so the immature worm dies in the CNS and no eggs or larvae are ever found in stool — and larvae are only rarely seen in CSF.","A":"Gnathostoma is the other cause of eosinophilic meningitis — but its history is eel or raw fish, and it usually gives migratory swellings or radiculomyelitis rather than this snail history. Both are acceptable neurological causes; the SNAIL decides it."},"img":"slides_i/L2_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q47","t":"Trichinella — source","s":"A 62-year-old man has fever and muscle pain. A MUSCLE BIOPSY shows a coiled larva inside a nurse cell. Where did he acquire it?","lec":"L2","sl":26,"c":"<b>Trichinella — source</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q47 — 62-year-old with fever and muscle pain; muscle biopsy shown; what is the source?</span>","o":{"E":"Eating poorly cooked pork or wild boar","D":"Eating unwashed vegetables","B":"Smoking","A":"Walking barefoot on contaminated soil","C":"Drinking untreated water"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A larva encysted in striated muscle is Trichinella spiralis, and the infective stage is the ENCYSTED LARVA in raw or undercooked pork, wild boar or other game meat.","D":"Vegetables carry Ascaris and Trichuris eggs — intestinal, never in muscle.","B":"Smoking is a distractor with no parasitic route.","A":"Skin penetration is hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Waterborne routes give protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba."},"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM31 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L2|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Microfilaria discriminator","s":"A night blood film from a patient in Narathiwat shows a sheathed microfilaria, tightly coiled, with a long empty head space, a nuclear column so densely packed that individual nuclei cannot be resolved, and TWO isolated nuclei at the very tip of the tail. Which species is it, and what does that predict about the limb involvement?","lec":"L2","sl":35,"c":"<b>Microfilaria discriminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Brugia malayi — disease below the knee and below the elbow, with the genitalia typically spared","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti — disease in the lower limbs, arm, breast and genitalia","E":"Brugia malayi — genital elephantiasis is the hallmark","A":"Wuchereria bancrofti — the two terminal nuclei are its defining feature","B":"Neither: terminal nuclei mean the microfilaria is unsheathed and therefore not a lymphatic filaria"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Two isolated TERMINAL nuclei + tightly coiled + long head space = B. malayi, and the deck says Brugian filariasis spares the genitals and affects leg below knee / arm below elbow.","C":"W. bancrofti has NO terminal nuclei, a SMALL head space, and separated nuclei that can be counted individually. The clinical half describes bancroftian disease correctly, which is the bait.","E":"Species right, clinics wrong — genital involvement is rare in Brugian filariasis. This is the commonest way to half-answer this item.","A":"Straight inversion of the tail feature. W. bancrofti's tail 'exhibits no terminal nuclei'.","B":"Both species are SHEATHED; terminal nuclei and sheathing are independent features."},"img":"slides_i/L2_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Eosinophilic CNS disease — which worm","s":"Two patients both have eosinophils in the CSF. Patient 1 ate raw snails and has severe headache with neck stiffness. Patient 2 ate raw eel, has had intermittent migratory swellings for months, and now has radiculomyelitis with a subarachnoid haemorrhage. Which pairing is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>Eosinophilic CNS disease — which worm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Patient 1 Angiostrongylus cantonensis, patient 2 Gnathostoma spinigerum","C":"Patient 1 Gnathostoma spinigerum, patient 2 Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Both are Angiostrongylus cantonensis; migratory swelling is an early phase of the same disease","A":"Both are Gnathostoma spinigerum; snails are its second intermediate host","B":"Patient 1 Angiostrongylus cantonensis, patient 2 Trichinella spiralis"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Snail + eosinophilic meningitis = rat lungworm. Migratory swelling + haemorrhagic CNS disease = Gnathostoma, which is the one that bleeds.","C":"The exposures are swapped. Snails are Angiostrongylus's intermediate host; eels are Gnathostoma's second intermediate host.","D":"Intermittent migratory swelling is a GNATHOSTOMA sign and appears nowhere under Angiostrongylus.","A":"Gnathostoma's second intermediate hosts are freshwater fish, eels and frogs — not snails.","B":"Trichinella causes myalgia and periorbital oedema, not CSF eosinophilia with subarachnoid haemorrhage."},"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Gnathostoma — three modes of infection","s":"A restaurant cook who says she never eats raw food develops intermittent migratory swelling of the forearm. Which statement explains how she could have acquired Gnathostoma spinigerum?","lec":"L2","sl":7,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — three modes of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Third-stage larvae from the infected meal she was preparing penetrated her skin directly while she handled it","E":"She must have eaten the food; skin penetration is not a recognised route for this worm","B":"Filariform larvae in soil penetrated her feet, as in hookworm","A":"Eggs on her hands were swallowed after handling raw fish","C":"A mosquito inoculated third-stage larvae while she worked"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The deck lists THREE routes: ingestion of the raw second intermediate or paratenic host, SKIN PENETRATION in a food handler from the infected meal, and drinking water containing third-stage larvae in Cyclops.","E":"This is the answer of someone who memorised only 'food-borne'. The food-handler route is explicitly on the slide.","B":"Filariform larvae in soil is hookworm/Strongyloides. Gnathostoma's infective stage is the THIRD-STAGE larva in an animal host.","A":"Gnathostoma is never acquired from eggs — humans are an accidental host and no egg stage reaches us.","C":"Mosquito inoculation is the FILARIAL route (W. bancrofti, B. malayi), not Gnathostoma."},"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Why stool is useless","s":"Why does the lecture group these worms as ones that 'cannot be diagnosed by stool examination'?","lec":"L2","sl":2,"c":"<b>Why stool is useless</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"In Gnathostoma and Angiostrongylus humans are an ACCIDENTAL host, so the third-stage larva never matures into an egg-laying adult; in Trichinella and the filariae the diagnostic stage sits in muscle or blood, not in the gut lumen","C":"Because their eggs are too small to be seen by routine concentration techniques","B":"Because all four are transmitted by vectors, and vector-borne parasites never appear in stool","D":"Because all of them live exclusively in the lymphatic system","A":"Because their adults live in the small intestine but release larvae only into the bloodstream"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and the two halves have different reasons — accidental-host biology for the first two, anatomical location of the diagnostic stage for the others.","C":"Size is irrelevant; there are no eggs to find. This is the trap for anyone who assumes a technique problem.","B":"Only the filariae are vector-borne. Gnathostoma, Angiostrongylus and Trichinella are all food-borne.","D":"Only W. bancrofti and B. malayi are lymphatic. Trichinella encysts in striated muscle; Angiostrongylus larvae end up in CSF.","A":"Trichinella's adult IS in the small intestine, but the point is that its diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in muscle — the option generalises a half-truth to all four."},"img":"slides_i/L2_2.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Angiostrongylus — treatment trap","s":"A man with confirmed eosinophilic meningitis from Angiostrongylus cantonensis has severe headache. Which management plan matches the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":21,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — treatment trap</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Supportive care, repeated lumbar puncture to relieve intracranial pressure and corticosteroids; anthelmintics are usually not given","E":"High-dose albendazole immediately, to kill the larvae before they burrow further","B":"Ivermectin plus surgical removal of the worm from the CSF","C":"Diethylcarbamazine, as for the other tissue nematodes","A":"No treatment of any kind, because the illness is self-limited"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the reasoning matters: killing larvae inside the CNS worsens the inflammatory response, so the deck steers to pressure relief and steroids.","E":"This is the reflex answer and the deck explicitly contradicts it — 'antihelminthic drugs are usually not used'.","B":"Surgical removal is mentioned for GNATHOSTOMA, and even there it is noted as difficult. Larvae are rarely even detected in CSF.","C":"DEC is the filariasis drug.","A":"The illness is self-limited, but the deck still lists active supportive measures — LP and corticosteroids. 'No treatment of any kind' overshoots."},"img":"slides_i/L2_21.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Trichinella — laboratory panel","s":"A man from the North of Thailand who ate raw boar two weeks ago now has fever, periorbital oedema and severe myalgia. Which set of investigations would confirm the diagnosis?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"<b>Trichinella — laboratory panel</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Muscle biopsy showing encysted larvae, ELISA/immunoblot serology, eosinophilia of 30–80%, and raised LDH, CK and AST","D":"Stool examination for encysted larvae, plus eosinophilia","E":"CSF analysis for eosinophils and larvae, plus raised CK","C":"Thick and thin blood smears for larvae, plus serology","B":"Colonoscopy with biopsy of the small-bowel mucosa, where the adult worms live"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — all four elements are on the slide, and the muscle enzymes are the detail most people omit because the myalgia feels self-explanatory.","D":"Encysted larvae are in MUSCLE, not stool. Trichinella cannot be diagnosed from stool at all.","E":"CSF eosinophilia belongs to Angiostrongylus and Gnathostoma.","C":"Blood smears for larvae is the FILARIASIS method; Trichinella larvae are not in peripheral blood.","B":"The adults are in small-intestinal mucosa, but the deck's diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle — biopsying the gut is not the listed method."},"img":"slides_i/L2_28.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Gnathostoma — hooklet rows","s":"A worm is teased out of a migrating skin swelling and shows a cephalic bulb bearing FOUR rows of hooklets. What is it, and why is that consistent with the host?","lec":"L2","sl":5,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — hooklet rows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"A third-stage larva — humans are an accidental host, so the parasite cannot develop past L3 into the 8-row adult","E":"An adult worm — the adult carries 4 rows and the larva 8","D":"A third-stage larva that will mature into an adult in the human stomach wall within weeks","C":"An adult female, identified by the cephalic bulb, which only adults possess","A":"An encysted larva, as in Trichinella"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on both halves. FOUR rows = L3; EIGHT rows = adult; and the adult only forms in cats, dogs and wild felines, where it lives in a tumour in the stomach wall.","E":"The counts are inverted — this is the whole discriminator, so inverting it is the intended miss.","D":"Humans are accidental hosts. The larva wanders but never matures, which is why stool is useless and why the swellings keep migrating.","C":"BOTH the adult and the L3 have a cephalic bulb; only the hooklet row count separates them.","A":"Encystment in muscle is Trichinella; Gnathostoma larvae migrate rather than encyst."},"img":"slides_i/L2_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Filarial vectors","s":"Which statement about the mosquito vectors of lymphatic filariasis is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":37,"c":"<b>Filarial vectors</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Wuchereria bancrofti is carried by Culex, Mansonia, Anopheles and Aedes, whereas Brugia malayi is carried by Mansonia and Anopheles","D":"Both species are carried by the same four genera, which is why they overlap geographically","B":"Brugia malayi is the broader of the two, adding Culex and Aedes to Mansonia and Anopheles","E":"Both are carried only by Anopheles, which is also the malaria vector","A":"Aedes carries Brugia malayi; Culex carries Wuchereria bancrofti; the other genera are not vectors"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct — the wider vector range is part of why bancroftian filariasis is pan-tropical while Brugian filariasis is confined to South and East Asia.","D":"The deck's table gives DIFFERENT lists for the two species; the difference is the examinable point.","B":"The breadth is inverted. W. bancrofti has the longer list.","E":"Anopheles carries both, but it is not the only vector of either.","A":"Aedes is listed for W. bancrofti, not B. malayi, and cutting the lists to one genus each discards Mansonia, which matters for Brugia."},"img":"slides_i/L2_37.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Angiostrongylus — host roles","s":"Which set of host roles for Angiostrongylus cantonensis is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — host roles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Rodents definitive (adults in the pulmonary arteries); Pila spp. and Achatina fulica intermediate; freshwater shrimp, crabs, frogs and monitors paratenic; humans accidental","D":"Humans definitive; rodents accidental; snails intermediate; crabs paratenic","C":"Rodents definitive; freshwater shrimp intermediate; snails paratenic; humans accidental","E":"Rodents definitive (adults in the stomach wall); Cyclops intermediate; snails paratenic","A":"Snails definitive; rodents intermediate; humans accidental"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the common name 'rat LUNG worm' encodes the habitat you are asked for.","D":"Humans never support the adult — that is precisely why the larva ends up lost in the CNS.","C":"Snail and shrimp roles are swapped. The larva DEVELOPS in the snail (intermediate); the shrimp merely carries it unchanged (paratenic).","E":"Stomach-wall habitat and Cyclops belong to GNATHOSTOMA. This option imports that worm's life cycle wholesale.","A":"A snail cannot be the definitive host; no sexual reproduction occurs there."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Trichinella — who is the definitive host","s":"Which statement about Trichinella spiralis in humans is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":26,"c":"<b>Trichinella — who is the definitive host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Humans are a definitive host — adults live in small-intestinal mucosa — yet the diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle, not anything in stool","D":"Humans are an accidental host, like in gnathostomiasis, so the larva never matures","E":"Humans are an intermediate host and pigs are the definitive host","B":"The adult lives in striated muscle and releases larvae into the gut","C":"Both the infective and the diagnostic stage are the encysted larva in pork"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the subtlety of this worm: being a definitive host does NOT mean stool is diagnostic, because it is the larva you detect and it has left the gut.","D":"The learning-guidance slide lists humans among the definitive hosts alongside pigs, boar, bears, wild felines and foxes.","E":"Inverted, and it collides with the slide's list.","B":"The reverse: adults in the intestinal mucosa, larvae encysted in muscle.","C":"Infective stage = encysted larvae in PORK; diagnostic stage = encysted larvae in HUMAN striated muscle. Same stage, different host — and the exam asks them separately."},"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Filariasis — treatment and control","s":"A district is running a lymphatic filariasis programme. Which combination matches the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":41,"c":"<b>Filariasis — treatment and control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Diethylcarbamazine with supportive treatment for cases; prevention by avoiding mosquito bites; control by mass treatment and vector control aimed at larvae and pupae","D":"Albendazole alone for cases; control by eradicating rats and snails","B":"Ivermectin for cases; prevention by wearing shoes","E":"Praziquantel for cases; control by sanitary disposal of faeces","A":"Corticosteroids for cases; control by closed-system livestock farming"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on all three limbs, and note the vector control targets the AQUATIC stages, not the adult mosquito.","D":"Eradicating rats and snails is Angiostrongylus control.","B":"Wearing shoes blocks soil-penetrating larvae — hookworm and Strongyloides, from L1.","E":"Praziquantel and sanitary disposal belong to the fluke and soil-transmitted lectures.","A":"Closed-system livestock farming is the TRICHINELLA control measure."},"img":"slides_i/L2_41.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Geography as a clue","s":"Which Thai geographical pairing matches the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":14,"c":"<b>Geography as a clue</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Brugia malayi from Chumphon down to Narathiwat; Wuchereria bancrofti in the border provinces such as Tak, Kanchanaburi, Ratchaburi and Ranong","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti from Chumphon down to Narathiwat; Brugia malayi in the border provinces","E":"Trichinella spiralis is concentrated in the North-east and Angiostrongylus in the North","A":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis does not occur in Thailand","B":"Gnathostoma spinigerum is restricted to Africa"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The southern peninsula belt is Brugian; the western border provinces are Bancroftian, tied to migrant populations.","D":"Straight swap of the two distributions.","E":"Inverted: Trichinella is the NORTH, Angiostrongylus the NORTH-EAST.","A":"The deck names Thailand's North-east region specifically for Angiostrongylus.","B":"Gnathostoma is Southeast Asia, South and Central America, and some parts of Africa — Southeast Asia first."},"img":"slides_i/L2_14.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Trichinella — control","s":"Which statement about trichinellosis management and control is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":30,"c":"<b>Trichinella — control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole or mebendazole is given together with prednisolone, and control rests on proper cooking plus closed-system livestock farming","E":"Albendazole is given alone; steroids are contraindicated because they cause hyperinfection","D":"No drug is used because the larvae are already encysted; only analgesia is given","C":"Ivermectin plus surgical excision of encysted larvae","B":"Diethylcarbamazine, because the larvae are tissue-dwelling"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The steroid is there to damp the inflammatory reaction to dying larvae in muscle.","E":"Corticosteroid-driven hyperinfection is the STRONGYLOIDES warning from L1. In trichinellosis prednisolone is part of the regimen.","D":"The deck lists active drug treatment, so 'no drug' is wrong.","C":"Excision of encysted muscle larvae is not a listed treatment.","B":"DEC belongs to filariasis."},"img":"slides_i/L2_30.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Taeniasis vs cysticercosis","s":"A patient has a painless cystic lesion removed surgically; inside is a larva with a scolex and hooks. What was the mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"<b>Taeniasis vs cysticercosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of raw pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae","C":"Ingestion of fruit or vegetables contaminated with Taenia solium EGGS","D":"Ingestion of raw beef containing Cysticercus bovis","B":"Ingestion of undercooked fish containing plerocercoid larvae","E":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The designed trap, and the single commonest error in the whole lecture. Raw pork containing a cysticercus gives you an ADULT WORM in the gut — taeniasis, not cysticercosis. Same worm, opposite end of the cycle.","C":"Correct. Cysticercosis is what happens when a human swallows the EGG and becomes an accidental intermediate host — the larva then encysts in tissue.","D":"T. saginata gives taeniasis only. Humans are not a permissive intermediate host for it, and the learning-guidance table prints a dash in that cell.","B":"Plerocercoid in fish is Diphyllobothrium latum, which gives an adult worm.","E":"No cestode enters through skin."},"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Species identification","s":"A gravid proglottid is injected with Indian ink and shows 20 uterine branches on one side of the main stem. Which species, and what is the safe way to remember it?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Species identification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Hymenolepis nana — the count applies to all cyclophyllideans","A":"Taenia solium — pork worms have more branches (>15)","B":"Taenia saginata — but only if the count is under 15","E":"Diphyllobothrium latum — its proglottids are wider than long","D":"Taenia saginata — cow is bigger than pig, so beef gets the bigger number (>15)"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"The count is a Taenia discrimination only.","A":"Reversed. T. solium has FEWER than 15.","B":"The threshold direction is wrong.","E":"D. latum's proglottids are indeed wider than long, but the branch count is not how it is identified, and it is not the organism described.","D":"Correct. >15 = T. saginata, <15 = T. solium. Everything about saginata is 'more' — longer worm, more proglottids, more branches."},"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Larval stages","s":"Which larval stage goes with which host?","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"<b>Larval stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"All four stages occur in the same intermediate host","E":"Cysticercoid in mammal muscle; cysticercus in an insect; plerocercoid in Cyclops","D":"Cysticercus in Cyclops; plerocercoid in an insect; procercoid in fish","A":"Cysticercus in mammal muscle (Taenia); cysticercoid in an insect (Hymenolepis); procercoid in Cyclops and plerocercoid in fish (Diphyllobothrium)","C":"Sparganum in mammal muscle; cysticercus in fish; procercoid in an insect"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Each stage is host-specific, which is exactly what the options test.","E":"The first two are swapped and the third is wrong.","D":"All three are misassigned.","A":"Correct. Two memory hooks: the '-oid' in cysticercoid carries the I of Insect; and 'Cyclops gets the PRO, the fish gets the PLERO'.","C":"Sparganum is the plerocercoid of Spirometra in HUMAN tissue, not in mammal muscle as part of a normal cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L3_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Hymenolepis","s":"Which set of statements about the two Hymenolepis species is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":25,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Neither autoinfects; both spread only through insects","B":"H. diminuta autoinfects and spreads person-to-person; H. nana needs an intermediate host","C":"Both autoinfect, and both require an insect intermediate host","D":"H. nana autoinfects, spreads person-to-person and needs no intermediate host; H. diminuta does none of these","E":"H. nana requires an intermediate host and H. diminuta spreads person-to-person"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Nana autoinfects.","B":"Completely reversed.","C":"Only nana autoinfects, and only diminuta needs the insect.","D":"Correct, and every line is the opposite of what intuition suggests. The dwarf tapeworm has the smaller egg but the extra features — and the 2569 slide adds that H. nana has TWO infective stages: the embryonated egg AND the cysticercoid.","E":"Reversed."},"img":"slides_i/L3_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Egg morphology","s":"Which egg description belongs to which cestode?","lec":"L3","sl":10,"c":"<b>Egg morphology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Radial striation = Taenia spp.; 4–8 polar filaments = H. nana; operculum with a terminal knob at 58–76 µm = D. latum","B":"Radial striation = H. nana; polar filaments = Taenia; operculum = H. diminuta","E":"Polar filaments = Taenia; radial striation = D. latum; operculum = H. nana","C":"All cestode eggs are operculated","D":"Radial striation = D. latum; operculum = Taenia; polar filaments = H. diminuta"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. And note what radial striation does NOT give you: it identifies the GENUS only. The species must come from the meal, a proglottid or PCR.","B":"All three are misassigned.","E":"All three are misassigned.","C":"D. latum is the ONLY operculated cestode egg. In L4 almost every fluke egg is operculated, which is where the confusion is engineered.","D":"All three are misassigned."},"img":"slides_i/L3_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Operculated eggs","s":"Two operculated eggs with a terminal knob are shown. One is 58–76 × 40–51 µm and unembryonated; the other is 25 × 12 µm and embryonated with a prominent shoulder. What are they, and what actually decides it?","lec":"L3","sl":36,"c":"<b>Operculated eggs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Both are D. latum at different maturities","C":"The big one is Opisthorchis viverrini and the small one is D. latum — shape decides","A":"The big unembryonated one is Diphyllobothrium latum, the small embryonated one is Opisthorchis viverrini — SIZE decides, because both have an operculum and a knob","E":"Both are Opisthorchis; the size difference is an artefact of focusing","B":"The presence of a knob identifies D. latum uniquely, so size is irrelevant"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"They are different organisms in different lectures, and only one is embryonated when passed.","C":"Reversed. D. latum is the big one; O. viverrini is 25 x 12 micrometres.","A":"Correct, and the lecturer flagged this cross-lecture trap herself: 'remember this size — in the afternoon you will meet an egg that also has an operculum and a knob, but is half as big.' Both features are shared, so MEASURE.","E":"The size difference is real and is the discriminator.","B":"Both eggs carry a knob, which is precisely why the knob cannot decide it. Spirometra mansoni is a third look-alike, effectively identical to D. latum but present in Thailand."},"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Treatment","s":"Why does the 2569 treatment slide add magnesium sulphate for taeniasis SOLIUM only?","lec":"L3","sl":19,"c":"<b>Treatment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"To increase praziquantel absorption","A":"Simply to expel the dead worm from the bowel","C":"Praziquantel disintegrates gravid proglottids and frees eggs, and T. solium eggs are infective for cysticercosis — the purgative evacuates them before they can be re-ingested","D":"To prevent the meningitis-like reaction of praziquantel","E":"To treat the constipation that taeniasis causes"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"MgSO4 is a purgative, not an absorption enhancer.","A":"The incomplete version, which is exactly what loses the mark.","C":"Correct, and the answer has to reach cysticercosis to earn the mark. This is the whole reason solium is treated differently from saginata.","D":"That is prevented with a corticosteroid.","E":"Taeniasis is usually asymptomatic or causes mild non-specific symptoms."},"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Diphyllobothrium","s":"Which combination points to Diphyllobothrium latum?","lec":"L3","sl":32,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Megaloblastic anaemia, a barrel-shaped egg, and a Thai rice-farming history","C":"Megaloblastic anaemia with hypersegmented neutrophils, a large operculated egg, and a travel history outside Thailand","B":"Iron-deficiency anaemia, an operculated egg, and raw crab consumption","A":"Megaloblastic anaemia, radially striated eggs, and raw beef consumption","D":"Eosinophilia, a non-operculated egg with a lateral spine, and freshwater swimming"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"A barrel egg is Trichuris (L1) and causes no anaemia of this type.","C":"Correct. The worm competes for vitamin B12, giving a megaloblastic picture — and the slide states twice that there is NO REPORT IN THAILAND, so a travel history matters.","B":"Hookworm causes iron deficiency; raw crab is Paragonimus.","A":"Radial striation is Taenia, which does not cause megaloblastic anaemia.","D":"A lateral spine on a non-operculated egg is Schistosoma mansoni (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_32.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Host roles","s":"For which parasite is the human an ACCIDENTAL intermediate host, and why is that different from being an accidental host of Angiostrongylus?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"<b>Host roles</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Both Taenia species equally","D":"Taenia saginata — humans cannot digest beef cysticerci","C":"Hymenolepis diminuta — humans are the definitive host of nana instead","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum — humans replace the fish","B":"Taenia solium in cysticercosis — the larva develops normally, just in the wrong mammal, whereas the Angiostrongylus larva can never mature at all"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Only T. solium forms cysticerci in humans; saginata does not.","D":"Humans do become infected from beef cysticerci — that is taeniasis saginata, with the human as definitive host.","C":"Humans are an accidental DEFINITIVE host for H. diminuta, which is a different role again, and the statement about nana is not the contrast asked for.","A":"Humans are the definitive host of D. latum.","B":"Correct, and the distinction is worth holding because the exam uses the word 'accidental' for both. In cysticercosis the cysticercus forms exactly as it would in a pig; in angiostrongyliasis the larva reaches a dead end."},"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q17","t":"Taenia species","s":"A patient gives a history of eating raw beef, and stool examination shows an egg with radial striation. What is the infection?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Taenia species</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q17 — raw beef plus radially striated egg</span>","o":{"C":"Hymenolepis diminuta","A":"Taenia solium","D":"Hymenolepis nana","E":"Taenia saginata","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"H. diminuta eggs are brown, spherical, 60–70 µm and have no filaments.","A":"T. solium would need a history of raw PORK.","D":"H. nana eggs are colourless with 4–8 polar filaments.","E":"Correct — and note carefully how the question is built: radial striation gives you only 'Taenia spp.', and the word BEEF is what finishes it.","B":"D. latum eggs are operculated with a terminal knob, and come from raw freshwater fish."},"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-cysti","t":"Cysticercosis","s":"A patient has a painless cystic structure removed; it contains a larva with a scolex and hooks. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 — painless cyst with scolex and hooks; mode of infection</span>","o":{"E":"Ingest fruit or vegetables contaminated with Taenia solium eggs","A":"Ingest raw pork with Cysticercus cellulosae","D":"Ingest raw beef with Cysticercus bovis","B":"Ingest fruit contaminated with Taenia saginata eggs","C":"Ingest undercooked insect containing cysticercoid larvae"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Cysticercosis follows swallowing the EGG, not the larva.","A":"The trap that sits in this option list every time: raw pork with a cysticercus gives an adult worm — taeniasis.","D":"Beef cysticerci give taeniasis saginata.","B":"T. saginata eggs swallowed by a human do nothing — humans are not a permissive intermediate host, and the learning-guidance table prints a dash in that cell.","C":"Cysticercoid in an insect is Hymenolepis."},"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q34"]},{"id":"I|L3|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q37","t":"Hymenolepis nana","s":"A 10-year-old boy living in an orphanage has diarrhoea, weight loss and malnutrition. Stool shows an oval transparent egg, 45 × 35 µm, with 4–8 polar filaments. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L3","sl":24,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q37 · BM29 SUM1 Q37 — orphanage, 4–8 polar filaments</span>","o":{"B":"Hymenolepis nana","A":"Hymenolepis diminuta","C":"Hookworm","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","D":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Polar filaments are unique to H. nana among the cestodes — and the orphanage is the second clue, because nana spreads person-to-person and needs the whole institution treated.","A":"H. diminuta eggs are brown, spherical, 60–70 µm and have NO polar filaments; it also needs an insect intermediate host and does not autoinfect.","C":"Hookworm eggs are thin-walled ovals with 4–8 CELLS — note how close that wording is to '4–8 polar filaments'.","E":"Strongyloides gives larvae, not eggs, in fresh stool.","D":"Enterobius eggs are D-shaped and collected from perianal skin."},"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q37"]},{"id":"I|L3|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q37","t":"Diphyllobothrium","s":"A 50-year-old man has eosinophilia and megaloblastic anaemia. Stool shows a yellow-brown egg, 40 × 60 µm, oval with an operculum and a terminal knob. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":36,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q37 — megaloblastic anaemia, operculum + terminal knob, 40×60 µm</span>","o":{"B":"Eating raw freshwater fish containing plerocercoid larvae","D":"Eating raw freshwater fish containing metacercariae","C":"Eating food contaminated with sparganum","A":"Eating undercooked beef containing cysticerci","E":"Eating undercooked insects containing cysticercoid larvae"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Megaloblastic anaemia plus a large operculated egg with a terminal knob is Diphyllobothrium latum, whose infective stage in fish is the PLEROCERCOID.","D":"Metacercaria belongs to the FLUKES — the same fish, a different stage, and the reason this option is here. Read the stage, not the fish.","C":"Sparganum is the plerocercoid of Spirometra in human tissue; it is a distractor in D. latum questions and never the answer for D. latum itself.","A":"Cysticercus in beef is T. saginata, which causes no megaloblastic anaemia.","E":"Cysticercoid in insects is Hymenolepis diminuta."},"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q39"]},{"id":"I|L3|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-auto","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Which cestode can cause autoinfection?","lec":"L3","sl":25,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 — autoinfection item with H. diminuta offered as the distractor</span>","o":{"D":"Spirometra spp.","A":"Hymenolepis diminuta","C":"Taenia saginata","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum","E":"Hymenolepis nana"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Spirometra needs Cyclops and a second intermediate host.","A":"H. diminuta is the deliberate split inside this question: it needs an insect intermediate host and cannot autoinfect.","C":"T. SOLIUM can autoinfect (as cysticercosis) but saginata cannot.","B":"D. latum needs a copepod and then a fish.","E":"Correct. H. nana's embryonated egg is infective the moment it is laid, so it can re-infect the same host — which is also why it spreads person-to-person and why a household or institution must be treated together."},"img":"slides_i/L3_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q34","t":"Indian ink — uterine branches","s":"An actively motile proglottid is expelled from the rectum. An Indian-ink injection shows 18 UTERINE BRANCHES. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Indian ink — uterine branches</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q34 — active proglottid from the rectum, Indian ink shows 18 uterine branches</span>","o":{"D":"Taeniasis saginata","A":"Taeniasis solium","E":"Cysticercosis","C":"Diphyllobothriasis","B":"Hymenolepiasis"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The lecture gives one number: MORE than 15 branches = T. saginata, FEWER than 15 = T. solium. 18 is more than 15. The extra clue agrees — only saginata's proglottids crawl out actively on their own.","A":"T. solium has fewer than 15 branches, and its proglottids are passed passively in stool.","E":"Cysticercosis is the LARVAL tissue disease from swallowing T. solium EGGS. There are no proglottids in the rectum in cysticercosis — this option confuses the adult tapeworm with the larva.","C":"Diphyllobothrium has a rosette-shaped central uterus, not lateral branches, and its egg is operculate with a terminal knob.","B":"Hymenolepis proglottids disintegrate in the bowel and are not recovered intact."},"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-taenia","t":"Taeniasis vs cysticercosis","s":"A 40-year-old man has a history of eating UNDERCOOKED PORK, and a Taenia-type organism is recovered on stool examination. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>Taeniasis vs cysticercosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — undercooked pork, organism recovered in stool; the lecturer's written feedback stressed the Taeniasis / Cysticercosis distinction</span>","o":{"B":"Cysticercosis","A":"Taeniasis solium","C":"Taeniasis saginata","E":"Gnathostomiasis","D":"Paragonimiasis"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"This is the distinction the examiner wrote feedback about. Cysticercosis is what happens when a human swallows the EGG and becomes the intermediate host — larvae in brain, eye and muscle, and NOTHING in the stool. Same worm, opposite stage, opposite direction.","A":"Correct. Pork + adult worm in the gut = intestinal taeniasis from T. solium. The infective stage swallowed was the CYSTICERCUS CELLULOSAE in the muscle of the pig.","C":"Beef, not pork.","E":"Gnathostoma comes from eel or freshwater fish and gives migratory swellings.","D":"Paragonimus comes from crab and goes to the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L3_16.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Scolex discriminator","s":"A whole tapeworm is expelled after treatment. The scolex carries four suckers PLUS a rostellum armed with a double crown of hooks. Which species is it, and what must you now warn the patient about?","lec":"L3","sl":8,"c":"<b>Scolex discriminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Taenia solium — this patient can also develop cysticercosis by swallowing its eggs, including from their own hands","D":"Taenia saginata — the rostellar hooks are its identifying feature","C":"Taenia solium — but cysticercosis is impossible in someone who already carries the adult worm","B":"Taenia saginata — the patient risks cysticercosis from eating undercooked beef","E":"Diphyllobothrium latum — the hooks are the bothria seen end-on"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. ARMED scolex (rostellum + hooks) = T. solium, and the same person's faecal–oral contamination is exactly how autoinfective cysticercosis happens.","D":"T. saginata is UNARMED — four suckers, no rostellum, no hooks. This is the single most tested morphological pair in the lecture.","C":"Species right, warning wrong, and dangerously so — carrying the adult is a RISK FACTOR for cysticercosis, not protection from it.","B":"Wrong species, and cysticercosis never comes from beef. Cysticercus bovis in beef causes taeniasis saginata, full stop.","E":"Bothria are sucking GROOVES on an elongated scolex, not hooks, and D. latum has no rostellum."},"img":"slides_i/L3_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Cysticercosis — what you must swallow","s":"A patient presents with new-onset seizures and brain imaging shows multiple cystic lesions. Which single statement about how this was acquired is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis — what you must swallow</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"He swallowed Taenia solium EGGS; in cysticercosis the human is an accidental host, and the egg — not the cysticercus — is the infective stage","E":"He ate undercooked pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae, which migrated to the brain instead of the gut","C":"He swallowed Taenia saginata eggs; both Taenia species cause cysticercosis","B":"He ate undercooked beef containing Cysticercus bovis","A":"He swallowed Taenia solium eggs, which makes him a definitive host carrying an adult worm in the brain"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the host-role half matters: for taeniasis the human is the DEFINITIVE host; for cysticercosis the human steps into the pig's place as an ACCIDENTAL (intermediate-type) host.","E":"Eating the cysticercus gives you the ADULT worm in the gut — that is taeniasis solium. It never disseminates.","C":"T. saginata eggs do NOT cause human cysticercosis; the slide leaves that cell of the table blank deliberately.","B":"Beef and C. bovis give taeniasis saginata only.","A":"The egg hatches into a larva that encysts — no adult worm forms, so he is not a definitive host here."},"img":"slides_i/L3_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Hymenolepis egg pair","s":"A stool smear from a child in an orphanage shows a small colourless egg, about 40 µm, containing a hexacanth embryo, with delicate filaments arising from the poles of the inner membrane. Which species is it, and what does that mean for the other children?","lec":"L3","sl":24,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis egg pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hymenolepis nana — it transmits person-to-person and can autoinfect, so the whole group is at risk without any insect involved","D":"Hymenolepis diminuta — the polar filaments are its hallmark","C":"Hymenolepis nana — but transmission requires eating a beetle or flea, so the other children are not at risk","B":"Hymenolepis diminuta — humans are the definitive host and spread it directly","E":"Taenia spp. — the radial striations have been mistaken for polar filaments"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. 4–8 POLAR FILAMENTS = H. nana, and it is the one cestode in the lecture with direct person-to-person spread plus intestinal autoinfection — which is why the deck cites Thai orphanage outbreaks.","D":"H. diminuta has NO polar filaments and is bigger (60–70 µm) and yellowish-brown.","C":"Species right, epidemiology wrong. Insect ingestion is ONE of H. nana's routes; the embryonated egg alone is also infective.","B":"For H. diminuta the definitive host is the RAT and the human is accidental — direct human spread does not occur.","E":"Taenia eggs are 31–43 µm with a thick radially striated shell and are dark brown — no filaments, and they would be reported as 'Taenia spp.'."},"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Praziquantel and the MgSO4 trap","s":"You are treating confirmed taeniasis solium with a single dose of praziquantel. Which additional step does the lecture specify, and why?","lec":"L3","sl":19,"c":"<b>Praziquantel and the MgSO4 trap</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Give MgSO4 — a purge that clears the disintegrating worm and its eggs quickly, reducing the risk of precipitating cysticercosis","C":"Give MgSO4 to improve praziquantel absorption","E":"Give albendazole as well, because praziquantel does not kill the scolex","B":"Give a corticosteroid, because dying worms in the gut cause anaphylaxis","A":"Nothing extra — the MgSO4 step applies to taeniasis saginata, where the proglottids are motile"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Praziquantel breaks the worm up and releases eggs into the bowel; a purge gets them out before they can hatch and cause autoinfective cysticercosis.","C":"MgSO4 is a purgative here, not an absorption enhancer.","E":"Albendazole is not listed for intestinal taeniasis in this deck — it belongs to the tissue-cyst setting.","B":"Steroids belong to neurocysticercosis management, not to clearing the intestinal worm.","A":"The slide attaches MgSO4 specifically to taeniasis SOLIUM, because only T. solium eggs are dangerous to the person passing them."},"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Diphyllobothrium — egg and anaemia","s":"A traveller returning from Japan has macrocytic anaemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. Stool shows an oval operculate egg about 65 µm long with a small knob at the abopercular end. Which pair of statements is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":36,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — egg and anaemia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the egg is UNembryonated when passed, and the anaemia is megaloblastic from the worm competing for vitamin B12","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the egg is embryonated when passed, and the anaemia is iron-deficiency from chronic blood loss","E":"Taenia saginata; the operculum is the characteristic feature of taeniid eggs","A":"Hymenolepis nana; the knob is a polar filament seen end-on, and the anaemia is nutritional","D":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the anaemia is haemolytic and the egg is indistinguishable from Taenia spp."},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on both. D. latum is the only cestode in this lecture with an OPERCULATED, UNEMBRYONATED egg, and the B12 story explains the hypersegmented neutrophils.","B":"Both halves are wrong in the plausible direction — the egg is unembryonated, and the anaemia is B12-related, not iron-related.","E":"Taeniid eggs are round, thick-shelled with radial striations and embryonated — no operculum anywhere.","A":"H. nana eggs are 30–50 µm with polar FILAMENTS, not a shell knob, and no operculum.","D":"Species right, but the mechanism is B12 competition, and D. latum eggs resemble SPIROMETRA, not Taenia."},"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Pseudophyllidean vs Cyclophyllidean","s":"Which set of features identifies a PSEUDOPHYLLIDEAN tapeworm?","lec":"L3","sl":4,"c":"<b>Pseudophyllidean vs Cyclophyllidean</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"A scolex bearing two sucking grooves (bothria), proglottids wider than long with a rosette-shaped uterus and a common genital pore on the flat surface","B":"A scolex with four cup-shaped suckers and a gravid proglottid whose uterine branches are counted with Indian ink","D":"A scolex with an armed rostellum and eggs containing a hexacanth embryo","C":"A scolex with bothria and an embryonated egg with radial striations","A":"A strobila with a lateral genital pore and cysticercoid larvae in insects"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct — all three features belong to Diphyllobothrium latum, the only pseudophyllidean the lecture covers in depth.","B":"Four suckers and uterine-branch counting are CYCLOPHYLLIDEAN (Taenia).","D":"An armed rostellum is T. solium — cyclophyllidean. Hexacanth embryos occur in both groups, so that half cannot discriminate.","C":"Bothria are right, but radially striated embryonated eggs are taeniid; D. latum's egg is thin, smooth, operculate and unembryonated.","A":"A lateral genital pore and cysticercoid larvae in insects describe Hymenolepis — cyclophyllidean."},"img":"slides_i/L3_4.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Hymenolepis — routes compared","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the two Hymenolepis species?","lec":"L3","sl":26,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis — routes compared</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"H. nana can be acquired by swallowing the embryonated egg, by swallowing an infected beetle or flea, or by autoinfection; H. diminuta is acquired only by swallowing an insect carrying a cysticercoid, and the human is an accidental host","C":"Both species require an insect intermediate host, and neither autoinfects","E":"H. diminuta autoinfects while H. nana does not, which is why H. diminuta is commoner in children","A":"H. nana requires a rat as definitive host; humans are accidental for both species","B":"Both are acquired by eating undercooked pork containing cysticercoid larvae"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. H. nana is the flexible one — that is why it spreads in crowded settings and why it is the only cestode here that autoinfects.","C":"H. nana does NOT require an insect; the egg alone is infective. And autoinfection is one of its defining features.","E":"Inverted on both counts. H. nana is the one found in Thai orphanage children.","A":"Humans and rodents are both definitive hosts for H. nana; only for H. diminuta is the human accidental.","B":"No Hymenolepis species comes from pork. Pork is T. solium."},"img":"slides_i/L3_26.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Diphyllobothrium — prevention","s":"A sushi restaurant asks how to make its freshwater fish safe from Diphyllobothrium latum. Which advice matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":42,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cook it, or freeze it below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","D":"Freeze it below 0 °C for 2 hours","B":"Soak it in vinegar or lemon juice, which kills plerocercoid larvae","C":"Nothing is needed, because D. latum has never been reported in Thailand","E":"Salt it heavily, as for Opisthorchis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the temperature AND the duration are both on the slide, and freezing is the practical option for a raw-fish menu.","D":"Too warm and far too brief; this is the number most people misremember.","B":"Acid marination does not kill plerocercoids; the deck offers only cooking and freezing.","C":"It is true that there is no report in Thailand, but imported fish and travel make the advice relevant — and the question asks what makes fish safe, not where the worm lives.","E":"Salting is not a listed measure for any of these, and Opisthorchis is a fluke from a different lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L3_42.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Reporting a Taenia egg","s":"A stool contains dark-brown, thick-shelled, radially striated eggs containing a hexacanth embryo. How should the laboratory report this, and how could the species be settled?","lec":"L3","sl":9,"c":"<b>Reporting a Taenia egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Report 'Taenia spp. egg' — the eggs of T. solium and T. saginata are indistinguishable — and settle the species on a gravid proglottid stained with Indian ink, or by PCR","B":"Report 'Taenia solium egg' — the radial striations are specific to T. solium","A":"Report 'Taenia saginata egg' — only T. saginata sheds eggs into stool; T. solium sheds proglottids only","E":"Report 'Taenia spp. egg' — the species can never be determined by any method","D":"Report 'cysticercus' — an embryonated egg in stool means cysticercosis"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and both species-level methods are named on the slides: >15 uterine branches = T. saginata, <15 = T. solium, or PCR.","B":"Radial striations belong to BOTH species — that is precisely why the report must stay at genus level.","A":"Both species shed eggs and proglottids. This is a fabricated distinction.","E":"The first half is right but the second is false — the deck gives two ways to reach species.","D":"Eggs in stool mean intestinal TAENIASIS. Cysticercosis is a tissue disease and is not diagnosed from stool."},"img":"slides_i/L3_9.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Cysticercosis — where the cysts go","s":"Which statement about the distribution of cysticerci in human cysticercosis matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":14,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis — where the cysts go</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The central nervous system is involved in 60–90% of cases, the eye in 13–46%, with subcutaneous and intramuscular cysts also recognised","E":"Subcutaneous cysts are the commonest presentation, with CNS involvement in under 10%","B":"Only the CNS is ever involved, which is why the disease is called neurocysticercosis","A":"The liver and lung are the commonest sites, as in hydatid disease","D":"The cysts lodge only in striated muscle, as in trichinellosis"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct — and the overlap of the two percentage ranges tells you many patients have both.","E":"Inverted. Subcutaneous cysts are the least clinically dangerous and are not the commonest.","B":"Neurocysticercosis is the commonest FORM, not the only one — the slide lists ocular, subcutaneous and intramuscular as well.","A":"Liver and lung is the ECHINOCOCCUS/hydatid pattern, which is not this organism.","D":"Muscle-only encystment is TRICHINELLA, from L2."},"img":"slides_i/L3_14.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Taenia — size and proglottid count","s":"Which pairing of adult worm dimensions is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":7,"c":"<b>Taenia — size and proglottid count</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Taenia saginata 4–12 m with 1,000–2,000 proglottids; Taenia solium 2–7 m with 800–1,000 proglottids","E":"Taenia solium 4–12 m with 1,000–2,000 proglottids; Taenia saginata 2–7 m with 800–1,000","D":"Both species reach 10 m and carry about 3,000 proglottids","C":"Taenia saginata is the shorter of the two because cattle are smaller intermediate hosts than pigs","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum is the shortest human tapeworm at 2–7 m"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The BEEF tapeworm is the bigger animal — a useful way to hold it, since it is also the one with more uterine branches.","E":"Straight swap of the two sets of numbers.","D":"10 m and ~3,000 proglottids are DIPHYLLOBOTHRIUM's figures, imported here as a distractor.","C":"T. saginata is the longer species, and the reasoning offered is invented.","A":"D. latum is the LONGEST human tapeworm, over 10 m."},"img":"slides_i/L3_7.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Hymenolepis treatment and control","s":"Which management plan for hymenolepiasis matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":30,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis treatment and control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg orally as a single dose before bedtime, with control of rodents, fleas and beetles alongside treating infected people","C":"Niclosamide for 7 days, with control aimed at cattle and pigs","D":"Albendazole 400 mg for 3 days, with freezing of fish as prevention","A":"Praziquantel plus MgSO4 purge, to prevent autoinfection with cysticerci","B":"Diethylcarbamazine, because the larval stage is a cysticercoid"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on drug, dose, timing and the control measures, which target the insect intermediate hosts and the rodent reservoir.","C":"Niclosamide appears for Taenia and D. latum; cattle and pigs are Taenia's intermediate hosts, not Hymenolepis's.","D":"Fish freezing is D. latum prevention.","A":"MgSO4 is specific to taeniasis solium; Hymenolepis autoinfection involves cysticercoids in the bowel, not tissue cysticerci.","B":"DEC is the filariasis drug from L2."},"img":"slides_i/L3_30.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection","s":"Beyond anaemia, which complications of heavy diphyllobothriasis does the lecture name?","lec":"L3","sl":39,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Intestinal obstruction, and cholecystitis or cholangitis from migrating proglottids","D":"Rectal prolapse and dysentery","E":"Loeffler's syndrome from larvae in the lung","A":"Seizures from cysts in the brain","B":"Protein-losing enteropathy with pitting oedema"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. A worm over 10 m long can obstruct, and its proglottids can wander into the biliary tree.","D":"Rectal prolapse and dysentery are TRICHURIS, from L1.","E":"D. latum has no pulmonary migration; Loeffler's belongs to Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides.","A":"Brain cysts are T. solium cysticercosis.","B":"Protein-losing enteropathy is CAPILLARIA philippinensis, from L1."},"img":"slides_i/L3_39.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Schistosoma","s":"In how many ways does Schistosoma break the pattern of every other fluke in this lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":59,"c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Dioecious; one intermediate host only; no redia and no metacercaria; non-operculated embryonated egg with a spine; acquired by skin penetration in water","C":"Dioecious; two intermediate hosts; operculated egg; acquired by ingestion","A":"Monoecious; one intermediate host; non-operculated egg; acquired by ingestion","B":"Dioecious; one intermediate host; operculated egg; acquired by skin penetration","D":"Monoecious; two intermediate hosts; non-operculated egg; acquired by skin penetration"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct — five separate breaks, and worth writing out from memory. If a stem mentions a male holding a female in a GYNECOPHORIC CANAL, no other trematode in the syllabus can be the answer.","C":"It has only ONE intermediate host (a snail) and its egg is not operculated.","A":"It is dioecious, not monoecious.","B":"Its egg is NOT operculated — that is the point.","D":"It is dioecious with one intermediate host."},"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Opisthorchis","s":"A 60-year-old man from Khon Kaen has obstructive jaundice and a mass-forming liver tumour. Stool shows a 25 × 12 µm embryonated operculated egg with a prominent shoulder and a terminal knob. What is the infective stage of this parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":13,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish","B":"Cercaria penetrating intact skin","D":"Embryonated egg ingested from contaminated vegetables","C":"Plerocercoid larva in freshwater fish","E":"Filariform larva penetrating the skin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and note what the question actually asks. The vignette identifies Opisthorchis viverrini; the question then asks about its STAGE. Read the last line of the stem before you read the options.","B":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma.","D":"Embryonated eggs from vegetables is the intestinal nematode route.","C":"Plerocercoid in fish is Diphyllobothrium latum (L3) — the same meal with a different stage.","E":"Filariform larvae belong to hookworm and Strongyloides."},"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Same meal, different worm","s":"Four parasites are acquired from 'raw freshwater food'. Which matching is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":17,"c":"<b>Same meal, different worm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Metacercaria on water plants = Paragonimus; metacercaria in crab = F. buski","E":"Metacercaria in crab = O. viverrini; metacercaria in fish = Paragonimus; plerocercoid on plants = F. buski","B":"All four are acquired as metacercariae from fish and cannot be separated","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish = O. viverrini; metacercaria in crab = Paragonimus; metacercaria on water plants = F. buski; plerocercoid in fish = D. latum","D":"Plerocercoid in crab = Paragonimus; metacercaria in fish = D. latum"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Reversed.","E":"Every pairing is shuffled.","B":"The food source is precisely what separates them.","C":"Correct, and these are not interchangeable 'raw aquatic food' — they lead to completely different ORGANS. Plant goes to the gut, fish goes to the bile duct, crab goes to the lung.","D":"Both are wrong; the stage names belong to the other organisms."},"img":"slides_i/L4_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Paragonimus","s":"A 50-year-old woman regularly eats ปูดอง (brief-pickled raw crab) in papaya salad. Is this safe, and what infection is she at risk of?","lec":"L4","sl":35,"c":"<b>Paragonimus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Not safe, but the risk is Opisthorchis viverrini","E":"Safe — the acid in vinegar kills the metacercaria within minutes","A":"Safe — Paragonimus is acquired only from freshwater fish","D":"Not safe — brief pickling in brine or vinegar does not kill the metacercaria, so she is at risk of paragonimiasis","C":"Not safe, but the risk is Fasciolopsis buski"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"O. viverrini comes from cyprinoid FISH.","E":"The specific misconception the lecturer wrote the slide to correct.","A":"Paragonimus comes from crab.","D":"Correct, and the teaching document says so explicitly. Salting, pickling and fermentation do NOT kill metacercariae — pla-ra, undercooked pla-som and ปูดอง are all full exposures.","C":"F. buski comes from raw aquatic PLANTS."},"img":"slides_i/L4_35.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Specimens","s":"Which specimen goes with which fluke?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"<b>Specimens</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Paragonimus → stool; S. haematobium → stool; Opisthorchis → sputum","C":"Paragonimus → sputum (morning, 10% KOH); Schistosoma haematobium → urine by filtration; Opisthorchis → stool by concentration technique","E":"Paragonimus → urine; S. haematobium → sputum; Opisthorchis → blood","D":"All three are diagnosed on stool examination","A":"All three are diagnosed on serology only"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Stool is second-line and often negative for Paragonimus, and it is the wrong specimen for S. haematobium.","C":"Correct. Sending a stool for a bladder fluke is a designed distractor, and for Opisthorchis a DIRECT simple smear is only about 12% sensitive — so if the options offer 'direct simple smear' against 'formalin ethyl-acetate concentration', take the concentration technique.","E":"All three are misassigned.","D":"The specimen choice is exactly what is being tested.","A":"Serology is the fall-back for EXTRAPULMONARY paragonimiasis, where there are no eggs to find."},"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Egg groups","s":"Which egg discrimination are you actually expected to make on morphology alone?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"No egg discriminations are expected; all species come from the history","C":"Fasciolopsis buski versus Fasciola hepatica — the operculum is larger in Fasciola","D":"Fasciola versus Echinostoma — the shell is thicker in Echinostoma","E":"Schistosoma japonicum versus S. mekongi — the spine is longer in mekongi","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini versus the small intestinal flukes — shoulder and knob prominent = Opisthorchis; both faint or absent = small intestinal fluke"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Too absolute: the O. viverrini versus small-intestinal-fluke discrimination IS expected.","C":"You are NOT expected to resolve this group. Report 'large intestinal fluke egg' and take the species from province and diet — BM28 asked exactly this and keyed Fasciola and Fasciolopsis as having identical eggs.","D":"Same group, same answer — not resolvable from the egg.","E":"These are separated by SIZE (51–73 vs 70–100 µm) and geography, not by spine length.","B":"Correct. This is the one pair the lecturer expects you to resolve from the egg itself."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Fasciolopsis","s":"A 50-year-old woman from Suphan Buri eats uncooked water chestnut and presents with diarrhoea plus facial and leg oedema. What is the parasite, and what is the oedema?","lec":"L4","sl":44,"c":"<b>Fasciolopsis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Opisthorchis viverrini — the oedema is from hepatic failure","A":"Fasciolopsis buski — the oedema is caused by the parasite (protein loss and malabsorption), not by heart or kidney disease","E":"Schistosoma mekongi — the oedema is from portal hypertension","C":"Paragonimus — the oedema is from pleural effusion","B":"Fasciola hepatica — the oedema is nephrotic"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Opisthorchis comes from fish, and its disease is biliary.","A":"Correct. Suphan Buri and raw water chestnut are the two clues, and the oedema is a recognised feature of heavy F. buski infection — not a competing diagnosis.","E":"Schistosoma is acquired from water contact, not a meal.","C":"Paragonimus comes from crab and affects the lung.","B":"Fasciola is a liver fluke of herbivores; the province and food here point to F. buski, and its eggs are indistinguishable anyway."},"img":"slides_i/L4_44.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Treatment","s":"Why is 'praziquantel' almost never a discriminating answer in this lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":24,"c":"<b>Treatment</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Because it treats all nine flukes — so a treatment question is really asking about the DOSE or regimen","C":"Because praziquantel is ineffective against trematodes","D":"Because albendazole has replaced it for all trematodes","E":"Because it is contraindicated in Schistosoma infection","A":"Because it is used only for the blood flukes"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the doses genuinely differ: 25 mg/kg three times daily for one day (Opisthorchis, Fasciolopsis), the same for two days (Paragonimus), 40 mg/kg (S. mansoni, S. haematobium) and 60 mg/kg (S. japonicum, S. mekongi).","C":"It is the drug of choice for all of them.","D":"Albendazole is the nematode and cysticercosis drug.","E":"Schistosomiasis is a core praziquantel indication.","A":"It covers the liver, lung and intestinal flukes too."},"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q42","t":"Opisthorchis","s":"Koi Pla (ก้อยปลา) is a famous dish in north-eastern Thailand. What is the most common long-term complication of eating such food when it is contaminated with a parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":9,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q42 — ก้อยปลา and its long-term complication</span>","o":{"E":"Prolapse of the rectal muscle","C":"Liver abscess with brownish (anchovy) pus","B":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Pyelonephritis","A":"Chondrosarcoma"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Rectal prolapse is heavy Trichuris trichiura infection (L1).","C":"Anchovy pus in a liver abscess is EXTRAINTESTINAL AMOEBIASIS from Entamoeba histolytica (L5) — a liver lesion, but a different organism entirely.","B":"Correct. Koi pla is raw cyprinoid fish, so the parasite is Opisthorchis viverrini, and chronic infection of the bile ducts leads to cholangiocarcinoma — typically after more than twenty years.","D":"Not a recognised complication of any fluke here.","A":"Not associated with parasitic infection."},"img":"slides_i/L4_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q14","t":"Egg groups","s":"A 40-year-old man from Suphan Buri has an egg in his stool measuring 140 × 85 µm, with a small operculum and a smooth thin wall. What is the most likely parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 Q14 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q14 — 140 × 85 µm egg from Suphan Buri</span>","o":{"B":"Paragonimus spp.","D":"Opisthorchis viverrini","C":"Fasciolopsis buski","E":"Taenia solium","A":"Taenia saginata"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Paragonimus eggs are 80–120 µm with a flattened operculum and a thickened posterior end, and the history would be raw crab.","D":"O. viverrini eggs are 25 × 12 µm — five times smaller — and are embryonated with a prominent shoulder.","C":"Correct. The size puts the egg in the large-intestinal-fluke group, which is morphologically indistinguishable — and SUPHAN BURI, an endemic province, is what finishes the question.","E":"Taenia eggs are spherical, 31–43 µm, with radial striation and no operculum.","A":"Same as D."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q14","BM29 Q14"]},{"id":"I|L4|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q-eggs","t":"Egg groups","s":"Which pair of parasite eggs have essentially identical features?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 — 'which parasite eggs have identical features?'</span>","o":{"C":"Opisthorchis and Schistosoma","A":"Fasciola and Fasciolopsis","E":"Paragonimus and Taenia","B":"Schistosoma mansoni and S. haematobium","D":"Opisthorchis and Diphyllobothrium"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Schistosoma eggs are non-operculated with a spine — nothing like Opisthorchis.","A":"Correct, and the lecture makes the point directly: the large operculated eggs of Fasciola hepatica, F. gigantica, Fasciolopsis buski and Echinostoma are reported simply as 'large intestinal fluke egg'. The species comes from province and diet. On an ADULT specimen, however, you can decide: F. buski has NO cephalic cone, Fasciola has one.","E":"Completely different sizes and structures.","B":"These are separated by spine position: lateral (mansoni) versus terminal (haematobium).","D":"These differ about five-fold in size, though both are operculated with a knob — which is the L3/L4 cross-lecture trap, not an identity."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q22","t":"Amoebic vs fluke liver disease","s":"Which long-term complication is correctly matched to its parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":20,"c":"<b>Amoebic vs fluke liver disease</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q22 — 'cirrhosis / colon cancer / liver abscess / bladder cancer / cholangiocarcinoma'</span>","o":{"D":"Cholangiocarcinoma — Opisthorchis viverrini","B":"Bladder cancer — Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Liver abscess with anchovy pus — Opisthorchis viverrini","E":"Colon cancer — Schistosoma haematobium","C":"Cirrhosis — Paragonimus spp."},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Chronic biliary infection and inflammation over decades leads to cholangiocarcinoma — which is why north-east Thailand has the world's highest incidence.","B":"Bladder cancer is associated with Schistosoma HAEMATOBIUM, which lives in the vesical plexus.","A":"Anchovy-pus liver abscess is Entamoeba histolytica (L5).","E":"S. haematobium affects the urinary tract; the intestinal schistosomes are mansoni, japonicum and mekongi.","C":"Paragonimus lives in the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L4_20.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-inf","t":"Infective stage","s":"A patient from Khon Kaen has cholangitis and obstructive jaundice; stool shows an operculated egg with a shoulder and a knob. Which of the following is the INFECTIVE stage of this parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":17,"c":"<b>Infective stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 — full Opisthorchis vignette then 'which is the infective stage?'</span>","o":{"A":"Metacercaria","E":"Cercaria","B":"Embryonated egg","C":"Filariform larva","D":"Rhabditiform larva"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The vignette names the organism; the question asks about its stage — and for Opisthorchis viverrini the infective stage is the metacercaria encysted in cyprinoid fish.","E":"The cercaria is what leaves the snail and encysts in the fish; it is not what infects a human. For Schistosoma, however, the cercaria IS the infective stage.","B":"The embryonated egg is the DIAGNOSTIC stage here, and the infective stage for the SNAIL.","C":"Filariform larvae belong to hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Rhabditiform larvae belong to Strongyloides."},"img":"slides_i/L4_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-inf"]},{"id":"I|L4|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-schisto","t":"Schistosoma","s":"A 50-year-old man has chronic diarrhoea and intestinal bleeding. Stool shows a non-operculated embryonated egg with a lateral spine. What is the parasite, and how was it acquired?","lec":"L4","sl":66,"c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 · BM31 — chronic diarrhoea with intestinal bleeding, non-operculated egg</span>","o":{"D":"Schistosoma mansoni, acquired when cercariae penetrated intact skin during freshwater contact","B":"Schistosoma haematobium, acquired by drinking contaminated water","C":"Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired from raw cyprinoid fish","E":"Fasciolopsis buski, acquired from raw water plants","A":"Paragonimus spp., acquired from raw crab"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. A LATERAL spine on a non-operculated egg is S. mansoni, and schistosomes are the only water-borne, skin-penetrating flukes. Note that the mouth and lips count as skin — drinking contaminated water can infect you.","B":"S. haematobium has a TERMINAL spine and appears in URINE with haematuria.","C":"Opisthorchis eggs are small and operculated, and the disease is biliary.","E":"F. buski eggs are large and operculated.","A":"Paragonimus eggs are operculated and appear in sputum."},"img":"slides_i/L4_66.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q24","t":"Paragonimus","s":"A man has a chronic cough with haemoptysis, and his sputum is RUSTY-BROWN. How did he acquire this infection?","lec":"L4","sl":37,"c":"<b>Paragonimus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 (rusty-brown sputum, mode of infection) · BM31 SUM1 Q24 (raw mountain crab, clinical manifestation) · BM28 SUM1 Q30 — asked in three cohorts</span>","o":{"B":"Eating raw cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","A":"Eating water caltrop containing metacercariae","D":"Skin penetration by cercariae in fresh water","E":"Eating raw or pickled freshwater (mountain) crab containing metacercariae","C":"Inhaling eggs in dust"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis, which goes to the bile duct, not the lung.","A":"Water caltrop and other water plants carry Fasciolopsis buski metacercariae, which stay in the intestine.","D":"Cercarial skin penetration is Schistosoma.","E":"Correct. Rusty-brown sputum is Paragonimus, the lung fluke, and its second intermediate host is the freshwater/mountain CRAB (or crayfish). Thai 'ปูดอง' — pickled crab — is the classic exposure.","C":"No trematode is acquired by inhalation. Note the classic trap: this picture is mistaken for pulmonary TB, and many patients are treated for TB first."},"img":"slides_i/L4_37.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-para","BM28 Q30"]},{"id":"I|L4|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q30","t":"Schistosoma haematobium","s":"A patient presents with HAEMATURIA. An oval egg with a TERMINAL SPINE is recovered. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L4","sl":63,"c":"<b>Schistosoma haematobium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q30 — haematuria with an oval egg bearing a TERMINAL spine</span>","o":{"A":"Penetration of the skin by filariform larvae","E":"Penetration of the skin by cercariae","B":"Penetration of the skin by rhabditiform larvae","C":"Drinking water contaminated with mature cysts","D":"Eating freshwater plants contaminated with metacercariae"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Filariform larvae are hookworm and Strongyloides — an intestinal worm, no haematuria.","E":"Correct. Terminal spine + haematuria = Schistosoma haematobium, and every schistosome is acquired when the fork-tailed CERCARIA penetrates intact skin during contact with fresh water. Learn the spine table: haematobium TERMINAL, mansoni LATERAL, japonicum/mekongi small LATERAL knob.","B":"The rhabditiform larva is a feeding, non-infective stage and never penetrates skin. This option is wrong on the stage alone.","C":"Mature cysts are protozoa (Entamoeba, Giardia).","D":"Freshwater plants carry Fasciolopsis buski."},"img":"slides_i/L4_63.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 3","t":"Schistosoma mansoni","s":"A 55-year-old woman is admitted with abdominal pain, dysentery and mild hepatomegaly. Stool examination shows an oval egg with a prominent LATERAL SPINE. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L4","sl":63,"c":"<b>Schistosoma mansoni</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Lect 3 — 55-year-old woman, dysentery with mild hepatomegaly, egg with a LATERAL spine</span>","o":{"B":"Fasciolopsis buski","E":"Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Paragonimus spp.","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Schistosoma mekongi"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Fasciolopsis is a very large (140 × 85 µm) operculate egg with no spine.","E":"Opisthorchis is a small OPERCULATE egg with a shoulder and a posterior knob, and the disease is biliary, not dysenteric.","A":"Paragonimus eggs are operculate and appear in SPUTUM.","C":"Correct. A large LATERAL spine on a stool egg is S. mansoni, which lives in the inferior mesenteric vein — hence bloody diarrhoea plus liver involvement from eggs embolising to the portal tracts.","D":"S. mekongi is the Mekong species and its egg carries only a SMALL LATERAL KNOB, not a prominent lateral spine — that size difference is the planted near-miss, and it is the species you would expect in a Thai patient. The question hands you 'prominent lateral spine' to force mansoni."},"img":"slides_i/L4_63.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-ov-prev","t":"Opisthorchis — prevention","s":"A 40-year-old man has indigestion, abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Stool analysis shows an ELECTRIC-LIGHT-BULB shaped egg, operculated, with a shoulder and one posterior knob. What is the most appropriate PREVENTION?","lec":"L4","sl":13,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — electric-light-bulb egg, operculate with shoulder and one posterior knob; what is the prevention?</span>","o":{"A":"Consistent use of footwear","B":"Appropriate cooking of freshwater fish","E":"Use of molluscicides","C":"Sanitary disposal of faeces","D":"Washing vegetables before eating"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Footwear prevents hookworm and Strongyloides.","B":"Correct. The egg is Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired by eating raw cyprinoid fish (koi pla). Prevention acts on the person's own exposure — cook the fish.","E":"Molluscicides attack the FIRST intermediate host (Bithynia snail). The examiner's feedback made the distinction explicit: prevention stops this person being infected; CONTROL breaks transmission in the community. Snail killing and sanitary disposal are control.","C":"Sanitary disposal is control of the egg-shedding end of the cycle, not prevention for this patient.","D":"Vegetables carry Ascaris, Trichuris and Fasciolopsis, not Opisthorchis."},"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Electric-light-bulb egg pair","s":"A stool contains a tiny operculated embryonated egg about 28 µm long with an 'electric light bulb' outline. Under oil it LACKS a prominent shoulder around the operculum and LACKS a distinct posterior knob. What should be reported?","lec":"L4","sl":13,"c":"<b>Electric-light-bulb egg pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Small intestinal fluke egg (Haplorchis spp.) — the absent shoulder and knob separate it from Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Opisthorchis viverrini egg — the electric-light-bulb appearance is specific to it","D":"Large intestinal fluke egg — Fasciolopsis buski has the same outline at this size","E":"Paragonimus spp. egg — the operculum and the small size fit","C":"Schistosoma mekongi egg — its minute lateral knob is easily missed"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. This is the highest-yield egg pair in the lecture: BOTH are bulb-shaped, embryonated and operculate, and only the SHOULDER and the posterior KNOB discriminate. Their absence means a heterophyid.","A":"O. viverrini has a well-developed operculum WITH a shoulder and a posterior knob — the stem removes exactly those two features.","D":"F. buski eggs are enormous (135–190 µm), unembryonated, and have a small indistinct operculum.","E":"Paragonimus eggs are 68–118 µm, UNembryonated, and have a thickened posterior end rather than a knob.","C":"Schistosome eggs are NON-operculated — that alone excludes every schistosome here."},"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Schistosome egg + snail + organ","s":"A urine specimen contains a non-operculated embryonated egg with a TERMINAL spine. Which trio is correct for this species?","lec":"L4","sl":61,"c":"<b>Schistosome egg + snail + organ</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Schistosoma haematobium — snail host Bulinus truncatus, adults in the vesical venous plexus, presenting with painless haematuria and a risk of bladder cancer","D":"Schistosoma mansoni — snail host Biomphalaria spp., adults in the mesenteric veins of the large intestine, presenting with intestinal bleeding","B":"Schistosoma haematobium — snail host Biomphalaria spp., adults in the mesenteric veins, presenting with bloody diarrhoea","A":"Schistosoma japonicum — snail host Oncomelania spp., adults in the mesenteric veins of the small intestine, presenting with haematuria","E":"Schistosoma mekongi — snail host Neotricula aperta, adults in the bladder plexus, presenting with haematuria"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the chain is worth memorising as a unit: terminal spine → urine → Bulinus → bladder plexus → painless haematuria → bladder cancer.","D":"S. mansoni has a LATERAL spine and is found in FAECES, not urine. Everything else in the option is internally consistent, which is what makes it the near-miss.","B":"Species right, snail and habitat imported from S. mansoni — the classic half-right answer.","A":"S. japonicum has a small LATERAL KNOB, not a terminal spine, and causes intestinal, not urinary, disease.","E":"S. mekongi has a MINUTE lateral knob and lives in mesenteric veins; no schistosome but haematobium occupies the bladder plexus."},"img":"slides_i/L4_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Infective stage — the odd one out","s":"Which statement about how the flukes in this lecture are acquired is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":59,"c":"<b>Infective stage — the odd one out</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Opisthorchis, Paragonimus and Fasciolopsis are all food-borne and their infective stage is the METACERCARIA; Schistosoma is water-borne and its infective stage is the FORK-TAILED CERCARIA, which penetrates skin","E":"All four are food-borne, and all four are acquired as metacercariae","B":"Schistosoma is food-borne from raw snails; the other three are acquired by skin penetration","C":"All four infect by skin penetration; only the vehicle differs","A":"Schistosoma's infective stage is the miracidium, which penetrates the skin during wading"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and it is the single structural fact that organises the whole lecture — one fluke behaves differently from the rest in stage, route AND transmission.","E":"Schistosoma has no metacercarial stage at all; it never encysts on food.","B":"Precisely inverted.","C":"Only Schistosoma penetrates skin. Opisthorchis, Paragonimus and Fasciolopsis are swallowed.","A":"The miracidium is what hatches from the egg and infects the SNAIL. The stage that infects humans is the fork-tailed cercaria."},"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Praziquantel dosing — Opisthorchis","s":"Which praziquantel regimen for opisthorchiasis matches the lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":24,"c":"<b>Praziquantel dosing — Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"An individual patient gets 25 mg/kg orally three times in one day; a community mass-treatment programme uses 40 mg/kg as a single dose before bedtime","D":"An individual patient gets 40 mg/kg as a single dose; community programmes use 25 mg/kg three times a day for three days","B":"Both settings use 25 mg/kg as a single dose","A":"Both settings use 10–15 mg/kg as a single dose before bedtime","E":"Individuals get albendazole 400 mg daily for three days; only communities receive praziquantel"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The logic is practical: a single supervised dose is what a mass campaign can deliver, so the dose is raised to compensate.","D":"The two regimens are swapped, and the duration is stretched — the commonest way this is misremembered.","B":"25 mg/kg is a THREE-times-a-day dose, not a single dose.","A":"10–15 mg/kg at bedtime is the HYMENOLEPIS regimen from L3.","E":"Albendazole is not the drug for flukes; praziquantel is used in both settings."},"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Opisthorchis — the prevention grid","s":"The deck separates prevention of opisthorchiasis from prevention of cholangiocarcinoma. Which cell of that grid is stated correctly?","lec":"L4","sl":24,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — the prevention grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Secondary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma = ultrasound or CT surveillance, because the aim is to catch the tumour early rather than to stop the fluke","B":"Primary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma = cooking freshwater fish properly","D":"Secondary prevention of opisthorchiasis = ultrasound or CT surveillance","A":"Primary prevention of opisthorchiasis = treating patients who already have opisthorchiasis","C":"There is no secondary prevention for either, because cholangiocarcinoma takes more than 20 years to develop"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Secondary prevention always means detecting the complication early; for CCA that is imaging.","B":"Cooking the fish is primary prevention of the INFECTION. Primary prevention of CCA is treating opisthorchiasis patients.","D":"Secondary prevention of the infection is six-monthly stool examination in endemic areas plus treating those found positive.","A":"Treating infected individuals is primary prevention of the CANCER, not of the infection.","C":"The 20-year latency is exactly why surveillance imaging is worth doing; the deck lists secondary prevention for both."},"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Paragonimus — the TB mimic","s":"A man from Nakhon Nayok has months of cough, chest pain, night sweats, weight loss and rusty-brown sputum. Two sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli are negative. What is the next best test and what would it show?","lec":"L4","sl":33,"c":"<b>Paragonimus — the TB mimic</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Examine a MORNING sputum sample for unembryonated operculate eggs with a thickened posterior end — Paragonimus, acquired from raw mountain crab","D":"Examine urine for non-operculated eggs with a terminal spine","B":"Examine stool for embryonated eggs with a shoulder and a posterior knob","A":"Serology only, because Paragonimus eggs never appear in sputum","E":"Bronchoalveolar lavage for fork-tailed cercariae"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on all three: the specimen (morning sputum), the egg description, and the exposure. Eggs also appear in stool because the patient swallows sputum.","D":"That describes S. haematobium in urine — wrong organ, wrong egg, wrong disease.","B":"Shoulder + knob + embryonated is OPISTHORCHIS, a biliary fluke — it does not cause a TB-like lung picture.","A":"Serology is listed, but specifically for EXTRAPULMONARY paragonimiasis. Sputum microscopy is the first-line test here.","E":"Cercariae are the schistosome stage and are never found in sputum."},"img":"slides_i/L4_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Large intestinal fluke egg — report it right","s":"A stool shows a very large (about 160 µm), symmetrically oval, thin-walled, brown-yellow UNembryonated egg with a small indistinct operculum. How should this be reported and why?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Large intestinal fluke egg — report it right</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"'Large intestinal fluke egg' — Fasciolopsis buski, Fasciola spp. and Echinostoma spp. eggs are morphologically similar, so species cannot be given from the egg alone","D":"'Fasciolopsis buski egg' — the indistinct operculum is unique to this species","B":"'Small intestinal fluke egg' — size is not a reportable criterion","C":"'Opisthorchis viverrini egg' — all operculate eggs in Thai stool are reported this way","A":"'Schistosoma mansoni egg' — the thin wall and large size fit"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The deck states the similarity twice, once under F. buski and once under Echinostoma, and gives the genus-level report explicitly.","D":"The indistinct operculum is shared across the group — it is a reason NOT to name the species.","B":"Small intestinal fluke eggs are 22–32 µm and embryonated; this one is five times larger and unembryonated.","C":"O. viverrini eggs are 25 µm and embryonated. Reporting by habit rather than by morphology is exactly the error being tested.","A":"Schistosome eggs are NON-operculated and carry a spine or knob."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Katayama fever","s":"A traveller who swam in Lake Malawi develops an itchy rash within hours, then six weeks later has fever, urticaria and eosinophilia. What are these two events?","lec":"L4","sl":65,"c":"<b>Katayama fever</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Cercarial dermatitis (swimmer's itch) at penetration, then Katayama fever — an antigen–antibody complex illness appearing 2–8 weeks later in a non-immune host","B":"Cercarial dermatitis, then chronic hepatosplenic schistosomiasis with portal hypertension","A":"Ground itch from hookworm larvae, then Loeffler's syndrome","D":"Larva currens from Strongyloides, then hyperinfection","E":"Katayama fever at penetration, then cercarial dermatitis as the eggs begin to be laid"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on mechanism and on timing — the 2–8 week window and the immune-complex mechanism are both stated on the slide.","B":"Hepatosplenic disease is CHRONIC, driven by egg granulomas and fibrosis over years, not six weeks.","A":"Ground itch and Loeffler's are the hookworm story from L1; this patient's exposure is freshwater swimming.","D":"Larva currens is Strongyloides, and there is no soil contact in the history.","E":"The sequence is inverted — the dermatitis is immediate, the fever is delayed."},"img":"slides_i/L4_65.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Fasciolopsis — the whole chain","s":"A woman from Suphan Buri who eats raw water caltrop grown in a pond beside a pig farm has abdominal discomfort and now facial and leg swelling. Which chain is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":46,"c":"<b>Fasciolopsis — the whole chain</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fasciolopsis buski — freshwater snail (Segmentina) 1st IH, the aquatic plant itself is the 2nd IH carrying metacercariae, humans and PIGS are definitive hosts, and the swelling reflects an allergic reaction to absorbed worm metabolites","E":"Fasciolopsis buski — the pig is the intermediate host and the plant merely carries eggs; the swelling is from protein loss","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini — the plants are contaminated by fish-eating cats; the swelling is from hypoalbuminaemia","D":"Schistosoma mekongi — pond water is the exposure and the swelling is from portal hypertension","C":"Paragonimus westermani — crabs in the same pond are the source and the swelling is allergic"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the pig detail is the point of the vignette: pigs are a true definitive host, which is why a pig farm beside the pond sustains transmission.","E":"The pig is a DEFINITIVE host, not an intermediate one, and a plant carries metacercariae, not eggs.","B":"Opisthorchis comes from raw cyprinoid FISH, not aquatic plants.","D":"Schistosome infection is by skin penetration while in the water, not by eating plants, and the history here is eating.","C":"Paragonimus comes from crab or crayfish and lodges in the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L4_46.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Where the eggs jam","s":"Which statement about chronic schistosomiasis is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":66,"c":"<b>Where the eggs jam</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Eggs trapped in the liver induce granuloma and fibrosis, raising portal venous pressure and producing hepatomegaly, splenomegaly and ascites; eggs reaching the lung can cause pulmonary hypertension","B":"The adult worms themselves obstruct the portal vein, which is what raises the pressure","E":"Liver damage comes from the fluke feeding on hepatocytes, as in Opisthorchis","D":"Ascites in schistosomiasis is caused by protein-losing enteropathy","A":"Pulmonary involvement occurs only in S. haematobium, because it is the species closest to the venous return"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. In schistosomiasis the pathology is the immune reaction to trapped EGGS, not to the worms — that is the concept the question is built on.","B":"Adults live in the veins but do not mechanically obstruct the portal system; the fibrosis around eggs does.","E":"Opisthorchis damages BILE DUCT epithelium; no schistosome feeds on hepatocytes.","D":"Protein-losing enteropathy is Capillaria philippinensis, from L1.","A":"Pulmonary schistosomiasis is listed generally, not restricted to one species."},"img":"slides_i/L4_66.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Echinostoma — the collar","s":"An adult intestinal fluke about 1 cm long has a head collar bearing a horseshoe-shaped row of spines around the oral sucker. How is it classified and named?","lec":"L4","sl":43,"c":"<b>Echinostoma — the collar</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"A MEDIUM intestinal fluke, Echinostoma spp. — the collar spines are its defining feature","A":"A LARGE intestinal fluke, Fasciolopsis buski — collar spines are present in all large flukes","C":"A SMALL intestinal fluke, Haplorchis spp. — spines mark the heterophyids","D":"A liver fluke, Opisthorchis viverrini — the collar is the ventral sucker seen obliquely","B":"A blood fluke, Schistosoma japonicum — the spines correspond to the egg's lateral knob"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both counts. The size band (0.5–2 cm) and the horseshoe collar spines are the two things the slide gives you.","A":"F. buski is a LARGE fluke, over 2 cm, and has no collar spines.","C":"Haplorchis species are under 0.5 cm — this worm is twice that at minimum.","D":"Opisthorchis is a leaf-like liver fluke with no collar spines at all.","B":"Schistosomes are blood flukes living in veins and have no circumoral disc."},"img":"slides_i/L4_43.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Schistosome adult anatomy","s":"Which feature distinguishes adult Schistosoma from the other trematodes in this lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":59,"c":"<b>Schistosome adult anatomy</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"The sexes are separate, and the male carries the slender female in a ventral gynecophoric canal; the other flukes are monoecious (hermaphroditic)","A":"Schistosoma is monoecious while the other flukes have separate sexes","B":"Schistosoma has no suckers, which is how it stays in the bloodstream","C":"Schistosoma has a stichosome oesophagus, unlike the other flukes","D":"Schistosoma is leaf-shaped with diagonal multilobed testes"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Schistosoma is the only dioecious fluke here, and the gynecophoric canal — the groove in which the male holds the female — is what the adult-worm slide is drawn to show.","A":"Inverted. Opisthorchis is explicitly labelled MONOECIOUS on its morphology slide; Schistosoma is the exception with separate sexes.","B":"Both oral and ventral suckers are labelled on the schistosome slide.","C":"A stichosome oesophagus belongs to Capillaria philippinensis, a nematode from L1.","D":"Leaf-shaped with diagonal multilobed testes and columnar vitelline glands is OPISTHORCHIS VIVERRINI."},"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Schistosomiasis prevention","s":"Which prevention advice for schistosomiasis matches the lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":69,"c":"<b>Schistosomiasis prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Avoid swimming and wading in freshwater in endemic areas, boil water for one minute before bathing to kill cercariae, and drink safe water because the mouth and lips can be a portal","A":"Cook freshwater fish thoroughly and freeze it below −10 °C","C":"Wear shoes and gloves when working in soil","E":"Avoid raw crab and crayfish","B":"Take praziquantel prophylactically before every freshwater exposure"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on all three points, including the detail that drinking unsafe water matters because cercariae can penetrate the oral mucosa.","A":"Cooking and freezing fish is Diphyllobothrium and Opisthorchis advice; schistosomes are not eaten.","C":"Shoes and gloves block soil-dwelling nematode larvae, not water-dwelling cercariae.","E":"Raw crab is the PARAGONIMUS exposure.","B":"Praziquantel is used for treatment and for community mass drug administration, not as pre-exposure prophylaxis."},"img":"slides_i/L4_69.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Food → organ mapping","s":"Which food-to-organ pairing from the wrap-up slide is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":72,"c":"<b>Food → organ mapping</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Raw crab → lung (Paragonimus); raw cyprinoid fish → bile duct (Opisthorchis); raw water chestnut or caltrop → small intestine (Fasciolopsis); skin penetration in water → blood vessels (Schistosoma)","B":"Raw crab → bile duct; raw fish → lung; water chestnut → blood vessels; skin penetration → small intestine","C":"Raw crab → small intestine; raw fish → lung; water chestnut → bile duct; skin penetration → blood vessels","A":"All four are acquired by eating, and all four end up in the intestine","E":"Raw fish → blood vessels; raw crab → small intestine; water chestnut → lung; skin penetration → bile duct"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct — this single slide is the fastest way to answer any 'which food / which organ' stem in the paper.","B":"Crab and fish are swapped, and the last two are scrambled.","C":"Crab and water chestnut are swapped; crab is the lung fluke.","A":"Schistosoma is NOT acquired by eating, and each fluke has its own habitat.","E":"Every pairing here is wrong."},"img":"slides_i/L4_72.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Urine OV-RDT","s":"Which laboratory approach to opisthorchiasis is listed in the lecture?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"<b>Urine OV-RDT</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"A urine OV-RDT strip that detects Opisthorchis ANTIGEN, used alongside stool microscopy, ELISA antibody detection and PCR","A":"Urine microscopy for Opisthorchis eggs, which are excreted renally","B":"Sputum examination, because the fluke migrates through the lung","D":"Blood film examination for microfilaria-like larvae","E":"Only stool microscopy — no serological or molecular test exists for this fluke"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the distinction matters: the urine test detects ANTIGEN, not eggs. The slide even carries the Thai petty-patent number.","A":"Opisthorchis eggs are shed in bile into the gut and appear in STOOL; they are not filtered into urine.","B":"The lung route belongs to Paragonimus.","D":"Blood films are for filariasis and malaria.","E":"ELISA and PCR are both listed explicitly."},"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Mode of infection","s":"Asked for the mode of infection of Entamoeba histolytica, which answer scores full marks?","lec":"L5","sl":13,"c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Ingestion of cysts","A":"Ingestion of the MATURE cyst with 4 nuclei from contaminated food or water","D":"Ingestion of trophozoites","B":"Faecal–oral transmission","C":"Ingestion of the immature cyst"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"This is the answer she marks as worth zero. It names the object without naming the stage that matters.","A":"Correct. The lecturer said in class, in so many words, that the short form scores nothing: 'อย่าตอบมาแค่ cyst ไม่ได้คะแนน'. An immature cyst is not infective, so the maturity IS the answer.","D":"Trophozoites are destroyed by gastric acid and are never infective by ingestion — except for Acanthamoeba, which does not use the gut at all.","B":"A route, not a mode of infection.","C":"An immature cyst is not infective, which is exactly why 'mature' must appear."},"img":"slides_i/L5_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Bloody diarrhoea","s":"Three organisms in this block produce bloody mucoid diarrhoea. Which feature identifies which?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>Bloody diarrhoea</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Ingested red blood cells inside the organism = E. histolytica; a large ciliated cell with a kidney-shaped macronucleus = Balantidium coli; a barrel egg with bipolar plugs = Trichuris trichiura","B":"Cilia = E. histolytica; ingested red cells = Balantidium; barrel egg = Giardia","E":"Falling-leaf motility = E. histolytica; ingested red cells = Trichuris; cilia = Balantidium","C":"All three are separable from the stool description alone","A":"Ingested red blood cells = Balantidium; kidney-shaped macronucleus = E. histolytica"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. She counted these three off in class and warned that you cannot answer from the stool description — you must look at the ORGANISM in the figure. Note also that the papers rotate the wording: dysentery, mucous bloody diarrhoea and bloody stool all mean the same thing.","B":"Every feature is misassigned; Giardia causes no bloody diarrhoea at all.","E":"Falling-leaf motility is GIARDIA, which is the odd one out here.","C":"The explicit point of her warning was the opposite.","A":"Reversed."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"Which organism in this lecture has the TROPHOZOITE as its infective stage, and why?","lec":"L5","sl":38,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Entamoeba coli — it is non-pathogenic and therefore not destroyed","E":"Entamoeba histolytica — its trophozoite is acid-resistant","D":"Balantidium coli — its cilia protect it from gastric acid","B":"Giardia intestinalis — its adhesive disc lets it attach before acid exposure","C":"Acanthamoeba — it enters through the eye, nose or broken skin, so nothing has to survive a stomach"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Non-pathogenic does not mean acid-resistant; E. coli also infects as a cyst.","E":"E. histolytica trophozoites are destroyed by gastric acid.","D":"Balantidium is acquired as a mature cyst.","B":"Giardia is acquired as a mature cyst.","C":"Correct, and this is the one place 'mature cyst' is the WRONG answer. 'Cyst = infective' is a rule about the FAECAL–ORAL ROUTE, not about protozoa in general — learn the reason and you cannot get it backwards."},"img":"slides_i/L5_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Amoebiasis sites","s":"How do the two clinical forms of amoebiasis differ, and what does each require?","lec":"L5","sl":21,"c":"<b>Amoebiasis sites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Lower abdominal pain with tenesmus = intestinal amoebiasis (stool); right upper quadrant pain with hepatomegaly = liver abscess (serology + imaging, and drainage)","A":"Lower abdominal pain = liver abscess; right upper quadrant pain = colitis","B":"Both present identically and are distinguished only by serology","E":"Lower abdominal pain = Balantidium; right upper quadrant pain = E. histolytica","C":"Both are diagnosed by stool examination alone"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Same organism, different disease, different specimen, different management. In extraintestinal disease the stool is often NEGATIVE because the organism has left the bowel.","A":"Reversed anatomy.","B":"The stem chooses the site deliberately, and the site is the discriminator.","E":"Both forms are E. histolytica; Balantidium causes colitis only.","C":"Stool is often negative in liver abscess — that is the trap."},"img":"slides_i/L5_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Giardia","s":"A 5-year-old has foul-smelling, fatty, watery diarrhoea with no blood and no mucus. Stool shows an organism with falling-leaf motility. What is it, and where does it live?","lec":"L5","sl":58,"c":"<b>Giardia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Entamoeba histolytica — the large intestine","A":"Giardia intestinalis — the SMALL intestine, although it encysts in the large intestine","E":"Balantidium coli — the large intestine","C":"Trichomonas vaginalis — the urogenital tract","B":"Acanthamoeba spp. — free-living in water"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"E. histolytica gives bloody mucoid diarrhoea with rapid unidirectional motility.","A":"Correct. The absence of blood and mucus is the ANSWER, not a caveat, and steatorrhoea is the word to hunt for. Giardia is the odd one out among today's gut protozoa — everything else lives in the colon. Note also the three synonyms: G. intestinalis = G. duodenalis = G. lamblia.","E":"Balantidium gives bloody mucoid diarrhoea, and it is a huge ciliated cell.","C":"Trichomonas gives greenish-yellow vaginal discharge with jerky motility.","B":"Acanthamoeba causes keratitis or encephalitis, not diarrhoea."},"img":"slides_i/L5_58.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Balantidium","s":"Which feature steers a bloody-diarrhoea stem towards Balantidium coli rather than Entamoeba histolytica?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Bloody mucoid stool","B":"A flask-shaped ulcer on biopsy","E":"Lower abdominal pain with tenesmus","A":"A cyst as the infective stage","D":"A history of pig contact or pig farming — Balantidium is the zoonotic one of the pair"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Shared by both, and by Trichuris.","B":"The flask-shaped ulcer is classically E. histolytica, but her Balantidium slide prints it too — so it does NOT decide. Look at the organism.","E":"Shared by both.","A":"Both are acquired as mature cysts, and for both the diagnostic stage is cyst AND trophozoite.","D":"Correct. The pig is the discriminator, because the two organisms give the same stool and the same site."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"A contact-lens wearer develops photophobia and severe keratitis. A cyst is seen with a wrinkled outer wall and a polyhedral inner wall. What is the organism, and what is the key preventive instruction?","lec":"L5","sl":38,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Acanthamoeba — never rinse or store lenses in tap water; use lens solution only","A":"Naegleria fowleri — avoid swimming in warm fresh water","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris — avoid soil contact","E":"Entamoeba histolytica — boil drinking water","D":"Giardia intestinalis — filter drinking water"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The two-layered cyst — wrinkled ECTOCYST outside, polyhedral ENDOCYST inside — is Acanthamoeba. Municipal tap water is safe to DRINK and still contains free-living amoebae, so the instruction is absolute: solution only.","A":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis after warm freshwater exposure, not keratitis.","B":"Balamuthia causes granulomatous encephalitis.","E":"E. histolytica is a gut and liver pathogen.","D":"Giardia causes diarrhoea."},"img":"slides_i/L5_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"Which host factor separates Acanthamoeba keratitis from granulomatous amoebic encephalitis?","lec":"L5","sl":40,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Both occur only in contact-lens wearers","C":"Keratitis occurs in the immunocompromised; GAE occurs in healthy people","E":"Both occur only in the immunocompromised","D":"Keratitis occurs in healthy contact-lens wearers; GAE occurs in the immunocompromised","B":"Host status has no bearing on which syndrome develops"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"GAE has no lens association.","C":"Reversed.","E":"Keratitis typically affects healthy lens wearers.","D":"Correct. If a stem mentions HIV, transplantation or chemotherapy, it is steering you to GAE.","B":"Host status is exactly what the stem uses to point you."},"img":"slides_i/L5_40.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Laboratory","s":"Which observation belongs to which preparation?","lec":"L5","sl":10,"c":"<b>Laboratory</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"All three observations can be made on any preparation","C":"Motility is best seen in iodine; nuclei are best seen in saline","E":"The chromatoid body is only visible on permanent stain","B":"Motility is a saline-only observation; the chromatoid body is seen in saline and the same material is called a glycogen mass in iodine; nuclei are essentially an iodine or permanent-stain observation","D":"Permanent stain is the correct first-line investigation in a general hospital"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Each preparation shows different things, which is exactly the point of the question.","C":"Iodine KILLS the organism, so motility is lost; nuclei are the thing iodine reveals.","E":"It is visible in a saline wet mount, where it is called a chromatoid body.","B":"Correct. The same structure has two names depending on the well — chromatoid body in saline, glycogen mass in iodine — which is why the question 'how do you identify it on an unstained wet mount?' has a different answer from 'how do you identify it definitively?'.","D":"A permanent stain is not available in a general hospital, so it is never the right first-line answer to 'how would you investigate this patient?'."},"img":"slides_i/L5_10.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Entamoeba dispar","s":"Why does the presence of ingested red blood cells inside an amoeba matter so much?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>Entamoeba dispar</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Because ingested red cells distinguish the cyst from the trophozoite","C":"Because red cells inside the organism indicate a liver abscess rather than colitis","E":"Because red cells in the stool are what define dysentery","D":"Because only Balantidium ingests red cells","A":"Because Entamoeba dispar is morphologically identical to E. histolytica but non-pathogenic, and does not ingest red cells — so the red cells prove you are looking at the pathogen"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Only the trophozoite feeds, but that is not why the feature matters.","C":"Erythrophagocytosis is a feature of invasive intestinal disease; in liver abscess the stool is often negative altogether.","E":"Red cells IN THE STOOL is just bleeding, which any dysentery gives you. Red cells INSIDE THE ORGANISM is the diagnosis — the distinction the question is built on.","D":"Balantidium does not characteristically show erythrophagocytosis.","A":"Correct. The lecturer said she will not set E. dispar directly but that you should know it exists — and its relevance is exactly this: it is why erythrophagocytosis is a diagnostic feature and not just a descriptive one."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q21","t":"Amoebic liver abscess","s":"A 50-year-old patient has right upper quadrant pain and bloody mucoid diarrhoea. Aspiration of a liver abscess yields anchovy-paste pus, and the organism shows amoeboid movement. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":22,"c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q21 · BM30 Q21 — anchovy pus with amoeboid movement</span>","o":{"E":"Entamoeba coli","B":"Entamoeba histolytica","C":"Acanthamoeba spp.","D":"Capillaria philippinensis","A":"Balantidium coli"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"E. coli is non-pathogenic and never causes invasive disease. Its cyst is LARGER than E. histolytica's and has more nuclei.","B":"Correct. Anchovy-paste pus is the classic description of an amoebic liver abscess, and this is extraintestinal amoebiasis.","C":"Acanthamoeba causes keratitis, encephalitis or cutaneous disease, never a liver abscess.","D":"Capillaria is an intestinal nematode causing chronic diarrhoea and protein loss (L1).","A":"Balantidium causes colitis but not liver abscess."},"img":"slides_i/L5_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L5|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q11","t":"Giardia","s":"A patient presents with foul-smelling diarrhoea. Stool examination reveals an organism with falling-leaf movement. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":58,"c":"<b>Giardia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q44 · BM29 SUM1 Q11 — foul-smelling stool, falling-leaf movement</span>","o":{"B":"Giardia intestinalis","C":"Entamoeba histolytica","E":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Balantidium coli"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Falling-leaf motility plus foul-smelling, fatty, NON-bloody diarrhoea is Giardia. Note that motility is a saline-wet-mount observation and is destroyed by iodine.","C":"E. histolytica shows rapid unidirectional movement and gives BLOODY mucoid diarrhoea.","E":"Cyclospora is a coccidian oocyst with no motility (L6).","D":"Cystoisospora is likewise a non-motile oocyst (L6).","A":"Balantidium moves by cilia and gives bloody diarrhoea."},"img":"slides_i/L5_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q44"]},{"id":"I|L5|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q-dys","t":"Trophozoite with RBC","s":"A patient has dysentery, tenesmus and lower abdominal pain. What must be found on stool examination to make the diagnosis?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>Trophozoite with RBC</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · BM31 · BM30 — dysentery + tenesmus, 'what must you find?'</span>","o":{"C":"An embryonated egg","A":"A sporulated oocyst 4–6 µm","D":"A spore 20–30 µm","E":"A trophozoite with red blood cells inside it","B":"A cyst 2 µm"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"An embryonated egg belongs to the helminths.","A":"A 4–6 µm sporulated oocyst is Cryptosporidium (L6), which causes WATERY diarrhoea WITHOUT blood, in the small bowel.","D":"No organism in the block matches that description; the L6 sizes are planted to tempt you.","E":"Correct, and this item has appeared three years running. The tell is 'dysentery' plus 'tenesmus' — blood and large bowel — which is Entamoeba histolytica.","B":"A 2 µm cyst points at Microsporidia's spore size (L6), again in the wrong clinical setting."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-dys","BM30 Q-dys"]},{"id":"I|L5|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q29","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"A 25-year-old woman has photophobia and visual loss. A cyst is seen with two layers, the outer one wrinkled. What is the infection?","lec":"L5","sl":37,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q29 · BM29 Q31 · BM30 — photophobia with a wrinkled two-layer cyst</span>","o":{"E":"Acanthamoeba spp.","C":"Naegleria fowleri","D":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Strongyloides stercoralis"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The double-walled cyst — wrinkled ectocyst, polyhedral endocyst — plus eye involvement is Acanthamoeba keratitis, and the risk factor asked about in the follow-up item is CONTACT LENSES.","C":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis after warm freshwater swimming; the prevention answer for that item is chlorination and pH adjustment.","D":"Balamuthia causes granulomatous encephalitis, not keratitis.","A":"E. histolytica does not infect the eye.","B":"A nematode, not an amoeba."},"img":"slides_i/L5_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-acanth","BM29 Q31"]},{"id":"I|L5|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q15","t":"Trichomonas","s":"A 35-year-old woman has greenish-yellow vaginal discharge and a haemorrhagic 'strawberry' cervix. What is the appropriate control measure, and what would you see in the discharge?","lec":"L5","sl":54,"c":"<b>Trichomonas</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q15 · BM28 Q45 — strawberry cervix, greenish-yellow discharge</span>","o":{"C":"Treat the patient only; the discharge shows a mature cyst","A":"Treat both the patient and her partner; the discharge shows a trophozoite with jerky motility","D":"Treat the patient and her whole family; the discharge shows falling-leaf motility","B":"Boil drinking water; the discharge shows a ciliated organism","E":"No treatment is needed; the condition is self-limiting"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Treating one partner guarantees reinfection.","A":"Correct. Trichomonas vaginalis is sexually transmitted, so the partner must be treated or reinfection is certain — and the organism has NO cyst stage, so the trophozoite is both the infective and the diagnostic stage, seen with a characteristic jerky motility.","D":"Family treatment is the Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana answer.","B":"Trichomonas is not water-borne.","E":"It is a treatable, transmissible infection."},"img":"slides_i/L5_54.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q45"]},{"id":"I|L5|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pool","t":"Free-living amoeba","s":"A child developed high fever and headache three days after swimming in a pool, and a free-living amoeba was identified. What is the appropriate prevention?","lec":"L5","sl":43,"c":"<b>Free-living amoeba</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM28 Q43 — child who swam in a pool, prevention</span>","o":{"B":"Never swim again","D":"Drink only clean water","E":"Maintain adequate chlorine levels and adjust the pH of the pool water","C":"Avoid swimming during the summer","A":"Avoid swimming in cold fresh water"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Absolute avoidance is not a practical or examinable control measure.","D":"Naegleria enters through the nose during swimming, not by drinking.","E":"Correct, and it is the answer key's own wording. ⚠ Note the contrast with L6: Cryptosporidium is RESISTANT to chlorine and needs FILTRATION instead. Same-sounding question, opposite answer — read which organism the stem gives you.","C":"Season is not the control point.","A":"The organism favours WARM fresh water, so this is the wrong way round."},"img":"slides_i/L5_43.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM30 Q-pool","BM28 Q43"]},{"id":"I|L5|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q25","t":"Balantidium coli","s":"A patient has bloody mucoid diarrhoea and abdominal pain. Stool examination shows a large oval trophozoite covered in short hair-like structures, with a KIDNEY-SHAPED MACRONUCLEUS. What is the organism?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium coli</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 (dysentery, trophozoite with cilia) · BM31 SUM1 Q25 · BM30 Formative — the only ciliate in the syllabus, asked in three cohorts</span>","o":{"E":"Balantidium coli","C":"Entamoeba histolytica","D":"Entamoeba coli","B":"Giardia intestinalis","A":"Acanthamoeba spp."},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is decided by two words. Balantidium coli is the ONLY ciliate that infects humans, so 'cilia' alone excludes everything else; the kidney-shaped macronucleus confirms it. It is also the largest protozoan in the syllabus at 50–200 µm.","C":"E. histolytica is the other dysentery protozoan, but it moves with a pseudopodium and contains ingested RED CELLS — no cilia, no macronucleus.","D":"E. coli is non-pathogenic and causes no dysentery.","B":"Giardia has flagella and shows falling-leaf motion with foul-smelling fatty stool, not dysentery.","A":"Acanthamoeba is free-living and causes keratitis or encephalitis, never colitis. Note the exam's three-way dysentery list: Trichuris, Entamoeba histolytica, Balantidium coli."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-balant","BM30 Q-balant"]},{"id":"I|L5|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q40","t":"Giardia — axoneme","s":"A patient has watery diarrhoea with FAT in the stool. Examination of the specimen shows AXONEMES. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":57,"c":"<b>Giardia — axoneme</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q40 — watery diarrhoea with fat in the stool, AXONEME seen on examination</span>","o":{"D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","C":"Microsporidia","E":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Trichomonas vaginalis","B":"Giardia intestinalis"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Cryptosporidium is a 4–6 µm acid-fast oocyst, and its diarrhoea is watery WITHOUT fat.","C":"Microsporidia spores are 1–2 µm with a coiled polar filament, seen on gram-chromotrope.","E":"Cystoisospora is a 25 × 15 µm ellipsoidal oocyst on modified acid-fast stain.","A":"Trichomonas is urogenital — vaginal discharge, not steatorrhoea.","B":"Correct. Axonemes — the internal rods of the flagella running down the midline — are a Giardia structure and are visible in BOTH the trophozoite and the cyst. Steatorrhoea follows because Giardia coats the duodenal mucosa and blocks fat absorption."},"img":"slides_i/L5_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q5 (58)","t":"Extra-intestinal amoebiasis","s":"What is the most common EXTRA-INTESTINAL manifestation of Entamoeba histolytica infection?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Extra-intestinal amoebiasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 58 & 60 — most common extra-intestinal form of invasive amoebiasis</span>","o":{"A":"Cutaneous amoebiasis","B":"Brain abscess","D":"Pleural effusion","E":"Peri-anal ulceration","C":"Liver abscess"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Skin is the rarest site of all. The slide ranks them and the examiner asks for the top of the list.","B":"Brain involvement happens but is rare, and it is what the NEXT item turns on.","D":"Pleura and lung come second, usually by direct extension from a liver abscess.","E":"Peri-anal ulceration is listed on the slide but is uncommon.","C":"Correct, and by a wide margin. Trophozoites travel up the portal vein, so the liver is hit first — right upper quadrant pain, fever, hepatomegaly, and the 'anchovy-sauce' pus that is really necrotic liver, not true pus."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q5 (60)"]},{"id":"I|L5|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q2 (60)","t":"Brain lesions — which amoeba","s":"Which of the following does NOT commonly produce a space-occupying lesion in the brain?","lec":"L5","sl":34,"c":"<b>Brain lesions — which amoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q2 — which organism does NOT produce a space-occupying brain lesion?</span>","o":{"E":"Toxoplasma gondii","D":"Acanthamoeba spp.","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Naegleria fowleri","C":"Entamoeba histolytica"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Toxoplasma in an immunocompromised host is THE classic ring-enhancing brain mass (L7).","D":"Acanthamoeba causes granulomatous amoebic encephalitis (GAE), a slow mass lesion.","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris also causes GAE.","A":"Naegleria fowleri causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis (PAM) — fulminant, after swimming in warm fresh water.","C":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. E. histolytica CAN reach the brain, but cerebral amoebiasis is rare — its signature lesion is in the LIVER. Everything else on this list is defined by what it does to the CNS."},"img":"slides_i/L5_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q13 (57)","t":"Amoebic colitis vs shigellosis","s":"Which finding is NOT typical of acute amoebic colitis?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Amoebic colitis vs shigellosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q13/16 — which finding is NOT typical of acute amoebic colitis?</span>","o":{"A":"Absence of severe dehydration","C":"Insidious onset over days to weeks","D":"Lower abdominal pain","E":"Tenesmus","B":"Abundant faecal leukocytes"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Correct of amoebiasis: stool volume is small and bloody rather than voluminous, so severe dehydration is uncommon.","C":"Amoebiasis creeps up over days to weeks, unlike the abrupt onset of bacillary dysentery.","D":"Lower abdominal pain is expected — the ulcers are in the caecum and sigmoid.","E":"Tenesmus is expected, and is one of the three keywords the exam reuses.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer, and this is the single fact that separates amoebic from bacillary dysentery at the bench. Amoebic trophozoites LYSE neutrophils, so faecal leukocytes are scanty. Abundant faecal leukocytes point to Shigella."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (60)","t":"Trichomonas — morphology","s":"A 25-year-old woman has a yellow vaginal discharge and a history of several sexual partners in the past two weeks. What is the characteristic appearance of the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":54,"c":"<b>Trichomonas — morphology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q4 — yellow discharge with multiple sexual partners; what does the organism look like?</span>","o":{"D":"Pear-shaped with two anterior nuclei and a large adhesive disc","A":"Pear-shaped with an undulating membrane and jerky motility","E":"Pear-shaped with a sucking disc and falling-leaf motion","B":"Round with four to eight ingested red cells","C":"Oval with cilia and a kidney-shaped macronucleus"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Two anterior nuclei and an adhesive disc are Giardia, which is intestinal.","A":"Correct. Trichomonas vaginalis exists ONLY as a trophozoite — there is no cyst — and it is recognised by the undulating membrane running down one side plus its twitching, jerky movement on a wet mount.","E":"'Sucking disc' plus falling-leaf motion is Giardia again, described a second way — the examiner plants Giardia twice because both organisms are pear-shaped flagellates.","B":"Ingested red cells are Entamoeba histolytica.","C":"Cilia and a kidney-shaped macronucleus are Balantidium coli."},"img":"slides_i/L5_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"histolytica vs coli — trophozoite","s":"An iron-haematoxylin stained stool smear shows an amoebic trophozoite with ONE nucleus that has an ECCENTRIC karyosome and IRREGULAR peripheral chromatin, and no ingested red cells. What is it, and what follows clinically?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>histolytica vs coli — trophozoite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Entamoeba coli — a non-pathogenic commensal, so this finding does not explain the patient's symptoms","D":"Entamoeba histolytica — the eccentric karyosome is its defining nuclear feature","A":"Entamoeba dispar — the absence of red cells proves the species","B":"Entamoeba histolytica, and the absence of red cells simply means the infection is early","C":"Acanthamoeba spp. — the large karyosome and food vacuoles fit"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. ECCENTRIC karyosome + IRREGULAR peripheral chromatin = E. coli, which is non-pathogenic — so you must keep looking for the real cause.","D":"E. histolytica has a CENTRIC karyosome with REGULAR peripheral chromatin. This is the exact inversion the table is drawn to prevent.","A":"E. dispar is morphologically IDENTICAL to E. histolytica — it has a centric karyosome too. Absent red cells alone cannot name it.","B":"Species wrong. And note that an E. histolytica trophozoite WITHOUT red cells cannot be distinguished from E. dispar at all.","C":"Acanthamoeba is a free-living amoeba of cornea and brain, not a stool commensal, and has acanthopodia."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Mature cyst — how many nuclei","s":"A cyst in an iodine preparation measures about 25 µm, contains EIGHT nuclei, and its chromatoid bars are splintery with sharp ends. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":11,"c":"<b>Mature cyst — how many nuclei</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"It is a mature Entamoeba coli cyst — E. coli matures at 8 nuclei with splintery chromatoid bars, whereas E. histolytica matures at 4 with rounded or cigar-shaped bars","A":"It is a mature Entamoeba histolytica cyst — 8 nuclei is the mature count for both species","B":"It is an immature Entamoeba histolytica cyst that has over-divided","E":"It is a Balantidium coli cyst — the splintery bars are its cilia seen in section","D":"It is a Giardia cyst — 8 nuclei is the mature Giardia count"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on both the nuclear count and the chromatoid bar shape — the two features the comparison slide puts side by side.","A":"E. histolytica never exceeds 4 nuclei; 4 IS its mature, infective count.","B":"There is no such thing as over-division past the mature count; the nuclear number is the species criterion.","E":"Balantidium cysts are 40–65 µm with a single kidney-shaped macronucleus and a thick wall.","D":"Giardia cysts have 2–4 nuclei with axonemes and median bodies, and are oval, not spherical."},"img":"slides_i/L5_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Acanthamoeba — the stage that infects","s":"Which statement about Acanthamoeba is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":36,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — the stage that infects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The TROPHOZOITE is the infective stage, entering through a soft contact lens, the nasal cavity or a skin lesion; both trophozoite and cyst are diagnostic","E":"The mature cyst is the infective stage and is swallowed in contaminated water, as in Entamoeba","A":"The cyst is infective and the trophozoite is the only diagnostic stage","C":"Only the trophozoite exists; Acanthamoeba has no cyst stage","B":"The infective stage is the fork-tailed cercaria released from freshwater snails"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and this is the exception worth flagging: for almost every other protozoan here the CYST is infective, so Acanthamoeba inverts the pattern you have been drilling.","E":"This is the pattern-matched wrong answer. Acanthamoeba is not acquired by swallowing, and the trophozoite is the invading stage.","A":"Inverted. Both stages are diagnostic (they are both found in corneal scrapings and biopsies); only the trophozoite infects.","C":"Acanthamoeba has a distinctive double-walled cyst — wrinkled ectocyst outside, polyhedral endocyst inside.","B":"Cercariae are schistosome stages from the trematode lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L5_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Giardia — trophozoite features","s":"A wet mount from a child with a month of fatty, foul-smelling, non-bloody diarrhoea shows a pear-shaped organism tumbling with a 'falling leaf' motion. Which set of features confirms it, and what is the treatment?","lec":"L5","sl":57,"c":"<b>Giardia — trophozoite features</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Two anterior nuclei, a large ventral adhesive disc, two median bodies, axonemes and four pairs of flagella — Giardia intestinalis, treated with metronidazole, paromomycin or tetracycline","D":"A single nucleus with a centric karyosome and ingested red cells — Entamoeba histolytica, treated with metronidazole","A":"A kidney-shaped macronucleus with surface cilia — Balantidium coli, treated with metronidazole or tinidazole","B":"Two anterior nuclei and four pairs of flagella — Giardia intestinalis, treated with albendazole alone","C":"An undulating membrane with a single nucleus — Trichomonas vaginalis, treated with metronidazole"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on every element. Note the symmetry of the organism — everything comes in pairs — and that the diarrhoea is watery/fatty WITHOUT blood or mucus.","D":"Ingested red cells and bloody mucous dysentery is the amoebic picture; this child has no blood in the stool.","A":"Balantidium is a large ciliate causing dysentery with flask-shaped ulcers, not steatorrhoea.","B":"Morphology right, drug wrong — albendazole is not on the treatment slide for giardiasis.","C":"Trichomonas is a urogenital flagellate, not a cause of steatorrhoea, and has only a trophozoite stage."},"img":"slides_i/L5_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Amoebic liver abscess — where the trophozoites are","s":"A man has right upper quadrant pain, fever and hepatomegaly, and aspiration yields anchovy-paste material. Which pair of statements is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess — where the trophozoites are</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Trophozoites are found at the EDGE of the lesion rather than in the centre of the pus, and treatment is metronidazole or tinidazole FOLLOWED by a luminal agent such as paromomycin, with aspiration","C":"Trophozoites are abundant in the centre of the anchovy pus, and metronidazole alone is sufficient","B":"Cysts are found in the abscess, and treatment is paromomycin alone","D":"The abscess contains E. dispar, and no treatment is needed because that species is non-invasive","E":"Treatment is albendazole plus aspiration, as for hydatid disease"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct on both. The centre of the abscess is necrotic debris; live trophozoites sit at the advancing margin — which is why an aspirate can be falsely negative.","C":"This is the intuitive but wrong half — and metronidazole alone leaves luminal cysts behind, so the patient stays infectious and can relapse.","B":"E. histolytica does not encyst in tissue; cysts form only in the bowel lumen.","D":"E. dispar is by definition non-invasive, so it cannot produce a liver abscess.","E":"Albendazole and aspiration is the ECHINOCOCCUS approach; this is amoebic, not hydatid."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Acanthamoeba — two diseases, two hosts","s":"Which pairing of Acanthamoeba syndrome, route and patient matches the lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":40,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — two diseases, two hosts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Acanthamoeba keratitis enters through the EYE and typically affects otherwise healthy soft contact lens wearers; granulomatous amoebic encephalitis enters through the nasal cavity or skin and affects immunocompromised patients, with a mortality approaching 100%","D":"Acanthamoeba keratitis affects immunocompromised patients and granulomatous amoebic encephalitis affects contact lens wearers","E":"Both syndromes follow swimming in freshwater and produce a fulminant meningitis within days","C":"Granulomatous amoebic encephalitis is diagnosed by corneal scraping; keratitis is diagnosed by lumbar puncture","B":"Both are diagnosed by stool examination, since Acanthamoeba is an intestinal amoeba"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct on syndrome, route, host and outcome — four facts the slide gives in one place.","D":"The two host groups are swapped. Immunosuppression is the GAE risk factor.","E":"Fulminant amoebic meningoencephalitis after freshwater swimming is the NAEGLERIA picture. GAE is described as CHRONIC.","C":"The specimens are swapped: corneal scraping for AK, lumbar puncture / brain biopsy / CT-MRI for GAE.","B":"Acanthamoeba is a FREE-LIVING amoeba of soil, fresh water and air-conditioning units, not a gut parasite."},"img":"slides_i/L5_40.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Balantidium — reproduction and ulcer","s":"Which statement about Balantidium coli is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium — reproduction and ulcer</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"It is the largest protozoan infecting humans; it reproduces asexually in the human gut by TRANSVERSE binary fission and sexually by conjugation; heavy infection produces flask-shaped ulcers that can perforate","E":"It reproduces in humans by LONGITUDINAL binary fission, like Giardia","C":"It is the smallest protozoan infecting humans and cannot cause ulceration","A":"Conjugation occurs in the human colon and is the source of the cysts passed in stool","B":"It has only a trophozoite stage, so transmission is direct person-to-person"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Note both the plane of fission (transverse — it is a ciliate) and that flask-shaped ulcers are NOT unique to Entamoeba.","E":"LONGITUDINAL binary fission is Giardia's. The direction of the split is a ciliate-vs-flagellate discriminator.","C":"It is explicitly the LARGEST protozoan in humans, and ulceration with perforation is listed.","A":"Conjugation is listed as its sexual process, but the slide attributes transverse binary fission to what happens in the human host; cysts arise by encystation, not conjugation.","B":"It has two stages, trophozoite AND cyst, and the mature cyst is the infective stage."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Giardia — where it lives, where it encysts","s":"Which statement about the site of each step in the Giardia life cycle is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":61,"c":"<b>Giardia — where it lives, where it encysts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Excystation and the trophozoite's habitat are in the SMALL intestine (duodenum and jejunum), but encystation occurs in the LARGE intestine","D":"Excystation, habitat and encystation all occur in the small intestine","E":"Excystation occurs in the stomach and the trophozoites live in the colon","A":"The trophozoite lives in the large intestine, which is why the diarrhoea contains blood and mucus","C":"Encystation occurs in the gallbladder, which is why the stool is fatty"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the split location is exactly the detail a careful stem will target. The adhesive disc anchors trophozoites to duodenal and jejunal mucosa; cysts form later, downstream.","D":"Half right — habitat yes, encystation no. The learning-guidance slide lists encystation in the large intestine on its own line.","E":"Excystation is in the small intestine, not the stomach.","A":"Giardia lives in the SMALL bowel and causes watery/fatty diarrhoea WITHOUT blood or mucus. Bloody mucous stool points to amoebiasis or balantidiasis.","C":"Giardia does not enter the gallbladder; the steatorrhoea comes from malabsorption at the small-bowel mucosa."},"img":"slides_i/L5_61.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"NSS vs iodine","s":"A cyst is examined first in normal saline and then in iodine. Which statement about what each preparation reveals is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":12,"c":"<b>NSS vs iodine</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Normal saline shows cigar-shaped or rounded chromatoid bodies; iodine stains the glycogen mass and makes the nuclei easier to count","D":"Iodine shows the chromatoid bodies; normal saline shows the glycogen mass","A":"Both preparations show identical features, so only one is needed","E":"Normal saline is used only for motility of trophozoites and reveals nothing in cysts","B":"Iodine dissolves the cyst wall, which is how the nuclei are released and counted"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct — this is precisely why the slide shows the same cyst twice, side by side.","D":"Straight swap of the two preparations.","A":"If they showed the same thing the laboratory would not do both; the slide's whole point is the contrast.","E":"Normal saline does show motility, but the chromatoid bodies are demonstrated in it too.","B":"Iodine stains; it does not dissolve the wall. Counting nuclei through an intact wall is exactly what it enables."},"img":"slides_i/L5_12.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"How many stages","s":"Which statement about the number and kind of life-cycle stages is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":6,"c":"<b>How many stages</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Trichomonas vaginalis has only a trophozoite; Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia intestinalis have a trophozoite and a cyst; Leishmania has a promastigote and an amastigote","C":"All four have a trophozoite and a cyst stage","A":"Trichomonas vaginalis has a trophozoite and a cyst, which is how it survives outside the host","E":"Leishmania has a trophozoite and a cyst; promastigote and amastigote are its vector stages only","D":"Giardia has only a trophozoite, which is why transmission requires direct sexual contact"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The slide sorts the four organisms by stage count on purpose, because the number of stages predicts how each one is transmitted.","C":"Trichomonas has NO cyst — which is why it needs direct contact and cannot survive a faecal–oral journey — and Leishmania's two stages are not trophozoite and cyst.","A":"A cyst is exactly what Trichomonas lacks; the absence is the point.","E":"Promastigote and amastigote ARE Leishmania's two stages; the amastigote is the intracellular form in the mammalian host, not a vector-only stage.","D":"Giardia has both a trophozoite and a cyst, and the mature 4-nucleus cyst is its infective stage."},"img":"slides_i/L5_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Balantidium — the pig link","s":"Which control measure is specific to balantidiasis rather than to the other intestinal protozoa in this lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":25,"c":"<b>Balantidium — the pig link</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Sanitary management of PIG faeces, and treating infected pigs as well as infected people","B":"Never using human faeces as fertiliser","E":"Boiling drinking water","D":"Washing vegetables thoroughly before eating","A":"Cleaning contact lenses with proper solution rather than tap water"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Pigs are the main host and the infection is explicitly zoonotic, so the control slide adds pig-directed measures that the Entamoeba and Giardia slides do not carry.","B":"This appears on the Entamoeba, Balantidium AND Giardia control slides — it does not discriminate.","E":"Shared with Giardia and Entamoeba.","D":"Shared with Giardia and Entamoeba.","A":"That is the ACANTHAMOEBA keratitis advice — a different organism entirely."},"img":"slides_i/L5_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Amoebiasis — routes of transmission","s":"Which list of transmission routes for Entamoeba histolytica matches the lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":13,"c":"<b>Amoebiasis — routes of transmission</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Faecal–oral via food-borne and water-borne spread, person-to-person, and sexual transmission by anal–oral contact","E":"Water-borne only, since the cyst is destroyed by drying","A":"Vector-borne by houseflies, plus water-borne","D":"Skin penetration by trophozoites, plus faecal–oral","C":"Air-borne inhalation of cysts, plus faecal–oral"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the sexual route is the one most often forgotten even though it is printed on both the transmission and the learning-guidance slide.","E":"Food-borne, person-to-person and sexual routes are all listed as well.","A":"Houseflies are mechanical vectors discussed in entomology, not a route listed here.","D":"Skin penetration is not a route for any Entamoeba; ingestion of the MATURE CYST is the mode of infection.","C":"Air-borne egg inhalation is ENTEROBIUS, from L1."},"img":"slides_i/L5_13.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Acanthamoeba — where it lives","s":"Which habitat list for Acanthamoeba matches the lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":35,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — where it lives</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Soil and warm fresh water — lakes, ponds, rivers, hot springs — plus taps, swimming pools and air-conditioning units","D":"Only the human large intestine, from which it is shed in cysts","E":"Only sea water and estuaries","C":"Only the cornea of contact lens wearers","A":"Only the soil of endemic tropical countries, with no aquatic reservoir"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the air-conditioning unit is the item people miss — it explains cases with no swimming history at all.","D":"Acanthamoeba is FREE-LIVING; it does not colonise the gut.","E":"The listed waters are fresh, not marine.","C":"The cornea is a site of DISEASE, not the environmental habitat.","A":"The distribution is worldwide and explicitly includes several aquatic reservoirs."},"img":"slides_i/L5_35.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L5|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Giardia — the numbers","s":"Which epidemiological statement about giardiasis matches the lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":55,"c":"<b>Giardia — the numbers</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Distribution is worldwide, with roughly 280 million new cases a year and prevalence of 15–20% in children, in whom it is commoner than in adults","B":"It is confined to the tropics and affects mainly adults","C":"Prevalence in children is under 1%, the same figure given for Balantidium coli","E":"About 50 million people are infected worldwide, of whom 90% are asymptomatic carriers","D":"It occurs only where pigs are kept"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct on distribution, incidence and the age skew.","B":"Distribution is worldwide, and children carry the higher burden.","C":"Under 1% is BALANTIDIUM's human prevalence figure, imported here.","E":"50 million infected with 90% asymptomatic is the ENTAMOEBA HISTOLYTICA slide.","D":"The pig association belongs to Balantidium coli."},"img":"slides_i/L5_55.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"The grid","s":"Which stain-and-size combination identifies which organism?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>The grid</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"mAFB 4–6 µm sporulated = Cryptosporidium; mAFB 8–12 µm unsporulated = Cyclospora; mAFB 25 × 15 µm ellipsoid = Cystoisospora; gram-chromotrope 1–2 µm = Microsporidia","E":"mAFB 1–2 µm = Cryptosporidium; gram-chromotrope 4–6 µm = Microsporidia","A":"All four are modified acid-fast and are separated by size alone","D":"All four are gram-chromotrope positive and are separated by sporulation state","B":"mAFB 10 µm sporulated = Cyclospora; mAFB 4–6 µm unsporulated = Cryptosporidium"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Learn it as a four-row grid: stain, size, sporulation state. Only the stain and the size change between the past-paper stems — everything else is identical.","E":"Both are reversed, and Microsporidia is never acid-fast.","A":"Microsporidia is the exception: gram-chromotrope only.","D":"Only Microsporidia uses gram-chromotrope.","B":"Both the sporulation states are reversed. Crypto is SPORULATED; Cyclospora is UNSPORULATED."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"An HIV patient has chronic watery diarrhoea. Modified acid-fast staining shows numerous red bodies 4–6 µm across. What is the diagnostic stage?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Unsporulated oocyst","A":"Sporulated oocyst","D":"Cyst","C":"Spore","B":"Trophozoite"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"The trap, and it is right for BOTH of the other coccidia — so the wrong answer is correct two-thirds of the time and the examiner knows it. Say it as a pair: 'Crypto sporulated, the other two unsporulated.'","A":"Correct. Cryptosporidium's oocyst is already SPORULATED when it is passed, which is also why it can autoinfect and why the infection becomes so severe in AIDS.","D":"'Cyst' belongs to the amoebae and Giardia (L5).","C":"'Spore' is Microsporidia, which is not acid-fast.","B":"Trophozoites are intracellular here and are not the diagnostic stage in stool."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Microsporidia","s":"Which three words, taken together, identify Microsporidia and exclude everything else in the lecture before you read the options?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Microsporidia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Barrel-shaped · iodine · 50 µm","E":"Ellipsoidal · modified acid-fast · 25 × 15 µm","C":"Round · modified acid-fast · 10 µm","A":"Round · modified acid-fast · 4–6 µm","D":"Belt-like · gram-chromotrope · 1–2 µm"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"That is a Trichuris egg from L1.","E":"That is Cystoisospora belli.","C":"That is Cyclospora cayetanensis.","A":"That is Cryptosporidium.","D":"Correct, and this three-word giveaway has been reused for at least five years. The 'belt' is the coiled polar filament inside the spore. Note that the gold-standard confirmatory method is ELECTRON MICROSCOPY, which makes sense at 1–2 µm."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Cyclospora vs Cryptosporidium","s":"Cyclospora and Cryptosporidium share a stain, a clinical picture, a habitat and a specimen. What are the only two things that separate them?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cyclospora vs Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Nothing separates them; they are reported together","C":"Stain (acid-fast vs gram-chromotrope) and habitat","A":"Host immune status and specimen type","D":"Shape (round vs ellipsoid) and the number of sporocysts","B":"Size (4–6 vs 8–12 µm) and sporulation state (sporulated vs unsporulated)"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"They are separately reportable organisms with different sizes.","C":"Both are modified acid-fast, and both live in the small bowel.","A":"Both affect the immunocompromised most severely and both are found in stool.","D":"Both are round; the sporocyst count is not the taught discriminator.","B":"Correct, and the exam uses both. A year-59 item asked how you know it is Cyclospora on mAFB and keyed '10 micron, round, UNSPORULATED' — both halves of that answer matter. If a stem tells you the oocyst is unsporulated, Cryptosporidium is excluded outright."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Cystoisospora","s":"Stool from a patient with months of watery diarrhoea and weight loss shows an ellipsoidal structure with a 'neck-like appearance', 25 × 15 µm, modified acid-fast. What is it?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Cystoisospora</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","B":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Microsporidia","C":"Blastocystis sp."},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Cyclospora is round and 8–12 µm.","D":"Cryptosporidium is round and 4–6 µm.","B":"Correct. 'Neck-like appearance' is the papers' own phrase for the tapered end of the ellipsoid — it does not appear on the slide, which is why it is worth planting now. A neat size anchor from the year-59 key: it is about the size of an Opisthorchis viverrini egg (25 × 12 µm).","E":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm and gram-chromotrope.","C":"Blastocystis is a different organism with a large central vacuole."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Zoonosis","s":"Which of these protozoa is NOT zoonotic?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Zoonosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Balantidium coli","D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","E":"Entamoeba histolytica","A":"Toxoplasma gondii","B":"Microsporidia"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Reservoirs include pigs, monkeys, dogs and rats.","D":"Reservoirs include cattle, buffalo, goats and sheep.","E":"Correct, and this item has been set twice with different option sets (years 59 and 60) and the same key. E. histolytica spreads faecal–orally through food, water, person-to-person and sexual (oral–anal) contact — but it is the HUMAN-ONLY one.","A":"The cat is the definitive host.","B":"Microsporidia is zoonotic. ⭐ Note the asymmetry inside L6: Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia are zoonotic; Cyclospora and Cystoisospora are not."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Prevention","s":"Why is chlorination the wrong answer for Cryptosporidium, and what is the right one?","lec":"L6","sl":14,"c":"<b>Prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are chlorine-RESISTANT; prevention is filtration or sterilisation of drinking water","E":"Chlorine is correct for Cryptosporidium but not for Giardia","B":"Chlorination works but only at pH below 6","D":"Boiling is ineffective because the oocyst is heat-stable","C":"No preventive measure exists"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the Milwaukee 1993 outbreak is the demonstration. ⚠ Contrast the free-living amoeba item from L5, where a child swims in a pool and the key IS 'add chlorine and adjust the pH'. Same-sounding question, opposite answers — read which organism the stem gives you.","E":"The opposite of the truth for Cryptosporidium.","B":"pH manipulation is the swimming-pool answer, not the Cryptosporidium one.","D":"Boiling is effective; the slide's wording is cleaned, filtered or sterilised water.","C":"Filtration is an effective and examinable measure."},"img":"slides_i/L6_14.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Cryptosporidium belongs to the autoinfection club even though the BM33 slides do not say so. What makes it possible?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"It survives in the bile duct and re-seeds the bowel","C":"Its trophozoite is acid-resistant and can re-invade","E":"It produces an egg that embryonates in the bowel lumen","B":"It migrates from the perianal skin back into the rectum","A":"Its oocyst is already sporulated when it forms, so it can excyst without ever leaving the gut"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Biliary cryptosporidiosis occurs in AIDS but is not the autoinfection mechanism.","C":"Trophozoites are intracellular and are not the re-infecting form.","E":"Sporozoa do not lay eggs.","B":"That route belongs to Enterobius.","A":"Correct. The BM30 answer key lists it explicitly alongside Taenia solium, Hymenolepis nana and Strongyloides stercoralis — and it follows logically from the sporulated oocyst. It is also why cryptosporidiosis becomes so severe in AIDS."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-crypto","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"An HIV patient presents with chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool stained with modified acid-fast shows numerous red vacuoles 4–6 µm in diameter. What is this diagnostic stage?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q24 — mAFB, 4–6 µm, 'what is the diagnostic stage?'</span>","o":{"A":"Spore","E":"Unsporulated oocyst","D":"Cyst","C":"Sporulated oocyst","B":"Trophozoite"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A spore is Microsporidia — and Microsporidia is never acid-fast.","E":"Correct for Cyclospora and Cystoisospora, which is precisely why it is the distractor here.","D":"A cyst is the L5 vocabulary (Entamoeba, Giardia, Balantidium).","C":"Correct, and set in near-identical words for three years running.","B":"Not a stool diagnostic stage for this organism."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-crypto","BM29 Q24"]},{"id":"I|L6|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-micro","t":"Microsporidia","s":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive man has chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool examination reveals oval organisms with a belt-like appearance, about 2 µm, using gram-chromotrope staining. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Microsporidia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q40 · year 57 — belt-like, gram-chromotrope, 1–2 µm</span>","o":{"B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","A":"Cryptosporidium spp.","D":"Microsporidia","C":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Blastocystis sp."},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Cyclospora is 8–12 µm and modified acid-fast.","A":"Cryptosporidium is 4–6 µm and modified ACID-FAST.","D":"Correct. Five appearances across four cohorts with the same three words. The 'belt' is the coiled polar filament. One BM30 version says DYSENTERY rather than watery diarrhoea — ignore that; the stain decides.","C":"Cystoisospora is 25 × 15 µm and modified acid-fast.","E":"Blastocystis is much larger with a prominent central vacuole. ⭐ Every distractor here would be acid-fast, so naming gram-chromotrope excludes them all before you read the list."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-micro","BM29 Q40","LS54 Q-micro (57)","BM28 Q46"]},{"id":"I|L6|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-aids","t":"Mode of infection","s":"A 50-year-old man with AIDS has a 3-month history of diarrhoea and 10 kg weight loss. What is the most likely mode of infection?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — AIDS, 3-month diarrhoea, 10 kg weight loss, 'mode of infection?'</span>","o":{"E":"Ingestion of unwashed vegetables","A":"Ingestion of mature cysts","B":"Ingestion of trophozoites","D":"Ingestion of uncooked pork","C":"Ingestion of water contaminated with sporulated oocysts"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Unwashed vegetables points at the soil-transmitted nematodes. ⭐ Every distractor is the right answer to a DIFFERENT lecture.","A":"'Mature cyst' is the answer for Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia (L5).","B":"Trophozoites are never infective by ingestion.","D":"Uncooked pork points at Taenia or Trichinella.","C":"Correct. The organism is never named — the lecturer's own written feedback explains the reasoning: AIDS plus chronic diarrhoea and weight loss means the opportunistic sporozoa group, so the mode of infection is ingestion of a sporulated oocyst."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-cysto","t":"Cystoisospora","s":"An HIV patient has chronic watery diarrhoea for three months. Stool examination shows an ellipsoidal structure with a neck-like appearance. What is the most likely mode of infection of this agent?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Cystoisospora</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 Q35 — ellipsoidal with neck-like appearance</span>","o":{"B":"Ingestion of a sporulated oocyst","D":"Ingestion of spores","C":"Ingestion of cysts","A":"Ingestion of mature cysts","E":"Ingestion of trophozoites"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The organism is Cystoisospora belli, and every coccidian in this lecture is acquired by swallowing a SPORULATED oocyst — note that this is the INFECTIVE form even for the two organisms whose oocysts are passed UNSPORULATED.","D":"Spores are Microsporidia.","C":"Cysts belong to the amoebae and Giardia.","A":"Mature cysts are the E. histolytica and Giardia answer.","E":"Trophozoites are never infective by ingestion."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q35"]},{"id":"I|L6|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q41","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"An outbreak of diarrhoea among HIV patients is reported in Town A. Investigation of the town's water supply finds a 5 µm round organism. What is the most likely pathogen?","lec":"L6","sl":8,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q41 — outbreak in HIV patients, 5 µm round organism in the water supply</span>","o":{"E":"Cryptosporidium spp.","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Blastocystis sp.","C":"Microsporidia"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A 5 µm round organism in a WATER SUPPLY causing an outbreak in immunocompromised people is Cryptosporidium — and the water route matters because the oocyst is chlorine-resistant, so filtration is what fails or succeeds.","B":"Cyclospora is 8–12 µm and is classically food-borne (raspberries and other fresh produce).","D":"Cystoisospora is 25 × 15 µm and ellipsoidal.","A":"Blastocystis is larger and is not the outbreak organism here.","C":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm."},"img":"slides_i/L6_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q8 (59)","t":"Cyclospora","s":"On a modified acid-fast stain, how do you know an oocyst is Cyclospora cayetanensis?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cyclospora</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Q8 cohort 59 — 'how do you know it is Cyclospora on mAFB?'</span>","o":{"D":"About 5 µm, round, and sporulated","E":"About 10 µm, round, with a large central vacuole","A":"About 10 µm, round, and UNSPORULATED","C":"About 25 µm, ellipsoidal, with a neck-like appearance","B":"About 1–2 µm with a belt-like appearance"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"5 µm and sporulated is Cryptosporidium.","E":"A large central vacuole describes Blastocystis; this was the actual distractor offered.","A":"Correct, and BOTH halves of the answer matter — the size and the sporulation state. Cyclospora's oocyst is passed unsporulated, which excludes Cryptosporidium outright.","C":"That is Cystoisospora belli.","B":"That is Microsporidia, and it would not be acid-fast at all."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q-zoo (60)","t":"Zoonosis","s":"Which of the following does NOT have zoonotic transmission?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Zoonosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 — 'which is NOT a zoonosis?', cohorts 59 and 60</span>","o":{"D":"Entamoeba histolytica","A":"Microsporidia","E":"Blastocystis sp.","C":"Toxoplasma gondii","B":"Cryptosporidium spp."},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the same key was given both times the question was set. The answer key's note: E. histolytica spreads faecal–orally via food, water, person-to-person and sexual contact, but has no animal reservoir.","A":"Microsporidia is zoonotic.","E":"Blastocystis is zoonotic.","C":"The cat is Toxoplasma's definitive host.","B":"Cattle, buffalo, goats and sheep are reservoirs for C. parvum."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q-zoo (59)"]},{"id":"I|L6|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q27","t":"When routine stool exam is negative","s":"An AIDS patient has chronic watery diarrhoea. A routine stool examination is reported as NEGATIVE. Which further test is most likely to make the parasitological diagnosis?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>When routine stool exam is negative</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q27 — AIDS patient with chronic watery diarrhoea whose routine stool examination is NEGATIVE; what should be requested?</span>","o":{"C":"Stool examination with modified acid-fast staining","B":"Stool examination with Giemsa stain","E":"Eosinophil count","A":"Duodenal aspiration for adult worms","D":"Serology for anti-parasite antibody"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and this is the whole reason the coccidia are taught as a block. Their oocysts are 4–25 µm, colourless and easily missed on a routine saline/iodine wet mount — you have to ASK for modified acid-fast staining. A 'negative' routine stool in an AIDS patient with chronic diarrhoea never excludes Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora or Cystoisospora.","B":"Giemsa is for blood parasites — malaria, Leishmania, Trypanosoma.","E":"Eosinophilia points to helminths; the intestinal sporozoa typically do NOT raise eosinophils.","A":"Duodenal aspiration is a Strongyloides/Giardia manoeuvre, and these organisms are not adult worms.","D":"Serology is not the routine route for any of these four. Remember the one exception in this lecture: Microsporidia needs gram-chromotrope, not acid-fast, with electron microscopy as the gold standard."},"img":"slides_i/L6_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written Q4 (57)","t":"Chronic diarrhoea in HIV — the four","s":"Which set names the organisms that cause CHRONIC WATERY DIARRHOEA in an HIV/AIDS patient?","lec":"L6","sl":6,"c":"<b>Chronic diarrhoea in HIV — the four</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 57 & 60 (written) — name the four organisms causing chronic watery diarrhoea in an HIV patient</span>","o":{"C":"Cryptosporidium spp., Entamoeba histolytica, Balantidium coli, Trichuris trichiura","E":"Cryptosporidium spp., Cyclospora cayetanensis, Cystoisospora belli, Microsporidia","D":"Giardia intestinalis, Cyclospora cayetanensis, Plasmodium spp., Microsporidia","B":"Cystoisospora belli, Toxoplasma gondii, Leishmania spp., Blastocystis sp.","A":"Cryptosporidium spp., Cystoisospora belli, Schistosoma mekongi, Trichomonas vaginalis"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Entamoeba, Balantidium and Trichuris cause DYSENTERY — bloody and mucoid, not watery.","E":"Correct, and this exact four-item list has been an examiner's written question in at least two cohorts. All four are opportunistic, all four give ACUTE SELF-LIMITED watery diarrhoea in the healthy and CHRONIC watery diarrhoea in the immunocompromised.","D":"Plasmodium is a blood parasite and causes no diarrhoea.","B":"Toxoplasma gives brain lesions in AIDS; Leishmania gives visceral disease.","A":"Schistosoma and Trichomonas are in neither the right organ nor the right lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L6_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 written Q4 (60)"]},{"id":"I|L6|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q17 (58)","t":"Megaloblastic anaemia","s":"Which parasitic infection is LEAST likely to cause megaloblastic anaemia?","lec":"L6","sl":25,"c":"<b>Megaloblastic anaemia</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 58 Q17 — which parasitic infection is LEAST likely to cause megaloblastic anaemia?</span>","o":{"D":"Isosporiasis (Cystoisospora belli)","B":"Amoebiasis","A":"Cyclosporiasis","C":"Cryptosporidiosis","E":"Microsporidiasis"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Cystoisospora blunts duodenal and jejunal villi.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. Entamoeba histolytica invades the colon and causes blood loss — an IRON-DEFICIENCY, microcytic picture. The other four damage the SMALL-BOWEL villi, causing malabsorption of vitamin B12 and folate, which is macrocytic.","A":"Cyclospora does the same in the small bowel.","C":"Cryptosporidium likewise causes villous atrophy.","E":"Microsporidia infects enterocytes directly. Do not confuse this with the OTHER classic cause of megaloblastic anaemia in the block — Diphyllobothrium latum (L3), which competes for B12 in the ileum."},"img":"slides_i/L6_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Sporulated or not — the key split","s":"Two children in the same ward have watery diarrhoea. One has Cryptosporidium, the other Cyclospora. Why is only one of them an immediate infection-control problem for the ward?","lec":"L6","sl":12,"c":"<b>Sporulated or not — the key split</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium sheds SPORULATED (already infective) oocysts, so person-to-person spread is possible at once; Cyclospora sheds UNSPORULATED oocysts that need 7–15 days outside the host to become infective","C":"Cyclospora sheds sporulated oocysts and Cryptosporidium unsporulated ones, so Cyclospora is the ward problem","B":"Both shed sporulated oocysts; the difference is only that Cryptosporidium oocysts are smaller","D":"Neither is transmissible person-to-person; both require a food or water vehicle","E":"Cryptosporidium is the problem because it is the only one of the two that stains with modified acid-fast"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and this single fact explains everything downstream — why Cryptosporidium causes daycare and hospital outbreaks and why Cyclospora shows up instead as food-borne clusters.","C":"Straight inversion of the two life cycles.","B":"Size differs too (4–6 µm vs 8–12 µm), but the sporulation state is the difference that matters clinically.","D":"Cryptosporidium's transmission slide explicitly lists person-to-person and zoonotic routes.","E":"Both stain with modified acid-fast — that is exactly why they are confused and why size and sporulation are the discriminators."},"img":"slides_i/L6_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Counting sporocysts and sporozoites","s":"Which description of the mature oocyst is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Counting sporocysts and sporozoites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Cryptosporidium: 4 sporozoites with NO sporocyst · Cyclospora: 2 sporocysts each holding 2 sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 2 sporocysts each holding 4 sporozoites","B":"Cryptosporidium: 2 sporocysts each holding 2 sporozoites · Cyclospora: 4 free sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 1 sporocyst holding 4 sporozoites","A":"All three contain 2 sporocysts with 4 sporozoites each","E":"Cryptosporidium: 8 sporozoites · Cyclospora: 8 sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 4 sporozoites","C":"Only Cystoisospora has sporozoites; the other two contain spores"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the arithmetic is worth holding: 4, then 2×2 = 4, then 2×4 = 8 — the biggest oocyst carries the most.","B":"Every assignment is shuffled. Cryptosporidium is the one with no sporocyst at all.","A":"That is Cystoisospora's arrangement applied to all three.","E":"Invented numbers; none of these is 8 except Cystoisospora's total.","C":"Spores are MICROSPORIDIA's stage — a different organism with a different stain."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Which stain","s":"A patient with AIDS has chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool modified acid-fast staining is negative. Which next step and rationale are correct?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>Which stain</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Request a Gram-chromotrope stain for Microsporidia — its 1–2 µm spores with a belt-like polar filament do not stain by modified acid-fast","A":"Repeat the modified acid-fast stain, because it detects all four intestinal sporozoa and must have been technically poor","C":"Request an iodine wet mount for amoebic cysts, because modified acid-fast excludes all protozoa","E":"Request a trichrome stain, which is the method for Microsporidia","D":"Stop testing — a negative modified acid-fast excludes an opportunistic parasite in this patient"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on stain, organism, size and morphology. The deck's final slide sets the two stains side by side for exactly this decision.","A":"Modified acid-fast covers Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora and Cystoisospora — it does NOT cover Microsporidia.","C":"Iodine wet mounts are used for amoebic and Giardia cysts (L5); they will not show a 1 µm spore.","E":"Trichrome and iron haematoxylin are the L5 amoeba/flagellate stains. The slide names Gram-chromotrope for Microsporidia.","D":"Microsporidia is precisely the organism a negative acid-fast fails to exclude, and it can also cause conjunctivitis and multiorgan disease."},"img":"slides_i/L6_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Size ladder","s":"An acid-fast stained stool contains ellipsoidal, oval structures roughly 25 × 15 µm. What are they?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Size ladder</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Cystoisospora belli oocysts — the largest and the only ellipsoidal one of the intestinal sporozoa","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis oocysts — 8–12 µm and spherical","A":"Cryptosporidium oocysts — 4–6 µm and spherical","D":"Microsporidia spores — 1–2 µm with a belt-like stripe","E":"Entamoeba histolytica cysts — 10–15 µm and spherical"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Shape settles it as fast as size does: Cystoisospora is ELLIPSOIDAL while Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora are spherical or round.","B":"Cyclospora is roughly half this size and round.","A":"Cryptosporidium is about a fifth of this size.","D":"Microsporidia spores are an order of magnitude smaller and need a different stain.","E":"Entamoeba cysts are not acid-fast, and the shape and size do not fit."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Chlorine resistance","s":"A swimming pool is linked to an outbreak of watery diarrhoea despite documented adequate chlorination. Which organism and which prevention measure fit?","lec":"L6","sl":8,"c":"<b>Chlorine resistance</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium — its oocyst resists chlorine, drying, freezing and salt water, so prevention relies on FILTRATION or boiling rather than chlorination","C":"Cyclospora — its oocyst is the chlorine-resistant one, and the answer is to raise the chlorine dose","B":"Entamoeba histolytica — its mature cyst survives chlorination, and the answer is metronidazole for swimmers","E":"Microsporidia — its spore is chlorine-resistant, and prevention is hand hygiene alone","D":"Any of these — all protozoan cysts and oocysts are equally chlorine-resistant"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the mechanistic reason the Milwaukee 1993 water-supply outbreak reached more than 400,000 people through a treated municipal system.","C":"Chlorine resistance is stated only on the Cryptosporidium slide, and raising the dose is the wrong lesson from it.","B":"Entamoeba is not the pool organism the deck highlights, and the measure offered treats cases rather than preventing exposure.","E":"Microsporidia's slide makes no chlorine-resistance claim; hand hygiene alone would not stop a waterborne source.","D":"The deck singles out Cryptosporidium for this property — generalising it is the trap."},"img":"slides_i/L6_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Cyclospora — the food vehicle","s":"Which statement about Cyclospora cayetanensis epidemiology matches the lecture?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cyclospora — the food vehicle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Transmission is mainly food-borne, with outbreaks classically linked to raspberries, and the 7–15 day sporulation delay means the food is contaminated well before it is eaten","D":"Transmission is mainly person-to-person in daycare centres, because the shed oocyst is already infective","E":"Transmission is zoonotic from cattle, as for Cryptosporidium","B":"Transmission is by ingesting spores in undercooked pork","A":"Transmission occurs only in immunocompromised people; healthy people cannot be infected"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the two facts explain each other — an oocyst that needs one to two weeks to ripen cannot spread directly, so it arrives on produce.","D":"Person-to-person spread requires an already-infective oocyst, which is CRYPTOSPORIDIUM's situation, not Cyclospora's.","E":"Zoonotic transmission is listed for Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia, not for Cyclospora.","B":"Spores and pork belong to other organisms entirely.","A":"Healthy people are infected too — the deck says they are asymptomatic or have self-limited watery diarrhoea."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Microsporidia beyond the gut","s":"Which statement about Microsporidia is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Microsporidia beyond the gut</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The SPORE is both the infective and the diagnostic stage; beyond diarrhoea it can cause conjunctivitis and systemic multiorgan disease, and the spore's belt-like stripe is the polar filament","A":"The oocyst is both infective and diagnostic, and disease is confined to the small intestine","C":"The spore is infective but the diagnostic stage is the trophozoite seen in biopsies","E":"It causes only keratitis and never diarrhoea","B":"It is not reported in Thailand"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on all three. Microsporidia is the organism in this lecture that leaves the gut, which is what makes it worth a separate stem.","A":"Microsporidia produces SPORES, not oocysts, and the deck lists eye and systemic disease explicitly.","C":"The life-cycle slide names the spore as both stages; no trophozoite is listed as diagnostic.","E":"Diarrhoea is its main presentation; conjunctivitis is an additional site.","B":"The deck carries a slide headed 'Reported in Thailand'."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Who spreads person-to-person","s":"Which two of the intestinal sporozoa have person-to-person and zoonotic transmission listed on their transmission slides?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Who spreads person-to-person</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium spp. and Microsporidia","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis and Cystoisospora belli","E":"Cryptosporidium spp. and Cyclospora cayetanensis","C":"Cystoisospora belli and Microsporidia","D":"All four have identical transmission routes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is consistent with their biology — both shed a stage that is infective the moment it leaves the body.","B":"Both of these list only food-borne and water-borne routes, because their oocysts must mature outside first.","E":"Cryptosporidium is right, Cyclospora is not.","C":"Microsporidia is right, Cystoisospora is not.","D":"The four transmission slides differ, and the difference is the examinable point."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Same symptom, two hosts","s":"Which statement about the clinical behaviour of the intestinal sporozoa is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":6,"c":"<b>Same symptom, two hosts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"In immunocompetent people the illness is asymptomatic or an acute self-limited watery diarrhoea; in HIV/AIDS it becomes chronic severe watery diarrhoea WITHOUT blood, with weight loss and dehydration","E":"In immunocompetent people the diarrhoea is bloody; in HIV/AIDS it is watery","A":"The diarrhoea contains blood and mucus in both groups, which distinguishes it from giardiasis","C":"Only people with HIV/AIDS can be infected at all","D":"Immunosuppressive drugs, travel and malnutrition are not risk factors; only HIV is"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and 'without blood' is the phrase to hold — it is what separates this group from amoebic colitis and balantidiasis.","E":"Neither group has bloody diarrhoea from these organisms.","A":"Blood and mucus points to Entamoeba histolytica or Balantidium coli, not to the sporozoa.","C":"Healthy people are infected; their infection is simply self-limited.","D":"The high-risk slide lists HIV/AIDS, immunosuppressive drugs, travellers AND malnutrition."},"img":"slides_i/L6_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Sporozoa by body system","s":"The lecture sorts the sporozoa by the body system they occupy. Which sorting is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":4,"c":"<b>Sporozoa by body system</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Gastrointestinal: Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora and Microsporidia · Haematopoietic and lymphatic: Toxoplasma gondii and Plasmodium spp., with Toxoplasma carrying the pregnancy-and-cat association","D":"Gastrointestinal: Toxoplasma and Plasmodium · Haematopoietic: Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora","B":"All the sporozoa are gastrointestinal; Plasmodium is a flagellate","C":"Cystoisospora belli is haematopoietic because it is an opportunistic parasite in AIDS","E":"Microsporidia is classified with the amoebae because it moves by pseudopodia"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, including the two 'others' — Blastocystis and Microsporidia sit outside the classical Sporozoa but are grouped with the gut organisms here.","D":"The two groups are swapped.","B":"Plasmodium is a sporozoan (Apicomplexa), and Toxoplasma and Plasmodium are placed in blood and lymphatic tissue.","C":"Being an AIDS-associated opportunist does not change its site; Cystoisospora is intestinal.","E":"Sporozoa lack organelles of motility altogether — that is the basis of the classification."},"img":"slides_i/L6_4.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Cystoisospora — two stains","s":"Which statement about detecting Cystoisospora belli is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Cystoisospora — two stains</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Its oocyst is large enough to be seen on a simple smear, and modified acid-fast staining confirms it; the stage found in stool is the UNSPORULATED oocyst with one sporocyst","A":"It can only be seen after modified acid-fast staining, and the stage in stool is the sporulated oocyst","D":"It requires Gram-chromotrope staining, like Microsporidia","B":"It is detected by antigen ELISA only; microscopy is insensitive","C":"Its diagnostic stage is the free sporozoite in stool"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both counts. Its size (20–33 µm) is what makes the simple smear feasible — the smaller coccidia are not reliably seen that way.","A":"The deck lists simple smear explicitly under lab investigation, and the stool stage is UNsporulated.","D":"Gram-chromotrope is for Microsporidia.","B":"No antigen ELISA is listed for this organism in the lecture.","C":"Sporozoites are inside sporocysts, not free in stool."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Prevention across the group","s":"Which prevention statement matches the lecture for this group of organisms?","lec":"L6","sl":14,"c":"<b>Prevention across the group</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Drink cleaned, filtered or sterilised water; wash vegetables — and for Cyclospora, fruit — thoroughly; keep good hand hygiene; control rests on treating carriers and defecating in a toilet","C":"Wear shoes and gloves when handling soil","D":"Freeze food below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","A":"Avoid raw freshwater crab and crayfish","B":"Boil bathing water for one minute to kill cercariae"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and note the FRUIT wording appears on the Cyclospora slide specifically, matching the raspberry outbreaks.","C":"Shoes and gloves block soil-transmitted nematode larvae (L1).","D":"Freezing is the Diphyllobothrium measure (L3) — and Cryptosporidium oocysts explicitly resist freezing.","A":"Raw crab is the Paragonimus exposure (L4).","B":"Boiling bathing water is schistosomiasis prevention (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L6_14.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Cryptosporidium — where it lives","s":"Which statement about Cryptosporidium's habitat and stages is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium — where it lives</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"It lives in the small intestine, and the SAME stage — the sporulated oocyst containing four sporozoites — is both the infective and the diagnostic stage","C":"It lives in the large intestine, which is why the diarrhoea is dysenteric","D":"It lives in the small intestine, but the diagnostic stage is the unsporulated oocyst","E":"It lives in the bile duct, like Opisthorchis","A":"It lives inside red blood cells, like Plasmodium"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the single-stage answer is what makes it directly transmissible — there is no maturation step between shedding and infecting.","C":"All four intestinal sporozoa inhabit the SMALL intestine, and the diarrhoea is watery, not dysenteric.","D":"Unsporulated shedding belongs to Cyclospora and Cystoisospora.","E":"Biliary habitat is Opisthorchis viverrini (L4).","A":"Intraerythrocytic life is Plasmodium, covered in L7."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Host status","s":"Which statement about host roles is correct for BOTH Toxoplasma and Plasmodium?","lec":"L7","sl":15,"c":"<b>Host status</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"The human is the definitive host of both, because that is where disease occurs","A":"The definitive host is where sexual reproduction occurs — the cat for Toxoplasma and the female Anopheles for Plasmodium — so the human is the intermediate host in both","D":"The human is the definitive host of Plasmodium and the intermediate host of Toxoplasma","B":"The mosquito is the intermediate host of Plasmodium because the parasite only passes through","C":"Host status is arbitrary and is not examinable"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Disease has nothing to do with host classification.","A":"Correct, and one rule covers both: definitive host = where SEXUAL reproduction happens. It is counter-intuitive, which is exactly why it is examined. And say FEMALE Anopheles — only females take blood meals, so 'Anopheles mosquito' without 'female' is an incomplete answer.","D":"The human is the intermediate host of both.","B":"The mosquito is the DEFINITIVE host of Plasmodium.","C":"It has been set repeatedly."},"img":"slides_i/L7_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Toxoplasma stages","s":"Which stages of Toxoplasma gondii are INFECTIVE for humans, and which are DIAGNOSTIC in humans?","lec":"L7","sl":16,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Infective: all three (oocyst, tachyzoite, bradyzoite). Diagnostic: tachyzoite and bradyzoite only","C":"Infective: oocyst only. Diagnostic: all three","E":"Infective and diagnostic are the same three stages","D":"Infective: tachyzoite only. Diagnostic: oocyst only","A":"Infective: bradyzoite only. Diagnostic: tachyzoite only"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Only the CAT produces oocysts, because only the cat hosts the sexual cycle — so an oocyst never appears in a human specimen, even though you can be infected by swallowing one. Say it as two lines: 'Infective in humans — all three. Diagnostic in humans — tachyzoite and bradyzoite.'","C":"You can also be infected by tachyzoites (unpasteurised milk, transfusion, transplacental) and bradyzoites (undercooked meat, transplant).","E":"They are different lists, and a question asking 'which stage would you find in this patient?' has a different answer from 'which stage can infect a human?'.","D":"Reversed.","A":"Both halves are wrong."},"img":"slides_i/L7_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Congenital triad","s":"A newborn of a mother exposed to cat faeces during pregnancy has which triad?","lec":"L7","sl":19,"c":"<b>Congenital triad</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Retinochoroiditis, MICROcephaly, intracranial calcification","D":"Retinochoroiditis, HYDROcephalus, intracranial calcification","E":"Myelitis, microcephaly, retinochoroiditis","C":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, myelitis","B":"Anencephaly, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The most dangerous distractor, because it is closest to the truth. Microcephaly belongs to ZIKA, a virus asked about in the virology half of the SAME exam.","D":"Correct — R-H-IC, and the key every year. Toxoplasma gives a BIG head (fluid); Zika gives a SMALL head.","E":"Same microcephaly trap, plus myelitis substituted for the calcification.","C":"Swaps out the intracranial calcification.","B":"Anencephaly is a neural-tube defect, not an infection at all."},"img":"slides_i/L7_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Species identification","s":"Which finding on a blood film identifies Plasmodium falciparum?","lec":"L7","sl":51,"c":"<b>Species identification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Amoeboid trophozoite","E":"Band form","B":"Comet shape","D":"Fimbriated red cell","A":"Ring form with double chromatin dots"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"An amoeboid trophozoite in an enlarged red cell with pink Schüffner's dots is P. VIVAX.","E":"The band form is P. MALARIAE.","B":"Comet shape is P. OVALE.","D":"A fimbriated (ragged-edged) red cell is P. OVALE.","A":"Correct, and set three years running. Every distractor is another species' signature, which is why the set is worth learning together."},"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Blood films","s":"A patient has fever and chills every 48 hours and malaria is suspected. Which method is best for SPECIES identification, and why?","lec":"L7","sl":44,"c":"<b>Blood films</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Malaria rapid diagnostic test — it names the species directly","A":"Thick blood smear — it concentrates about 20 times more blood into one field","C":"Thin blood smear — the red cells stay intact, so red-cell size, Schüffner's dots and shape are visible","B":"ELISA for antibody — it distinguishes species by antigen","D":"Modified acid-fast staining"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"RDTs detect antigen and are a screening tool, not the species-identification method taught here.","A":"Not wrong as a test — it is simply the answer to a DIFFERENT question. Thick films lyse the red cells, so you lose the very features that name the species. Learn it as a pair: THICK = is there malaria? THIN = which malaria?","C":"Correct, and the lecturer's own written feedback says so: you can see the parasite's characteristics, the various dots and the appearance of the infected red cells, which is what tells the species apart.","B":"Antibody detection cannot distinguish current species-level infection.","D":"Modified acid-fast is L6's stain for the intestinal sporozoa — reading 'acid-fast' in a malaria question should feel wrong immediately."},"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Relapse vs recrudescence","s":"What is the difference between relapse and recrudescence in malaria?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Relapse vs recrudescence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The terms are interchangeable","D":"Relapse comes from the blood; recrudescence comes from the liver","C":"Both come from hypnozoites, and any species can relapse","E":"Relapse occurs within 24 hours; recrudescence occurs after a year","B":"Relapse comes from dormant LIVER hypnozoites (P. vivax and P. ovale only); recrudescence comes from persisting BLOOD-stage parasites, usually P. falciparum after incomplete treatment"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"They are distinguished as a written question in the archive.","D":"Reversed.","C":"Only vivax and ovale form hypnozoites.","E":"Neither is defined by a time interval.","B":"Correct. One line: relapse comes from the liver, recrudescence comes from the blood. Therefore relapse means you failed to give PRIMAQUINE; recrudescence means you gave too little of the blood-stage drug."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Three verbs","s":"Four parasites enter through the skin. Which verb belongs to which?","lec":"L7","sl":32,"c":"<b>Three verbs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Plasmodium is deposited; filarial larvae are injected; Schistosoma is deposited","B":"Plasmodium penetrates intact skin; Schistosoma is injected; Leishmania is deposited","E":"All four are injected by an arthropod vector","C":"All four penetrate intact skin","A":"Plasmodium sporozoites are INJECTED; filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED and penetrate the bite wound; Schistosoma cercariae PENETRATE INTACT SKIN; Leishmania promastigotes are INJECTED by the sand fly"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"All three assignments are wrong.","B":"Every verb is misassigned, and Schistosoma has no vector at all.","E":"Schistosoma has no arthropod vector.","C":"Only Schistosoma does this.","A":"Correct, and the papers swap them deliberately — BM31's Leishmania question offered 'injection of sporozoite during mosquito's blood meals' as a wrong option, i.e. the malaria answer planted in a non-malaria stem."},"img":"slides_i/L7_32.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Complications","s":"Which is the most serious complication of P. malariae infection?","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Complications</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Irreversible nephrotic syndrome (quartan malarial nephropathy)","E":"Cerebral malaria","B":"Severe anaemia","D":"Lactic acidosis","C":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A nice discriminating fact: the mild, chronic species has the one renal complication.","E":"Cerebral malaria belongs to P. FALCIPARUM and is its commonest severe manifestation.","B":"Severe anaemia is most characteristic of falciparum.","D":"Lactic acidosis accompanies severe falciparum malaria.","C":"Also a falciparum complication."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Anaemia","s":"What are the three mechanisms of anaemia in malaria?","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Anaemia</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Rupture of infected red cells only","C":"Rupture of infected red cells; destruction and clearance of NON-infected red cells; dyserythropoiesis mediated by TNF-alpha","D":"Iron loss from gut bleeding, haemolysis and folate deficiency","E":"Splenic sequestration, renal failure and blood loss","A":"Autoimmune haemolysis alone"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"That accounts for only part of the red-cell loss.","C":"Correct. The part people leave out is 'AND NON-INFECTED' — it is why the anaemia is out of proportion to the parasitaemia.","D":"Gut bleeding is hookworm; folate deficiency is not the mechanism here.","E":"Splenomegaly contributes to clearance, but renal failure and blood loss are not the taught mechanisms.","A":"Immune-mediated destruction of uninfected cells is one of three, not the whole story."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Clinical","s":"Which feature is NOT common in Plasmodium falciparum infection, and why does it matter?","lec":"L7","sl":41,"c":"<b>Clinical</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Fever with chills","E":"A general rash — its absence separates malaria from dengue and from lymphatic filariasis","B":"Anaemia","A":"Splenomegaly","C":"Headache"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"The malarial paroxysm — cold stage, hot stage, sweating stage — is the defining symptom.","E":"Correct, and set in two cohorts. Malaria characteristically has neither rash nor lymphadenopathy. That is what separates it from dengue (rash, myalgia) and from filariasis (lymphadenopathy) in a 'fever in a returning traveller' stem — and mechanistically it fits, because sequestration is least in the skin.","B":"Anaemia is expected.","A":"Splenomegaly is common.","C":"Headache is common."},"img":"slides_i/L7_41.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q39","t":"Congenital toxoplasmosis","s":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman was exposed to cat faeces. Which triad affects the newborn?","lec":"L7","sl":19,"c":"<b>Congenital toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q39 · BM30 SUM1 Q41 · BM29 — pregnancy, cat faeces, which triad</span>","o":{"D":"Retinochoroiditis, microcephaly, intracranial calcification","E":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification","C":"Myelitis, microcephaly, retinochoroiditis","B":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, myelitis","A":"Anencephaly, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"The closest and therefore most dangerous distractor: MICROcephaly is ZIKA, which the same exam asks about in its virology half.","E":"Correct — R-H-IC, keyed identically in three cohorts, and dressed up again on this year's Road-to-NL slide as a veterinarian with first-trimester lymphadenopathy whose newborn has hydrocephalus and intracranial calcification.","C":"Same microcephaly trap.","B":"Drops the calcification.","A":"Anencephaly is a neural-tube defect, not an infection."},"img":"slides_i/L7_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q41","BM29 Q-triad"]},{"id":"I|L7|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pf","t":"P. falciparum film","s":"A child with fever is suspected of Plasmodium falciparum infection. What finding on the blood film would confirm the species?","lec":"L7","sl":59,"c":"<b>P. falciparum film</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q18 — what do you find in P. falciparum?</span>","o":{"E":"Amoeboid form","A":"Band form","B":"Comet shape","C":"Fimbriated red cell","D":"Ring form with double chromatin"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"Amoeboid trophozoite is P. vivax.","A":"Band form is P. malariae.","B":"Comet shape is P. ovale.","C":"Fimbriated red cell is P. ovale.","D":"Correct. Multiple infection of one red cell and the double-chromatin ring are falciparum's signatures, along with the banana-shaped gametocyte."},"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-pf","BM29 Q18"]},{"id":"I|L7|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q42","t":"P. vivax","s":"A patient has fever with chills and sweating every 48 hours. The blood film shows red cells larger than normal, a pseudopodia-like parasite, and pink dots. Which species?","lec":"L7","sl":61,"c":"<b>P. vivax</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q42 — 48-hour fever, enlarged RBC, pseudopodia, pink dots</span>","o":{"A":"Plasmodium vivax","D":"Plasmodium ovale","B":"Plasmodium knowlesi","C":"Plasmodium malariae","E":"Plasmodium falciparum"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Four independent pointers all agree: 48-hour periodicity, ENLARGED red cell, AMOEBOID (pseudopodia-like) trophozoite, and PINK Schüffner's dots.","D":"P. ovale also fits '48 hours' and also enlarges the red cell — what separates it is the FIMBRIATED, comet-shaped red cell rather than an amoeboid parasite.","B":"P. knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle.","C":"P. malariae has a 72-hour cycle and a band form in a normal-sized red cell.","E":"P. falciparum gives an irregular fever and does not enlarge the red cell."},"img":"slides_i/L7_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q11","t":"Falciparum stages in blood","s":"A man with paroxysmal fever and myalgia, who is not immunocompromised, is diagnosed with UNCOMPLICATED Plasmodium falciparum malaria. Which stages would you expect on his blood film?","lec":"L7","sl":59,"c":"<b>Falciparum stages in blood</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 SUM1 Q13 · BM29 SUM1 Q13 — which stages are found in the blood? ⚠ The BM30 and BM29 answer keys DISAGREE on this item; the BM33 slides settle it.</span>","o":{"C":"Trophozoite, schizont and gametocyte","E":"Trophozoite and gametocyte only","A":"Cyst, tachyzoite and bradyzoite","B":"Hypnozoite, schizont and cyst","D":"Ookinete, trophozoite and cyst"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"⚠ This is the answer BM29's student-written key gives, and it is wrong; BM30's key highlights 'trophozoite and gametocyte only', which agrees with the slides. If schizonts DO appear in a peripheral film, that is a marker of SEVERE malaria — so the word 'uncomplicated' in the stem is doing the work. Where two circulating keys disagree, follow the BM33 deck.","E":"Correct, and the lecture says so twice. Slide 59 prints beside the mature schizont: 'This stage is NOT found in uncomplicated malaria patients', because maturing parasites CYTOADHERE to deep capillary endothelium and disappear from the circulation. Slide 63 then lists the diagnostic stages of P. falciparum as early trophozoite (ring forms, double chromatin, multiple infection, Accolé, Maurer's clefts) and the banana-shaped gametocyte — no schizont.","A":"That is Toxoplasma gondii's life cycle, taught in the same lecture.","B":"Hypnozoites are in the LIVER, and P. falciparum makes none at all — only vivax and ovale do.","D":"The ookinete is a MOSQUITO stage, formed in the midgut after fertilisation."},"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q13","BM29 Q13"]},{"id":"I|L7|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-film","t":"Diagnosis","s":"A case of malaria is suspected in a patient with fever and chills every two days. What is the best method for SPECIES identification?","lec":"L7","sl":44,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — fever and chills every 2 days, best method for species identification</span>","o":{"C":"Thin blood smear","D":"Thick blood smear","B":"Malaria rapid diagnostic test","E":"ELISA for antibody detection","A":"Modified acid-fast staining"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the lecturer's written feedback explains it: the thin film preserves the infected red cell, so you can see its size, the dots and the parasite's shape — which is what names the species.","D":"The designed distractor. A thick film concentrates about 20 times more blood and is better for DETECTION, but it lyses the red cells and destroys the species features.","B":"An RDT screens; it is not the taught species method.","E":"Antibody cannot establish the current species.","A":"Modified acid-fast belongs to L6's intestinal sporozoa — it should feel wrong in a malaria question."},"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q12 (59)","t":"Toxoplasma in HIV","s":"An HIV-positive patient has a space-occupying lesion in the brain. Which organism is most likely?","lec":"L7","sl":17,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma in HIV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Q12 (59) — HIV patient with a brain lesion</span>","o":{"B":"Entamoeba histolytica","C":"Toxoplasma gondii","E":"Naegleria fowleri","D":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Acanthamoeba spp."},"a":"C","r":{"B":"A companion item asks which organism does NOT cause a brain lesion, and the key is E. histolytica — it makes a LIVER abscess instead.","C":"Correct. Toxoplasmic encephalitis is the classic reactivation disease of the immunocompromised, and BM32's paper carries the treatment follow-up (pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine, with leucovorin for the marrow toxicity).","E":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis, an acute fulminant meningitis rather than a mass lesion.","D":"Balamuthia does cause granulomatous encephalitis, but it is far rarer and is not the expected answer in HIV.","A":"Acanthamoeba causes GAE in the immunocompromised, again far less commonly than Toxoplasma."},"img":"slides_i/L7_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written (57)","t":"Toxoplasma routes","s":"Which source-and-route set is correct for the three infective stages of Toxoplasma gondii?","lec":"L7","sl":13,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma routes</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 — written question, cohorts 57 and 60: source and route for all three stages</span>","o":{"C":"Only the bradyzoite is infective for humans","A":"Sporulated oocyst → undercooked meat; tachyzoite → cat faeces; bradyzoite → milk","D":"All three stages are acquired only from cat faeces","E":"Sporulated oocyst → transplacental; tachyzoite → cat faeces; bradyzoite → water","B":"Sporulated oocyst → food or water contaminated with cat faeces; tachyzoite → unpasteurised milk, blood transfusion, transplacental; bradyzoite → undercooked meat or organ, organ transplantation"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"All three stages are infective for humans — that is the point of the written question.","A":"Every source is misassigned.","D":"Meat and milk are both established routes.","E":"All three are misassigned.","B":"Correct, and it is the marking key's own answer. A neat way to hold it: the three lines are grouped by PHYSICAL STATE — faeces, fluid, solid tissue."},"img":"slides_i/L7_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 written (60)"]},{"id":"I|L7|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q25 (58)","t":"Complications","s":"Which is the most serious complication of Plasmodium malariae infection?","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Complications</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Q25 (58) — most serious complication of P. malariae</span>","o":{"A":"Lactic acidosis","B":"Cerebral malaria","D":"Severe anaemia","C":"Irreversible nephrotic syndrome","E":"Renal failure from blackwater fever"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Lactic acidosis accompanies severe falciparum.","B":"Cerebral malaria belongs to P. falciparum.","D":"Severe anaemia is a falciparum complication.","C":"Correct — quartan malarial nephropathy, an immune-complex glomerulopathy that does not reverse with antimalarial treatment.","E":"Blackwater fever is massive intravascular haemolysis in falciparum infection."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (59)","t":"Plasmodium ovale","s":"A 30-year-old man has relapsing fever every 48 hours. The blood film shows FIMBRIATED, comet-shaped red cells with Schüffner's dots. Which species is this?","lec":"L7","sl":62,"c":"<b>Plasmodium ovale</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 59 Q4 — 48-hour fever with FIMBRIATED red cells</span>","o":{"C":"Plasmodium malariae","B":"Plasmodium vivax","A":"Plasmodium falciparum","D":"Plasmodium ovale","E":"Plasmodium knowlesi"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"P. malariae runs on a 72-hour cycle with a BAND-form trophozoite and rosette schizonts.","B":"P. vivax also gives a 48-hour cycle, enlarged cells and Schüffner's dots — which is exactly why the examiner adds 'fimbriated'. Vivax shows an AMOEBOID trophozoite and MORE than 12 merozoites per schizont.","A":"P. falciparum gives Maurer's clefts, ring forms with double chromatin dots, and banana-shaped gametocytes; the red cell is not enlarged.","D":"Correct. Fimbriated (ragged-edged) or comet-shaped red cells are unique to P. ovale. Its mature schizont holds FEWER than 12 merozoites (6–12).","E":"P. knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle and mimics malariae."},"img":"slides_i/L7_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q22 (57)","t":"What falciparum does NOT do","s":"Which finding is NOT expected in malaria?","lec":"L7","sl":41,"c":"<b>What falciparum does NOT do</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 57 & 58 — 'what is NOT common in P. falciparum infection?'</span>","o":{"C":"Jaundice","D":"Fever with chills and rigors","B":"Generalised rash","E":"Anaemia","A":"Headache, nausea and muscle pain"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Jaundice follows haemolysis and is listed on the same slide.","D":"The malaria paroxysm — cold stage, hot stage, sweating stage — is the classic presentation.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer, and the lecture prints it as a line of its own: 'No rash and No lymphadenopathy.' This is how malaria is separated from dengue and from the viral exanthems, which are its main clinical rivals in a febrile Thai patient.","E":"Anaemia is expected, from lysis of both infected AND uninfected red cells.","A":"These are the non-specific early symptoms the lecture warns are indistinguishable from flu."},"img":"slides_i/L7_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q22 (58)","LS54 Q8 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L7|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q1 (57)","t":"Severe malaria","s":"All of the following are recognised complications of SEVERE malaria EXCEPT:","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Severe malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q1 — complications of severe malaria, EXCEPT</span>","o":{"A":"Acute renal failure","C":"Cerebral malaria","B":"Acute hepatic failure","E":"Pulmonary oedema","D":"Black water fever with haemoglobinuria"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Acute renal failure is on the list.","C":"Cerebral malaria is listed first and is the MOST COMMON severe complication.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. Jaundice occurs in severe malaria, but it is HAEMOLYTIC — the liver itself does not fail. Acute hepatic failure is not on the WHO severe-malaria list the lecture reproduces.","E":"Pulmonary oedema is on the list.","D":"Black water fever — massive intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinuria — is on the list. All of these are effectively P. falciparum, the major species for severe disease."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written Q3 (57)","t":"Hypnozoite and relapse","s":"A true RELAPSE of malaria — as opposed to recrudescence — is caused by which of the following?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Hypnozoite and relapse</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 written Q3–4 — define relapse and distinguish it from recrudescence</span>","o":{"C":"Persistence of small numbers of asexual parasites in the blood after incomplete treatment","E":"Reactivation of dormant hypnozoites in the liver, in P. vivax and P. ovale only","B":"Re-infection from a second mosquito bite","D":"Release of gametocytes from the bone marrow","A":"Reactivation of dormant hypnozoites, in all five species"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"That defines RECRUDESCENCE, which is the falciparum pattern — no hypnozoite, so any recurrence must come from surviving blood-stage parasites.","E":"Correct. Only P. VIVAX and P. OVALE make hypnozoites — sporozoites that sit dormant in the hepatocyte instead of undergoing schizogony. That is why these two need PRIMAQUINE added to chloroquine: chloroquine clears the blood stage, primaquine kills the liver stage.","B":"Re-infection is a new infection, not a relapse.","D":"Gametocytes are the transmissible stage and do not cause clinical recurrence.","A":"The species restriction is the entire point of the question; falciparum, malariae and knowlesi do not form hypnozoites."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 written Q4 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L7|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q49","t":"Stages in the human host","s":"Which stages of Plasmodium are found in the HUMAN host?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Stages in the human host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q49 — which stages of Plasmodium are found in the human host?</span>","o":{"C":"Tachyzoite and bradyzoite only","A":"Sporozoite, ookinete, oocyst, gametocyte","B":"Trophozoite, cyst and mature cyst only","D":"Sporozoite, hepatic schizont (± hypnozoite), merozoite, trophozoite, erythrocytic schizont, gametocyte","E":"Merozoite, ookinete and sporozoite only"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Tachyzoite and bradyzoite belong to Toxoplasma gondii, taught in the same lecture — a deliberate near-miss.","A":"Ookinete and oocyst are MOSQUITO stages — the sporogonic cycle in the gut wall.","B":"Trophozoite and cyst are the intestinal protozoa (L5).","D":"Correct. The human is the INTERMEDIATE host and carries everything from the injected sporozoite through to the gametocyte. The mosquito is the DEFINITIVE host because that is where sexual reproduction happens.","E":"Mixes human and mosquito stages and omits most of them."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Which host is which","s":"A stem asks you to name the definitive host of Plasmodium. Which answer is correct, and on what principle?","lec":"L7","sl":31,"c":"<b>Which host is which</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"The female Anopheles mosquito — sexual reproduction (gamogony and sporogony) occurs in it, and the definitive host is defined by sexual reproduction","D":"Humans — the definitive host is the one that becomes ill","C":"Humans — the definitive host is the one in which the parasite spends most of its life","A":"The female Anopheles mosquito — because it is the vector, and vectors are always definitive hosts","B":"Both, because Plasmodium reproduces sexually in the human liver and in the mosquito gut"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is the counter-intuitive one: the mosquito is the definitive host and we are merely the intermediate host where schizogony happens.","D":"Disease severity has nothing to do with host classification — a tempting but wrong principle.","C":"Duration is also irrelevant to the definition.","A":"Right answer, wrong reason. Being a vector does not make an arthropod a definitive host — in filariasis (L2) the mosquito is the INTERMEDIATE host.","B":"No sexual reproduction occurs in humans. The liver stage is schizogony, which is asexual."},"img":"slides_i/L7_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Species ID — the two-step algorithm","s":"A thin film shows ENLARGED infected red cells and a mature schizont containing about 8 merozoites. Which species is it?","lec":"L7","sl":51,"c":"<b>Species ID — the two-step algorithm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Plasmodium ovale — enlarged RBC narrows it to vivax or ovale, and fewer than 12 merozoites settles it as ovale","C":"Plasmodium vivax — enlarged RBC means vivax, and merozoite number does not discriminate","B":"Plasmodium malariae — 6–12 merozoites is the malariae count","A":"Plasmodium falciparum — schizonts with few merozoites are typical of falciparum","E":"Plasmodium knowlesi — it is the only species with enlarged cells and few merozoites"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the algorithm is worth running in this order every time: RBC size first, then merozoite count.","C":"Merozoite count is exactly what separates vivax (>12) from ovale (<12) once the cell is enlarged.","B":"P. malariae also has 6–12 merozoites, but it infects NORMAL-sized cells — step 1 has already excluded it.","A":"P. falciparum infects normal-sized cells, has >12 merozoites, and its schizonts are usually absent from peripheral blood altogether.","E":"The deck states P. knowlesi cannot be distinguished by microscopy, so it is never the answer to a morphology stem."},"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Growing trophozoite shapes","s":"Which pairing of growing-trophozoite appearance with species is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":55,"c":"<b>Growing trophozoite shapes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Band form across the red cell = P. malariae; amoeboid with pseudopodia-like extensions = P. vivax; fimbriated, comet-shaped red cell = P. ovale","D":"Band form = P. vivax; amoeboid = P. malariae; comet-shaped cell = P. falciparum","A":"Amoeboid = P. ovale; band = P. falciparum; comet-shaped cell = P. vivax","E":"All four species show an amoeboid growing trophozoite; only the gametocyte discriminates","C":"Band form = P. falciparum, which is why its schizonts are sequestered"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on all three, and each fits its cell: vivax stretches inside an enlarged cell, malariae stretches across a normal one, ovale distorts the cell into a comet.","D":"Band and amoeboid are swapped, and the comet cell is ovale, not falciparum.","A":"Every assignment is wrong.","E":"The growing trophozoite is one of the most useful discriminators in the deck; the gametocyte helps only for falciparum.","C":"P. falciparum's growing trophozoite is described simply as 'only bigger'; sequestration explains the missing schizont, not a band form."},"img":"slides_i/L7_55.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Toxoplasma — route carries stage","s":"Which pairing of transmission route with the Toxoplasma stage responsible is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":13,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma — route carries stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Unpasteurised milk, blood transfusion and mother-to-child transmission all deliver the TACHYZOITE; raw meat and organ transplantation deliver the BRADYZOITE; contaminated food or water from cat faeces delivers the OOCYST","C":"All three routes deliver the oocyst, since it is the only environmentally resistant stage","D":"Raw meat delivers the tachyzoite; transplacental transmission delivers the bradyzoite","A":"Blood transfusion delivers the oocyst; raw meat delivers the tachyzoite","B":"Only the oocyst is infective; tachyzoites and bradyzoites are diagnostic stages only"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The route tells you the stage, and the stage tells you the prevention — which is why the deck draws the same information twice.","C":"The oocyst comes ONLY from cat faeces. Milk, blood and the placenta carry tachyzoites; meat and transplanted organs carry bradyzoites.","D":"Exactly inverted: raw meat carries the tissue-cyst BRADYZOITE, and congenital transmission carries the TACHYZOITE.","A":"Both halves inverted — transfusion carries tachyzoites and raw meat carries bradyzoites.","B":"The deck states plainly that ALL parasite stages are infective stages; that is what makes Toxoplasma so hard to avoid."},"img":"slides_i/L7_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Hypnozoite and periodicity","s":"Which statement about the malaria life cycle is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Hypnozoite and periodicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Only P. vivax and P. ovale form hypnozoites, which stay dormant in hepatocytes; the erythrocytic cycle takes 48 h in vivax and ovale, 72 h in P. malariae, and 24–48 h in P. falciparum","D":"All five species form hypnozoites, which is why relapse can follow any malaria","E":"Hypnozoites are dormant forms inside red cells, and the erythrocytic cycle is 48 h in every species","C":"P. malariae forms hypnozoites and has the shortest erythrocytic cycle","A":"P. falciparum forms hypnozoites, which is why it causes cerebral malaria"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on both the species restriction and all three cycle lengths — and the hypnozoite is why vivax and ovale need radical cure.","D":"Only vivax and ovale form hypnozoites; falciparum and malariae do not relapse from the liver.","E":"Hypnozoites are in HEPATOCYTES, not red cells, and the cycle lengths differ between species.","C":"P. malariae has the LONGEST cycle at 72 h and forms no hypnozoite.","A":"P. falciparum forms no hypnozoite; cerebral malaria comes from sequestration of infected red cells, not from liver forms."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Falciparum film features","s":"A traveller from the Thai–Myanmar border has a film showing numerous small rings occupying about a fifth of each red cell, several cells with two parasites, some rings hugging the cell margin, and a banana-shaped gametocyte. No schizonts are seen. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":59,"c":"<b>Falciparum film features</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"This is P. falciparum, and the ABSENCE of schizonts is expected because its schizont-containing cells sequester in deep capillaries rather than circulating","A":"This is P. falciparum, and the absence of schizonts means the infection is too early to treat","B":"This is P. vivax, because multiple infection of one cell is its hallmark","C":"This is P. malariae, because its rings are the smallest of the four species","D":"The banana-shaped gametocyte excludes falciparum, since banana forms are seen only in P. ovale"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Every listed feature is falciparum, and the deck notes explicitly that the schizont stage is not found in uncomplicated malaria because those red cells adhere in deep vessels.","A":"Species right, inference wrong — and dangerously so. Falciparum is the species you treat urgently, not the one you wait on.","B":"Multiple infection of a single cell is a FALCIPARUM feature; vivax enlarges the cell and shows Schuffner’s dots.","C":"P. malariae infects normal-sized cells but is recognised by its BAND form and rosette schizonts with 6–12 merozoites.","D":"The banana (crescent) gametocyte is the classic falciparum finding; P. ovale gametocytes are round."},"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Dots and clefts","s":"Which statement about stippling in infected red cells is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":47,"c":"<b>Dots and clefts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Schüffner's dots appear in P. vivax and P. ovale, both of which enlarge the red cell; Maurer's clefts appear in P. falciparum, which does not","C":"Schüffner's dots appear in P. falciparum and Maurer's clefts in P. vivax","A":"Schüffner's dots appear in all four species and therefore cannot discriminate","D":"Maurer's clefts are the malarial pigment seen at high power","E":"Stippling appears only in P. malariae, which is why its band form is so distinct"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the pairing is easy to hold: the two species that swell the cell are the two that show Schuffner’s dots.","C":"Straight swap of the two.","A":"If stippling were universal it would be useless — its restriction to vivax and ovale is precisely its diagnostic value.","D":"Malarial pigment (haemozoin) is yellowish-brown granular material inside the parasite; Maurer’s clefts are red-cell membrane structures.","E":"P. malariae shows no Schuffner’s dots; its discriminator is the band-form trophozoite in a normal-sized cell."},"img":"slides_i/L7_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Toxoplasma oocyst timing","s":"A pregnant woman asks whether she must give away her indoor cat. Which reply is consistent with the lecture?","lec":"L7","sl":9,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma oocyst timing</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The oocyst is shed UNsporulated and takes 3–5 days to become infective, so daily litter-box emptying (ideally by someone else), cooking meat thoroughly and washing vegetables matter more than rehoming the cat","D":"The oocyst is infective the moment it is passed, so any contact with the litter box transmits infection immediately","C":"Cats shed tachyzoites in their faeces, so the litter box is the main danger and the cat must go","B":"Toxoplasma is transmitted only by raw meat, so the cat is irrelevant","E":"The oocyst needs 7–15 days to sporulate, the same as Cyclospora"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the 3–5 day window is the practical point: a litter box emptied every day never contains an infective oocyst.","D":"The oocyst leaves the cat UNsporulated, which is exactly why daily cleaning works.","C":"Cats shed OOCYSTS, not tachyzoites, and rehoming is not what the control slide recommends.","B":"Raw meat is one route among several — the deck lists six modes of infection.","E":"7–15 days is CYCLOSPORA’s sporulation time from L6; Toxoplasma takes 3–5 days."},"img":"slides_i/L7_9.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Tachyzoite vs bradyzoite","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the two Toxoplasma stages found in humans?","lec":"L7","sl":10,"c":"<b>Tachyzoite vs bradyzoite</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"The tachyzoite is a crescent-shaped rapidly multiplying form, 5–12 × 2–4 µm, found in nucleated cells and body fluids such as blood, milk and CSF; the bradyzoite is the slowly multiplying form inside a tissue cyst over 200 µm across holding 50–3,000 organisms","D":"The bradyzoite is the rapidly multiplying form in body fluids and the tachyzoite is the dormant tissue form","E":"Both are found only in cat intestinal epithelium","A":"The tissue cyst contains 4 sporozoites within 2 sporocysts","C":"Neither is infective; only the oocyst can transmit toxoplasmosis"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on shape, size, speed and location for both stages — the names encode the speed: tachy- fast, brady- slow.","D":"Inverted. Tachy means fast; it is the tachyzoite that multiplies rapidly in body fluids.","E":"Cat intestinal epithelium is where the SEXUAL cycle and oocyst production occur; tachyzoites and bradyzoites occupy tissues throughout the intermediate host.","A":"Two sporocysts with four sporozoites each describes the sporulated OOCYST, not the tissue cyst, which holds 50–3,000 bradyzoites.","C":"All three stages are infective — stated explicitly on the stages slide."},"img":"slides_i/L7_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"No rash, no lymphadenopathy","s":"A febrile traveller returning from Ranong has chills, headache, myalgia, nausea and jaundice, but no rash and no enlarged lymph nodes. Which statement is most useful?","lec":"L7","sl":40,"c":"<b>No rash, no lymphadenopathy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Absence of rash and lymphadenopathy is consistent with malaria and helps separate it from dengue and from toxoplasmosis, and the classical paroxysm runs cold stage → hot stage → sweating stage","E":"Absence of rash argues against malaria, which typically produces a maculopapular eruption","D":"Lymphadenopathy is the cardinal sign of malaria and its absence excludes the diagnosis","C":"The paroxysm runs hot stage → cold stage → sweating stage","B":"Jaundice excludes malaria, since haemolysis does not occur"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The deck prints ‘No rash and No lymphadenopathy’ on the symptom slide precisely because that is how malaria is separated from its febrile mimics.","E":"Malaria produces no rash — that is the point of the slide.","D":"Lymphadenopathy is a TOXOPLASMOSIS sign, and its absence supports rather than excludes malaria.","C":"The paroxysm begins with the COLD stage (rigors), then hot, then sweating.","B":"Jaundice and anaemia are both listed under uncomplicated malaria; haemolysis is central to the disease."},"img":"slides_i/L7_40.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Severe malaria","s":"Which statement about severe malaria matches the lecture?","lec":"L7","sl":39,"c":"<b>Severe malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Severe and complicated malaria is chiefly P. falciparum, cerebral malaria is its commonest severe manifestation, and haemoglobinuria from massive intravascular haemolysis is called blackwater fever","E":"Severe malaria is chiefly P. vivax because of its hypnozoites, and blackwater fever is renal in origin","C":"P. malariae causes the most severe disease because of its 72-hour cycle","A":"Cerebral malaria is rare; acute renal failure is the commonest severe manifestation","B":"Blackwater fever refers to black stools from gastrointestinal bleeding in DIC"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on species, on the commonest complication, and on the meaning of blackwater fever — dark urine from free haemoglobin, not from the gut.","E":"Hypnozoites cause RELAPSE, not severity. Severe disease is falciparum.","C":"P. malariae causes the mildest, most chronic disease of the four.","A":"Cerebral malaria is explicitly named as the commonest severe manifestation.","B":"Blackwater fever is haemoglobinURIA — the darkness is in the urine."},"img":"slides_i/L7_39.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Toxoplasma diagnosis","s":"Which statement about diagnosing toxoplasmosis matches the lecture?","lec":"L7","sl":20,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Serological antibody testing (IgG, IgM, IgA, IgE) is the primary method, with Giemsa-stained smears of lymph node, blood, CSF, bone marrow or placenta for tachyzoites, plus PCR and CT/MRI for brain lesions","B":"Stool examination for oocysts is the primary method in humans","C":"Modified acid-fast staining of stool is the method of choice","D":"Diagnosis is made only by tissue biopsy showing bradyzoite cysts; serology is unhelpful","A":"Thick and thin blood films are the primary method, as for malaria"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and note the breadth of acceptable specimens for tachyzoite detection — placenta is the one people forget in a congenital case.","B":"Humans do not shed Toxoplasma oocysts; only cats do.","C":"Modified acid-fast is for the intestinal coccidia of L6.","D":"Serology is named as the PRIMARY method, so calling it unhelpful inverts the slide.","A":"Blood films are the malaria method; Toxoplasma is not an intraerythrocytic parasite."},"img":"slides_i/L7_20.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Three cycles","s":"Which set of names for the three phases of the Plasmodium life cycle is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":33,"c":"<b>Three cycles</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Exo-erythrocytic cycle in the human liver, erythrocytic cycle in human red cells, and sporogonic cycle in the female Anopheles","E":"Sporogonic cycle in the human liver, erythrocytic cycle in red cells, and exo-erythrocytic cycle in the mosquito","A":"Schizogony in the mosquito, gamogony in the liver, and sporogony in red cells","B":"Exo-erythrocytic cycle in red cells and erythrocytic cycle in the liver","C":"There are only two cycles, one in each host"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Two of the three happen in us, which is why the human is the intermediate host twice over and the mosquito only once.","E":"Sporogony happens in the MOSQUITO; ‘exo-erythrocytic’ means outside the red cell, i.e. the liver.","A":"Schizogony is asexual replication in humans; gamogony and sporogony occur in the mosquito.","B":"The two are swapped — exo-erythrocytic is by definition the non-red-cell (liver) phase.","C":"The deck states there are THREE cycles."},"img":"slides_i/L7_33.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"Blood films and RDT","s":"Which statement about malaria laboratory methods matches the lecture?","lec":"L7","sl":44,"c":"<b>Blood films and RDT</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The thin film is used for species identification while the thick film concentrates more blood and so detects low parasitaemia; antigen-detecting rapid tests and PCR are also listed","A":"The thick film is used for species identification and the thin film for sensitivity","C":"Only PCR can identify the species; microscopy merely confirms that malaria is present","E":"Rapid diagnostic tests detect antibody, so they are positive only after two weeks","B":"Serology replaces microscopy in endemic areas"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The thin film preserves red-cell morphology, which is what the species algorithm depends on; the thick film lyses the cells to pack more blood into the field.","A":"Swapped. A thick film destroys the red-cell outline, so RBC size — step 1 of the algorithm — cannot be judged on it.","C":"Microscopy is what identifies the species in routine practice; PCR is listed as an additional molecular method.","E":"The rapid test listed detects ANTIGEN, so it is positive during active parasitaemia.","B":"No such replacement is described; microscopy remains central."},"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"P. knowlesi","s":"Which statement about Plasmodium knowlesi in Thailand matches the lecture?","lec":"L7","sl":25,"c":"<b>P. knowlesi</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"It cannot be distinguished from the other species by light microscopy, and Thai case numbers have risen sharply, with Ranong, Songkhla and Trat reporting the most","D":"It is readily identified on a thin film by its banana-shaped gametocyte","C":"It has disappeared from Thailand since the malaria elimination programme began","E":"It is the species that forms hypnozoites and therefore relapses","A":"It infects only macaques and has never been reported in humans in Thailand"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on both: the deck marks it ไม่สามารถแยกได้ด้วยกล้องจุลทรรศน์ and gives the provincial breakdown, concentrated along the borders.","D":"Banana-shaped gametocytes belong to P. falciparum; knowlesi closely resembles P. malariae on film.","C":"Cases have RISEN, from 2 to 105, and provinces previously declared malaria-free have seen renewed transmission.","E":"Hypnozoites are formed by P. vivax and P. ovale.","A":"Human cases are exactly what the slide is reporting."},"img":"slides_i/L7_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Answer format","s":"A photograph of a chigger mite is shown and you are asked 'What is the medical importance?'. Which written answer scores the mark?","lec":"L8","sl":13,"c":"<b>Answer format</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Scrub typhus","E":"Vector of scrub typhus","A":"Orientia tsutsugamushi","B":"It causes scrub typhus","C":"Eschar resembling a cigarette burn"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Slide 65 marks a bare 'scrub typhus' WRONG in red — this is the single commonest way the mark is lost.","E":"Correct. Every answer must open with one of three phrases — 'Direct injury…', 'Vector of…' or 'Intermediate host of…'. 'Vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi' would also have scored.","A":"Naming the organism alone fails for the same reason. The lecturer will accept the organism, but only after 'vector of'.","B":"Wrong verb. The mite CARRIES the organism; it does not cause the disease. He used the same point about Anopheles and malaria.","C":"A clinical sign, not a medical importance."},"img":"slides_i/L8_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Chigger mite","s":"An arachnid specimen measures 0.2 mm, has a dorsal scutum, and has THREE pairs of legs. What is it, and why does the leg count fit?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Demodex — its legs are bud-like and easily miscounted","A":"A hard tick — adult hard ticks have 3 pairs of legs","C":"A soft tick — it has no scutum, which is why only 3 pairs are visible","B":"A chigger mite larva — the larva is the only parasitic stage and it has 3 pairs of legs","E":"Sarcoptes scabiei — burrowing mites lose a pair of legs"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Demodex has 4 pairs of stubby legs and a cylindrical body, and lives inside a hair follicle.","A":"Adult and nymphal ticks have 4 pairs, and a tick is 2–10 mm — ten to fifty times this size.","C":"Soft ticks do lack a scutum, but this specimen has one, and they still have 4 pairs of legs.","B":"Correct, and this is the lecture's one deliberate exception to 'arachnid = 8 legs'. The adult and nymph are free-living with 4 pairs; only the 6-legged larva, the 'chigger', feeds on a host.","E":"Sarcoptes has 4 pairs of legs; nothing loses a pair."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Ticks","s":"What is the single feature that separates a hard tick from a soft tick, and what does it explain about how the two look when engorged?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Ticks</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The hard tick has festoons; the soft tick has a capitulum","B":"The soft tick has a scutum; the hard tick's cuticle is flexible","E":"The hard tick has four pairs of legs; the soft tick has three","A":"The hard tick has a scutum, which locks its body shape; the soft tick has none and swells like a balloon","D":"The hard tick feeds on blood; the soft tick does not feed"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Festoons are a hard-tick feature, but the capitulum is present in both — it is simply not VISIBLE from above in a soft tick.","B":"Exactly reversed. Ixodidae (hard) have the scutum; Argasidae (soft) do not.","E":"Both have four pairs as adults; only the LARVA of either has three.","A":"Correct. The scutum is a rigid dorsal plate. With it the outline is fixed; without it the body expands as it fills with blood or eggs — the lecturer's own image.","D":"Both are blood-feeding ectoparasites."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"Under the microscope a mosquito has palpi as long as its proboscis and wing margins with alternating white and black scales. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":1005,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Vector of Japanese B encephalitis","A":"Vector of dengue virus, chikungunya and Zika virus","E":"Vector of malaria, and vector of lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti)","B":"Vector of lymphatic filariasis (Brugia malayi)","D":"Mechanical transmitter of enteric pathogens"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Japanese B encephalitis is Culex, which is plain brown with short palpi.","A":"That is Aedes, identified by snow-white scales on the thorax and short palpi.","E":"Correct. Palpi equal to the proboscis makes it an ANOPHELINE, and the spotted wing confirms Anopheles — the malaria vector, which also carries W. bancrofti.","B":"B. malayi is carried by Mansonia only — the exception that every matching question is built on.","D":"Mosquitoes are biological, not mechanical, transmitters."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1005.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"Which feature should you look at FIRST to tell Aedes from Culex?","lec":"L8","sl":1013,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Resting at 45 degrees to the surface","C":"A trilobed scutellum","A":"Short palpi relative to the proboscis","D":"Snow-white scales on the thorax","E":"Pilose antennae"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"An Anopheles feature — and useless in the lab anyway, since a mounted specimen is not resting on anything.","C":"Shared by all three culicines (Culex, Aedes, Mansonia), so it cannot separate them.","A":"Also shared by all three culicines; it only separates them from Anopheles.","D":"Correct. The lecturer said to go straight to the thorax: Aedes carries scales 'like snow settled on it'. Culex is uniformly brown with no distinguishing marking at all — it is identified by the ABSENCE of a feature.","E":"Pilose antennae mark a FEMALE, not a genus."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1013.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Rove beetle","s":"A student wakes with burning, itchy red patches on the front of the elbow AND on the facing surface of the forearm, having crushed an insect there the night before. What is the diagnosis, and why do the two patches match?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Paederus dermatitis — paederin transferred to the opposite skin surface when the joint was flexed","E":"Classical scabiasis — the mite burrowed along the flexure","C":"Pediculosis — the louse fed at two adjacent points","A":"Bedbug bites — bugs feed in rows along exposed skin","D":"Norwegian scabiasis — crusted lesions appear symmetrically"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. This is the 'kissing lesion'. Paederin digests tissue, so wherever the toxin lands it burns, and a flexed joint presses the two skin surfaces together — giving mirror-image lesions. The phrase 'after crushing the arthropod' appears in every past-paper version.","E":"Scabies gives BURROWS, classically between the fingers, with night-time itch and no burning and no crushed insect.","C":"Lice cause itching without a mirror-image burn.","A":"Bedbug lesions are separate papules in a line, not matched patches, and there is no crushing history.","D":"Crusted scabies occurs in the immunocompromised with a huge mite burden — a different clinical picture entirely."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Beetles","s":"Which beetle toxin can threaten vision, and what does it do to skin?","lec":"L8","sl":35,"c":"<b>Beetles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Paederin — it causes large blisters, and in the eye conjunctivitis only","D":"Cantharidin — it causes large blisters, and in the eye keratitis and corneal ulceration","E":"Cantharidin — it digests tissue, producing kissing and linear lesions","A":"Paederin — it causes corneal ulceration and renal failure","B":"Both toxins are identical; only the beetle's name differs"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Paederin belongs to the ROVE beetle and gives pustules and kissing/linear lesions, with no ocular hazard listed.","D":"Correct. Cantharidin is the blister beetle's toxin. Skin: vesicles and large blisters. Eye: conjunctivitis, keratitis, corneal abrasion and ulceration, which may threaten vision. Ingested, it can cause renal failure, shock and death.","E":"Tissue digestion and the kissing lesion are paederin's, not cantharidin's.","A":"The toxin name is wrong for both effects.","B":"They are different molecules with clearly different clinical pictures — which is exactly what the exam tests."},"img":"slides_i/L8_35.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Vector types","s":"What defines a MECHANICAL transmitter?","lec":"L8","sl":16,"c":"<b>Vector types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"The arthropod must take a blood meal to transmit","E":"The pathogen multiplies inside the arthropod but does not change stage","C":"The pathogen develops but does not multiply inside the arthropod","D":"The pathogen neither multiplies nor develops inside the arthropod","A":"The arthropod is always an insect rather than an arachnid"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"The house fly and cockroach, the two commonest mechanical vectors, do not bite at all.","E":"Multiplication inside the vector makes it BIOLOGICAL.","C":"Development inside the vector also makes it biological.","D":"Correct, and it is printed on slide 16 as the key characteristic. The organism is simply carried on the body or feet — picked up from faeces and dropped on food, drink or tissue.","A":"Irrelevant to the definition — and both classes contain biological vectors."},"img":"slides_i/L8_16.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Flea","s":"Which single arthropod in this lecture can correctly be answered under ALL THREE headings of medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":51,"c":"<b>Flea</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The flea","B":"The chigger mite","D":"The rove beetle","C":"The tsetse fly","E":"Demodex"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — the lecturer called it the answer that 'covers the universe'. VECTOR: plague and murine typhus (Xenopsylla cheopis), cat-scratch disease (Ctenocephalides felis). INTERMEDIATE HOST: Dipylidium caninum, Hymenolepis nana, H. diminuta. DIRECT INJURY: flea-allergy dermatitis.","B":"Vector only.","D":"Direct injury only — it carries nothing and hosts nothing.","C":"Vector only.","E":"Direct injury only (folliculitis, inflammatory acne)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_51.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Blood-sucking flies","s":"Match the three blood-feeding Diptera to their one identifying feature.","lec":"L8","sl":31,"c":"<b>Blood-sucking flies</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Tsetse = hairy body; kissing bug = forward-projecting proboscis; sand fly = wings forming an 'X'","D":"Tsetse = wings forming an 'X'; kissing bug = hairy body; sand fly = forward-projecting proboscis","E":"Tsetse = forward-projecting proboscis; kissing bug = wings forming an 'X'; sand fly = hairy body and wings","A":"All three are identified by a piercing-sucking proboscis and cannot be separated on morphology","C":"Tsetse = cone-shaped head; kissing bug = spotted wings; sand fly = short palpi"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Same three features, shuffled again.","D":"All three features are present in the lecture but assigned to the wrong insects.","E":"Correct. One feature each is all the lecturer asked for: the tsetse's proboscis projects straight forward off the head, the kissing bug's wings overlap into a distinct letter X, and the sand fly is 'a mosquito with hair on it' — hairy body AND wings.","A":"All three do have piercing mouthparts, but each has a printed distinguishing feature — which is exactly what the exam asks for.","C":"A cone-shaped head belongs to the kissing bug, spotted wings to Anopheles, and short palpi to the culicine mosquitoes."},"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Trypanosomes","s":"Which pairing of parasite, vector and continent is correct?","lec":"L8","sl":31,"c":"<b>Trypanosomes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Trypanosoma brucei — sand fly — visceral leishmaniasis","A":"Trypanosoma brucei — kissing bug — American trypanosomiasis","E":"Trypanosoma cruzi — tsetse fly — African sleeping sickness","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi — sand fly — Chagas disease","B":"Trypanosoma brucei — tsetse fly — African sleeping sickness"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Two errors: wrong vector and wrong disease.","A":"The vector and the continent have both been swapped. The kissing bug carries T. cruzi in the Americas.","E":"The parasite and vector are mismatched.","D":"The sand fly carries Leishmania, not a trypanosome.","B":"Correct. T. brucei travels with the tsetse fly (Glossina) and causes AFRICAN trypanosomiasis, i.e. sleeping sickness."},"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Mosquito breeding","s":"Which breeding site matches which mosquito?","lec":"L8","sl":1011,"c":"<b>Mosquito breeding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Anopheles = clean natural slow-moving water; Culex = dirty low-oxygen water; Aedes = clean water in household containers; Mansonia = swamps with aquatic plants","D":"Anopheles = household containers; Culex = forest streams; Aedes = dirty drains; Mansonia = rice fields","E":"Anopheles = dirty water; Culex = household containers; Aedes = forest streams; Mansonia = brackish water","B":"All four breed in any standing water and cannot be separated by habitat","C":"Anopheles = swamps with aquatic plants; Culex = rock pools; Aedes = animal footprints; Mansonia = water jars"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and this four-line summary is printed verbatim as an answer key in the หลักสูตร 54 bank. Water quality is the fastest way to hold the grid: wild-clean, dirty, household-clean, plant.","D":"Every pairing is shuffled.","E":"Anopheles and Culex are swapped, as are Aedes and Mansonia.","B":"Habitat is exactly what these questions test.","C":"Rock pools and animal footprints are Anopheles sites; water jars are Aedes sites."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Filariasis","s":"Three of the four mosquito genera taught carry the same filarial species and one carries a different one. Which is the odd one out?","lec":"L8","sl":1010,"c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Anopheles, which carries Brugia malayi while the other three carry W. bancrofti","D":"Mansonia, which carries Brugia malayi while the other three carry Wuchereria bancrofti","A":"Aedes, which carries Brugia timori while the other three carry W. bancrofti","C":"Culex, which carries no filaria at all","E":"All four carry W. bancrofti; none carries B. malayi"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Anopheles carries W. bancrofti, like Culex and Aedes.","D":"Correct, and this single split is the highest-value line in the whole mosquito table — a vector-matching question can only be built on it. Mansonia is also commonest in southern Thailand.","A":"Aedes carries W. bancrofti; B. timori is not taught here.","C":"Culex is listed as a W. bancrofti vector.","E":"Mansonia's species is B. malayi, printed on its own slide."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1010.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Myiasis","s":"Which stage of the fly causes myiasis, and which classification is NOT used on the slide?","lec":"L8","sl":26,"c":"<b>Myiasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The larva (maggot); haematogenous myiasis is not a listed type","A":"The adult female; cavitary myiasis is not a listed type","E":"The larva; wound myiasis is not a listed type","B":"The pupa; cutaneous myiasis is not a listed type","D":"The egg; furuncular myiasis is not a listed type"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The three listed types are CUTANEOUS (furuncular or creeping/migratory), WOUND and CAVITARY (nasal, oral, ophthalmic, intestinal, ears, urogenital, rarely brain). Maggots invade from the surface — there is no bloodborne form.","A":"The adult only lays the eggs; cavitary myiasis is listed.","E":"Wound myiasis is one of the three listed types.","B":"The pupa is non-feeding; cutaneous myiasis is listed.","D":"Eggs are washed off by cleaning; furuncular is a listed subtype of cutaneous."},"img":"slides_i/L8_26.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Intermediate hosts","s":"Which pairing of arthropod and helminth is correct?","lec":"L8","sl":19,"c":"<b>Intermediate hosts</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Freshwater crab = intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana","E":"Cyclops = second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","A":"Cyclops = first intermediate host of Gnathostoma spinigerum","B":"Flea = second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","C":"Chigger mite = intermediate host of Dipylidium caninum"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Hymenolepis uses the FLEA.","E":"Paragonimus uses the freshwater/mountain CRAB as its second intermediate host; the first is a small freshwater snail.","A":"Correct, and printed on slide 19 with 'first' spelt out.","B":"The flea hosts Hymenolepis and Dipylidium, not Paragonimus.","C":"The chigger mite is a vector, not an intermediate host — it hosts nothing."},"img":"slides_i/L8_19.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Mosquito eggs","s":"A lab image shows mosquito eggs laid singly, each boat-shaped with a float on either side. Which genus?","lec":"L8","sl":1025,"c":"<b>Mosquito eggs</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Mansonia","D":"Culex","C":"Aedes","B":"Anopheles","A":"Toxorhynchites"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Mansonia lays a sunflower-shaped cluster with pointed tips that pierce aquatic plant roots.","D":"Culex lays a raft, described on the slide as a cup-shaped corolla, like a ring of flower petals.","C":"Aedes also lays singly, but the eggs are rugby-ball / oval shaped with a patterned shell and NO floats. They also survive drought for up to a year.","B":"Correct. Boat-shaped with lateral floats, laid singly — the floats are the discriminator.","A":"Toxorhynchites is the large non-biting mosquito mentioned only in passing."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1025.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Demodex","s":"Which description matches Demodex spp., and what is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":62,"c":"<b>Demodex</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Leathery body without a scutum; causes tick paralysis","B":"Laterally flattened with jumping hind legs; causes flea-allergy dermatitis","D":"Cylindrical body with four pairs of bud-like legs, living in hair follicles; causes folliculitis and inflammatory acne","C":"Three pairs of legs with a dorsal scutum; vector of scrub typhus","A":"Cone-shaped head with an X on the wings; vector of Chagas disease"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"That is the soft tick.","B":"That is the flea.","D":"Correct. It is the only arthropod in the lecture whose habitat is inside a human hair follicle, and a Thai report attributes roughly 30% of inflammatory acne to it. Treating that acne with anti-inflammatories alone leaves the mite in place.","C":"That is the chigger mite larva.","A":"That is the kissing bug."},"img":"slides_i/L8_62.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Prevention","s":"A rove beetle is crawling on your forearm. What should you do?","lec":"L8","sl":36,"c":"<b>Prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Gently blow it away, or flick it off with a piece of paper","A":"Slap it quickly so it cannot move further","C":"Brush it off firmly with the palm, whose skin is thick enough to resist the toxin","D":"Rinse it off with hot water","E":"Spray insecticide onto the skin"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Crushing releases paederin instantly; blowing or brushing with paper does not.","A":"Slapping is exactly what produces the dermatitis.","C":"The specific warning he gave: the palm resists, but you then scratch elsewhere and spread the toxin over the body. There are case reports.","D":"Wash with soap and water and then apply a cold compress — but only after the beetle is off, and heat is not part of the advice.","E":"Spraying insecticide on your own skin is marked wrong wherever it appears — for chigger prevention the answer is REPELLENT."},"img":"slides_i/L8_36.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q41","t":"Chigger mite","s":"A man has a skin lesion described as an eschar resembling a cigarette burn. What bit him?","lec":"L8","sl":60,"c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q41 · BM30 SUM1 Q42 — 'eschar cigarette-like'</span>","o":{"B":"Head louse","A":"Bedbug","E":"Soft tick","D":"Scabies mite","C":"Chigger mite"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Head lice cause scalp itching and nits on hair shafts, with no eschar.","A":"Bedbug bites are separate itchy papules, often in a row along exposed skin — there is no necrotic crust.","E":"A soft tick bite can cause relapsing fever and tick paralysis but does not produce this eschar.","D":"Scabies produces burrows and intense night itch, classically between the fingers.","C":"Correct. The eschar is the signature of the chigger-mite bite and of scrub typhus, and the lecturer told the class in so many words that NL asks about this lesion."},"img":"slides_i/L8_60.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q42"]},{"id":"I|L8|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q41+","t":"Chigger mite","s":"A 45-year-old male farmer presents with a week of fever, headache and myalgia. He works in rice fields in a rural area. Examination shows a black crusted wound. What is the most likely pathogen?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Slide 69 (NL2023) · BM32 SUM2 Q41+ · BM31 SUM2 Q34 · BM29</span>","o":{"E":"Leptospira interrogans","A":"Orientia tsutsugamushi","C":"Clostridium perfringens","B":"Sporothrix schenckii","D":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Leptospirosis is the deliberate near-miss: a febrile rice-field worker with myalgia fits perfectly — until you look at the eschar, which leptospirosis does not produce.","A":"Correct. The eschar is what makes this scrub typhus rather than anything else on the list, and the rice field is where chigger mites live.","C":"Gas gangrene follows a contaminated penetrating wound and is acutely toxic, not a week of fever.","B":"Sporotrichosis follows inoculation from vegetation and gives nodules that ascend along a lymphatic channel.","D":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a HARD TICK disease of the Americas, not a Thai mite disease."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q34","BM30 slide 69 / NL2023","BM29 Q4"]},{"id":"I|L8|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q34","t":"Rove beetle","s":"A patient has a kissing lesion with burning and itching at the flexure of the right arm after crushing an arthropod. What is the most likely diagnosis?","lec":"L8","sl":35,"c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q34 · BM30 SUM1 Q36 · BM29 · BM30 formative</span>","o":{"D":"Local necrotizing ulcer","B":"Classical scabiasis","C":"Pediculosis","A":"Paederus dermatitis","E":"Norwegian scabiasis"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"A necrotizing ulcer is a deeper, progressive lesion, not a symmetrical flexural burn.","B":"Scabies gives burrows, typically in finger webs, with night-time itch and no burning, and no crushing history.","C":"Lice cause itch without a mirror-image burn.","A":"Correct. Three words give it away: KISSING lesion, BURNING, and 'after crushing'. Paederin digests tissue and transfers to the facing skin surface when the joint bends.","E":"Crusted scabies occurs in the immunocompromised with an enormous mite burden — a different picture."},"img":"slides_i/L8_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q36","BM30 Paederus item","BM29 Q2"]},{"id":"I|L8|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q38","t":"Hard tick","s":"A patient was bitten by a small, round, black arthropod that has a scutum and a capitulum visible at the anterior end. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Hard tick</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q38 · BM29 — 'scutum and visible capitulum'</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of leishmaniasis","B":"Vector of filariasis","E":"Vector of hepatitis B","C":"Vector of scrub typhus","D":"Vector of Lyme disease"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Leishmaniasis is carried by the sand fly.","B":"Filariasis is mosquito-borne.","E":"Hepatitis B is bloodborne and has no arthropod vector.","C":"The planted near-miss: scrub typhus is the CHIGGER MITE, which also has a scutum but has 3 pairs of legs and is ten to fifty times smaller.","D":"Correct. The answer key spells out the reasoning: four pairs of legs, a scutum on the dorsal body and a VISIBLE capitulum identify a HARD tick — vector of Lyme disease and Rocky Mountain spotted fever."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q-tick"]},{"id":"I|L8|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q32 (57)","t":"Vector matching","s":"Which of the following vectors does NOT match its organism or disease?","lec":"L8","sl":18,"c":"<b>Vector matching</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q32 · cohorts 57 and 58</span>","o":{"A":"Culex spp. — Japanese B encephalitis","D":"Anopheles spp. — Plasmodium spp.","C":"Sand fly — plague","B":"Mansonia spp. — Brugia malayi","E":"Tsetse fly — trypanosomiasis"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Correct pairing.","D":"Correct pairing, printed on the biological-vector slide.","C":"Correct — this is the wrong pairing. The sand fly carries LEISHMANIASIS; plague is carried by the FLEA (Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea).","B":"Correct, and the one species split worth memorising — only Mansonia carries B. malayi.","E":"Correct: Glossina carries Trypanosoma brucei."},"img":"slides_i/L8_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 Q32 (58)"]},{"id":"I|L8|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q9","t":"Sand fly","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with a painless lesion on his left arm after being bitten by a small, hairy, mosquito-like insect. Skin biopsy shows oval-shaped organisms with a kinetoplast within macrophages. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Sand fly</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q9 — painless lesion, kinetoplast in macrophages</span>","o":{"C":"Cyclosporiasis","D":"Malaria","A":"Toxoplasmosis","B":"Leishmaniasis","E":"Lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Cyclospora is a faecal-oral intestinal coccidian.","D":"Malaria is Anopheles-borne and the parasites sit inside red cells, not macrophages.","A":"Toxoplasma is acquired from cat faeces or undercooked meat, not from a bite, and has no kinetoplast.","B":"Correct. 'Small, hairy, mosquito-like' is the slide's description of the SAND FLY word for word, and amastigotes with a kinetoplast inside macrophages are Leishmania.","E":"Filariasis gives lymphatic obstruction, and the microfilariae are in blood, not in macrophages."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q6","t":"Pubic louse","s":"A 25-year-old woman has itching of her eyelids and pubic area. A small arthropod is isolated from her eyelash and pubic hair. Which is the most effective prevention for this patient?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Pubic louse</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q6 · BM29 เฉลย · BM31 SUM1 — eyelash and pubic hair</span>","o":{"B":"Treating the patient and her family","C":"Using a condom","E":"Avoiding head-to-head contact","A":"Avoiding sexual activity with the infested person","D":"Avoiding prolonged antibiotic use"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Sensible for treating an existing infestation, but the question asks about prevention of transmission.","C":"The trap, and the BM29 key says so explicitly: the louse lives on the HAIR AROUND the genitals, not on the organ, so a condom does not prevent transmission.","E":"That is the HEAD louse measure — the wrong louse for this site.","A":"Correct. Pthirus pubis spreads by close body contact during sex, so the effective measure is avoiding contact with the infested partner.","D":"Antibiotics have no role in a louse infestation."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q6","BM29 Q6"]},{"id":"I|L8|25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q5 (59)","t":"Aedes breeding","s":"Where does the mosquito that transmits dengue lay its eggs?","lec":"L8","sl":1019,"c":"<b>Aedes breeding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q5 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"C":"Clean water stored in household containers","E":"Dirty drainage water","A":"Water troughs used by household cattle","D":"Standing water in the forest","B":"Under the leaves of aquatic plants"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Aedes is domestic: water jars, uncovered tanks, plant-pot saucers, cupboard-leg dishes, vases, tyres, tins and coconut shells. The answer key prints a four-line summary of all four genera.","E":"That is Culex, which prefers dirty, low-oxygen water.","A":"Also Culex territory — water contaminated with animal dung.","D":"That is Anopheles: rock pools, animal footprints and slow-moving forest streams.","B":"That is Mansonia, whose larvae attach to the roots of aquatic plants."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1019.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (59)","t":"Head louse","s":"A mother finds nits on her daughter's hair. What is the organism?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Head louse</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q6 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"D":"Body louse","C":"Pubic louse","E":"Head louse","A":"Scabies mite","B":"Demodex"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"The body louse lives in CLOTHING SEAMS, not on hair — and it is the only louse that is a disease vector.","C":"The pubic louse is found on pubic hair, eyelashes, eyebrows and axillae.","E":"Correct. Nits are louse eggs cemented to hair shafts, and the scalp is the head louse's site. SITE is the discriminator in every louse question.","A":"Scabies produces burrows in skin folds, not eggs on hair.","B":"Demodex lives inside hair follicles and is invisible to the naked eye."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Scabies","s":"A child has intense itching and burrow tracks between the fingers; siblings and classmates are affected too. Skin biopsy shows a mite with four pairs of legs. What is it?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Scabies</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q26 · BM28 Q47 · หลักสูตร 54 Q11 (59)</span>","o":{"B":"Pediculus humanus capitis","D":"Pthirus pubis","C":"Sarcoptes scabiei","E":"Pediculus humanus corporis","A":"Demodex spp."},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Head lice live on the scalp.","D":"The pubic louse is found on pubic hair and eyelashes and makes no burrows.","C":"Correct. Burrows in skin folds, night-time itch and spread among close contacts is classical scabies.","E":"Body lice live in clothing seams.","A":"Demodex is a follicle mite that causes folliculitis and acne, not burrows between the fingers."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM28 Q47","LS54 Q11 (59)"]},{"id":"I|L8|28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q27","t":"Hymenoptera","s":"Which of the following is the most severe allergic reaction to a Hymenoptera sting?","lec":"L8","sl":44,"c":"<b>Hymenoptera</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q27</span>","o":{"A":"Anaphylaxis","C":"Swelling","B":"Rash","D":"Headache","E":"Itch"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the single medical importance the lecturer said to remember for bees, wasps and ants. Treatment is epinephrine plus antihistamine and systemic steroid.","C":"Part of the LOCAL reaction, along with redness, itching and pain.","B":"A local or cutaneous manifestation, not the most severe.","D":"Not a listed sting reaction at all.","E":"Part of the local reaction."},"img":"slides_i/L8_44.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"slide 67 / NL","t":"Bee sting","s":"A farmer presents with a red papule, swelling and intense pain on the right arm after being stung by an arthropod. A stinger was found in the middle of the swollen area. What is the most likely causative arthropod?","lec":"L8","sl":67,"c":"<b>Bee sting</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Slide 67 · NL worked question with the key shown</span>","o":{"E":"Centipede","D":"Wasp","C":"Spider","A":"Hornet","B":"Bee"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Centipedes bite with modified forelegs and leave no sting.","D":"A wasp's sting is smooth, stays attached and can be used repeatedly — it leaves nothing behind.","C":"Spiders bite with fangs; they have no sting.","A":"Hornets, like wasps, retain their sting.","B":"Correct, and the word that decides it is STINGER. The honeybee's sting is barbed; it tears away with part of the abdomen and stays in the skin — after which the bee dies. That is the reasoning the lecturer gave when the class objected."},"img":"slides_i/L8_67.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q13 (59)","t":"Rove beetle","s":"Which toxin does the rove beetle secrete?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q13 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"A":"Apitoxin","D":"Cantharidin","B":"Melittin","E":"Paederin","C":"Tetrodotoxin"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Apitoxin is bee venom.","D":"Cantharidin is the BLISTER beetle's toxin — the classic swap, and the reason both beetles are taught together.","B":"Melittin is a component of bee venom.","E":"Correct. Paederin is carried in the beetle's abdomen and is released when the insect is crushed.","C":"Tetrodotoxin belongs to pufferfish and some other animals, not to any arthropod here."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (60)","t":"Cockroach","s":"Which of the following best illustrates the role of a mechanical transmitter?","lec":"L8","sl":17,"c":"<b>Cockroach</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q6 · cohort 60</span>","o":{"A":"A sand fly in which Leishmania develops into promastigotes","E":"An Anopheles mosquito in which Plasmodium completes sexual reproduction","C":"A chigger mite in which Orientia multiplies before being inoculated","B":"A flea in which Yersinia pestis multiplies and blocks the proventriculus","D":"A cockroach walking through faeces and then across food, carrying organisms on its legs"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Stage development inside the fly is biological transmission.","E":"Development and multiplication inside the vector makes this biological.","C":"Multiplication inside the mite is biological transmission.","B":"Multiplication inside the flea is biological transmission.","D":"Correct. Nothing about the organism changes inside the cockroach — it is simply moved from one surface to another. Note that the cockroach can ALSO act biologically for some protozoa, which is why the house fly is the safest 'purely mechanical' example."},"img":"slides_i/L8_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q8","t":"Leishmania — mode of infection","s":"A 40-year-old man has a painless ulcer on his left arm with a raised border and a central crater. Skin biopsy shows numerous ovoid organisms with a KINETOPLAST inside macrophages. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Leishmania — mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q8 — painless ulcer with a raised border and central crater; kinetoplast-bearing organisms inside macrophages. Asks the MODE, where BM30 Q9 asked the diagnosis.</span>","o":{"E":"Ingestion of a sporulated oocyst","D":"Injection of sporozoites during a mosquito's blood meal","C":"Injection of third-stage larvae through the bite wound during a mosquito's blood meal","A":"Ingestion of a mature cyst","B":"Injection of promastigotes through the skin when an infected female sand fly takes a blood meal"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Sporulated oocysts are swallowed — Cryptosporidium, Toxoplasma.","D":"Sporozoites injected by a mosquito are malaria.","C":"Third-stage larvae injected by a mosquito are lymphatic filariasis.","A":"Mature cysts are swallowed — Entamoeba and Giardia.","B":"Correct. The amastigote (ovoid, with a kinetoplast, inside macrophages) is the stage you SEE; the PROMASTIGOTE is the stage that is INJECTED by the female Phlebotomus sand fly. The exam repeatedly makes you flip between the diagnostic stage and the infective stage of the same organism."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q7 (60)","t":"Sand fly","s":"You visit a country where a small mosquito-like insect is common: it is very HAIRY on body and wings, and holds its wings up in a V at rest. Which disease is this country likely to carry?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Sand fly</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q7 — a mosquito-like insect, very hairy, wings held up at an angle at rest; what disease risk does the country carry?</span>","o":{"A":"Scrub typhus","B":"Malaria","D":"Dengue","E":"Leishmaniasis","C":"Lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Scrub typhus is transmitted by the chigger MITE, an arachnid larva, not a fly.","B":"Malaria's vector is Anopheles, which rests with its body at 45° to the surface but is not described as hairy.","D":"Dengue's vector is Aedes, recognised by black-and-white banded legs.","E":"Correct. Hairy body and wings, 1.5–2.5 mm, V-shaped wings held erect at rest = sand fly, Phlebotomus spp., the vector of leishmaniasis. Only the female takes blood, and it is a POOL feeder with short mouthparts.","C":"Filariasis is carried by Culex, Mansonia, Aedes and Anopheles."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-flea","t":"Flea","s":"A flea is shown. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":51,"c":"<b>Flea</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — flea shown; state the medical importance. The examiner's feedback listed all three roles.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of scrub typhus","D":"Vector of Lyme disease","B":"Causative agent of Paederus dermatitis","C":"Intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana and H. diminuta","E":"Vector of lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Scrub typhus is the chigger mite.","D":"Lyme disease is the HARD TICK.","B":"Paederus dermatitis is the rove beetle (แมลงก้นกระดก).","C":"Correct, and the lecture gives the flea THREE roles: intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana, H. diminuta and Dipylidium caninum; VECTOR of plague and murine typhus (Xenopsylla cheopis) and cat-scratch disease (Ctenocephalides felis); and a cause of flea-allergy dermatitis. Any of the three earns the mark — but phrase it as a role, not a bare disease name.","E":"Filariasis is mosquitoes."},"img":"slides_i/L8_51.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (57)","t":"Chigger — prevention","s":"Soldiers training in scrub grassland are developing scrub typhus. What is the most appropriate prevention?","lec":"L8","sl":60,"c":"<b>Chigger — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q4 — a soldier catches scrub typhus; how is it prevented?</span>","o":{"B":"Eliminating standing water around the camp","D":"Sleeping under an insecticide-treated bed net","E":"Taking weekly doxycycline for life","C":"Wearing boots and long trousers treated with repellent, and clearing scrub vegetation","A":"Avoiding sexual contact with infected persons"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Removing standing water targets mosquito breeding, not mites.","D":"Bed nets work for night-biting MOSQUITOES. Chiggers are picked up from vegetation by day.","E":"Doxycycline is the TREATMENT, and chemoprophylaxis is not the lecture's prevention answer.","C":"Correct. The chigger is the six-legged LARVAL mite; it waits on grass tips and attaches to skin at points where clothing is tight. Barrier clothing plus repellent plus habitat clearance is the answer, which is why the option mentions boots.","A":"Scrub typhus is not sexually transmitted. Recall the lesion: an eschar like a cigarette burn."},"img":"slides_i/L8_60.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (57)","t":"Aedes — the three arboviruses","s":"Which mosquito transmits Chikungunya virus?","lec":"L8","sl":1018,"c":"<b>Aedes — the three arboviruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q6 — the Chikungunya vector</span>","o":{"E":"Aedes spp.","C":"Culex spp.","D":"Anopheles spp.","A":"Mansonia spp.","B":"Phlebotomus spp."},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Learn Aedes as a set of FOUR: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti). It breeds in clean stored household water and lays boat-shaped eggs with lateral floats.","C":"Culex carries Japanese B encephalitis and filariasis, and breeds in dirty water.","D":"Anopheles carries malaria and breeds in slow-moving clean stream water.","A":"Mansonia carries Brugia malayi and lays eggs in a sunflower-shaped cluster under water plants.","B":"Phlebotomus is the sand fly — leishmaniasis, not an arbovirus."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1018.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Class by antennae, legs, wings","s":"An arthropod removed from a patient has NO antennae, NO wings, FOUR pairs of legs as an adult, and a body in two parts (cephalothorax and abdomen). Which class is it, and what follows?","lec":"L8","sl":12,"c":"<b>Class by antennae, legs, wings</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Arachnida — which is why its larva has only 3 pairs of legs, as in the chigger mite","C":"Insecta — 4 pairs of legs is the adult insect count","A":"Crustacea — 2 pairs of antennae would have been lost during removal","D":"Chilopoda — one pair of legs per body segment","B":"Diplopoda — two pairs of legs per body segment"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both. The adult 4 / larva 3 leg-pair rule is exactly what makes the chigger LARVA the parasitic stage you must recognise.","C":"Insects have ONE pair of antennae, THREE pairs of legs, two pairs of wings and a three-part body.","A":"Crustacea have TWO pairs of antennae and five pairs of legs — and antennae are not something that simply falls off in the description.","D":"Chilopoda (centipedes) have one pair of antennae and many segments each bearing one pair of legs.","B":"Diplopoda (millipedes) have one pair of antennae and two pairs of legs per segment."},"img":"slides_i/L8_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Mechanical vector — the named diseases","s":"Which statement about mechanical transmission is correct?","lec":"L8","sl":16,"c":"<b>Mechanical vector — the named diseases</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The pathogen neither multiplies nor develops inside the arthropod; the lecture's examples are amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica), poliomyelitis and Shigella gastroenteritis carried by houseflies","B":"The pathogen multiplies inside the arthropod but does not change form; the example is plague in the oriental rat flea","C":"Mechanical transmission requires the arthropod to bite, which is why only blood-sucking flies qualify","E":"Mechanical transmission is the route for malaria, since Plasmodium is only carried and not changed by the mosquito","A":"Mechanical vectors are also intermediate hosts, because the pathogen depends on them"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The defining feature is that nothing happens to the pathogen inside the fly — it is carried from faeces to food and dropped there.","B":"Plague in the oriental rat flea is BIOLOGICAL transmission; Yersinia multiplies in the flea.","C":"Mechanical vectors are typically NON-biting flies. Biting is not required — contaminated feet and body parts are enough.","E":"Plasmodium undergoes its entire sexual cycle in the mosquito, which is the definition of biological transmission.","A":"An intermediate host is one the parasite REQUIRES for development. A mechanical vector is dispensable — that is the whole distinction."},"img":"slides_i/L8_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|39","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"40","t":"Paederin vs cantharidin","s":"A farmer brushed an insect off his arm and later developed blistering. Which statement correctly separates the two beetles in this lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Paederin vs cantharidin</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"The rove beetle (Paederus spp.) releases PAEDERIN and typically gives linear and 'kissing' lesions; the blister beetle (Meloidae) releases CANTHARIDIN, which can also cause keratitis and corneal ulceration if it reaches the eye and systemic toxicity with renal failure if swallowed","A":"The rove beetle releases cantharidin and the blister beetle releases paederin","E":"Both release paederin; only the dose differs","B":"Neither beetle bites or stings, so neither can cause dermatitis","D":"The rove beetle's toxin causes systemic renal failure and the blister beetle's is confined to the skin"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The two toxins have different names and different reach — cantharidin is the one with eye and systemic routes listed.","A":"The toxin names are swapped. Paederus → paederin; Meloidae → cantharidin.","E":"Two distinct toxins are named in the deck, and their effect profiles differ.","B":"Neither beetle bites or stings — but both release toxin on contact, which is exactly why the advice is to blow or brush them off rather than crush them.","D":"Inverted: it is CANTHARIDIN that causes haematemesis, haematuria, renal failure and shock when ingested."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|40","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"41","t":"Chigger mite — which stage bites","s":"A rice farmer has fever, headache, myalgia, enlarged lymph nodes and an eschar. Which statement about the responsible arthropod is correct, and how must the medical importance be written?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Chigger mite — which stage bites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Only the LARVAL stage of the trombiculid mite is parasitic (3 pairs of legs, dorsal scutum, 0.1–0.3 mm); the answer must be written as 'Vector of scrub typhus / vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi', not as the bare disease name","D":"The adult mite is the parasitic stage, and writing 'scrub typhus' alone is a complete answer","B":"Both nymph and adult feed on humans, and the larva is free-living","A":"The mite is an insect, so the larva has 4 pairs of legs and the adult 3","E":"The eschar is produced by the toxin of the mite, not by an infectious agent, so no pathogen need be named"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on both. The deck devotes a whole slide to marking ‘ตอบลอย ๆ scrub typhus ✗’ — a bare disease name scores nothing.","D":"Adults and nymphs are free-living; and the answer-format slide explicitly rejects the bare disease name.","B":"Exactly inverted — the larva is the only parasitic stage.","A":"Mites are ARACHNIDS, not insects: adults have 4 pairs of legs and the larva 3.","E":"The eschar marks the bite site where Orientia tsutsugamushi was inoculated; naming the pathogen is precisely what the answer format requires."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|41","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"42","t":"Hard vs soft tick","s":"Which statement about ticks matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Hard vs soft tick</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hard ticks (Ixodidae) have a scutum and transmit Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, tick-borne relapsing fever, tick-borne meningoencephalitis, SFTS virus and babesiosis; soft ticks (Argasidae) lack a scutum and are listed for tick-borne relapsing fever — and BOTH can cause tick bite paralysis by toxin","B":"Soft ticks have the scutum and carry all the listed diseases; hard ticks carry none","E":"Only hard ticks can cause tick bite paralysis, because only they attach for long periods","D":"Babesiosis is a viral disease of hard ticks","C":"Ticks are insects and their larvae have 4 pairs of legs"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, including the detail that BOTH families are credited with tick bite paralysis, which is toxin-mediated rather than infectious.","B":"The scutum is the HARD tick’s defining plate — that is what ‘hard’ refers to.","E":"The deck lists tick bite paralysis under both hard and soft ticks.","D":"Babesia is a PROTOZOAN, listed under ‘vector of protozoan diseases’ and described as malaria-like.","C":"Ticks are arachnids; the larva has 3 pairs of legs and the nymph and adult 4."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|42","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"43","t":"Sting apparatus and the reaction","s":"Which statement about Hymenoptera stings matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":42,"c":"<b>Sting apparatus and the reaction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The stinger is a modified OVIPOSITOR combined with a venom sac, so only females sting; a 'large local reaction' means swelling over 10 cm that enlarges for 24–48 hours and is dangerous on the head, neck, tongue or throat","E":"The stinger is a modified mouthpart, so both sexes sting; a large local reaction is any swelling over 2 cm","C":"Any swelling after a sting is by definition a systemic reaction requiring adrenaline","B":"Large local reactions resolve within 6 hours; anything longer is anaphylaxis","A":"Only wasps sting; bees bite and inject venom through the mandibles"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the ovipositor origin is the reason only females sting — a point worth carrying because it explains the anatomy rather than just naming it.","E":"The ovipositor is an egg-laying structure, not a mouthpart, and 2 cm is not the threshold.","C":"Local and systemic reactions are explicitly separated, and only the systemic one calls for adrenaline.","B":"A large local reaction ENLARGES over 24–48 hours — duration alone does not make it anaphylaxis.","A":"Both bees and wasps sting; the slide covers them together."},"img":"slides_i/L8_42.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|43","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"44","t":"Sting management","s":"Which management statement matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":47,"c":"<b>Sting management</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Remove the sting, use cold compression with an antihistamine and a steroid for a local reaction, and give epinephrine with an antihistamine and systemic steroid for anaphylaxis; a fire ant sting characteristically forms a STERILE pustule within 24 hours","A":"A fire ant pustule is infected and requires antibiotics within 24 hours","B":"Epinephrine is the first step for every sting, regardless of reaction type","C":"The sting should be left in place, since removing it squeezes more venom in","E":"Cold compression is used for anaphylaxis and epinephrine for local reactions"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct across all three limbs, and the word STERILE matters — the fire ant pustule is a venom effect, not an infection.","A":"Calling it infected leads to unnecessary antibiotics; the deck specifies a sterile pustule.","B":"Epinephrine is reserved for the systemic reaction; local reactions get cold compression, antihistamine and steroid.","C":"The first listed step is to REMOVE the sting.","E":"Inverted — the two treatments are matched to the wrong severities."},"img":"slides_i/L8_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|44","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"45","t":"Flea — two species, two roles","s":"Which statement about fleas matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":49,"c":"<b>Flea — two species, two roles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea, transmits plague and murine typhus; Ctenocephalides felis, the cat flea, transmits cat scratch disease; and fleas also serve as INTERMEDIATE HOSTS for Dipylidium caninum, Hymenolepis nana and H. diminuta","C":"Ctenocephalides felis transmits plague and Xenopsylla cheopis transmits cat scratch disease","B":"Fleas are vectors only; no helminth uses them as an intermediate host","A":"Fleas have two pairs of wings, which is how they move between hosts","D":"Pulex irritans is the oriental rat flea and the vector of plague"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and note the flea appears in TWO of the three categories of medical importance — vector and intermediate host — plus flea-allergy dermatitis.","C":"The two species are swapped.","B":"Fleas are named intermediate hosts for three cestodes on the same slide.","A":"Fleas have NO wings; they have modified hind legs for jumping.","D":"Pulex irritans is the HUMAN flea; Xenopsylla is the oriental rat flea."},"img":"slides_i/L8_49.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|45","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"46","t":"Myiasis — the three types","s":"Which classification of myiasis matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":26,"c":"<b>Myiasis — the three types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Cutaneous myiasis (furuncular or creeping/migratory), wound myiasis in open wounds, and cavitary myiasis of the nose, mouth, eye, intestine, ears, urogenital tract and rarely the brain — all caused by fly LARVAE (maggots)","C":"Myiasis is caused by adult flies feeding on tissue, and is classified as biting or non-biting","B":"Myiasis has only two forms, cutaneous and intestinal, and is caused by blood-sucking flies","A":"Creeping myiasis is caused by hookworm larvae in the skin","E":"Cavitary myiasis refers to maggots in a body cavity of a corpse only and has no clinical relevance"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the mechanism is what to hold: it is the LARVA, not the adult fly, that invades tissue.","C":"Adult flies have sponging mouthparts and absorb liquid food; they do not invade tissue.","B":"The deck gives three forms, and the responsible flies are the non-blood-sucking house, blow and flesh flies.","A":"Creeping eruption from larvae in skin is animal HOOKWORM (L1); creeping myiasis is a migratory maggot.","E":"Cavitary myiasis is listed with named living-patient sites including nose, mouth, eye, ears, intestine, urogenital tract and rarely brain."},"img":"slides_i/L8_26.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|46","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"47","t":"Scutellum plus palpi","s":"A mosquito has a ROUND scutellum, palpi as long as the proboscis, spotted wings, and rests at about 45° to the surface. Which genus, and what does it transmit?","lec":"L8","sl":1006,"c":"<b>Scutellum plus palpi</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Anopheles — vector of malaria and of lymphatic filariasis due to Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Culex — vector of Japanese encephalitis and Wuchereria bancrofti","B":"Aedes — vector of dengue, chikungunya and Zika","A":"Mansonia — vector of Brugia malayi","C":"Toxorhynchites — a non-biting mosquito that transmits nothing"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Round scutellum and long palpi both point to Anophelinae, and the 45° resting posture and spotted wings confirm it.","D":"Culex has a TRILOBED scutellum and SHORT palpi, and rests parallel to the surface.","B":"Aedes also has a trilobed scutellum and short palpi, plus snow-white scales and a pointed abdominal tip.","A":"Mansonia has a trilobed scutellum and broad, mixed dark-and-pale wing scales.","C":"Toxorhynchites is listed as a third subfamily but has none of these features and is not a medical vector."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1006.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|47","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"48","t":"Antennae tell you the sex","s":"A mosquito caught indoors has bushy, feathery (plumose) antennae. What follows?","lec":"L8","sl":1004,"c":"<b>Antennae tell you the sex</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"It is a MALE, and males feed on plant nectar rather than blood, so it cannot be the one transmitting disease","C":"It is a FEMALE, and plumose antennae help it locate hosts, so it is the disease-transmitting sex","B":"It is a male, but males also take blood meals, so it can still transmit disease","D":"Antennal type varies with genus, not sex, and plumose antennae identify Anopheles","A":"It is a female of the genus Toxorhynchites, the only genus with plumose antennae"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Plumose = male, pilose = female, and only the female takes a blood meal — so the antenna tells you at a glance whether the specimen matters epidemiologically.","C":"Pilose (sparsely haired) antennae mark the female.","B":"Males feed on nectar; the deck states this explicitly.","D":"Antennal type marks SEX, not genus — every genus has both.","A":"Plumose antennae occur in the males of all mosquito genera."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1004.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|48","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"49","t":"Breeding site → genus","s":"Which breeding-site pairing matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":1011,"c":"<b>Breeding site → genus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Anopheles: clean natural water — seepage, flowing streams, rock pools, animal footprints, rice fields and brackish water · Culex: dirty, low-oxygen water such as drains and water with animal droppings · Aedes: clean water in containers in and around houses · Mansonia: ponds and swamps covered with aquatic plants","C":"Anopheles: water jars inside houses · Aedes: flowing forest streams · Culex: plant-covered swamps · Mansonia: rice fields","E":"All four breed in the same clean container water; only their biting times differ","B":"Aedes breeds in dirty drain water, which is why dengue follows poor sanitation","D":"Mansonia breeds in brackish coastal water, which is why Brugian filariasis is coastal"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and each site explains its disease: Aedes’s household containers put dengue in towns, while Anopheles’s forest and border streams keep malaria rural.","C":"Every pairing is swapped.","E":"The four slides give four different habitats; biting time differs too (Aedes by day, Anopheles and Culex at night).","B":"Aedes breeds in CLEAN stored water — jars, vases, saucers — which is why dengue control is about containers, not drains.","D":"Mansonia’s sites are weed-covered ponds and swamps; brackish water is listed under Anopheles."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|49","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"50","t":"Larval control per genus","s":"Which control measure is matched correctly to its mosquito, and why?","lec":"L8","sl":1020,"c":"<b>Larval control per genus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Removing the aquatic weeds from a pond controls MANSONIA, because its larvae and pupae attach to the roots of water plants for air","A":"Removing aquatic weeds controls AEDES, because its larvae feed on the plants","D":"Adding temephos (abate) to natural forest streams is the main measure against Mansonia","E":"Releasing larvivorous fish is listed only for Anopheles, since its larvae live in open water","B":"Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis (Bti) is a chemical larvicide used against adult Aedes"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the mechanism is the point — Mansonia immatures obtain air from plant roots, so removing the plants removes the habitat.","A":"Aedes larvae live in stored water in containers, not among pond weeds.","D":"Temephos (abate) is listed under AEDES larval control, applied to stored water.","E":"Larvivorous fish appear under both Culex and Aedes control in the deck.","B":"Bti is a BACTERIAL larvicide used against LARVAE, not a chemical against adults."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1020.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|50","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"51","t":"Tsetse vs kissing bug","s":"Which morphological pairing is correct?","lec":"L8","sl":31,"c":"<b>Tsetse vs kissing bug</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Tsetse fly (Glossina): 6–15 mm, large head and eyes, a forward-projecting piercing proboscis, wings crossed over the abdomen at rest — vector of Trypanosoma brucei · Kissing bug (Triatoma): about 2.5 cm, cone-shaped head, wings overlapping to form a distinct 'X' — vector of Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"The tsetse fly has the cone-shaped head and the 'X' wings; the kissing bug has the forward-projecting proboscis","C":"Both transmit Trypanosoma cruzi; only their geography differs","E":"The kissing bug transmits sleeping sickness and the tsetse fly transmits Chagas disease","A":"Neither is a biological vector; both merely carry trypanosomes on their mouthparts"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on size, morphology and pathogen for both. The ‘X’ over the back is the fastest way to call the kissing bug.","B":"The two sets of features are swapped.","C":"Tsetse carries T. brucei (African sleeping sickness); the kissing bug carries T. cruzi (American Chagas disease).","E":"The diseases are swapped along with the vectors.","A":"Both are biological vectors — the trypanosomes develop inside them."},"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|51","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"52","t":"The mosquito vector grid","s":"Which mosquito–pathogen pairing is NOT supported by the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":1010,"c":"<b>The mosquito vector grid</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Mansonia spp. as a vector of dengue virus","D":"Aedes spp. as a vector of Zika virus","C":"Culex spp. as a vector of Japanese encephalitis virus","A":"Anopheles spp. as a vector of Plasmodium spp.","E":"Mansonia spp. as a vector of Brugia malayi"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct — this is the unsupported pairing. Mansonia’s slide lists only Brugia malayi; dengue belongs to Aedes.","D":"Supported: Aedes is listed for dengue, chikungunya, Zika and Wuchereria bancrofti.","C":"Supported: Culex is listed for Japanese B encephalitis and Wuchereria bancrofti.","A":"Supported: Anopheles is listed for malaria and Wuchereria bancrofti.","E":"Supported: this is Mansonia’s only listed pathogen."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1010.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Malaria regimens","s":"A 28-year-old returns from a forested border district with fever. A film shows P. falciparum. He is alert, has no organ dysfunction, and is keeping oral fluids down. Which regimen fits?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Chloroquine alone","C":"Artesunate by intravenous bolus over 1–2 minutes","B":"Artesunate + mefloquine, given orally","D":"Chloroquine + primaquine","A":"Dihydroartemisinin + mefloquine"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species (P. malariae, P. knowlesi).","C":"IV artesunate is reserved for SEVERE malaria. Nothing in the stem meets a severity criterion.","B":"Correct. No organ dysfunction and able to swallow means UNCOMPLICATED, and for P. falciparum the slide's first-line oral option is artesunate + mefloquine.","D":"Chloroquine + primaquine is the vivax/ovale regimen — primaquine is added to clear hypnozoites, which falciparum does not form.","A":"A fabricated pairing, and the most common distractor. Dihydroartemisinin partners PIPERAQUINE; mefloquine partners artesunate."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Severity criteria","s":"Which single finding is enough on its own to classify malaria as severe and move the patient to intravenous artesunate?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Severity criteria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"A temperature of 39.5 °C","A":"Persistent vomiting that prevents the patient taking tablets","B":"A palpable spleen","E":"A positive rapid diagnostic test","D":"Headache and myalgia"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Fever is universal in malaria and is not a severity criterion.","A":"Correct. 'Nausea, vomiting' and 'cannot take medication' are listed as severity criteria in their own right — because an oral drug that comes straight back up will fail, whatever the parasite is doing.","B":"Splenomegaly is an expected finding, not a complication.","E":"A positive test establishes the diagnosis, not the severity.","D":"Both are ordinary malaria symptoms."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Mechanisms","s":"Ivermectin and praziquantel both paralyse the parasite, but by opposite routes. Which pairing is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Mechanisms</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Both raise calcium permeability; ivermectin also causes vacuolization","B":"Ivermectin raises calcium permeability causing spastic paralysis; praziquantel opens chloride channels causing flaccid paralysis","C":"Both open chloride channels; only the target tissue differs","D":"Ivermectin blocks microtubules; praziquantel blocks fumarate reductase","E":"Ivermectin opens chloride channels causing flaccid paralysis; praziquantel raises calcium permeability causing spastic paralysis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Vacuolization is praziquantel's second mechanism, but ivermectin does not use calcium.","B":"Exactly reversed — and this reversal is the commonest way the mark is lost.","C":"Only ivermectin uses chloride.","D":"Microtubules and fumarate reductase are both ALBENDAZOLE's targets.","E":"Correct. Chloride entering a cell makes it LESS excitable, so the pharyngeal muscle goes limp; calcium is the universal contraction signal, so praziquantel produces sustained contraction."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Adverse effects","s":"Three weeks after being successfully treated with intravenous artesunate for severe malaria, a patient returns with anaemia, jaundice and a falling urine output. Films show no parasites. What is the most likely cause?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Adverse effects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Sulfadiazine crystalluria","E":"Relapse from dormant liver hypnozoites","C":"Blackwater fever from the original infection","A":"Chloroquine-induced retinopathy","B":"Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Sulfadiazine was not given; crystalluria also causes stones and haematuria rather than haemolysis.","E":"Hypnozoites belong to P. vivax and P. ovale, not to the falciparum infection artesunate was given for — and a relapse would show parasites.","C":"Blackwater fever is intravascular haemolysis during acute falciparum infection, not three weeks after clearance.","A":"Chloroquine was not given, and retinopathy needs long-term use and affects vision, not the kidney.","B":"Correct. The slide dates it at 'about 3 weeks after treatment' and names the consequence: the haemolytic load causes ACUTE KIDNEY FAILURE. The lecturer singled this out as the one artesunate fact to keep."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Drug interactions","s":"A patient with neurocysticercosis is treated with praziquantel and, as expected, also receives dexamethasone and phenytoin. Three weeks later the cysts are unchanged. What most likely happened?","lec":"L9","sl":9,"c":"<b>Drug interactions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Dexamethasone and phenytoin induce CYP3A4 and lowered the praziquantel concentration","E":"Praziquantel does not cross into the central nervous system","C":"The corticosteroid suppressed the immune killing the drug depends on","A":"Phenytoin displaced praziquantel from plasma albumin","B":"Praziquantel needs a high-fat meal that was withheld"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and clinically awkward: the two co-drugs you are obliged to give — a steroid for the inflammation and an antiepileptic for the seizures — are both on the slide's CYP3A4 inducer list, along with rifampin and carbamazepine.","E":"The opposite is true. Praziquantel is 'widely distributed and enters the brain', which is precisely why it is usable here.","C":"The drug kills by raising intracellular calcium; it does not depend on the host immune response.","A":"Displacement would raise free drug, not lower the effect, and is not the mechanism described.","B":"Fatty-meal absorption is albendazole's and chloroquine's footnote, not praziquantel's."},"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Ivermectin","s":"Which single pharmacokinetic property of ivermectin explains BOTH its very low toxicity and its uselessness against a helminth in the central nervous system?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Ivermectin</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Excretion in the faeces","A":"Minimal blood–brain barrier crossing","C":"Its 100-fold selectivity for the parasite GABA receptor","D":"Good absorption from the gut","B":"Pregnancy category C"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"The excretion route does not determine CNS penetration.","A":"Correct — one line, two consequences. Staying out of the brain spares human GABA receptors (few adverse effects) and also means the drug never reaches a CNS parasite.","C":"Real, and it contributes to safety, but it says nothing about why CNS parasites are not reached.","D":"Absorption determines how much gets into the blood, not whether it enters the brain.","B":"A pregnancy category is a safety classification, not a mechanism."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Ivermectin","s":"A patient with strongyloidiasis develops fever, headache, dizziness and hypotension within hours of the first ivermectin dose. What would have prevented this?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Ivermectin</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"An antihistamine","B":"A lower ivermectin dose","E":"Corticosteroid premedication","D":"Leucovorin","A":"Giving the drug with a high-fat meal"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Antihistamine is the wrong axis — this is not a type I hypersensitivity to the drug.","B":"Wrong logic. Reducing the dose does not stop an immune reaction to parasite antigen; it only leaves more worms alive.","E":"Correct. This is the Mazzotti-like reaction: the HOST'S IMMUNE RESPONSE to dying microfilariae, not drug toxicity — so it is suppressed with a corticosteroid, which is exactly what the slide lists.","D":"Leucovorin rescues pyrimethamine's marrow toxicity and has no role here.","A":"Food affects absorption, not the immune reaction."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Albendazole","s":"A patient on prolonged albendazole develops hair loss and a falling white cell count. Which single property of the drug accounts for both?","lec":"L9","sl":6,"c":"<b>Albendazole</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"It inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules, and therefore the mitotic spindle","E":"It is hepatotoxic","A":"It antagonises folate","C":"It generates free radicals in host membranes","B":"It is eliminated by renal excretion"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Microtubules build the spindle, so the tissues that divide fastest take the damage: hair follicles (alopecia), bone marrow (leukopenia) and the fetus (impaired development). One mechanism, three effects.","E":"Hepatotoxicity is a separate listed effect and explains neither finding.","A":"Folate antagonism is pyrimethamine's and sulfadiazine's mechanism.","C":"Free-radical damage is the artesunate/chloroquine mechanism, and it acts on the parasite.","B":"The excretion route causes neither effect."},"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Toxoplasmosis","s":"Leucovorin is added to pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine. Why does it rescue the patient without also rescuing Toxoplasma?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Leucovorin is given at a dose too low to affect the parasite","D":"Leucovorin is actively excluded from the parasite by an efflux pump","C":"Leucovorin only reaches the bone marrow and not the brain","A":"Leucovorin inactivates sulfadiazine but not pyrimethamine","B":"Humans can absorb ready-made folinic acid, whereas the parasite must synthesise folate itself"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"A subtherapeutic dose would not prevent the pancytopenia either.","D":"No efflux mechanism is described; the difference is uptake capability, not export.","C":"Distribution is not the explanation, and cerebral toxoplasmosis is treated with this regimen anyway.","A":"Leucovorin does not inactivate either drug.","B":"Correct. Supplying the finished product bypasses the blocked human pathway; the parasite has no uptake route for it and stays blocked. Note also that leucovorin and folinic acid are the SAME substance — the lecturer warned that NL alternates between the two words."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Pregnancy & G6PD","s":"A pregnant woman in her third trimester needs treatment. Which statement about the drugs in this lecture is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":13,"c":"<b>Pregnancy & G6PD</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Chloroquine and pyrimethamine are stated to be safe; sulfadiazine risks kernicterus late in pregnancy","B":"Artesunate and chloroquine are both safe; pyrimethamine is contraindicated","E":"Albendazole is safe because it is a prodrug activated only in the worm","A":"Ivermectin is category A and may be used without restriction","C":"All antiparasitic drugs are contraindicated in pregnancy"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Those are the only two drugs the slides mark as safe, and sulfadiazine displaces bilirubin from albumin, which risks bilirubin encephalopathy in the newborn.","B":"Artesunate causes FETAL RESORPTION; pyrimethamine is the safe one.","E":"Albendazole inhibits mitosis and impairs fetal development — it is not selectively activated in the worm.","A":"Ivermectin is category C, and should not be used during breastfeeding.","C":"Too absolute — chloroquine and pyrimethamine are explicitly safe."},"img":"slides_i/L9_13.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Mechanisms","s":"Which pair of drugs in this lecture share an identical mechanism of action?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Mechanisms</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pyrimethamine and chloroquine","B":"Ivermectin and praziquantel","D":"Albendazole and praziquantel","E":"Artesunate and chloroquine","C":"Sulfadiazine and ivermectin"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Pyrimethamine is an antifolate; chloroquine works on heme.","B":"Opposite ions — chloride versus calcium.","D":"Albendazole starves the worm of ATP; praziquantel floods it with calcium.","E":"Correct. Slides 12 and 13 print the same sentence for both: inhibits heme polymerization and hemozoin → toxic free heme → free radical → destabilizes membrane.","C":"Two unrelated mechanisms, in two different parasites."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Artesunate","s":"Why is artesunate always combined with a partner drug rather than used alone?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Artesunate</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"It cannot be given by mouth","C":"It is a prodrug and needs a second drug to activate it","D":"It is inactive against P. falciparum on its own","B":"The partner drug prevents delayed haemolysis","A":"Its half-life is only about 45 minutes, so alone it would be cleared before the infection is"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Oral artesunate is exactly what uncomplicated malaria receives.","C":"Artesunate is converted to dihydroartemisinin by the body, not by the partner drug.","D":"It is highly active against falciparum — that is why it is first-line.","B":"Nothing prevents the delayed haemolysis; it is a recognised late adverse effect.","A":"Correct. The slide prints t½ 20–45 min for the prodrug and 45 min for dihydroartemisinin, then the instruction '+ Drug partner (Long acting)'. This is the whole design principle of artemisinin-based combination therapy: a fast killer plus a slow mop-up."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Sulfadiazine","s":"How is sulfadiazine crystalluria prevented?","lec":"L9","sl":16,"c":"<b>Sulfadiazine</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Fluid restriction to reduce urine volume","A":"Acidification of the urine","B":"Vigorous hydration and alkalinisation of the urine to pH above 7.15","D":"Adding leucovorin","C":"Giving the drug with a high-fat meal"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Concentrating the urine makes precipitation worse.","A":"The exact opposite, and the planted error — acid urine makes crystals MORE likely.","B":"Correct, and printed on the slide. Sulfonamides are poorly soluble in acidic, concentrated urine, so you dilute it and raise the pH.","D":"Leucovorin addresses pyrimethamine's marrow toxicity, not solubility.","C":"Food affects absorption of albendazole and chloroquine, not sulfadiazine solubility."},"img":"slides_i/L9_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Hypnozoites","s":"Why is primaquine added to chloroquine for P. vivax and P. ovale but not for P. malariae?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Hypnozoites</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Only vivax and ovale are chloroquine-resistant","C":"Only vivax and ovale form dormant liver hypnozoites, and only primaquine clears them","D":"Only vivax and ovale cause severe anaemia","B":"P. malariae is treated with artesunate instead","E":"Primaquine is contraindicated in P. malariae infection"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Resistance is a separate issue, and resistance would upgrade the case to complicated malaria.","C":"Correct. Chloroquine cures the blood stage and the illness; without primaquine the sleeping liver forms wake weeks later and the patient relapses.","D":"Severe anaemia is more characteristic of falciparum.","B":"P. malariae is treated with chloroquine.","E":"There is no such contraindication; it is simply unnecessary."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Strongyloidiasis","s":"A patient has a serpiginous urticarial rash, intermittent wheeze and eosinophilia. Strongyloidiasis is confirmed. Which drug is first choice, and why is the alternative second?","lec":"L9","sl":3,"c":"<b>Strongyloidiasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole, because ivermectin cannot be given orally","C":"Ivermectin, because it is more effective than albendazole for this infection","B":"Praziquantel, because the parasite is a nematode","E":"Albendazole, because ivermectin does not reach the gut","D":"Ivermectin, because albendazole is contraindicated in eosinophilia"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Ivermectin is well absorbed from the gut and is an oral drug.","C":"Correct. The slide prints ivermectin first and larger, and the lecturer said plainly that ivermectin is the more effective of the two here.","B":"Praziquantel is for cestodes and trematodes, not nematodes.","E":"Ivermectin is excreted in the faeces and acts in the gut perfectly well.","D":"No such contraindication exists."},"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Cysticercosis","s":"A previously well 30-year-old has a first seizure. He is afebrile. Imaging shows multiple cystic brain lesions. Which drug is first choice?","lec":"L9","sl":7,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Praziquantel","A":"Albendazole","E":"Ivermectin","D":"Artesunate","B":"Pyrimethamine"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Praziquantel works and does enter the brain, but the slide marks it second.","A":"Correct. Seizures WITHOUT fever plus cystic brain lesions is neurocysticercosis, and the slide prints 'Albendazole > Praziquantel'.","E":"Ivermectin has minimal blood–brain barrier crossing and is useless for a CNS parasite.","D":"Artesunate is an antimalarial.","B":"Pyrimethamine treats toxoplasmosis — the other HIV-associated brain lesion, which is the intended near-miss."},"img":"slides_i/L9_7.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Route","s":"How is artesunate administered in severe malaria?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Route</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Continuous intravenous infusion over 24 hours","D":"Intravenous bolus over 1–2 minutes, not as a continuous infusion","B":"Intramuscular depot injection weekly","E":"Oral loading dose followed by continuous infusion","A":"Rectal suppository only"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Explicitly excluded on the slide.","D":"Correct, and both halves are printed on the slides — 'bolus injection, 1-2 min' and, in red, 'Do not continuous infusion'.","B":"No depot preparation is described.","E":"Severe malaria means oral therapy is unreliable, which is why the IV route was chosen.","A":"Not the route taught here."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Antifolates","s":"Pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine are given together. Which enzymes do they block, and what is the design called?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Antifolates</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Both block hemozoin formation — additive toxicity","E":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHPS and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR — sequential blockade","D":"Both block DHFR at different sites — competitive synergy","B":"Pyrimethamine blocks thymidylate synthase and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR","C":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHFR and sulfadiazine blocks DHPS — sequential blockade of one pathway"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Hemozoin is the antimalarial mechanism.","E":"The enzymes are the right pair but assigned to the wrong drugs.","D":"They block different enzymes, not the same one.","B":"Thymidylate synthase is not the target described in this lecture.","C":"Correct. Sulfadiazine competes with PABA at dihydropteroate synthase, the earlier step; pyrimethamine blocks dihydrofolate reductase, the later one. Hitting one road twice is far harder to escape — the same design as co-trimoxazole."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Malaria regimens","s":"A 40-year-old man was diagnosed with uncomplicated malaria from Plasmodium falciparum. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · Infectious summative 1 · Lect 7 Antiparasitic drugs</span>","o":{"A":"Chloroquine","B":"Artesunate","C":"Primaquine","E":"Artesunate + Mefloquine","D":"Dihydroartemisinin + Mefloquine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species.","B":"Artesunate alone has a 45-minute half-life and always needs a long-acting partner.","C":"Primaquine is added to chloroquine for vivax and ovale, to clear hypnozoites; it is never used alone for falciparum.","E":"Correct. The slide's first-listed option for uncomplicated falciparum malaria.","D":"The classic fabricated pairing. Dihydroartemisinin partners PIPERAQUINE; mefloquine partners artesunate. Reading the two names quickly is exactly how this distractor works."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q30 (57)","t":"G6PD","s":"Which drug most commonly causes haemolysis in a patient with G6PD deficiency?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>G6PD</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q30 · cohorts 57 and 58</span>","o":{"B":"Artesunate","A":"Chloroquine","C":"Tetracycline","D":"Primaquine","E":"Mefloquine"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Artesunate causes DELAYED haemolysis at about three weeks in anyone, which is a different phenomenon from G6PD-triggered haemolysis.","A":"Chloroquine is the drug marked SAFE IN PREGNANCY and carries no G6PD warning here.","C":"Not an antimalarial in this lecture at all — included to test whether you are reading the list.","D":"Correct, and set in two cohorts with the same five options. The slide carries the warning 'Precaution in G-6-PD' next to primaquine.","E":"Mefloquine is the partner drug; no G6PD warning is given."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q30 (58)"]},{"id":"I|L9|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Toxoplasmosis","s":"A 50-year-old man with HIV presents with limb weakness and seizures. Blood tests are positive for Toxoplasma. He is already receiving pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine. Which drug will most help reduce his symptoms?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · Infectious summative 1 · Lect 7 Antiparasitic drugs</span>","o":{"B":"Co-trimoxazole","D":"Leucovorin","C":"Carbamazepine","A":"Dexamethasone","E":"Mannitol"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"An alternative anti-Toxoplasma regimen — he is already on effective therapy, and swapping it does not relieve today's symptoms.","D":"The reflex answer whenever pyrimethamine appears, and the reason this question works. Leucovorin treats the DRUG'S marrow toxicity, and the stem mentions no cytopenia.","C":"An antiepileptic addresses the seizure but does nothing for the limb weakness.","A":"Correct. Read what is asked: his SYMPTOMS. Both the limb weakness and the seizures come from oedema around the cerebral lesion, and a corticosteroid reduces that — covering both at once.","E":"Osmotic therapy is for acute raised intracranial pressure, not the listed treatment for this presentation."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"first-line set","t":"Malaria regimens","s":"Which statement about first-line malaria therapy matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 · first-line therapy items</span>","o":{"E":"Malariae and knowlesi require primaquine to prevent relapse","A":"All species are treated with chloroquine plus primaquine","D":"Complicated malaria is treated with oral artesunate plus mefloquine","C":"Vivax and ovale are treated with artesunate plus lumefantrine","B":"Complicated falciparum malaria is treated with intravenous artesunate; vivax and ovale with chloroquine plus primaquine; malariae and knowlesi with chloroquine alone"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Neither species forms hypnozoites, so there is nothing for primaquine to clear.","A":"Primaquine is only for the two hypnozoite-forming species, and falciparum needs an artemisinin combination.","D":"Complicated malaria means the oral route is unreliable — it is treated intravenously.","C":"Artesunate/lumefantrine is a falciparum option.","B":"Correct. This is the answer key's own three-line summary, and it matches slides 10–11 exactly."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q16 (58)","t":"Opportunistic infection","s":"Which of the following is NOT typically defined as an AIDS-associated opportunistic infection?","lec":"L9","sl":3,"c":"<b>Opportunistic infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q16 · cohort 58</span>","o":{"C":"Intestinal microsporidiosis","A":"Cryptosporidiosis","B":"Toxoplasmic encephalitis","E":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis","D":"Cyclosporiasis"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"A recognised opportunistic intestinal infection in advanced HIV.","A":"A recognised AIDS-defining diarrhoeal infection.","B":"The classic AIDS-associated brain lesion — and the one this lecture treats with pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine.","E":"Correct. The answer key's note is the reason: disseminated strongyloidiasis is characteristically precipitated by CORTICOSTEROID therapy rather than by HIV, which is why it sits outside the AIDS-defining list.","D":"Also seen in advanced HIV."},"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q20f (59)","t":"Co-trimoxazole","s":"Co-trimoxazole is listed as an alternative regimen for toxoplasmosis. What is it, and why does it work against a protozoan?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Co-trimoxazole</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 321-2 Q20f · cohorts 59 and 60</span>","o":{"B":"A macrolide combination with intrinsic antiprotozoal activity","A":"Trimethoprim plus sulfadiazine — it blocks heme polymerization","C":"Trimethoprim plus sulfamethoxazole — they block DHFR and DHPS, the same sequential folate blockade as pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine","D":"A sulfonamide alone, which blocks DHFR","E":"A folate supplement that starves the parasite by feedback inhibition"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Neither component is a macrolide.","A":"Heme polymerization is the antimalarial mechanism, and the second drug is sulfamethoxazole.","C":"Correct. The lecturer pointed out that the same two-enzyme attack is why an antibacterial combination is effective against Toxoplasma, and said it would be revisited in the antibacterial topic.","D":"Co-trimoxazole is two drugs, and the sulfonamide half blocks DHPS, not DHFR.","E":"It is not a supplement; that describes leucovorin, which does the opposite."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q20f (60)"]},{"id":"I|L9|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q16 (59)","t":"Vivax / ovale radical cure","s":"A traveller returns with P. vivax malaria. Which regimen does the lecture give, and why is the second drug needed?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Vivax / ovale radical cure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 59 Q16 & 57 written Q7 — the per-species malaria regimens</span>","o":{"C":"Chloroquine alone — vivax has no dormant liver stage","A":"Chloroquine plus primaquine — chloroquine clears the blood stage, primaquine kills the liver hypnozoite and prevents relapse","D":"Artesunate plus mefloquine — vivax is treated exactly like falciparum","B":"Primaquine alone — it covers both the blood and the liver stage","E":"Intravenous artesunate — all vivax infections are classed as complicated"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Chloroquine has no activity against hypnozoites, so chloroquine alone gives relapse weeks to months later.","A":"Correct, and it is the same for P. OVALE. These are the only two species with hypnozoites, so they are the only two needing a radical cure. Check G6PD first — primaquine causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, which the lecture flags as a precaution.","D":"Artesunate + mefloquine is the uncomplicated FALCIPARUM regimen. P. malariae and P. knowlesi take chloroquine alone.","B":"Primaquine is a weak blood schizonticide; it is a partner drug, never a solo cure.","E":"IV artesunate is reserved for SEVERE/complicated malaria — organ dysfunction, pregnancy, or inability to take oral drugs."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 written Q7 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L9|25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Praziquantel","s":"What is the mechanism by which praziquantel kills flukes and tapeworms?","lec":"L9","sl":9,"c":"<b>Praziquantel</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Lecture-flagged — the lecture devotes a slide to praziquantel's mechanism and to the CYP3A4 interaction that matters when it is combined with dexamethasone in neurocysticercosis</span>","o":{"E":"It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase in the parasite","B":"It blocks microtubule polymerisation and glucose uptake in the worm","D":"It opens glutamate-gated chloride channels, causing flaccid paralysis","C":"It increases calcium permeability of the tegument, causing spastic paralysis and vacuolisation","A":"It generates free radicals after activation by haem iron"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"DHFR inhibition is pyrimethamine (with sulfadiazine, DHPS) for toxoplasmosis.","B":"Microtubules and glucose uptake are ALBENDAZOLE and mebendazole.","D":"Glutamate-gated chloride channels and FLACCID paralysis are IVERMECTIN — the exact opposite paralysis, which is the planted contrast.","C":"Correct. Calcium floods in, the worm contracts hard (SPASTIC paralysis), the tegument vacuolates and the parasite is dislodged. Praziquantel crosses into the brain, which is why it is used in neurocysticercosis.","A":"Haem-activated free radicals are the ARTEMISININS. Watch the interaction the slide flags: dexamethasone, phenytoin, rifampicin and carbamazepine induce CYP3A4 and LOWER praziquantel levels."},"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Artesunate — how to give it","s":"A patient with severe falciparum malaria is prescribed intravenous artesunate. Which statement about administration and pharmacology is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Artesunate — how to give it</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is given as a BOLUS over 1–2 minutes and must NOT be given by continuous infusion; it is a prodrug converted to dihydroartemisinin, whose half-life is only about 45 minutes, which is why a long-acting partner drug is added","E":"It is given by continuous infusion over 24 hours to maintain steady levels, because its half-life is short","C":"It is the active drug itself, and dihydroartemisinin is an inactive metabolite excreted in bile","D":"It is given orally in severe malaria, since absorption is excellent","B":"No partner drug is needed, because artesunate's long half-life covers the whole erythrocytic cycle"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct on route, timing, prodrug status and the reason for combination therapy — four separate facts the slides set out together.","E":"The deck explicitly says 'Do not continuous infusion'. The short half-life is precisely why a PARTNER drug, not an infusion, solves the problem.","C":"Inverted: artesunate is the prodrug and dihydroartemisinin is the ACTIVE metabolite.","D":"Severe malaria is defined partly by being unable to take oral medication, which is why the intravenous route is chosen.","B":"Artesunate's half-life is short, not long — hence mefloquine, lumefantrine or piperaquine as the partner."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Delayed haemolysis","s":"Three weeks after successful artesunate treatment, a patient returns with new anaemia and rising creatinine. Blood films show no parasites. What is the most likely explanation?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Delayed haemolysis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis — a recognised effect appearing about three weeks after treatment that can progress to acute kidney failure","E":"Relapse from hypnozoites, which requires primaquine","D":"Recrudescence from artesunate resistance, requiring a repeat course","A":"Chloroquine-induced retinopathy with secondary renal injury","B":"Sulfadiazine crystalluria"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the negative film is the key — the haemolysis is a drug effect on previously parasitised red cells, not ongoing infection.","E":"Relapse would show parasites on the film, and hypnozoites belong to P. vivax and P. ovale.","D":"Recrudescence also means parasites return to the blood; the film here is negative.","A":"Chloroquine's long-term ocular effect is blurred vision, and it is not implicated in this timeline.","B":"Crystalluria is a SULFADIAZINE effect from the toxoplasmosis regimen, not part of malaria treatment."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"The antifolate pair","s":"A patient with cerebral toxoplasmosis is started on pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine plus leucovorin. Which explanation of this three-drug regimen is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>The antifolate pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Sulfadiazine blocks DHPS and pyrimethamine blocks DHFR, two sequential steps of the same folate pathway; leucovorin is preformed folinic acid that rescues human marrow, which the parasite cannot use","C":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHPS and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR; leucovorin increases pyrimethamine absorption","E":"Leucovorin is given to prevent sulfadiazine crystalluria","B":"All three drugs act on the same enzyme, so the combination simply increases potency","A":"Leucovorin is an antiparasitic in its own right and is what actually clears the tissue cysts"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the rescue logic is the examinable part — supplying downstream folinic acid protects the host without protecting the parasite.","C":"The two enzymes are swapped. Sulfonamides are the DHPS agents; pyrimethamine is the DHFR inhibitor.","E":"Crystalluria is managed by vigorous hydration and urine alkalinisation to pH above 7.15, not by leucovorin.","B":"Two different enzymes in sequence is exactly the point — that is what makes the blockade sequential rather than merely additive.","A":"Leucovorin has no antiparasitic action; it is purely a host-protective supplement."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Praziquantel and its CYP3A4 problem","s":"A patient with neurocysticercosis is on dexamethasone and phenytoin. Which statement about praziquantel in this setting is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":8,"c":"<b>Praziquantel and its CYP3A4 problem</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Both drugs are CYP3A4 INDUCERS and increase praziquantel's metabolism, lowering its concentration — which is one reason albendazole is preferred over praziquantel for neurocysticercosis","C":"Both are CYP3A4 inhibitors and raise praziquantel levels, risking toxicity","A":"Neither interacts with praziquantel; the interaction listed is with albendazole","E":"Dexamethasone should be stopped, because corticosteroids have no role in neurocysticercosis","B":"Phenytoin should be stopped, because antiepileptics are contraindicated with praziquantel"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the clinical irony is worth holding: the very drugs you must co-prescribe for the inflammation and the seizures undercut praziquantel.","C":"Induction, not inhibition — the slide names them under CYP3A4 inducers and shows metabolism increasing.","A":"The interaction box sits on the praziquantel slide specifically.","E":"Corticosteroids are explicitly part of the regimen, with dexamethasone 0.1 mg/kg/day intravenously.","B":"Antiepileptics are also explicitly part of the regimen — the patient is seizing."},"img":"slides_i/L9_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Ivermectin — why it is safe in us","s":"Which statement explains ivermectin's safety margin and its contraindications?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Ivermectin — why it is safe in us</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"It opens GABA-gated chloride channels for which it has about 100 times more affinity than the human receptor, and it barely crosses the blood–brain barrier — which is why it is avoided in meningitis, where the barrier is disrupted, and why it synergises with GABA-A agonists","E":"It is safe because it is not absorbed from the gut at all and acts only in the lumen","B":"It is safe because humans have no GABA receptors; the contraindication in meningitis is unrelated to the BBB","D":"It crosses the blood–brain barrier freely, which is why it is effective in cerebral parasitic disease","A":"It is pregnancy category A and is the preferred agent in breastfeeding women"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the meningitis contraindication only makes sense once you link it to the barrier — an inflamed meninges lets the drug reach human GABA receptors.","E":"The deck states it is WELL ABSORBED from the gut; it is then excreted in the faeces.","B":"Humans certainly have GABA receptors — the 100-fold selectivity, not their absence, is the safety margin.","D":"Minimal BBB crossing is stated explicitly, and it is a safety feature, not an efficacy one.","A":"It is pregnancy category C and should NOT be used during breastfeeding."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Albendazole — absorption and harms","s":"Which statement about albendazole matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":6,"c":"<b>Albendazole — absorption and harms</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"It is a prodrug with low water solubility and poor oral bioavailability whose absorption improves when taken with a HIGH-FAT meal; it is eliminated renally, and prolonged use can cause reversible leukopenia and alopecia as well as hepatotoxicity","A":"It should be taken on an empty stomach, since food reduces its absorption","B":"It is eliminated entirely by biliary excretion, so it is contraindicated in renal failure","E":"Its mechanism is to increase calcium permeability, causing spastic paralysis of the worm","C":"It is safe in pregnancy, because it acts only on parasite microtubules"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct on formulation, the fat-meal point, the route of elimination and all three adverse effects.","A":"The opposite — a HIGH-FAT meal is what rescues its poor bioavailability, which matters when you need systemic levels for tissue cysts.","B":"The deck states RENAL excretion.","E":"Calcium influx with spastic paralysis is PRAZIQUANTEL. Albendazole immobilises the worm by disrupting microtubules and starving it of ATP.","C":"It inhibits mitosis and impairs fetal development — the opposite of safe."},"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Two mechanisms, two paralyses","s":"Which pairing of drug with mechanism is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Two mechanisms, two paralyses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Ivermectin opens GABA-gated CHLORIDE channels causing paralysis of pharyngeal muscles · Praziquantel increases CALCIUM permeability causing spastic paralysis and vacuolisation · Albendazole inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules and impairs glucose uptake and fumarate reductase","B":"Ivermectin increases calcium permeability · Praziquantel opens chloride channels · Albendazole inhibits heme polymerisation","A":"All three act by inhibiting heme polymerisation and generating toxic free heme","C":"Albendazole increases calcium permeability and praziquantel blocks glucose uptake","E":"Ivermectin inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, which is why leucovorin is co-administered"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The two ion mechanisms are worth contrasting directly: chloride in → the worm goes limp; calcium in → the worm clamps down and is dislodged.","B":"The two ions are swapped, and heme polymerisation is the ANTIMALARIAL mechanism.","A":"Heme polymerisation inhibition belongs to artesunate and chloroquine, not to the anthelmintics.","C":"Both halves are swapped between praziquantel and albendazole.","E":"DHFR inhibition is PYRIMETHAMINE, which is why leucovorin accompanies it — not ivermectin."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Uncomplicated malaria by species","s":"Which statement about treating UNCOMPLICATED malaria matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Uncomplicated malaria by species</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"P. falciparum is treated with an artemisinin combination such as artesunate + mefloquine, dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine or artesunate/lumefantrine; the other species get chloroquine, with primaquine added for P. vivax and P. ovale under G6PD precaution; and drug resistance is managed as complicated malaria with intravenous artesunate","D":"All species are treated with chloroquine alone; artemisinins are reserved for severe disease","A":"Primaquine is added for P. falciparum to eradicate its hypnozoites","E":"Mefloquine is given alone as monotherapy for falciparum malaria","C":"Resistant uncomplicated malaria is managed with a longer oral course of the same combination"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct across species, partner drugs, radical cure and the resistance rule.","D":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species; falciparum needs an artemisinin combination.","A":"P. falciparum forms no hypnozoites. Primaquine is added for P. vivax and P. ovale.","E":"Mefloquine appears only as the PARTNER to artesunate, never alone.","C":"The deck states that drug resistance is treated as COMPLICATED malaria with intravenous artesunate."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"What makes malaria severe","s":"Which patient with a positive blood film should be treated as SEVERE malaria according to the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>What makes malaria severe</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"A pregnant woman with falciparum parasitaemia and no organ dysfunction","C":"A man with a parasitaemia of 0.1% who is afebrile and eating normally","E":"A child whose film shows P. malariae band forms and who has mild headache","D":"A traveller with P. vivax and a normal full blood count who tolerates tablets","A":"A man whose film is negative but whose rapid test is positive, with no symptoms"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Pregnancy is listed on the severe-malaria slide in its own right, alongside organ dysfunction and inability to take oral medication — no complication is required.","C":"Parasites without organ dysfunction, pregnancy or vomiting is uncomplicated malaria.","E":"P. malariae with a mild headache and no organ dysfunction is uncomplicated.","D":"A normal blood count and tolerated tablets fails every severity criterion.","A":"The slide requires malaria parasites DETECTED IN BLOOD as the starting point, and there is no organ dysfunction here."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Pregnancy and breastfeeding grid","s":"Which statement about these drugs in pregnancy and lactation matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Pregnancy and breastfeeding grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Chloroquine is stated to be safe in pregnancy and pyrimethamine safe in pregnancy and breastfeeding, whereas sulfadiazine risks kernicterus in late pregnancy and should not be used while breastfeeding, ivermectin is category C and not for breastfeeding, and albendazole impairs fetal development","D":"All five drugs are contraindicated in pregnancy without exception","A":"Sulfadiazine is the safest of the group in late pregnancy because it is an old, well-studied drug","C":"Albendazole is safe in pregnancy and chloroquine is contraindicated","B":"Ivermectin is category A and pyrimethamine is contraindicated in breastfeeding"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and the contrast within the toxoplasmosis regimen itself is the sharp edge: pyrimethamine is safe in pregnancy while its partner sulfadiazine is not, late on.","D":"Chloroquine and pyrimethamine are both stated to be safe in pregnancy.","A":"Sulfadiazine carries the kernicterus warning in late pregnancy — the opposite of safest.","C":"Both halves inverted.","B":"Ivermectin is category C, and pyrimethamine is stated safe in breastfeeding."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"Sulfadiazine — the two manoeuvres","s":"Which precaution belongs specifically to sulfadiazine?","lec":"L9","sl":16,"c":"<b>Sulfadiazine — the two manoeuvres</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Vigorous hydration with urine alkalinisation to a pH above 7.15, to prevent crystalluria — plus caution in G6PD deficiency because of haemolytic anaemia","C":"Taking the dose with a high-fat meal to improve absorption","E":"Giving it as a bolus over 1–2 minutes and never by infusion","A":"Co-administering leucovorin to prevent its own bone marrow suppression","D":"Avoiding it in meningitis because of blood–brain barrier disruption"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Both manoeuvres sit on the sulfadiazine slide, and the pH figure — above 7.15 — is the kind of number a stem will quote back at you.","C":"The high-fat meal point belongs to albendazole and chloroquine.","E":"Bolus over 1–2 minutes is intravenous ARTESUNATE.","A":"Leucovorin is paired with PYRIMETHAMINE’s marrow suppression, not sulfadiazine’s.","D":"The meningitis caution belongs to IVERMECTIN."},"img":"slides_i/L9_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Chloroquine pharmacokinetics","s":"Which statement about chloroquine matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":13,"c":"<b>Chloroquine pharmacokinetics</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Its half-life is 20–60 days, it is 50–60% protein bound, absorption improves with a high-fat meal, it is excreted in urine, and long-term use can blur vision","D":"Its half-life is 20–45 minutes, matching artesunate's","C":"It is excreted unchanged in the faeces and is unaffected by food","E":"Its characteristic long-term adverse effect is alopecia with reversible leukopenia","A":"It acts by inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct on all five points. The very long half-life is what allows weekly dosing and also why toxicity accumulates with chronic use.","D":"20–45 minutes is ARTESUNATE’s half-life. Chloroquine’s is measured in DAYS — a thousand-fold difference and an easy trap when the numbers look similar on the page.","C":"It is excreted in the URINE, and a high-fat meal improves absorption.","E":"Alopecia with reversible leukopenia is ALBENDAZOLE’s prolonged-use profile.","A":"Chloroquine inhibits heme polymerisation; DHFR inhibition is pyrimethamine."},"img":"slides_i/L9_13.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Strongyloidiasis — the clinical picture","s":"Which statement about strongyloidiasis from the pharmacology lecture is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":3,"c":"<b>Strongyloidiasis — the clinical picture</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It may be asymptomatic, or cause malabsorption, pneumonia and ARDS, and a serpiginous urticarial or linear rash; ivermectin and albendazole are the treatments, and corticosteroids are flagged as a hazard","B":"It presents with bloody dysentery and rectal prolapse, and is treated with praziquantel","E":"Corticosteroids are part of the treatment, as they are in neurocysticercosis","D":"It is treated with chloroquine plus primaquine","C":"The rash is a fixed maculopapular eruption that does not migrate"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the corticosteroid flag matters — it is the same hyperinfection hazard L1 warns about, arriving here from the pharmacology side.","B":"Bloody dysentery with rectal prolapse is TRICHURIS (L1), and praziquantel is for flukes and tapeworms.","E":"Corticosteroids belong to NEUROCYSTICERCOSIS treatment; in strongyloidiasis they are the danger.","D":"Chloroquine and primaquine are antimalarials.","C":"The rash is described as serpiginous and linear — it migrates, which is what larva currens means."},"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L9|39","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"40","t":"Pyrimethamine — tolerability","s":"A patient on pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis has abdominal pain and vomiting, and the full blood count shows a falling haemoglobin, white cell and platelet count. What do the lecture's two listed manoeuvres address?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Pyrimethamine — tolerability</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Take the drug WITH a meal for the GI intolerance, and give leucovorin (folinic acid) for the reversible pancytopenia","A":"Take the drug on an empty stomach for the GI intolerance, and stop pyrimethamine permanently for the cytopenia","D":"Give leucovorin for the GI intolerance and alkalinise the urine for the cytopenia","E":"Give a corticosteroid for both effects","C":"Neither effect is described for pyrimethamine; both belong to sulfadiazine"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Each adverse effect on the slide is printed next to its own countermeasure — meal for the gut, leucovorin for the marrow.","A":"The slide says to take it WITH a meal, and the pancytopenia is reversible and managed with leucovorin rather than by abandoning the drug.","D":"The two countermeasures are swapped, and urine alkalinisation belongs to sulfadiazine.","E":"Corticosteroids play no part in managing either effect.","C":"Both are listed explicitly under pyrimethamine; sulfadiazine has its own separate profile."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Naked vs enveloped","s":"Why does a NAKED virus survive the gastrointestinal tract while an enveloped virus usually does not?","lec":"L10","sl":10,"c":"<b>Naked vs enveloped</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"The protein capsid resists acid, bile detergents and digestive enzymes, whereas a lipid envelope is dissolved by them","A":"Naked viruses carry enzymes that neutralise stomach acid","D":"Naked viruses replicate faster than the gut can clear them","E":"Enveloped viruses cannot bind receptors on gut epithelium","C":"Naked viruses have a double-stranded genome, which is chemically more stable"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and this one fact drives the whole transmission table. The envelope is a LIPID bilayer taken from the host membrane, so acid, bile salts, detergents and drying destroy it; a protein capsid does not care. That is why faecal–oral gastroenteritis viruses are naked and respiratory/blood-borne viruses can afford an envelope.","A":"No virus carries acid-neutralising enzymes.","D":"Replication speed is irrelevant — the particle is destroyed before it reaches a cell.","E":"Receptor binding is not the limiting step; particle survival is.","C":"Strandedness confers no environmental stability. The practical corollary: enveloped viruses are killed by 70% alcohol gel, naked ones much less so."},"img":"slides_i/L10_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Where each genome replicates","s":"Which statement about the SITE of viral genome replication is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":28,"c":"<b>Where each genome replicates</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"DNA viruses replicate in the nucleus EXCEPT poxvirus; RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm EXCEPT influenza and the retroviruses","A":"All DNA viruses replicate in the nucleus and all RNA viruses in the cytoplasm, with no exceptions","B":"All viruses replicate in the nucleus because they need host polymerases","D":"DNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm; RNA viruses replicate in the nucleus","C":"The site depends on whether the virus is enveloped"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The summary slide states the rule and its exceptions in exactly this form. Poxvirus is big enough to carry its own DNA polymerase, so it stays in the cytoplasm; influenza steals host nuclear capped primers; retroviruses must integrate, which requires the nucleus.","A":"It is the EXCEPTIONS the examiner asks about — a rule with no exceptions is the giveaway that the option is wrong.","B":"Most RNA viruses never enter the nucleus; they bring their own RdRp.","D":"Exactly reversed.","C":"The envelope has nothing to do with replication site."},"img":"slides_i/L10_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Polarity of the genome","s":"A virus has a (+) single-stranded RNA genome. What follows directly from that?","lec":"L10","sl":14,"c":"<b>Polarity of the genome</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The genome acts as mRNA and can be translated immediately, so naked genome alone is infectious","C":"The virion must carry its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase in the particle","E":"The genome must be reverse transcribed before any protein is made","D":"The genome must enter the nucleus to be transcribed by host RNA polymerase II","B":"The genome is translated only after it is converted to double-stranded DNA"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. (+)ssRNA reads like mRNA, so the ribosome translates it the moment it is uncoated — which also means the purified genome on its own is infectious. Picornaviridae and Coronaviridae work this way.","C":"That is (−)ssRNA. A negative-sense genome cannot be read, so the virion MUST package RdRp — influenza, rabies, measles.","E":"Reverse transcription is Baltimore VI (retroviruses) and VII (HBV) only.","D":"Nuclear transcription by host Pol II is the DNA-virus route.","B":"Again retroviruses only."},"img":"slides_i/L10_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"The DNA virus list","s":"Which set contains ONLY DNA viruses?","lec":"L10","sl":16,"c":"<b>The DNA virus list</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Herpesviridae, Hepadnaviridae, Adenoviridae, Poxviridae, Papillomaviridae, Polyomaviridae, Parvoviridae","B":"Herpesviridae, Orthomyxoviridae, Adenoviridae, Retroviridae","E":"Poxviridae, Reoviridae, Picornaviridae, Parvoviridae","D":"Adenoviridae, Flaviviridae, Togaviridae, Papillomaviridae","C":"Hepadnaviridae, Coronaviridae, Rhabdoviridae, Polyomaviridae"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — and the lecture gives you the mnemonic on the slide: HHAPPPPy (Herpes, Hepadna, Adeno, Pox, Papilloma, Polyoma, Parvo). Everything NOT on that list is an RNA virus.","B":"Orthomyxo (influenza) and Retro are RNA.","E":"Reo and Picorna are RNA.","D":"Flavi and Toga are RNA — these are the arboviruses of L14.","C":"Corona and Rhabdo are RNA. Learn the rule that goes with the list: all RNA viruses are ssRNA EXCEPT Reoviridae, which is dsRNA."},"img":"slides_i/L10_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Tropism","s":"What determines the TROPISM of a virus — the tissue it can infect?","lec":"L10","sl":23,"c":"<b>Tropism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Matching between the viral attachment protein and a receptor on the host cell, plus host proteases and transcription factors that permit the cycle to finish","E":"The nucleic acid type of the virus — DNA viruses infect epithelium, RNA viruses infect nerve","A":"Whether the virus is enveloped or naked","B":"The size of the virion relative to the capillary pore size of the organ","D":"The route by which the patient acquired the virus"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Tropism is decided first at ATTACHMENT — no receptor, no entry — and then by whether the cell supplies the proteases, transcription factors and other machinery the virus needs. Both halves are on the slide, and past papers have tested both.","E":"Genome type says nothing about which tissue is infected. This exact wrong option has been offered in a real paper.","A":"Naked and enveloped viruses are found in every tissue.","B":"Particle size does not select tissue.","D":"Route determines where infection STARTS, not which cells are permissive."},"img":"slides_i/L10_23.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Mutation vocabulary","s":"Match the change to its name: a surface protein alters gradually so that existing antibody no longer inhibits the virus.","lec":"L10","sl":39,"c":"<b>Mutation vocabulary</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Antigenic variation (antigenic drift)","C":"Reassortment (antigenic shift)","A":"Attenuating mutation","E":"Conditional lethal mutation","B":"Complementation"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. DRIFT is gradual point mutation of a surface protein — immune evasion, and the reason the influenza vaccine is reformulated yearly. RNA viruses drift faster than DNA viruses because RdRp has no proofreading.","C":"SHIFT is the sudden swap of whole genome SEGMENTS between two viruses infecting one cell — that is reassortment, and it causes pandemics.","A":"An attenuating mutation makes the virus cause LESS serious disease.","E":"A conditional lethal mutant fails to grow only under a restrictive condition, e.g. temperature.","B":"Complementation is one virus supplying a gene product for another — not a mutation at all."},"img":"slides_i/L10_39.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Interactions between viruses","s":"Two different viruses infect the same cell. Progeny particles are produced that carry the GENOME of virus A inside the CAPSID of virus B, but their offspring revert to normal virus A. What is this?","lec":"L10","sl":41,"c":"<b>Interactions between viruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Phenotypic mixing","C":"Recombination","E":"Reassortment","D":"Complementation","A":"Antigenic drift"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the give-away is that the change is NOT heritable — the genome itself was never altered, only the coat it happened to be packaged in. One generation later it is gone.","C":"Recombination is genuine intramolecular exchange of nucleic acid, so it IS heritable.","E":"Reassortment swaps whole segments and is also heritable — and it needs a SEGMENTED genome.","D":"Complementation is a helper virus supplying a missing gene product to a defective virus; again nothing is inherited, but the defect is functional, not structural.","A":"Drift is point mutation of surface protein."},"img":"slides_i/L10_41.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Subviral agents","s":"Which statement about atypical and subviral agents is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":36,"c":"<b>Subviral agents</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"A defective virus has nucleic acid and protein but needs a HELPER virus to replicate — hepatitis D virus needs hepatitis B","D":"A viroid is a small protein with no nucleic acid","A":"A prion is a naked circular RNA molecule","B":"A defective virus replicates normally but cannot assemble a capsid","E":"Bacteriophages are a subviral agent because they lack a genome"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and HDV is the example worth remembering clinically: it cannot make its own envelope, so it borrows HBsAg — which is why HBV vaccination also prevents hepatitis D.","D":"A viroid is naked circular RNA with NO protein; the protein-only agent is the prion.","A":"Reversed — prion = protein, viroid = RNA.","B":"The defect is in replication itself, not assembly alone.","E":"Bacteriophages are complete viruses that infect bacteria, and they are the most abundant members of the human virome."},"img":"slides_i/L10_36.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Eclipse and window","s":"What is the ECLIPSE phase of a viral growth cycle?","lec":"L10","sl":22,"c":"<b>Eclipse and window</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"The interval after entry during which no infectious virion can be recovered from the cell, because the genome has been uncoated and progeny are not yet assembled","B":"The interval between infection and the first detectable antibody","C":"The period during which the patient is infectious but has no symptoms","A":"The time taken for the virus to travel from the portal of entry to its target organ","E":"The phase in which the viral genome is integrated and silent"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct — 'eclipse' because if you lyse the cell during it you find NOTHING infectious; the genome exists but is naked and unassembled.","B":"That is the WINDOW PERIOD, which is a serology concept and the reason early HIV antibody tests miss acute infection. The lecture puts these two side by side precisely because students conflate them.","C":"That describes asymptomatic shedding.","A":"That is dissemination.","E":"That is latency."},"img":"slides_i/L10_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Virome","s":"Which statement about the human VIROME is correct?","lec":"L10","sl":47,"c":"<b>Virome</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"It is the whole collection of viruses in and on the body, and bacteriophages of the order Caudovirales are its most abundant members","D":"It consists only of eukaryotic viruses actively causing disease","C":"It is identical at every body site and stable from birth to old age","A":"It has no interaction with the immune system","B":"It is composed mainly of DNA viruses that have integrated into human chromosomes"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Most of the virome is bacteriophage, not human virus, and Caudovirales dominate — which is exactly the fact a recent past paper turned into a clinical stem about IBD.","D":"The virome includes phages, plant viruses from food and quiescent human viruses.","C":"It differs sharply by body site and changes with age.","A":"It helps equilibrate innate and adaptive responses and activates antiviral IFNs and IL-22.","B":"Endogenous retroviral elements exist but are not the bulk of the virome."},"img":"slides_i/L10_47.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q42","t":"Enteric virus characteristics","s":"An outbreak of fever, nausea and watery diarrhoea occurs in a school. Stool examination shows a (+)ssRNA virus. Which property does this virus have?","lec":"L10","sl":11,"c":"<b>Enteric virus characteristics</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q43 · BM32 SUM1 Q42 · BM30 Formative virology Q2 · BM28 Q18 — the gastroenteritis-outbreak stem, asked in four papers</span>","o":{"A":"It is NAKED, so it resists gastric acid and digestive enzymes, and it replicates in the cytoplasm","B":"It is rapidly destroyed under ordinary environmental conditions","D":"It uses the host cell's DNA polymerase for genome replication","E":"It carries its own DNA polymerase","C":"It is readily inactivated by any detergent"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is two deductions in one. GI symptoms ⇒ the particle survived the gut ⇒ NAKED. And an RNA genome ⇒ replication in the CYTOPLASM using RdRp, not the nucleus.","B":"The opposite: naked viruses are environmentally stable, which is why they spread so efficiently in schools, cruise ships and nursing homes.","D":"An RNA virus cannot use a DNA polymerase at all — the BM30 answer key writes this out explicitly as the reason.","E":"Same error: no DNA polymerase is involved.","C":"Detergents dissolve LIPID envelopes. A naked virus has no envelope to dissolve, which is why alcohol gel is comparatively poor against it."},"img":"slides_i/L10_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q43","BM30 virology Q2","BM28 Q18"]},{"id":"I|L10|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q45","t":"Reassortment and pandemics","s":"A novel ENVELOPED virus with a NEGATIVE-sense SEGMENTED RNA genome appears, and within months is declared a pandemic. Which genetic change is responsible?","lec":"L10","sl":42,"c":"<b>Reassortment and pandemics</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q45 · BM29 SUM1 Q44 · BM28 Q5 — 'which genetic change leads to pandemics?', asked in three cohorts with the same five options</span>","o":{"B":"Reassortment","C":"Recombination","A":"Complementation","D":"Antigenic variation","E":"Conditional lethal mutation"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The word SEGMENTED is the whole question: only viruses with segmented genomes — influenza and the reoviruses — can reassort, swapping entire segments when two strains infect one cell. That is antigenic SHIFT, and shift is what makes a pandemic because no one has pre-existing immunity.","C":"Recombination is intramolecular exchange and does not require segments; it drives gradual change, not a pandemic.","A":"Complementation is a helper virus supplying a gene product — nothing heritable happens.","D":"Antigenic variation is DRIFT: gradual, causes seasonal epidemics, not pandemics. This is the planted near-miss, because drift and shift are both 'antigenic change'.","E":"Conditional lethal mutants are laboratory tools."},"img":"slides_i/L10_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q44","BM28 Q5"]},{"id":"I|L10|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q41","t":"Viral attachment protein","s":"Which part of an enveloped virus is both the part that mediates ENTRY into the cell and the rational target for a vaccine?","lec":"L10","sl":23,"c":"<b>Viral attachment protein</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q44 (answer recorded as Viral Attachment Protein) · BM32 SUM1 Q41</span>","o":{"D":"The spike — the viral attachment protein on the envelope","B":"The genome","C":"The capsid","E":"The matrix protein beneath the envelope","A":"The viral polymerase carried in the particle"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The viral attachment protein (VAP), seen as the spike, binds the host receptor — so it determines tropism AND it is the surface the antibody must block. Neutralising antibody is by definition antibody against the VAP.","B":"The genome is inside and never seen by antibody.","C":"In an ENVELOPED virus the capsid is internal, so antibody against it does not neutralise. In a naked virus the capsid IS the outer surface and does carry the attachment site — that distinction is what the examiner is checking.","E":"Matrix protein is internal and structural.","A":"The packaged polymerase is internal."},"img":"slides_i/L10_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q44"]},{"id":"I|L10|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q44","t":"Negative-sense RNA virus","s":"A nasopharyngeal swab yields an ENVELOPED virus with a (−)ssRNA genome. Which property does it have?","lec":"L10","sl":29,"c":"<b>Negative-sense RNA virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q44 — nasopharyngeal swab grows an enveloped (−)ssRNA virus; which property follows?</span>","o":{"A":"The virion contains its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","B":"Its genome can be translated directly into viral protein","D":"Reverse transcriptase inhibitors block its replication","E":"Genome replication occurs in both the nucleus and the cytoplasm","C":"Its genome integrates into the host chromosome"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is forced by the polarity. A (−) strand cannot be read by a ribosome, and the cell has no enzyme that copies RNA into RNA, so the virus MUST bring RdRp in the particle. Its naked genome is therefore NOT infectious.","B":"Direct translation is the (+)ssRNA property — the exact opposite.","D":"Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses (Baltimore VI) and HBV (VII).","E":"Influenza is the odd (−)ssRNA virus that uses the nucleus, but 'both nucleus and cytoplasm' is not the general property being asked for.","C":"Integration is a retrovirus property."},"img":"slides_i/L10_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q89","t":"Retrovirus — integration","s":"A 27-year-old man has flu-like symptoms. Testing shows a viral genome INTEGRATED into the chromosomes of his lymphocytes. Which description fits this virus?","lec":"L10","sl":30,"c":"<b>Retrovirus — integration</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q89 — flu-like illness; viral genome found INTEGRATED into the chromosome of lymphocytes</span>","o":{"E":"An RNA virus that carries reverse transcriptase","C":"An RNA virus whose genome replicates in the cytoplasm without a DNA intermediate","A":"A DNA virus whose genome replicates in the host nucleus","B":"An RNA virus that carries RdRp","D":"A DNA virus whose genome encodes RdRp"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Integration into a host chromosome requires a DNA copy, and an RNA virus can only make one with REVERSE TRANSCRIPTASE. RNA genome + lymphocytes + integration = a retrovirus (HIV or HTLV-1).","C":"No DNA intermediate means no integration — that is the ordinary (+)ssRNA strategy.","A":"A DNA virus in the nucleus is plausible-sounding, but the stem's point is the RNA-to-DNA step; and the classic integrating lymphotropic viruses here are retroviruses.","B":"RdRp copies RNA to RNA and can never produce the DNA needed to integrate.","D":"No DNA virus encodes RdRp."},"img":"slides_i/L10_30.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q43","t":"Uncoating blocked","s":"Drug X has antiviral activity because it competes with the CAPSID for binding to the viral genome. What is the consequence for the replication cycle?","lec":"L10","sl":25,"c":"<b>Uncoating blocked</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q43 — a drug competes with the capsid for the viral genome; what accumulates?</span>","o":{"E":"Accumulation of viral genome inside the cell, because progeny cannot be packaged","D":"Increased cellular attachment","B":"Increased viral release","A":"Accumulation of viral non-structural protein outside the cell","C":"Increased production of viral capsid protein"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Capsid binding to genome IS the assembly step. Block it and everything upstream still works — entry, transcription, genome replication — so copies pile up INTRACELLULARLY with no way to leave.","D":"Attachment happens before this block and is unaffected.","B":"Release falls to near zero, not rises.","A":"Non-structural proteins act inside the cell; they are not exported.","C":"Capsid synthesis is unchanged — the drug stops the capsid from doing its job, not from being made."},"img":"slides_i/L10_25.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q90","t":"Attenuating mutation","s":"The Omicron variant of SARS-CoV-2 was reported to cause milder disease than earlier variants. Which mechanism reduced its virulence?","lec":"L10","sl":40,"c":"<b>Attenuating mutation</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q90 — why the Omicron variant caused milder disease</span>","o":{"D":"Attenuating mutation","A":"Phenotypic mixing","C":"Antigenic variation","B":"Conditional lethal mutation","E":"Drug-resistance mutation"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the definition is on the slide almost word for word: an attenuating mutation produces variants that cause LESS SERIOUS disease. The same principle underlies live attenuated vaccines.","A":"Phenotypic mixing changes the coat for a single generation and is not heritable, so it cannot define a variant.","C":"Antigenic variation is immune ESCAPE — Omicron did that too, but escape explains transmissibility, not reduced severity. This is the planted near-miss.","B":"Conditional lethal mutants only fail under restrictive laboratory conditions.","E":"Drug resistance says nothing about virulence."},"img":"slides_i/L10_40.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q48","t":"Virome and IBD","s":"A 43-year-old man is diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease. What change in the gut VIROME would be expected?","lec":"L10","sl":53,"c":"<b>Virome and IBD</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q48 — a 43-year-old diagnosed with IBD; what happens to the gut virome?</span>","o":{"C":"Expansion of Caudovirales with a fall in Microviridae, and reduced overall diversity","A":"Expansion of Microviridae with a fall in Caudovirales","D":"Complete loss of all bacteriophages","B":"A rise in eukaryotic plant viruses only","E":"No change — the virome is unrelated to bowel disease"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The lecture's dysbiosis slide states it directly: expansion of Caudovirales drives TLR-9-dependent activation of CD4+ T cells and prolonged inflammation in IBD, alongside a fall in diversity.","A":"Reversed. Microviridae expansion is not the IBD signature.","D":"Phages are expanded, not lost — this is a shift in composition, not an elimination.","B":"Plant viruses come from diet and are not the driver here.","E":"The whole reason virome is in this lecture is that its composition tracks with disease — ulcerative colitis, IBD and C. difficile infection are the examples given."},"img":"slides_i/L10_53.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q1","t":"What defines a virus","s":"Which description most likely identifies a VIRUS?","lec":"L10","sl":6,"c":"<b>What defines a virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q1 (examiner-written) — which pathogen is most likely to be the virus?</span>","o":{"A":"Depends entirely on a host cell for replication","E":"Can be observed under a light microscope","C":"Can be cultured on blood agar","D":"Has both DNA and RNA as its genome","B":"Contains ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi complex"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — obligate intracellular parasitism is THE defining property. A virus has no ribosomes and no energy-generating machinery, so it must borrow the cell's.","E":"At 20–300 nm viruses are below the resolution of light microscopy; you need electron microscopy (Mimivirus is the curiosity exception).","C":"Viruses cannot grow on cell-free media — they need cell culture, embryonated eggs or animals.","D":"A virus contains RNA or DNA, never both.","B":"Organelles are the mark of a cell, which is exactly what a virus is not."},"img":"slides_i/L10_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q4","t":"Family–genome matching","s":"Which pairing of virus family and characteristic is CORRECT?","lec":"L10","sl":16,"c":"<b>Family–genome matching</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q4 — which correlation between virus family and characteristic is correct?</span>","o":{"C":"Reoviridae — dsRNA genome","D":"Parvoviridae — ssRNA genome","B":"Papillomaviridae — segmented genome","A":"Poxviridae — replication in the nucleus","E":"Retroviridae — replication entirely in the cytoplasm"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and it is the single exception worth memorising: every RNA virus is ssRNA EXCEPT Reoviridae, which is dsRNA and segmented (rotavirus lives here).","D":"Parvoviridae is the ssDNA family — it is on the HHAPPPPy DNA list.","B":"Papillomavirus has a circular dsDNA genome in one piece; segmented genomes belong to influenza and the reoviruses.","A":"Poxvirus is the famous DNA-virus exception that replicates in the CYTOPLASM, because it carries its own polymerase.","E":"Retroviruses must reach the nucleus to integrate."},"img":"slides_i/L10_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q46","t":"Virus interaction — naming it","s":"Two influenza viruses infect one cell and produce a hybrid strain carrying gene segments from both parents. What is this interaction called?","lec":"L10","sl":43,"c":"<b>Virus interaction — naming it</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q46 — name the interaction occurring between the two viruses in Q45</span>","o":{"B":"Reassortment","C":"Phenotypic mixing","D":"Complementation","A":"Attenuation","E":"Antigenic shift caused by point mutation"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Segments from both parents packaged into one heritable hybrid is reassortment — the mechanism of antigenic shift.","C":"Phenotypic mixing swaps the COAT only, and the change disappears in the next generation.","D":"Complementation is a helper supplying a missing product to a defective virus; no genetic material changes hands.","A":"Attenuation is loss of virulence.","E":"Antigenic shift is real, but it is caused by reassortment, NOT by point mutation — point mutation is drift. The option is self-contradictory, which is how you spot it."},"img":"slides_i/L10_43.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L11|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Pathogenicity vs virulence","s":"Rabies virus is described as HIGH pathogenicity and HIGH virulence; hepatitis A virus as LOW and LOW; measles as HIGH pathogenicity but LOW virulence. What do the two words mean?","lec":"L11","sl":7,"c":"<b>Pathogenicity vs virulence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Pathogenicity is the ability to cause disease at all; virulence is the SEVERITY of the disease once caused","C":"Pathogenicity is severity; virulence is the ability to cause disease at all","E":"Pathogenicity refers to the agent, virulence refers to the host's immune status","D":"They are synonyms, and the slide uses both only for emphasis","A":"Pathogenicity is measured in the laboratory; virulence is measured in the field"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the table is built to force the distinction. Measles infects almost everyone it reaches (high pathogenicity) but usually does not kill them (low virulence). Rabies is high on both — it infects and it kills.","C":"Exactly reversed, and this is the option that catches people.","E":"Both terms describe the AGENT. Host factors are handled separately on the host slide.","D":"They are deliberately contrasted, not synonymous.","A":"Neither is defined by where it is measured."},"img":"slides_i/L11_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Incidence vs prevalence","s":"Which pair of definitions is correct?","lec":"L11","sl":11,"c":"<b>Incidence vs prevalence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Incidence = rate of NEW cases in a period; prevalence = TOTAL existing cases at a point","E":"Incidence = total existing cases; prevalence = rate of new cases","A":"Incidence = cases in an epidemic; prevalence = cases in an endemic state","D":"Incidence = deaths from the disease; prevalence = cases of the disease","C":"Incidence = cases per hospital; prevalence = cases per population"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. New cases flow IN — incidence. Prevalence is the standing pool, and it grows when either incidence rises or people survive longer with the disease.","E":"Reversed — the single most common error in this topic.","A":"Endemic, epidemic and pandemic describe the LEVEL of occurrence, a separate axis entirely.","D":"Deaths are mortality rate; illness is morbidity rate.","C":"Neither term is defined by the denominator being a hospital."},"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"The five steps","s":"What are the five steps of viral pathogenesis, in the order the lecture repeats them?","lec":"L11","sl":18,"c":"<b>The five steps</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Viral entry and replication → viral spread → cell injury → recovery → virus shedding","A":"Exposure → adhesion → colonisation → invasion → infection","B":"Attachment → penetration → uncoating → replication → release","C":"Incubation → prodrome → onset → acute phase → convalescence","E":"Entry → latency → reactivation → transformation → shedding"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The lecturer puts this five-box diagram back on screen four separate times, greying out the boxes already covered — that repetition is the clearest signal in the deck about what is examinable.","A":"That is the steps of BACTERIAL pathogenesis; it has appeared as a distractor in a real paper.","B":"Those are the steps of viral REPLICATION inside one cell (L10) — a different scale entirely.","C":"Those are the clinical STAGES OF ILLNESS, which the lecture also covers but on a separate slide.","E":"Latency and transformation are possible OUTCOMES, not steps every infection passes through."},"img":"slides_i/L11_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"The three cellular outcomes","s":"The lecture divides cell injury into three types of viral pathogenesis at the cellular level. Which set is it?","lec":"L11","sl":32,"c":"<b>The three cellular outcomes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Cell death · transformation · cell fusion (syncytia formation)","A":"Cell death · apoptosis · necrosis","E":"Lysis · budding · latency","C":"Inflammation · fibrosis · scarring","D":"Attachment · penetration · uncoating"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Three outcomes, three different diseases: death gives lytic illness, transformation gives cancer (L13), and fusion gives the multinucleated giant cell you see on a Tzanck smear or in measles.","A":"Apoptosis and necrosis are sub-types of cell death, so this list collapses to one item.","E":"Budding is a release mechanism, not an injury.","C":"These are tissue-level consequences, not the cellular classification the lecture uses.","D":"These are replication steps from L10."},"img":"slides_i/L11_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Inclusion bodies","s":"Where would you expect to find inclusion bodies, and why?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Inclusion bodies</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Intranuclear for DNA viruses and intracytoplasmic for RNA viruses, because inclusions mark the site of transcription and genome replication","C":"Always intranuclear, whatever the genome","A":"Always intracytoplasmic, whatever the genome","B":"Intranuclear for RNA viruses and intracytoplasmic for DNA viruses","D":"Only in enveloped viruses, at the site of budding"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is a derivable rule rather than a memorised list: an inclusion body marks where transcription and genome replication happen, and that is the nucleus for DNA viruses and the cytoplasm for RNA viruses.","C":"CMV's owl's eye is intranuclear, but rabies' Negri body is not.","A":"The reverse error.","B":"Exactly inverted.","D":"Inclusions have nothing to do with the envelope. Remember the exception that proves the rule: poxvirus is a DNA virus that replicates — and forms its inclusions — in the cytoplasm."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Viral spread","s":"A virus enters through the respiratory tract but causes disease in the brain. Which term describes this, and what is the usual route?","lec":"L11","sl":30,"c":"<b>Viral spread</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Systemic infection, most often by haematogenous spread — viraemia carrying virus from the portal of entry to a distant target organ","B":"Localised infection, spreading by direct cell-to-cell contact only","E":"Latent infection, because the virus must integrate before moving","C":"Abortive infection, because the virus fails to complete its cycle at the entry site","A":"Transformation, because the virus alters the growth of neurons"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. A SYSTEMIC infection is one that spreads beyond the site of entry, and the commonest vehicle is the blood — primary viraemia seeds an organ, replication there produces a larger secondary viraemia, and the target organ is reached.","B":"A localised infection replicates only at the entry site — the common cold, for example.","E":"Latency requires no movement at all.","C":"An abortive infection produces no progeny, so nothing spreads anywhere.","A":"Transformation is an oncogenic outcome, not a mode of spread. The other route worth knowing is NEURAL spread along axons — how rabies and HSV reach the CNS."},"img":"slides_i/L11_30.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Immune evasion menu","s":"Which of the following is an immune-evasion strategy the lecture attributes to viruses?","lec":"L11","sl":52,"c":"<b>Immune evasion menu</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Producing VIROKINES — viral proteins that mimic host cytokines or their receptors and jam normal immune signalling","E":"Producing interferons to protect the infected cell","D":"Increasing MHC class I expression so infected cells are recognised faster","B":"Destroying plasma cells directly in the bone marrow","A":"Inducing apoptosis of the infected cell to release progeny sooner"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Virokines and viral cytokine-receptor mimics are on the evasion slide, and this exact idea reappeared as a poxvirus clinical stem in the most recent paper.","E":"Interferon is the HOST's antiviral defence; viruses work to BLOCK its synthesis and signalling, not to produce it.","D":"Viruses DOWN-regulate MHC I to hide from cytotoxic T cells — which then exposes them to NK cells, so they must evade those too.","B":"Direct destruction of plasma cells is not a listed mechanism, and it has been used as a wrong option in a real paper.","A":"Inducing apoptosis of the host cell is a host defence that limits virus production; viruses more often DELAY it."},"img":"slides_i/L11_52.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Routes of entry","s":"Which is the MOST COMMON route of viral entry into the human body?","lec":"L11","sl":21,"c":"<b>Routes of entry</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Respiratory tract","A":"Gastrointestinal tract","E":"Skin","C":"Urogenital tract","D":"Conjunctiva"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the slide says it in as many words: the respiratory tract is the most common route of viral entry, by aerosolised droplets from coughing and sneezing or by contact with saliva.","A":"The GI tract is second and is the route for the naked, acid-resistant viruses.","E":"Skin is a natural barrier and needs a break, a bite or an injection.","C":"The urogenital route matters for HSV, HPV and HBV but is far from the commonest overall.","D":"Conjunctival entry is explicitly described as a RARE route."},"img":"slides_i/L11_21.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L11|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Immunopathology","s":"What does the lecture mean by IMMUNOPATHOLOGY in viral infection?","lec":"L11","sl":61,"c":"<b>Immunopathology</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Damage caused by the host's own immune response rather than by the virus itself, e.g. a cytokine storm","E":"Failure of the immune system to respond at all","D":"The virus attacking immune cells directly","C":"Autoantibodies present before the infection began","B":"A vaccine failing to generate protective antibody"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Sometimes the response does more harm than the virus: an excess of pro-inflammatory cytokines — a cytokine storm — is the example the lecture gives, and it is what kills in severe influenza and COVID-19.","E":"Failure to respond is immunodeficiency, the opposite problem.","D":"A virus attacking immune cells is direct cytopathology, as in HIV.","C":"Pre-existing autoantibody is autoimmunity unconnected to this infection.","B":"Vaccine failure is an immunisation issue, not immunopathology."},"img":"slides_i/L11_61.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L11|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-phase","t":"When to sample for virus","s":"A 27-year-old man presents with a systemic viral infection. Which period is BEST for testing for the PRESENCE OF THE VIRUS?","lec":"L11","sl":16,"c":"<b>When to sample for virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q47 · BM31 SUM1 · BM29 SUM1 · BM28 Q8 — the single most repeated virology item in the archive, asked in four cohorts with identical options</span>","o":{"A":"Acute phase","D":"Incubation period","C":"Prodrome","B":"Onset","E":"Recovery (convalescent) phase"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. On the lecture's curve, viral titre PEAKS in the acute phase, which is when antigen and nucleic-acid detection are most sensitive. Note what the question asks — the VIRUS, not the antibody.","D":"Titre is still climbing and may be below the limit of detection.","C":"Prodrome is early and the load is still low.","B":"Onset is the very start of the acute illness; the peak has not been reached.","E":"By convalescence the virus is being cleared. Convalescence is when you look for ANTIBODY — and a four-fold rise between acute and convalescent serum is the classic serological proof. Getting the two halves of that curve the wrong way round is the whole trap."},"img":"slides_i/L11_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q47","BM29 Q-phase","BM28 Q8"]},{"id":"I|L11|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q46","t":"What is true of viral pathogenesis","s":"Which statement about viral pathogenesis is CORRECT?","lec":"L11","sl":17,"c":"<b>What is true of viral pathogenesis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q46 — 'which statement about viral pathogenesis is CORRECT?' The BM30 key writes out why each distractor is false.</span>","o":{"E":"How severely a given virus affects one person depends partly on that person's own genetic make-up","D":"Mother-to-child transmission is a form of horizontal transmission","A":"The GI tract is the most common route of viral entry","B":"Tissue tropism is determined by the nucleic acid type of the virus","C":"Specific antibody first appears during the convalescent phase"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The host-factor slide lists immunity, genetics, general health and exposure level — outcome is an interplay of viral AND host factors, not a property of the virus alone.","D":"Mother to child is VERTICAL transmission. Horizontal is between individuals in a population.","A":"The RESPIRATORY tract is the most common route; the slide says so explicitly.","B":"Tropism is set by receptors, proteases and transcription factors — not by whether the genome is DNA or RNA.","C":"Specific antibody begins to rise in the ACUTE phase and peaks in convalescence. (In a previously vaccinated person it can be detected very early indeed.)"},"img":"slides_i/L11_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q48","t":"Cytopathic effect — owl's eye","s":"A 48-year-old is admitted to ICU with fever and abdominal pain from CMV infection. Which cytopathic effect would be found in the tissue?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Cytopathic effect — owl's eye</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q48 — ICU patient with CMV infection; what CPE is seen in tissue?</span>","o":{"C":"Owl's eye appearance — a large intranuclear inclusion with a clear halo","A":"Negri bodies","E":"Molluscum bodies","D":"Syncytia formation","B":"Koilocytes"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and it follows from the rule: CMV is a DNA (herpes) virus, so its inclusion is INTRANUCLEAR, and the classic description is the owl's eye.","A":"Negri bodies are the intracytoplasmic inclusion of RABIES — an RNA virus, exactly as the rule predicts.","E":"Molluscum bodies belong to molluscum contagiosum (a poxvirus), and note that poxvirus is the DNA virus that replicates in the CYTOPLASM, so its inclusion is cytoplasmic too.","D":"Syncytia are seen with RSV, measles, HSV and VZV.","B":"Koilocytes are the HPV change on a Pap smear (L13)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q3","t":"HSV — outcome of infection","s":"Which is an outcome of herpes simplex virus infection of host cells?","lec":"L11","sl":35,"c":"<b>HSV — outcome of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q3 (examiner-written, official answer CELL FUSION)</span>","o":{"D":"Cell fusion","A":"Cell division","E":"Transformation","C":"Cell detachment","B":"Cell lysis"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and it is the examiner's own answer key. HSV alters the infected cell membrane so infected and uninfected cells fuse into a MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELL — which is precisely what you see on a Tzanck smear, and why that smear is diagnostic.","A":"Viruses do not drive normal cell division; a virus that drives proliferation is doing TRANSFORMATION.","E":"HSV is not an oncogenic herpesvirus; EBV and HHV-8 are (L13).","C":"Detachment is a non-specific CPE seen with many viruses, not the HSV signature.","B":"HSV does lyse cells, but the option the examiner wanted is the one unique to it. When two options are both partly true, take the more SPECIFIC one."},"img":"slides_i/L11_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q51","t":"Tropism in a clinical stem","s":"Virus X replicates in neural cells but not in liver cells. What primarily determines this pattern?","lec":"L11","sl":28,"c":"<b>Tropism in a clinical stem</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q51 — virus X replicates in neural cells but not liver cells; what determines this?</span>","o":{"E":"Tropism — the match between the viral attachment protein and a receptor present on the host cell","D":"Presence of co-infecting bacteria","C":"Viral mutation rate","A":"The host's cytokine profile","B":"Antibody-dependent enhancement"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. No receptor, no entry. Receptor distribution is the first and strongest determinant of which tissue a virus can infect, which is why poliovirus hits motor neurons and HIV hits CD4+ cells.","D":"Bacterial co-infection does not create viral receptors.","C":"Mutation rate affects evolution over time, not the tissue pattern of a single infection.","A":"Cytokines shape the RESPONSE to infection, not which cells are permissive.","B":"ADE is a dengue mechanism (L14) that worsens disease; it does not set tropism."},"img":"slides_i/L11_28.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q49","t":"Epidemiological measures","s":"Which epidemiological measure best describes the number of NEW cases of influenza in a population during a specified time period?","lec":"L11","sl":11,"c":"<b>Epidemiological measures</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q49 — which measure describes the number of NEW influenza cases in a period?</span>","o":{"E":"Incidence","D":"Prevalence","C":"Outbreak","B":"Morbidity rate","A":"Endemic"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct — new cases over a defined period is the definition of incidence.","D":"Prevalence counts ALL existing cases, old and new, at a point in time.","C":"An outbreak is a sudden rise in cases in a limited area; it is a description of occurrence, not a measure.","B":"Morbidity rate is the general rate of illness in a population and is not specific to new cases.","A":"Endemic describes a disease being continuously present at a baseline level."},"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q50","t":"Transmission routes","s":"Which of the following viruses is commonly transmitted by the RESPIRATORY route?","lec":"L11","sl":46,"c":"<b>Transmission routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q50 — which virus is commonly transmitted by the respiratory route?</span>","o":{"A":"Influenza virus","D":"Hepatitis B virus","E":"Dengue virus","B":"HTLV-1","C":"Hepatitis C virus"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Influenza spreads by aerosolised droplets from coughing and sneezing — the respiratory tract is both its portal of entry and its portal of exit.","D":"HBV is blood-borne, sexual and vertical — the slide lists it under transmucosal/urogenital and blood.","E":"Dengue is vector-borne by Aedes (L14).","B":"HTLV-1 is transmitted by breast milk, sexual contact and blood.","C":"HCV is predominantly blood-borne."},"img":"slides_i/L11_46.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q52","t":"Rabies inclusion","s":"What structure is characteristically found in a rabies-infected neuron?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Rabies inclusion</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q52 — what structure is found in a rabies-infected cell?</span>","o":{"D":"Negri body — an intracytoplasmic inclusion","A":"Owl's eye intranuclear inclusion","E":"Koilocyte","C":"Multinucleated giant cell","B":"Molluscum body"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the location is not arbitrary: rabies is an RNA virus (Rhabdoviridae), RNA viruses replicate in the CYTOPLASM, so the inclusion is cytoplasmic. Negri bodies are found in hippocampal and Purkinje neurons.","A":"Owl's eye is CMV — a DNA virus, hence intranuclear.","E":"Koilocytes are HPV.","C":"Giant cells are HSV, VZV, measles and RSV.","B":"Molluscum bodies are molluscum contagiosum."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q54","t":"Virokines","s":"A patient with poxvirus infection is found to have reduced complement activity because the virus secretes a protein that MIMICS a host complement regulator. What is the role of this viral product?","lec":"L11","sl":52,"c":"<b>Virokines</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q54 — a poxvirus secretes a protein mimicking a host complement receptor, reducing complement activity; what is this product's role?</span>","o":{"C":"Immune evasion — it acts as a decoy for a host immune mediator and blocks complement-mediated destruction","E":"It functions as the viral polymerase","B":"It serves as a viral adhesion molecule for attachment","D":"It is a structural capsid protein","A":"It is a viral protease needed for maturation"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. This is molecular mimicry used for immune evasion: by copying a host complement regulator, the virus prevents complement from opsonising and lysing infected cells. The lecture groups it with virokines and viroceptors.","E":"A secreted decoy has no polymerase function, and polymerase acts inside the cell.","B":"An adhesion molecule would INCREASE attachment; this protein is secreted to neutralise a host defence.","D":"It is secreted, not packaged into the particle.","A":"Proteases act on viral polyprotein inside the cell and do not touch complement."},"img":"slides_i/L11_52.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q53","t":"HSV recurrence","s":"A 40-year-old woman has recurrent cold sores. HSV is confirmed. Which mechanism explains the recurrences?","lec":"L11","sl":42,"c":"<b>HSV recurrence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q53 & Q71 — recurrent cold sores; what mechanism is responsible?</span>","o":{"C":"Latency — the genome persists in sensory ganglion neurons without active replication, then reactivates","B":"Continuous low-grade replication in the skin that never fully clears","E":"Repeated re-infection from new external exposures","D":"Integration of the viral genome into the host chromosome, transforming the cell","A":"Antigenic drift producing a new serotype each episode"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. HSV establishes latency in sensory ganglia — the trigeminal ganglion for HSV-1. The genome sits there making almost no protein, so the cell presents little antigen and cytotoxic T cells cannot find it; stress, fever or UV light reactivate it and the virus travels back down the axon to the same dermatome.","B":"If it replicated continuously the lesion would never heal between episodes.","E":"Re-infection cannot explain why the lesion returns to exactly the same site.","D":"HSV does not integrate, and it does not transform cells.","A":"Drift would give a genuinely new antigenic strain; recurrence here is the SAME virus waking up. The same latency principle explains shingles from VZV."},"img":"slides_i/L11_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM32 Q71"]},{"id":"I|L11|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q7","t":"Determinants of severity","s":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that makes a viral infection more severe?","lec":"L11","sl":17,"c":"<b>Determinants of severity</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q7 — which is NOT a factor making a viral infection severe? (recorded answer: antiviral drug)</span>","o":{"A":"Administration of an effective antiviral drug","E":"The infecting dose of virus","C":"The host's age and immune status","B":"The route of entry","D":"The virulence of the strain"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. An effective antiviral makes the infection LESS severe — it belongs on the other side of the ledger from every other option.","E":"A larger infecting dose gives more severe disease.","C":"Age and immune status are listed host factors; the very young, the old and the immunocompromised do worse.","B":"Route matters — the same virus delivered intravenously can be far worse than one acquired across a mucosa.","D":"Strain virulence is a viral factor and directly affects severity."},"img":"slides_i/L11_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L12|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"The discriminator table","s":"Match each maculopapular exanthem to the ONE feature that identifies it.","lec":"L12","sl":26,"c":"<b>The discriminator table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Measles = Koplik spots; Rubella = posterior auricular/occipital lymphadenopathy; Erythema infectiosum = slapped cheek then lacy rash; Roseola = rash appears AS THE FEVER FALLS","B":"Measles = slapped cheek; Rubella = Koplik spots; Roseola = lacy rash on the limbs","A":"Measles = rash as the fever falls; Roseola = Koplik spots; Rubella = slapped cheek","C":"All four begin on the trunk and spread outward, and cannot be told apart clinically","E":"Rubella = Koplik spots; Measles = posterior auricular lymphadenopathy"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Four diseases, four one-line discriminators — and every past-paper exanthem item in this block is answered by one of them. Learn this row and you have the topic.","B":"Slapped cheek is parvovirus B19, not measles. Koplik is measles, not rubella.","A":"Rash appearing as the fever breaks is roseola (HHV-6/7), which is the exact trap in one BM30 item.","C":"They are distinguished clinically, and the exam expects you to do it.","E":"Both halves reversed."},"img":"slides_i/L12_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L12|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Measles","s":"A 6-year-old has high fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and photophobia, with small white spots on the buccal mucosa. What is the diagnosis and what is the buccal finding called?","lec":"L12","sl":10,"c":"<b>Measles</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Measles — Koplik spots, which appear in the prodrome BEFORE the skin rash","D":"Rubella — Forchheimer spots, which appear with the rash","C":"Scarlet fever — strawberry tongue","B":"Hand, foot and mouth disease — herpangina","E":"Roseola — oral vesicles"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Remember measles as the THREE Cs plus a fourth: Cough, Coryza, Conjunctivitis — and Koplik spots. Koplik is an ENANTHEM (mucosal) and is the earliest specific sign, appearing 1–2 days before the rash; photophobia comes with the conjunctivitis.","D":"Forchheimer spots exist in rubella but are non-specific; rubella's discriminator is tender posterior auricular and occipital nodes.","C":"Scarlet fever is bacterial (Streptococcus) — it is the 'second disease' in the classic numbering and is not a viral exanthem.","B":"HFMD gives vesicles on hands, feet and mouth, not Koplik spots and not the three Cs.","E":"Roseola gives no enanthem worth the name. Measles is an ENVELOPED (−)ssRNA paramyxovirus, and its late complication is SSPE."},"img":"slides_i/L12_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L12|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Varicella vs smallpox","s":"How does the rash of chickenpox differ from that of smallpox?","lec":"L12","sl":36,"c":"<b>Varicella vs smallpox</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"Chickenpox lesions are at SEVERAL DIFFERENT STAGES at once and are densest on the trunk; smallpox lesions are all at the SAME stage and are densest on the face and limbs","E":"Chickenpox lesions are all at the same stage; smallpox lesions are at several stages","B":"Both are identical and can only be separated by PCR","C":"Chickenpox is centrifugal, smallpox centripetal","A":"Chickenpox spares the mucosa; smallpox spares the trunk entirely"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and these two features are the whole comparison: STAGE and DISTRIBUTION. Chickenpox comes in crops, so macules, papules, vesicles and crusts coexist — the 'several stages' phrase that past papers reuse verbatim. Smallpox evolves in lockstep.","E":"Exactly reversed.","B":"They are distinguished clinically; PCR confirms.","C":"The direction words are the wrong way round and are not how the lecture frames it.","A":"Chickenpox does involve mucosa, and smallpox does not spare the trunk — it is simply less dense there."},"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L12|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Parvovirus B19","s":"Why does parvovirus B19 cause an APLASTIC CRISIS in a patient with sickle cell disease but only a rash in a healthy child?","lec":"L12","sl":20,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"B19 infects erythroid progenitor cells and halts red-cell production; a healthy marrow tolerates the pause, but a patient whose red cells already have a short lifespan cannot","E":"B19 destroys mature red cells directly by haemolysis","D":"B19 causes iron deficiency by blocking absorption","B":"B19 suppresses the whole marrow, causing pancytopenia in everyone","C":"B19 induces autoantibodies against red-cell antigens"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. B19 uses the P antigen (globoside) to enter erythroid progenitors in the marrow and shuts down erythropoiesis for about a week. Normal red cells live 120 days, so nothing is noticed; sickle cells live ~20 days, so the haemoglobin falls off a cliff.","E":"It kills the PRECURSORS, not the circulating cells.","D":"A BM30 distractor claimed aplastic crisis arises from iron deficiency. It does not — the block is on production, not on substrate.","B":"The defect is confined to the red-cell line.","C":"No autoantibody is involved. Two other B19 facts worth holding: it is a NAKED ssDNA virus, and once the rash is present the patient is no longer infectious."},"img":"slides_i/L12_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L12|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"HSV-1 vs HSV-2 vs VZV","s":"Which statement about the herpes vesicular exanthems is correct?","lec":"L12","sl":30,"c":"<b>HSV-1 vs HSV-2 vs VZV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion and recurs as herpes labialis; HSV-2 is latent in sacral ganglia and recurs as genital herpes; VZV is latent in dorsal root ganglia and recurs as zoster in a dermatome","D":"All three are latent in B lymphocytes","C":"HSV-1 causes genital lesions and HSV-2 causes cold sores, exclusively","A":"Only VZV establishes latency; HSV infections are fully cleared","B":"Acyclovir eradicates latent virus and cures all three"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and the pattern is one idea repeated: herpesviruses hide in NEURONS and come back in the territory of the nerve they hid in. That is why zoster is limited to a dermatome.","D":"B-cell latency is EBV (and HHV-8). This option has been offered in a real paper about a genital HSV case.","C":"Either type can infect either site; the stated pattern is only the usual one.","A":"All three herpesviruses establish lifelong latency.","B":"Acyclovir suppresses replication; it cannot touch the latent genome, which is why 'acyclovir can cure this disease' is a wrong option in a real paper."},"img":"slides_i/L12_30.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Tzanck smear","s":"A Tzanck smear from the base of a vesicle shows MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELLS. Which viruses does this indicate?","lec":"L12","sl":33,"c":"<b>Tzanck smear</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"HSV-1, HSV-2 or VZV — it confirms a herpes-group infection but does not separate them","D":"Parvovirus B19 only","C":"Measles virus only","A":"Coxsackie A virus and enterovirus 71","E":"Variola or monkeypox virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the limitation matters as much as the finding: the smear says 'herpes group', and you need PCR or culture to say which one. The giant cell arises because these viruses fuse infected with uninfected cells (the cell-fusion CPE of L11).","D":"B19 causes no vesicles at all.","C":"Measles does produce multinucleated giant cells — Warthin–Finkeldey cells — but in respiratory tissue, not in a skin vesicle.","A":"HFMD vesicles do not show herpetic giant cells.","E":"Poxvirus lesions show intracytoplasmic Guarnieri/molluscum-type inclusions instead."},"img":"slides_i/L12_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Congenital rubella","s":"A woman contracts rubella in the first trimester. What is the classic triad in the newborn?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Congenital rubella</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sensorineural deafness, cataract and congenital heart disease (patent ductus arteriosus)","B":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus and intracranial calcification","C":"Microcephaly, limb contractures and ocular abnormality","D":"Hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice and purpura alone","E":"Cleft lip, spina bifida and clubfoot"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and DEAFNESS is the single commonest defect — which is exactly what one recent paper asked. Risk is highest in the first trimester and falls thereafter.","B":"That triad is congenital TOXOPLASMOSIS (L7) — the exam deliberately runs the congenital infections together, so keep them separate.","C":"Microcephaly with limb contractures is congenital ZIKA (L14).","D":"The blueberry-muffin picture occurs in CRS but is not the classic triad.","E":"These are structural defects unrelated to rubella. In the mother herself, the commonest complication is polyarthritis, especially in women."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Infectious mononucleosis","s":"A 22-year-old has two weeks of fever, sore throat, cervical lymphadenopathy and a mildly enlarged spleen. What would the blood tests show?","lec":"L12","sl":40,"c":"<b>Infectious mononucleosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Atypical lymphocytosis with a POSITIVE heterophile antibody test","C":"A low lymphocyte percentage with neutrophilia","A":"Raised haematocrit","B":"Positive HBsAg","D":"Marked eosinophilia"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. EBV infectious mononucleosis gives atypical (reactive) lymphocytes and a positive heterophile (Monospot/Paul-Bunnell) test. EBV is latent in B LYMPHOCYTES, entering via CD21.","C":"Lymphocytes RISE, they do not fall — this exact inversion has been offered as an option.","A":"Haematocrit is unaffected.","B":"HBsAg belongs to hepatitis B, and that is a different clinical picture.","D":"Eosinophilia points to parasites, not EBV. One practical point: advise against contact sport because of the risk of splenic rupture."},"img":"slides_i/L12_40.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L12|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q60","t":"Chickenpox — several stages","s":"A 7-year-old has a rash on the trunk in which macules, papules, vesicles and crusts are ALL PRESENT AT THE SAME TIME. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L12","sl":36,"c":"<b>Chickenpox — several stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q60 · BM31 SUM1 Q60 · BM29 SUM1 Q62 — 'lesions at several stages at once', asked in three cohorts running</span>","o":{"D":"Varicella-zoster virus","B":"HSV-1","A":"HHV-6","C":"Parvovirus B19","E":"Coxsackie A virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Chickenpox arrives in successive crops, so lesions of different ages coexist — and it is densest on the TRUNK. Those two features together are the standard answer to this stem.","B":"HSV gives grouped vesicles at one site, all of the same age.","A":"HHV-6 causes roseola, which is a maculopapular rash with no vesicles.","C":"B19 gives slapped cheek and a lacy rash — again no vesicles.","E":"Coxsackie A gives HFMD, with vesicles confined to hands, feet and mouth. Smallpox is the real near-miss: same lesion type, but all at ONE stage and densest on face and extremities."},"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q60","BM29 Q62"]},{"id":"I|L12|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-roseola","t":"Roseola — rash after the fever","s":"A 7-month-old infant had three days of high fever. As the fever SUBSIDED, a maculopapular rash appeared. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L12","sl":25,"c":"<b>Roseola — rash after the fever</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q63 · BM31 SUM1 — a 7-month-old whose rash appears 3 days AFTER the fever subsides. ⚠ The student-made BM30 key records 'Rubella'; that is an error.</span>","o":{"C":"HHV-6 or HHV-7","A":"Rubella virus","E":"Measles virus","D":"Parvovirus B19","B":"HPV"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum, the 'sixth disease') is defined by its timing: several days of high fever in a well-looking infant, then the fever BREAKS and only then does the rash appear. The lecture prints the note 'As fever subsides, exanthem appears' on the HHV-6/7 slide.","A":"Rubella's rash comes WITH the fever, and its marker is tender posterior auricular and occipital nodes. ⚠ Be careful here: the circulating BM30 answer key marks Rubella for this stem, and it is wrong — BM31's version of the same question lists HHV 6,7 as an option precisely because that is the answer. Trust the timing, not the key.","E":"Measles brings cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and Koplik spots, and the rash appears while the child is still very febrile.","D":"B19 gives the slapped cheek then a lacy rash, typically in school-age children.","B":"HPV causes warts, not a febrile exanthem."},"img":"slides_i/L12_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q63"]},{"id":"I|L12|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q61","t":"Parvovirus B19 — aplastic crisis","s":"Which statement about parvovirus B19 is CORRECT?","lec":"L12","sl":20,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 — aplastic crisis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q61 · BM28 Q42 — which statement about parvovirus B19 is correct / 'aplastic anaemia' keyword</span>","o":{"B":"Once the rash has appeared the patient is no longer infectious","E":"Aplastic crisis occurs because the virus causes iron deficiency","C":"The virus attaches to the A antigen of the red cell","A":"It is an enveloped RNA virus","D":"It causes congenital cataract and deafness"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and it is the clinically useful fact: B19 is transmitted by respiratory droplets during the febrile PRODROME, and by the time the slapped cheek appears the rash is immune-mediated and the child can go back to school.","E":"The BM30 key records this as the intended answer, but the reasoning on the slide is different and better: the virus kills ERYTHROID PROGENITORS in the marrow, so production stops. Iron is not the problem.","C":"The receptor is the P antigen (globoside), not A antigen — a one-letter trap.","A":"B19 is a NAKED ssDNA parvovirus, which is why it survives well in the environment.","D":"Cataract and deafness are congenital RUBELLA. B19 in pregnancy causes hydrops fetalis."},"img":"slides_i/L12_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q42"]},{"id":"I|L12|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q7","t":"Measles — Koplik spots","s":"A 6-year-old boy has fever, cough, conjunctivitis and photophobia, with Koplik's spots on the oral mucosa. What is the causative virus?","lec":"L12","sl":10,"c":"<b>Measles — Koplik spots</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q7 (examiner-written) · BM28 Q27 — cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, photophobia and Koplik spots</span>","o":{"C":"Measles virus","A":"Rubella virus","B":"HHV-6","E":"Coxsackie A virus","D":"Mumps virus"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct — Koplik spots are pathognomonic for measles, and the three Cs plus photophobia complete the picture.","A":"Rubella is milder, without the three Cs, and is marked by posterior auricular lymphadenopathy.","B":"HHV-6 gives roseola: high fever in an infant, rash only after the fever falls.","E":"Coxsackie A gives HFMD and herpangina — vesicles, not Koplik spots.","D":"Mumps gives parotitis rather than an exanthem. There is no specific antiviral for measles; care is supportive, and prevention is the vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L12_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q27"]},{"id":"I|L12|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q57","t":"Monkeypox","s":"A man has fever, enlarged lymph nodes and vesicles containing yellow fluid. His partner, who is from West Africa, has the same rash. What is the causative virus?","lec":"L12","sl":48,"c":"<b>Monkeypox</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q57 · BM31 SUM1 Q62 · BM30 SUM1 Q62 — febrile illness, enlarged lymph nodes, vesicles with yellow fluid, a West African partner with the same rash</span>","o":{"C":"Monkeypox virus (Mpox)","B":"Varicella-zoster virus","A":"HSV-1","D":"Rubella virus","E":"Molluscum contagiosum virus"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and LYMPHADENOPATHY is the deciding feature: it is what separates mpox from smallpox, which does not cause it. Add the West African link (the Clade II lineage) and the deep, well-circumscribed pustules. Treatment is tecovirimat; diagnosis is PCR on a lesion swab.","B":"Chickenpox lesions are at several stages at once, are superficial, and prominent lymphadenopathy is not typical.","A":"HSV gives grouped vesicles at a single site without this systemic picture.","D":"Rubella's rash is maculopapular, not vesicular.","E":"Molluscum gives painless umbilicated papules with no fever."},"img":"slides_i/L12_48.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q62","BM30 Q62"]},{"id":"I|L12|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-match","t":"Herpes labialis","s":"A 25-year-old woman has a blister on her lip that has recurred several times. Which disease-and-virus pairing is correct?","lec":"L12","sl":31,"c":"<b>Herpes labialis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q60 · BM31 SUM1 — a 25-year-old with a recurrent blister on the lip; match the disease to its virus</span>","o":{"E":"Herpes labialis — HSV-1","D":"Herpes zoster — Coxsackie A virus","C":"Smallpox — Coxsackie A virus","B":"Hand, foot and mouth disease — Enterovirus 71 only","A":"Chickenpox — HSV-2"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A cold sore that keeps returning to the same spot is HSV-1 reactivating from the trigeminal ganglion.","D":"Herpes zoster is VARICELLA-ZOSTER virus.","C":"Smallpox is VARIOLA virus.","B":"HFMD is caused by Coxsackie A viruses AND enterovirus 71 — 'only' makes the option false.","A":"Chickenpox is VZV, not HSV-2."},"img":"slides_i/L12_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q60"]},{"id":"I|L12|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-tzanck","t":"Genital herpes","s":"A 35-year-old man has painful vesicular lesions on the penis for 1–2 days. A Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells. Which statement about this disease is CORRECT?","lec":"L12","sl":32,"c":"<b>Genital herpes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q21 · BM31 SUM1 — painful vesicular penile lesions, Tzanck smear showing multinucleated giant cells; which statement is correct?</span>","o":{"D":"Aciclovir shortens and suppresses episodes but cannot cure the infection","C":"The causative virus is latent in B lymphocytes","A":"The causative virus is transmitted by the oral–faecal route","E":"The varicella vaccine prevents this infection","B":"The causative virus commonly causes infertility in men"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Genital herpes is usually HSV-2, latent in the SACRAL ganglia. Aciclovir inhibits viral DNA polymerase during replication; the latent genome in the neuron is untouched, so recurrences continue and the infection is lifelong.","C":"B-cell latency is EBV. Herpes simplex hides in NEURONS.","A":"HSV-2 is sexually transmitted, not faecal–oral.","E":"The varicella vaccine covers VZV only; there is no licensed HSV vaccine.","B":"HSV does not cause male infertility — mumps orchitis is the classic viral cause of that."},"img":"slides_i/L12_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L12|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q61","t":"Infectious mononucleosis","s":"A 22-year-old man has had fever for two weeks, with an erythematous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy and a mildly enlarged spleen. What would the laboratory show?","lec":"L12","sl":41,"c":"<b>Infectious mononucleosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q61 — 2 weeks of fever, erythematous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy, mildly enlarged spleen; what will the laboratory show?</span>","o":{"A":"Heterophile antibody positive","D":"HBsAg positive","E":"Raised haematocrit","C":"Reduced lymphocyte percentage","B":"Marked eosinophilia"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly in a young adult is EBV infectious mononucleosis, and the heterophile (Monospot) test is the bedside confirmation, backed by atypical lymphocytes on the film.","D":"Hepatitis B gives a different picture and no pharyngitis.","E":"Haematocrit is not raised.","C":"Lymphocytes RISE — this inversion is the planted trap.","B":"Eosinophilia suggests parasites. A classic clinical pearl: giving ampicillin to a patient with IM produces a florid maculopapular rash."},"img":"slides_i/L12_41.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q26","t":"Erythema infectiosum","s":"A 6-year-old girl has fever and a LACY, reticular rash. Her mother says the rash started on her cheeks yesterday and spread to the trunk and limbs this morning. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L12","sl":22,"c":"<b>Erythema infectiosum</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q26 — 6-year-old with fever and a LACY rash that began on the cheeks yesterday and spread to trunk and limbs today</span>","o":{"B":"Parvovirus B19","A":"Measles virus","D":"Rubella virus","E":"HHV-6","C":"Varicella-zoster virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Slapped cheek first, then a LACY (reticular) rash on trunk and limbs one to two days later — that sequence is erythema infectiosum, the fifth disease, caused by parvovirus B19.","A":"Measles also starts on the face, but the rash is confluent maculopapular and comes with the three Cs and Koplik spots.","D":"Rubella's rash is pinky maculopapular and is accompanied by tender occipital nodes.","E":"Roseola's rash appears only after the fever falls and is not lacy.","C":"VZV is vesicular."},"img":"slides_i/L12_22.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q58","t":"Congenital rubella","s":"A newborn has congenital rubella. What is the most likely complication?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Congenital rubella</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q58 — the most likely complication in a newborn with congenital rubella</span>","o":{"E":"Sensorineural deafness","B":"Thrombocytopenia","C":"Intracranial calcification","A":"Limb contractures","D":"Cholestatic jaundice"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct — deafness is the commonest single defect of congenital rubella syndrome, alongside cataract and patent ductus arteriosus in the classic triad.","B":"Thrombocytopenia does occur (the blueberry-muffin rash) but is much less common than deafness, which makes it the well-designed near-miss.","C":"Intracranial calcification with hydrocephalus and retinochoroiditis is congenital TOXOPLASMOSIS.","A":"Limb contractures with microcephaly suggest congenital Zika.","D":"Cholestasis is not a feature."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q25","t":"Who is at risk from rubella","s":"A 23-year-old man is diagnosed with rubella. Which contact is at GREATEST risk of serious consequences?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Who is at risk from rubella</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q25 — a 23-year-old man has rubella; who around him is at greatest risk?</span>","o":{"B":"A woman in the first trimester of pregnancy","A":"A man of uncertain immune status","D":"Someone who has previously had the disease","C":"Someone who has completed the full rubella vaccine course","E":"Someone whose rubella-specific IgG is positive"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Rubella is mild in the patient himself; the entire public-health concern is the FETUS. First-trimester infection carries the highest risk of congenital rubella syndrome.","A":"An unsure adult man might catch a mild illness — unpleasant, not serious.","D":"Past infection confers durable immunity.","C":"A fully vaccinated person is protected.","E":"Positive specific IgG means existing immunity — which is exactly why it is offered here as a plausible-sounding wrong answer."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L12|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q79","t":"RT-PCR in an exanthem","s":"A 5-year-old has fever, swollen lymph nodes and a rash that began on the face and spread to the body. A throat swab is tested by RT-PCR. What does RT-PCR contribute to the diagnosis?","lec":"L12","sl":18,"c":"<b>RT-PCR in an exanthem</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q79 — a 5-year-old with fever, rash spreading from face to body and swollen nodes; a throat swab is tested by RT-PCR. What does RT-PCR contribute?</span>","o":{"C":"It identifies the viral RNA genome directly, confirming which virus is present","B":"It measures the child's immune response to the virus","E":"It visualises virus particles under the microscope","A":"It detects antibody produced against the virus","D":"It cultures the virus so that its cytopathic effect can be read"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. RT = reverse transcription, so the assay first turns viral RNA into cDNA and then amplifies it — it is a direct test for the GENOME of an RNA virus. In this stem (measles or rubella) it names the pathogen during the acute phase, when the virus is still being shed.","B":"Measuring the immune response is serology — IgM and IgG.","E":"Visualising particles is electron microscopy.","A":"Antibody detection is ELISA, HI or immunochromatography, not PCR.","D":"Culture and CPE reading is a separate, much slower method. Match the method to the target: genome → PCR, antigen → ELISA/immunochromatography, antibody → serology, particle → EM, cell change → light microscopy."},"img":"slides_i/L12_18.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L13|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"The summary table","s":"Which row of the oncogenic-virus table is CORRECT?","lec":"L13","sl":35,"c":"<b>The summary table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HPV — dsDNA — cervical cancer — E6 and E7 — inactivation of p53 and pRb","D":"EBV — ssRNA — Burkitt lymphoma — LANA — inactivation of p53","C":"HBV — dsRNA — hepatocellular carcinoma — E6 — chronic inflammation only","E":"HTLV-1 — dsDNA — adult T-cell leukaemia — HBx — transactivation","B":"HHV-8 — ssRNA — Kaposi's sarcoma — EBNA-1 — direct integration"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and this row is the most-asked fact in the lecture. Learn the whole table as five rows: HPV/E6+E7, EBV/EBNA+LMP, HHV-8/vIRF+vIL-6, HBV/HBx, HTLV-1/Tax.","D":"EBV is a dsDNA herpesvirus, its oncoproteins are EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1 and LMP-2, and Burkitt lymphoma turns on c-MYC deregulation. LANA belongs to HHV-8.","C":"HBV is partially circular dsDNA and its oncoprotein is HBx.","E":"HTLV-1 is an enveloped (+)ssRNA retrovirus and its oncoprotein is Tax.","B":"HHV-8 is a dsDNA herpesvirus; EBNA-1 is EBV's."},"img":"slides_i/L13_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Direct vs indirect","s":"What distinguishes a DIRECT from an INDIRECT mechanism of viral oncogenesis?","lec":"L13","sl":8,"c":"<b>Direct vs indirect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Direct: the virus expresses an oncoprotein that drives the cell cycle in the transformed cell itself. Indirect: the virus causes chronic inflammation or immunosuppression, and the cancer arises as a consequence","D":"Direct means DNA viruses and indirect means RNA viruses","B":"Direct means the virus integrates; indirect means it does not","A":"Direct means the cancer appears within weeks; indirect means it takes years","C":"Direct applies to solid tumours and indirect to leukaemias"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. HPV, EBV and HTLV-1 work mainly DIRECTLY, through oncoproteins expressed in the cell that becomes malignant. HBV and HCV work largely INDIRECTLY, through decades of chronic inflammation, regeneration and cirrhosis.","D":"Both DNA and RNA viruses appear on both sides — HTLV-1 is an RNA virus acting directly.","B":"HCV does not integrate at all, yet causes cancer; and integration is not what the direct/indirect split means.","A":"Every viral cancer takes years to decades. Speed is not the distinction.","C":"HPV gives a solid tumour directly; HTLV-1 gives a leukaemia directly."},"img":"slides_i/L13_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"p53 and pRb","s":"Why does inactivating BOTH p53 and pRb transform a cell?","lec":"L13","sl":9,"c":"<b>p53 and pRb</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"pRb normally holds the cell at the G1/S checkpoint and p53 normally triggers arrest or apoptosis when DNA is damaged — losing both removes the brake AND the emergency stop","C":"p53 and pRb are proto-oncogenes, so inactivating them switches on proliferation directly","E":"p53 repairs DNA and pRb replicates it, so cells cannot divide without them","B":"Both are growth factors, and their loss starves the cell into malignancy","D":"Both are viral proteins normally supplied by the host"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. pRb is the gatekeeper of G1/S — phosphorylate or inactivate it and the cell enters S phase unchecked. p53 is the guardian of the genome — it halts the cycle for repair or triggers apoptosis. Remove the brake and you get uncontrolled division; remove the emergency stop and the damaged cell survives to accumulate more mutations.","C":"They are TUMOUR SUPPRESSOR genes, the opposite category. Proto-oncogenes (such as c-MYC) cause cancer when switched ON, not off.","E":"Neither protein performs DNA replication itself.","B":"Neither is a growth factor.","D":"Both are host proteins, which is precisely why the virus must supply proteins to disable them."},"img":"slides_i/L13_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"HPV risk stratification","s":"A healthy woman is found to be infected with HPV type 11. What is the expected outcome?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>HPV risk stratification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Genital warts (condylomata acuminata); types 6 and 11 are LOW-risk and do not cause cervical cancer","A":"High risk of cervical cancer within five years","B":"Lifelong latency in B lymphocytes","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma","D":"Adult T-cell leukaemia"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. HPV types are split by transforming potential: 6 and 11 are low-risk and produce anogenital warts; 16 and 18 are high-risk and produce cervical cancer.","A":"High cervical-cancer risk belongs to 16 and 18, not 11.","B":"HPV infects squamous epithelium; B-cell latency is EBV.","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8.","D":"Adult T-cell leukaemia is HTLV-1. Most HPV infections in an immunocompetent host clear within about two years."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Koilocytes and the Pap smear","s":"A Pap smear shows squamous cells with perinuclear halos and irregular nuclei (koilocytes). What does this indicate and what is the next step?","lec":"L13","sl":20,"c":"<b>Koilocytes and the Pap smear</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"HPV cytopathic change; follow with HPV DNA testing by PCR, and colposcopy with biopsy if abnormalities persist","E":"Herpes simplex infection; start aciclovir","A":"Adenocarcinoma; proceed straight to hysterectomy","C":"Candida infection; treat with topical antifungal","D":"A normal finding requiring no action"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The koilocyte — a squamous cell with a perinuclear halo and an irregular, raisin-like nucleus — is the cytological footprint of HPV. The lecture's investigation list is exactly this: Pap smear, then HPV DNA by PCR, then biopsy if findings are abnormal.","E":"Herpes gives multinucleated giant cells on a Tzanck smear, not halos.","A":"Koilocytosis is a low-grade squamous change (LSIL), not adenocarcinoma, and hysterectomy is never the next step after a smear.","C":"Candida is identified by budding yeasts and pseudohyphae.","D":"Koilocytosis is abnormal and requires follow-up."},"img":"slides_i/L13_20.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"HBV serology","s":"Which serological pattern indicates immunity from VACCINATION rather than from past infection?","lec":"L13","sl":27,"c":"<b>HBV serology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs POSITIVE, anti-HBc NEGATIVE","B":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc positive","D":"HBsAg positive, anti-HBs negative, anti-HBc positive","A":"HBsAg positive, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc negative","E":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, anti-HBc positive"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and anti-HBc is the discriminator. The vaccine contains only surface antigen, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs and NEVER anti-HBc. Anti-HBc positive means the person met the whole virus.","B":"Anti-HBs positive WITH anti-HBc positive is immunity from RESOLVED NATURAL INFECTION.","D":"HBsAg positive with anti-HBc positive is current infection.","A":"HBsAg and anti-HBs both positive is an unusual pattern and not a vaccination profile.","E":"Isolated anti-HBc is the window period, a resolved infection with waned anti-HBs, or occult infection. This panel is worth learning cold — recent papers build hepatitis stems on it."},"img":"slides_i/L13_27.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"HTLV-1","s":"Which statement about HTLV-1 is correct?","lec":"L13","sl":32,"c":"<b>HTLV-1</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"It is an enveloped (+)ssRNA retrovirus transmitted by breast milk, sex and blood, and it causes adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma via the Tax protein","E":"It is a dsDNA herpesvirus causing Burkitt lymphoma","A":"It causes Kaposi's sarcoma in patients with AIDS","D":"It is prevented by the hepatitis B vaccine","C":"It transforms B lymphocytes through the EBNA-1 protein"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. HTLV-1 sits in Retroviridae with HIV: enveloped, (+)ssRNA, reverse transcriptase. Unlike most retroviruses, which carry only gag, pol and env, it has extra regulatory genes — Tax being the transforming one.","E":"Burkitt lymphoma is EBV, a dsDNA herpesvirus.","A":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8.","D":"The hepatitis B vaccine prevents HBV — and, because HDV needs HBsAg, hepatitis D as well. It does nothing for HTLV-1.","C":"EBNA-1 and B-cell transformation are EBV; HTLV-1 targets CD4+ T cells."},"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"EBV-associated cancers","s":"Which set of malignancies is associated with EBV?","lec":"L13","sl":21,"c":"<b>EBV-associated cancers</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease","D":"Cervical cancer and anogenital warts","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma","B":"Adult T-cell leukaemia and tropical spastic paraparesis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. EBV's range is wide because it immortalises B cells and infects nasopharyngeal epithelium: endemic Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and PTLD in the immunosuppressed.","D":"Cervical cancer and warts are HPV.","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma are HHV-8.","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is HBV and HCV; cholangiocarcinoma is the liver FLUKE Opisthorchis (L4), not a virus at all.","B":"Both of those are HTLV-1."},"img":"slides_i/L13_21.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L13|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pap","t":"HPV — E6 and E7","s":"A Pap smear shows abnormal squamous epithelial cells. Which molecular event underlies this?","lec":"L13","sl":17,"c":"<b>HPV — E6 and E7</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q53 · BM31 SUM1 · BM29 SUM1 · BM28 Q38 — abnormal squamous cells on a Pap smear; which mechanism? Asked in four cohorts, and the ORDER of the two targets is always the trap.</span>","o":{"D":"E6 inhibits p53 and E7 inhibits pRb, respectively","C":"E6 inhibits pRb and E7 inhibits p53, respectively","E":"LANA inhibits p53 and pRb","B":"EBNA-1 drives c-myc deregulation","A":"HBx transactivates host growth genes"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Keep the order by counting: E6 with p53 — the SMALLER number goes with the SMALLER number if you read 6 before 7 and p53 before pRb in the paper's own phrasing. E6 targets p53 for degradation; E7 binds and inactivates pRb. Together they abolish both the G1/S brake and the apoptotic safety net.","C":"The swapped version, and it is offered as an option in every cohort that has asked this. Reading past the first three words is what loses the mark here.","E":"LANA is an HHV-8 protein.","B":"EBNA-1 is EBV; c-myc deregulation is the Burkitt mechanism.","A":"HBx is hepatitis B."},"img":"slides_i/L13_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q53","BM29 Q-pap","BM28 Q38"]},{"id":"I|L13|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q67","t":"Kaposi's sarcoma","s":"A 50-year-old man with AIDS has multiple cutaneous vascular lesions and oral nodules. Biopsy confirms Kaposi's sarcoma. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L13","sl":23,"c":"<b>Kaposi's sarcoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q67 · BM31 SUM1 · BM30 SUM1 Q55 · BM29 SUM1 Q57 · BM30 Formative virology Q6 — five appearances, identical options</span>","o":{"A":"HHV-8","C":"HBV","D":"HCV","E":"HPV","B":"HTLV-1"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. HHV-8 is also called Kaposi's sarcoma-associated herpesvirus (KSHV) — the name gives the answer away, which is presumably why the item has survived five cohorts. It also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease, and it works through lytic proteins such as vIRFs, vIL-6, vCCLs and vGPCR, plus latent LANA.","C":"HBV causes hepatocellular carcinoma.","D":"HCV also causes hepatocellular carcinoma.","E":"HPV causes cervical and other anogenital cancers.","B":"HTLV-1 causes adult T-cell leukaemia."},"img":"slides_i/L13_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-kaposi","BM30 Q55","BM29 Q57","BM30 virology Q6"]},{"id":"I|L13|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q56","t":"HCC — reading the serology","s":"A 45-year-old man has weight loss, jaundice and hepatomegaly. HBsAg NEGATIVE, anti-HBs POSITIVE, anti-HBc NEGATIVE, AFP high, MRI shows hepatocellular carcinoma. Which viral oncoprotein is implicated?","lec":"L13","sl":27,"c":"<b>HCC — reading the serology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q58 · BM31 SUM1 Q56 — HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc NEGATIVE, high AFP, HCC on MRI. BM31 asks for the oncoprotein instead of the virus.</span>","o":{"D":"HBx","C":"NS3","A":"HBc","E":"LANA","B":"EBNA-1"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct as the answer the archive records, and HBx is the HBV oncoprotein — it transactivates host growth-control genes and the integrated HBV DNA disrupts the genome. ⚠ Read the serology carefully, though: anti-HBc NEGATIVE with anti-HBs positive is the VACCINATED pattern, not past infection, so this stem as recalled is internally inconsistent. Learn the oncoprotein; treat the serology numbers as the thing to check in the real paper.","C":"NS3 is a hepatitis C protein.","A":"HBc is the core structural antigen, not an oncoprotein.","E":"LANA is HHV-8.","B":"EBNA-1 is EBV."},"img":"slides_i/L13_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q58"]},{"id":"I|L13|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q54","t":"HCC — the HCV version","s":"A patient with hepatocellular carcinoma has anti-HAV NEGATIVE, anti-HBs POSITIVE and anti-HCV POSITIVE. Which infection is responsible for the tumour?","lec":"L13","sl":29,"c":"<b>HCC — the HCV version</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q54 — anti-HAV negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HCV POSITIVE, with hepatocellular carcinoma</span>","o":{"E":"Hepatitis C","D":"Hepatitis A","B":"Hepatitis B","C":"Co-infection of hepatitis A and C","A":"Co-infection of hepatitis B and C"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is decided by which antibody means what. Anti-HCV POSITIVE indicates HCV infection. Anti-HBs positive on its own means IMMUNITY to hepatitis B (vaccination or resolved infection), not current infection — you would need HBsAg for that. So the only active oncogenic infection here is HCV.","D":"Hepatitis A causes an acute self-limited illness and never chronic infection or cancer; anti-HAV negative rules it out anyway.","B":"Anti-HBs positive without HBsAg is protection, not disease.","C":"Both halves fail.","A":"There is no evidence of active HBV. HCV is an enveloped (+)ssRNA flavivirus whose oncogenesis is largely INDIRECT, through chronic inflammation and cirrhosis."},"img":"slides_i/L13_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q59","t":"EBV — Burkitt mechanism","s":"By which mechanism does Epstein-Barr virus drive Burkitt lymphoma?","lec":"L13","sl":22,"c":"<b>EBV — Burkitt mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q59 · BM28 Q41 — the mechanism of Epstein-Barr virus oncogenesis / the Burkitt question</span>","o":{"C":"c-MYC deregulation following a chromosomal translocation in an EBV-immortalised B cell","B":"Inactivation of p53","A":"Inactivation of pRb","E":"Hepatitis B X transactivation","D":"Chronic inflammation alone"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. EBV immortalises B lymphocytes through EBNA and LMP proteins; on that background a t(8;14) translocation moves c-MYC next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain enhancer, so a master proliferation gene is driven permanently on.","B":"p53 inactivation is HPV E6 (and a lytic HHV-8 effect).","A":"pRb inactivation is HPV E7.","E":"X transactivation is HBV.","D":"Chronic inflammation is the INDIRECT mechanism used by HBV and HCV; EBV's route here is a defined genetic lesion."},"img":"slides_i/L13_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q41"]},{"id":"I|L13|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-hpv11","t":"Low-risk HPV","s":"A healthy woman acquires HPV type 11. What is the most likely consequence?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>Low-risk HPV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q66 · BM31 SUM1 — a healthy woman acquires HPV type 11; what happens?</span>","o":{"B":"Warts (condylomata acuminata), since types 6 and 11 are low-risk","D":"High risk of cervical cancer","E":"The virus will persist for life in every case","A":"Increased risk of developing AIDS","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The lecture splits HPV by transforming potential: 6 and 11 are LOW-risk and give anogenital warts; 16 and 18 are HIGH-risk and give cervical cancer. Two numbers each — that is the whole classification.","D":"Cervical cancer risk belongs to 16 and 18.","E":"Most HPV infections in an immunocompetent host clear within about two years, which is why 'will clear within 2 years' also appears as an option in these papers.","A":"HPV has no causal link to AIDS.","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM29 Q66"]},{"id":"I|L13|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q72","t":"Genital warts","s":"A 32-year-old woman has multiple PAINLESS, CAULIFLOWER-LIKE lesions around the genital area. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>Genital warts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q72 — a 32-year-old woman with multiple painless cauliflower-like lesions around the genital area</span>","o":{"D":"HPV subtype 6","C":"HPV subtype 16","B":"HSV-1","E":"HSV-2","A":"Molluscum contagiosum virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Cauliflower-shaped and PAINLESS is condyloma acuminatum, caused by the low-risk types 6 and 11.","C":"HPV 16 is high-risk and causes cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and carcinoma, which do not present as exophytic warts.","B":"HSV lesions are PAINFUL vesicles and ulcers — the word 'painless' is placed in the stem to exclude them.","E":"Same reasoning; HSV-2 genital herpes is painful and recurrent.","A":"Molluscum gives small umbilicated papules, not cauliflower masses."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q37","t":"HTLV-1","s":"A patient is diagnosed with adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma. Which virus is implicated?","lec":"L13","sl":32,"c":"<b>HTLV-1</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q37 — a patient with adult T-cell leukaemia; which virus?</span>","o":{"C":"HTLV-1","A":"EBV","E":"HHV-8","D":"HPV 18","B":"HBV"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. HTLV-1 is a retrovirus that infects CD4+ T cells; besides ATLL it causes HTLV-1-associated myelopathy (tropical spastic paraparesis). Its oncoprotein is Tax, and note that most retroviruses carry only gag, pol and env — HTLV-1's extra regulatory genes are what make it oncogenic.","A":"EBV transforms B cells, not T cells.","E":"HHV-8 causes Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma.","D":"HPV 18 causes cervical cancer.","B":"HBV causes hepatocellular carcinoma."},"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q40","t":"HBV — the X gene","s":"Which hepatitis B gene product acts by TRANSACTIVATION of host growth-control genes?","lec":"L13","sl":28,"c":"<b>HBV — the X gene</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q40 — the HBV gene responsible for transactivation</span>","o":{"C":"HBx","B":"HBsAg","E":"HBcAg","D":"HBV polymerase","A":"HBeAg"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The X protein transactivates cellular promoters and interferes with DNA repair and apoptosis; together with integration of viral DNA and the chronic inflammation of long-standing hepatitis, it makes chronic carriers the high-risk group for HCC.","B":"HBsAg is the surface antigen — the marker of active infection and the component of the vaccine.","E":"HBcAg is the core protein; anti-HBc marks past or present natural infection.","D":"The polymerase is a reverse transcriptase (HBV is Baltimore VII) but is not the oncoprotein.","A":"HBeAg is a marker of high replication and infectivity."},"img":"slides_i/L13_28.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L13|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q39","t":"HPV and condylomata","s":"Which HPV types cause condylomata acuminata?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>HPV and condylomata</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q39 — which HPV strains cause condylomata acuminatum?</span>","o":{"B":"6 and 11","D":"16 and 18","E":"31 and 33","A":"1 and 2","C":"52 and 58"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct — the low-risk pair. Hold the four numbers as two pairs: 6/11 warts, 16/18 cancer.","D":"16 and 18 are the high-risk pair behind roughly 70% of cervical cancers.","E":"31 and 33 are additional high-risk types.","A":"1 and 2 cause common skin warts, not anogenital condylomata.","C":"52 and 58 are further high-risk types covered by the nonavalent vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L14|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"The comparison table","s":"Dengue, chikungunya and Zika all present as fever with rash after a mosquito bite. Which set of discriminators is correct?","lec":"L14","sl":62,"c":"<b>The comparison table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Dengue — thrombocytopenia, leukopenia and haemorrhage; Chikungunya — severe, persistent POLYARTHRITIS; Zika — usually mild or asymptomatic, with conjunctivitis, and microcephaly if in pregnancy","D":"Dengue — severe polyarthritis; Chikungunya — thrombocytopenia and haemorrhage; Zika — encephalitis","A":"All three are clinically identical and can only be separated by RT-PCR","B":"Dengue — jaundice; Chikungunya — microcephaly; Zika — shock","C":"Dengue — encephalitis; Chikungunya — hepatitis; Zika — haemorrhage"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and this is the row the final summary slide is built on. One word each: dengue BLEEDS, chikungunya ACHES, Zika is QUIET until someone is pregnant.","D":"The first two are swapped — and this swap is exactly the option offered in a recent paper.","A":"Serology and PCR confirm, but the exam expects clinical separation.","B":"Jaundice is YELLOW FEVER; microcephaly is Zika; shock is dengue.","C":"Encephalitis belongs to Japanese encephalitis and West Nile, not dengue."},"img":"slides_i/L14_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","s":"Why is a SECOND dengue infection with a different serotype more dangerous than the first?","lec":"L14","sl":24,"c":"<b>Antibody-dependent enhancement</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Cross-reactive, non-neutralising antibody from the first infection binds the new serotype and carries it into Fc-receptor-bearing cells — antibody-dependent enhancement","C":"The second serotype is intrinsically more virulent than the first","B":"The first infection destroys the bone marrow, so the patient cannot respond","D":"Antibody from the first infection neutralises the patient's own platelets","A":"The second infection is always caused by a mosquito carrying a larger viral dose"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and ADE is the mechanism the lecturer marks with a double asterisk. Antibody raised against serotype 1 cross-reacts with serotype 2 but does not neutralise it; the antibody-virus complex is then taken up through Fc receptors on monocytes and macrophages, so MORE cells are infected, not fewer. The result is the cytokine surge and plasma leakage of DHF/DSS.","C":"All four serotypes can cause severe disease; the lecture notes DEN-2 as highest risk, but that is a modifier, not the mechanism.","B":"The marrow is not destroyed — the thrombocytopenia is consumptive and immune-mediated.","D":"Anti-platelet autoantibody plays a minor part but is not ADE.","A":"Dose is not the explanation. Two related ideas travel with ADE: ORIGINAL ANTIGENIC SIN, where cross-reactive memory T cells respond suboptimally to the new serotype, and immune-complex activation of endothelium."},"img":"slides_i/L14_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"The critical phase","s":"In dengue, WHEN does the patient most often deteriorate into shock?","lec":"L14","sl":22,"c":"<b>The critical phase</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"At DEFERVESCENCE — as the fever falls, around days 3–7, when plasma leakage begins","C":"On the first day of fever, at the peak of viraemia","D":"Only after the rash has completely resolved","E":"During the convalescent phase, once appetite returns","B":"Two to three weeks after the acute illness"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the most counter-intuitive fact in the lecture: the patient looks better as the fever settles, and that is exactly when the critical phase of plasma leakage begins. Watch for a rising haematocrit with a falling platelet count.","C":"Viraemia peaks early, but shock does not — the damage is immune-mediated and lags behind.","D":"Shock precedes, not follows, resolution of the rash.","E":"Return of appetite, stabilising vital signs and bradycardia mark RECOVERY, which is the phase after the danger has passed.","B":"Far too late. Practically: never discharge a dengue patient just because the fever has broken."},"img":"slides_i/L14_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Which arbovirus attacks which organ","s":"Match the arbovirus to the target the lecture assigns it.","lec":"L14","sl":12,"c":"<b>Which arbovirus attacks which organ</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Yellow fever → hepatocyte (jaundice); Dengue → endothelium and blood elements (haemorrhage); Japanese encephalitis and West Nile → neurons (encephalitis)","C":"Yellow fever → neurons; Dengue → hepatocyte; West Nile → endothelium","D":"All arboviruses target vascular endothelium only","A":"Chikungunya → hepatocyte; Zika → endothelium","E":"Japanese encephalitis → hepatocyte; Yellow fever → neurons"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct — the disease-syndrome slide maps each virus to its target tissue, and the clinical picture falls straight out of it: liver → jaundice, endothelium and platelets → bleeding and shock, neurons → encephalitis.","C":"Yellow fever is named for the jaundice it causes; it is hepatotropic.","D":"Only dengue is primarily endothelial.","A":"Chikungunya targets joints and Zika is neurotropic for the fetal brain.","E":"Both reversed."},"img":"slides_i/L14_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Dengue laboratory windows","s":"A patient is on day 2 of a suspected dengue illness. Which test is most likely to be positive?","lec":"L14","sl":30,"c":"<b>Dengue laboratory windows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"NS1 antigen or RT-PCR — both are positive early, in the first few days of fever","E":"IgG, which appears on day 1 of a primary infection","C":"IgM, which is reliably positive from the first day of fever","D":"A four-fold rise in paired sera, which can be demonstrated on day 2","A":"Tourniquet test, which is a specific virological test"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The lecture's table is a timeline: NS1 antigen and PCR are the EARLY tests (first ~5 days, while the patient is viraemic), IgM appears around day 5 and IgG later. Day 2 is squarely in the antigen/PCR window.","E":"In a PRIMARY infection IgG appears late; early IgG suggests a SECONDARY infection, which is itself a useful clue.","C":"IgM is usually not detectable until around day 5.","D":"A four-fold rise needs paired acute AND convalescent sera, so it is retrospective by definition.","A":"The tourniquet test detects capillary fragility (>10 petechiae per square inch after 5 minutes). It supports the clinical diagnosis but is not virological."},"img":"slides_i/L14_30.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Transmission cycles","s":"Japanese encephalitis virus is maintained in nature by which cycle?","lec":"L14","sl":8,"c":"<b>Transmission cycles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"An animal–arthropod–animal cycle, with pigs and wading birds as amplifying hosts and Culex as the vector; humans are DEAD-END hosts","B":"A man–arthropod–man cycle in which humans are the reservoir","E":"Direct person-to-person respiratory transmission","C":"A cycle requiring a freshwater snail as intermediate host","A":"Transmission by the Aedes mosquito from human to human only"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. JE runs an ANIMAL–arthropod–animal cycle: pigs are the amplifying host, wading birds the maintenance reservoir, Culex the vector. Humans are incidental DEAD-END hosts because our viraemia is too low to re-infect a mosquito — which is why living near pig farms is the risk factor.","B":"A man–arthropod–man cycle is DENGUE, where humans are the reservoir.","E":"No arbovirus spreads by the respiratory route.","C":"Snails are intermediate hosts for trematodes, not viruses.","A":"JE's vector is Culex, not Aedes."},"img":"slides_i/L14_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Zika complications","s":"Which two complications of Zika virus infection does the lecture emphasise?","lec":"L14","sl":41,"c":"<b>Zika complications</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Congenital microcephaly after infection in pregnancy, and Guillain-Barré syndrome in adults","B":"Haemorrhagic shock and hepatic failure","E":"Chronic destructive arthritis and permanent joint deformity","A":"Aplastic crisis and hydrops fetalis","D":"Cholangiocarcinoma and portal hypertension"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. These are the two that made Zika notorious: congenital microcephaly in babies infected in utero, and Guillain-Barré syndrome — an immune attack on peripheral nerves causing weakness and sometimes paralysis — in infected adults.","B":"Haemorrhagic shock and hepatic failure belong to dengue and yellow fever.","E":"Chronic destructive arthritis is chikungunya.","A":"Aplastic crisis and hydrops fetalis are parvovirus B19 (L12).","D":"Cholangiocarcinoma is the liver fluke Opisthorchis (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_41.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Which family","s":"Which grouping of arbovirus families is correct?","lec":"L14","sl":7,"c":"<b>Which family</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Flaviviridae: yellow fever, dengue, Zika, Japanese encephalitis, West Nile. Togaviridae: chikungunya","E":"Flaviviridae: chikungunya only. Togaviridae: dengue and Zika","A":"All arboviruses belong to Flaviviridae","B":"Flaviviridae: dengue and chikungunya. Togaviridae: West Nile","D":"All arboviruses are Bunyaviridae"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Five Flaviviridae and one Togaviridae is the whole taxonomy you need for this lecture. Both families are enveloped with linear (+)ssRNA genomes; chikungunya sits in the genus Alphavirus within Togaviridae.","E":"Chikungunya is the Togaviridae member, not a flavivirus.","A":"Chikungunya is the exception that makes the question worth asking.","B":"Both halves wrong — West Nile is a flavivirus.","D":"Bunyaviruses include Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever, but they are not the whole group."},"img":"slides_i/L14_7.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L14|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q63","t":"Chikungunya — arthritis","s":"Which feature BEST differentiates chikungunya virus infection from dengue?","lec":"L14","sl":57,"c":"<b>Chikungunya — arthritis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q63 · BM31 SUM1 · BM28 Q17 — 'the major clinical manifestation of Chikungunya' / 'what best differentiates chikungunya from dengue?'</span>","o":{"A":"Severe polyarthritis","B":"Rash","C":"Retro-orbital pain","E":"Thrombocytopenia","D":"Haemorrhage"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The name itself means 'that which bends up' — the joint pain is severe, symmetrical, often in the small joints, and can persist for months after the fever has gone. Chikungunya is the ONLY one of the three in which arthritis dominates.","B":"All three — dengue, chikungunya and Zika — produce a rash, so rash separates nothing.","C":"Retro-orbital pain is classically DENGUE.","E":"Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia are dengue's laboratory signature; chikungunya usually spares the platelets.","D":"Haemorrhage is dengue. Same mosquito (Aedes), same region, often the same outbreak — the joints are what tell them apart."},"img":"slides_i/L14_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-chik","BM28 Q17"]},{"id":"I|L14|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q58","t":"Zika in pregnancy","s":"A pregnant woman is infected with Zika virus. What is the most likely serious consequence?","lec":"L14","sl":42,"c":"<b>Zika in pregnancy</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q58 — a pregnant woman infected with Zika; what is the most likely outcome?</span>","o":{"E":"Microcephaly in the infant","C":"Guillain-Barré syndrome in the infant","A":"Rash in the mother","B":"Haemorrhagic fever in the mother","D":"Congenital cataract and deafness"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Zika crosses the placenta and is neurotropic for the developing fetal brain, producing microcephaly and the wider congenital Zika syndrome. This is the whole reason Zika became an emergency in 2015–16.","C":"Guillain-Barré is the neurological complication in INFECTED ADULTS, not in the infant — reading which person the option refers to is the trick here.","A":"Rash occurs but is trivial; only about one in four infected people has any symptoms at all.","B":"Haemorrhagic fever is dengue.","D":"Cataract and deafness are congenital RUBELLA (L12)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q57","t":"Japanese encephalitis — prevention","s":"Which is the recommended prevention of Japanese encephalitis?","lec":"L14","sl":51,"c":"<b>Japanese encephalitis — prevention</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q57 · the lecturer's own quiz slide 64 — the recommended prevention of JE</span>","o":{"A":"Vaccination","D":"Administration of antiviral drugs","B":"Use of antibiotics","E":"Indoor residual spraying of insecticide","C":"Use of antimalarial drugs"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. There is NO specific treatment for JE, and fewer than 1% of those infected develop clinical disease — but when encephalitis does develop it is severe and often fatal. So the answer is prevention, and an effective vaccine exists (it is in the Thai EPI schedule). Mosquito avoidance and repellent are the secondary measures the lecturer's own quiz slide names.","D":"No antiviral is available for JE.","B":"Antibiotics have no role against a virus.","E":"Culex breeds outdoors in rice paddies, so indoor residual spraying — a malaria tactic aimed at indoor-resting Anopheles — is poorly matched.","C":"Antimalarials do nothing against a virus."},"img":"slides_i/L14_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q56","t":"Recognising dengue","s":"Which patient is most likely to have dengue?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>Recognising dengue</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q56 · Q59 — two stems in the same paper asking which patient has dengue</span>","o":{"D":"A man with high fever, chills, severe muscle and joint ache, retro-orbital pain and a rash over the whole body","C":"A patient with headache and neck stiffness","E":"A patient with high blood pressure and fever","B":"A man with a dry cough who has diabetes","A":"A patient with malaise, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting alone"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. High fever + severe myalgia and arthralgia + retro-orbital pain + generalised rash is the textbook dengue fever picture — hence the old name 'breakbone fever'.","C":"Headache with neck stiffness points to meningitis or encephalitis — think Japanese encephalitis or West Nile among the arboviruses.","E":"Hypertension with fever is non-specific and is not a dengue feature.","B":"A dry cough suggests a respiratory virus; dengue is not primarily respiratory.","A":"Malaise, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are WARNING SIGNS in an established dengue case, but on their own they describe any number of illnesses. The examiner offered this option in the same paper to see whether you would pick warning signs over the diagnostic picture."},"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q59"]},{"id":"I|L14|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q5","t":"West Nile virus","s":"What is the most severe clinical manifestation of West Nile virus infection?","lec":"L14","sl":54,"c":"<b>West Nile virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q5 (examiner-written) — the most severe clinical manifestation of West Nile virus infection</span>","o":{"C":"Encephalitis","E":"Jaundice","B":"Arthritis","A":"Rash","D":"Malaise"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and it follows from the family: West Nile belongs to the Japanese encephalitis antigenic serocomplex, so it is neurotropic. About 80% of infections are asymptomatic, ~20% give West Nile fever, and a small minority progress to neuroinvasive disease — encephalitis, meningitis or acute flaccid paralysis.","E":"Jaundice is YELLOW FEVER, whose target is the hepatocyte.","B":"Arthritis is chikungunya.","A":"Rash occurs in West Nile fever but is not the severe manifestation.","D":"Malaise is part of the mild febrile illness."},"img":"slides_i/L14_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q64","t":"Zika transmission routes","s":"Which route of Zika virus transmission is LEAST likely?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"<b>Zika transmission routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q64 — which route of Zika transmission is LEAST likely?</span>","o":{"C":"Breast milk","A":"Mosquito bite","B":"Sexual contact","D":"Vertical (mother to fetus)","E":"Blood transfusion"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct as the LEAST likely. Zika has been detected in breast milk, but breastfeeding transmission is not established and WHO continues to recommend breastfeeding — so it is the weakest of the five routes.","A":"The mosquito bite is the primary route.","B":"Sexual transmission is well documented, and the virus persists in SEMEN for up to about 6 months — far longer than in blood, saliva or urine.","D":"Vertical transmission is the route behind congenital Zika syndrome.","E":"Blood-transfusion transmission has been documented, which is why donors from outbreak areas are deferred."},"img":"slides_i/L14_37.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q19","t":"Pig farms and mosquitoes","s":"A community lives beside pig farms where mosquitoes are abundant. Which viral infection should be anticipated?","lec":"L14","sl":47,"c":"<b>Pig farms and mosquitoes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q19 — a pig farm with many mosquitoes; which virus should be expected?</span>","o":{"B":"Japanese encephalitis virus","A":"Dengue virus","C":"Chikungunya virus","D":"Zika virus","E":"Yellow fever virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Pigs are the AMPLIFYING host for Japanese encephalitis: the virus multiplies to high titre in them without making them ill, so mosquitoes feeding nearby pick it up and can pass it to humans. Pig farm + mosquitoes + rice paddies (Culex breeding) is the JE setting.","A":"Dengue is urban and human-to-human via Aedes; pigs play no part.","C":"Chikungunya likewise cycles through humans and Aedes.","D":"Zika is Aedes-borne with a human cycle.","E":"Yellow fever's sylvatic cycle involves monkeys, not pigs, and it does not occur in Asia."},"img":"slides_i/L14_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"L14 quiz","t":"Animal reservoir","s":"Which of the following viruses causing human disease has an ANIMAL RESERVOIR?","lec":"L14","sl":8,"c":"<b>Animal reservoir</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's OWN quiz, slides 5 and 63 (asked twice in the deck) — which virus causing human disease has an animal reservoir?</span>","o":{"B":"Japanese encephalitis virus","C":"Cytomegalovirus","A":"Hepatitis C virus","E":"Human papillomavirus","D":"Varicella-zoster virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the lecturer asked this twice in her own deck — at the start and again at the end — which is about as strong a signal as a lecture gives. JE is maintained in pigs and wading birds; humans are dead-end hosts.","C":"CMV is a human herpesvirus with no animal reservoir.","A":"Hepatitis C is a strictly human infection.","E":"HPV is human-specific — the name says so.","D":"VZV is human-only. Of these five, only the arbovirus is a zoonosis."},"img":"slides_i/L14_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q62","t":"Dengue laboratory picture","s":"Which laboratory pattern fits dengue fever?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>Dengue laboratory picture</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q62 — which laboratory values rise or fall in dengue</span>","o":{"A":"Platelets DOWN, white cells DOWN, haematocrit UP, transaminases UP","C":"Platelets up, white cells up, haematocrit down","D":"Eosinophils markedly raised with a normal platelet count","E":"Haematocrit down with a raised platelet count","B":"Isolated lymphocytosis with normal platelets and haematocrit"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the four move together for one reason: plasma leaks out of the vessels. Platelets fall, white cells fall, the haematocrit RISES because the red cells are left behind in a shrinking plasma volume, and the transaminases rise from liver involvement.","C":"Every direction is inverted.","D":"Eosinophilia points to parasites.","E":"A falling haematocrit suggests frank bleeding or fluid overload rather than early leakage — and a rising haematocrit with falling platelets is the pairing that should prompt admission.","B":"Dengue does not give an isolated lymphocytosis; that is closer to infectious mononucleosis."},"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L14|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (57)","t":"Aedes as vector","s":"Which statement about the mosquito vector of dengue is correct?","lec":"L14","sl":20,"c":"<b>Aedes as vector</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank cohort 57 Q6 · cohort 59 Q5 — the Aedes questions, cross-referenced from the L8 entomology bank</span>","o":{"D":"Aedes aegypti bites by day, breeds in CLEAN stored water around the house, and also transmits chikungunya and Zika","A":"Aedes breeds in dirty drain water and bites only at night","C":"Culex is the dengue vector and breeds in clean household water","B":"Anopheles transmits dengue as well as malaria","E":"Mansonia is the dengue vector and breeds under aquatic plants"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the breeding site is the control message: Aedes lays in CLEAN standing water in and around houses — water jars, vases, plant saucers, old tyres — so vector control is a household task. It bites by day and carries dengue, chikungunya, Zika and Wuchereria bancrofti.","A":"Dirty water is Culex; Aedes is a day-biter.","C":"Culex breeds in dirty water and carries Japanese encephalitis and filariasis.","B":"Anopheles carries malaria and breeds in slow, clean stream water.","E":"Mansonia carries Brugia malayi and attaches its eggs under aquatic plants."},"img":"slides_i/L14_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q5 (59)"]},{"id":"I|LAB|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Larval morphology","s":"Under the microscope you must separate a hookworm rhabditiform larva from a Strongyloides rhabditiform larva. Which pair of features does it?","lec":"LAB","sl":31,"c":"<b>Larval morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hookworm: LONG buccal cavity, SMALL genital primordium. Strongyloides: SHORT buccal cavity, LARGE genital primordium","C":"Hookworm: short buccal cavity, large genital primordium. Strongyloides: long buccal cavity, small genital primordium","D":"Hookworm has a notched tail; Strongyloides has a pointed tail","E":"Hookworm has a filariform oesophagus; Strongyloides has a rhabditiform oesophagus","B":"They are morphologically identical and are separated only by culture"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and this is the single most examinable comparison in Lab 1. Memory hook: the worm that BITES the gut wall has the LONG mouth.","C":"Exactly reversed.","D":"Tail shape separates the FILARIFORM larvae, not the rhabditiform ones — and it is the other way round: Strongyloides has the NOTCHED tail, hookworm the POINTED tail.","E":"Both rhabditiform larvae have a rhabditiform (bulbous) oesophagus; that is what the name means.","B":"They are routinely separated on these two features."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Larval morphology","s":"Which filariform larva has a NOTCHED tail?","lec":"LAB","sl":32,"c":"<b>Larval morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","A":"Hookworm","B":"Trichinella spiralis","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Strongyloides filariform larva = notched (forked) tail; hookworm filariform larva = pointed tail. Both have a filariform (elongated) oesophagus.","A":"Hookworm's filariform larva has a POINTED tail.","B":"Trichinella's larva is coiled inside a nurse cell in muscle, not a free filariform larva.","D":"Wuchereria's blood stage is a sheathed microfilaria, not a filariform larva.","C":"Ascaris is diagnosed on eggs; its larvae are not a lab specimen here."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Microfilariae","s":"Which feature separates the microfilaria of Brugia malayi from that of Wuchereria bancrofti on a stained blood film?","lec":"LAB","sl":53,"c":"<b>Microfilariae</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"B. malayi has TERMINAL nuclei extending into the tail tip and a kinked, irregular body; W. bancrofti has a smoothly curved body with no terminal nuclei","B":"W. bancrofti has terminal nuclei; B. malayi has none","C":"B. malayi has no sheath; W. bancrofti has a sheath","D":"W. bancrofti is unsheathed and B. malayi is sheathed","E":"They are identical and are separated only by the vector"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The worksheet describes W. bancrofti as 'ลำตัวโค้งสวยงาม' — gracefully curved — and B. malayi as 'ลำตัวโค้งแบบขดงอ', kinked. The terminal nuclei are the decisive feature.","B":"Reversed.","C":"BOTH are sheathed.","D":"Both are sheathed.","E":"They are separated on morphology in the lab, which is the point of the specimen."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Ascaris eggs","s":"Four forms of the Ascaris egg are demonstrated. Which description matches the DECORTICATED egg?","lec":"LAB","sl":13,"c":"<b>Ascaris eggs</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Round or oval, COLOURLESS, with no outer albuminous layer","D":"Oval to round, brown-yellow, with a thick mamillated albuminous coat","A":"Oval and irregular, 78–105 µm, brown-yellow with a thin albuminous layer","E":"Round, containing a coiled larva, and infective","C":"Barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. 'Decorticated' means the outer albuminous coat has been lost, which also removes the bile staining — hence colourless.","D":"That is the FERTILIZED egg, the classic form.","A":"That is the UNFERTILIZED egg — larger, more irregular, and with a thinner coat.","E":"That is the EMBRYONATED egg, which is the infective stage after two to three weeks in soil.","C":"That is Trichuris trichiura."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Opisthorchis","s":"What is the specific key point of the Opisthorchis viverrini egg as the lab worksheet describes it?","lec":"LAB","sl":1004,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"25 × 12 µm, 'electric light bulb' appearance, musk-melon surface texture, embryonated, with a shoulder and a terminal knob","E":"58–76 × 40–51 µm, unembryonated, with an operculum and a terminal knob","B":"140 × 85 µm with a faint operculum and a smooth thin shell","D":"Non-operculated with a lateral spine","C":"Barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — and the phrases 'electric light bulb' and 'musk-melon texture' are the worksheet's own wording, which is what a lab short-answer marks.","E":"That is Diphyllobothrium latum — also operculated with a knob, but more than twice the size and unembryonated. Measuring is what separates them.","B":"That is the large intestinal fluke group (Fasciolopsis, Fasciola, Echinostoma).","D":"That is Schistosoma mansoni.","C":"That is Trichuris trichiura."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1004.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Small intestinal flukes","s":"A 25 µm operculated egg is seen. How do you decide between Opisthorchis viverrini and a small intestinal fluke, and what do you report?","lec":"LAB","sl":1010,"c":"<b>Small intestinal flukes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Shoulder and knob PROMINENT = Opisthorchis; both faint or absent = report 'small intestinal fluke egg'","D":"Shoulder and knob absent = Opisthorchis; prominent = small intestinal fluke","E":"Size alone decides — Opisthorchis is twice as large","B":"Only PCR can separate them","A":"They are reported together as 'liver fluke egg'"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. This is the ONE egg discrimination the lecturer expects you to make on morphology alone. The small intestinal fluke egg is 22–32 x 11–12 micrometres, also with an 'electric light bulb' shape, but with a faint or absent shoulder and knob.","D":"Reversed.","E":"They overlap in size — 25 x 12 versus 22–32 x 11–12 micrometres — which is exactly why the shoulder and knob are needed.","B":"Light microscopy is the taught method.","A":"The small intestinal fluke egg is reported under its own name, which is what makes this discrimination worth marks."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1010.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Schistosoma","s":"Which Schistosoma egg has a LATERAL spine, and how do the other three differ?","lec":"LAB","sl":1011,"c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"S. mansoni has a lateral spine; S. haematobium has a terminal spine; S. japonicum (70–100 µm) and S. mekongi (51–73 µm) have a small lateral knob and differ by size","C":"S. haematobium has a lateral spine; S. mansoni has a terminal spine","E":"All four have terminal spines and differ only in size","B":"S. japonicum has the lateral spine; S. mansoni has a knob","A":"Schistosoma eggs are operculated and have no spine"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Spine position first, then size for the japonicum/mekongi pair — and mekongi is the Mekong-basin species, which is the geographical hint.","C":"Reversed — and S. haematobium is the URINE specimen, found by filtration.","E":"Only haematobium's spine is terminal.","B":"S. japonicum has a small lateral KNOB, not a spine.","A":"Schistosoma eggs are the one NON-operculated fluke group, and they are embryonated when passed."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Taenia scolex","s":"Two Taenia scolices are demonstrated. Which description is correct?","lec":"LAB","sl":1018,"c":"<b>Taenia scolex</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"T. solium has a rostellum with hooks; T. saginata has four suckers and NO rostellum or hooks","D":"T. saginata has a rostellum with hooks; T. solium has none","B":"Both have rostellar hooks and are separated by sucker number","A":"Neither has a rostellum; both are separated by the uterine branch count only","E":"T. solium has six suckers; T. saginata has four"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and it is the fastest discriminator on an adult specimen. Memory hook: pork (solium) is 'armed' with hooks; beef (saginata) is 'unarmed'. The uterine-branch count (<15 vs >15) is the other method, done on a gravid proglottid with Indian ink.","D":"Reversed.","B":"Both have four suckers; only solium has hooks.","A":"T. solium does have a rostellum.","E":"All cyclophyllidean cestodes have four suckers."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1018.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Angiostrongylus","s":"The adult Angiostrongylus cantonensis specimen is described by a distinctive pattern. What is it and what causes it?","lec":"LAB","sl":45,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A 'barber's pole' appearance — the red blood-filled intestine spirals around the white uterus","E":"A 'barber's pole' appearance — alternating bands of pigmented cuticle","B":"A 'whip-like' shape with a narrow anterior and thick posterior","D":"A 'cephalic bulb with hooklets' at the anterior end","C":"A coiled larva inside a nurse cell"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The intestine is red because it is filled with blood; the uterus is white; the two interwind, giving the barber's-pole stripe. It is the key point for this specimen.","E":"The stripe is internal anatomy showing through, not cuticular pigment.","B":"Whip-like is Trichuris trichiura.","D":"The cephalic bulb with hooklets is Gnathostoma spinigerum.","C":"That is Trichinella spiralis."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_45.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|LAB|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Gnathostoma","s":"How do the adult and third-stage larva of Gnathostoma spinigerum differ on the demonstration slide?","lec":"LAB","sl":42,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"C":"Both have a cephalic bulb, but the adult has 8 rows of cephalic hooklets and the L3 larva has 4","B":"Only the adult has a cephalic bulb; the larva has none","D":"Only the larva has hooklets; the adult has suckers","A":"The adult has 4 rows and the larva has 8","E":"Neither has hooklets; they are separated by size alone"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Cephalic bulb in both, and the ROW COUNT is what the worksheet asks for.","B":"Both have the bulb.","D":"Suckers belong to the flatworms, not to a nematode.","A":"Reversed.","E":"The hooklet rows are the specific key point."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_42.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|LAB|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Stool examination","s":"What is the advantage of the concentration technique over a direct simple smear?","lec":"LAB","sl":5,"c":"<b>Stool examination</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"D":"It examines a far larger volume of stool, so it detects light infections that a direct smear misses","B":"It preserves motility, which a direct smear destroys","C":"It allows the species to be identified where a direct smear cannot","E":"It is quicker and needs no centrifuge","A":"It is the only method that shows trophozoites"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct — sensitivity is the whole point. A direct simple smear for Opisthorchis viverrini is only about 12% sensitive, which is why 'formalin ethyl-acetate concentration' beats 'direct simple smear' whenever both appear in an option list.","B":"The opposite: concentration KILLS the organisms, so motility is a direct-wet-mount observation only.","C":"Species identification is a morphology question, and both methods show morphology.","E":"Concentration is slower and does need a centrifuge — its advantage is sensitivity, not speed.","A":"Trophozoites are best seen on a fresh direct wet mount, because concentration destroys them."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_5.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|LAB|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"On the Lab 5 worksheet you must state which genus is Anopheline and which are Culicine. Which grouping is correct?","lec":"LAB","sl":4005,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"B":"Anopheline = Anopheles only; Culicine = Culex, Aedes and Mansonia","D":"Anopheline = Anopheles and Culex; Culicine = Aedes and Mansonia","C":"Anopheline = Aedes only; Culicine = the other three","E":"Anopheline = Mansonia only; Culicine = the other three","A":"All four genera are Culicine"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. One genus against three — which is why step one of the identification (palpi as long as the proboscis?) splits 1 from 3.","D":"Culex is a culicine.","C":"Aedes is a culicine.","E":"Mansonia is a culicine.","A":"Anopheles is the anopheline."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4005.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|LAB|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"lab Q","t":"Trichuris","s":"A lab specimen shows a yellow-brown, barrel-shaped egg with a mucoid plug at each end. Give the scientific name, the stage, and the key identification.","lec":"LAB","sl":20,"c":"<b>Trichuris</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab — barrel-shaped egg with bipolar mucoid plug</span>","o":{"D":"Trichuris trichiura · unembryonated egg · barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs, yellow-brown","E":"Capillaria philippinensis · unembryonated egg · peanut-shaped with flattened bipolar plugs","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides · fertilized egg · thick mamillated albuminous coat","A":"Enterobius vermicularis · embryonated egg · flattened on one side","B":"Hookworm · unembryonated egg · thin shell with 4–8 cells"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and this is the BM28 answer key's exact wording for all three parts.","E":"The designed near-miss: Capillaria is also bipolar, but PEANUT-shaped with FLATTENED plugs and a striated shell, and it is 36–45 µm rather than 49–65 µm.","C":"Ascaris eggs are oval to round with a bumpy outer coat, not barrel-shaped.","A":"Enterobius eggs are D-shaped (plano-convex) and colourless.","B":"Hookworm eggs are transparent ovals with a segmented interior."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q23","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"A lab specimen 5 µm across is shown. Give the scientific name, the stage, the key point and the staining technique.","lec":"LAB","sl":3001,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q23 — 5 µm round organism, stain and stage asked</span>","o":{"C":"Cryptosporidium spp. · sporulated (mature) oocyst containing 4 sporozoites · round, 4–6 µm · modified acid-fast stain","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis · unsporulated oocyst · round, 8–12 µm · modified acid-fast stain","D":"Microsporidia · spore · belt-like, 1–2 µm · gram-chromotrope stain","A":"Cystoisospora belli · unsporulated oocyst · ellipsoidal, 25 × 15 µm · modified acid-fast stain","E":"Entamoeba histolytica · mature cyst · 10–15 µm with 4 nuclei · iodine stain"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on all four parts, and the size is what gives it away — 5 µm sits inside Cryptosporidium's 4–6 µm range and nowhere near the others.","B":"Cyclospora is roughly twice the size.","D":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm and is never acid-fast.","A":"Cystoisospora is five times the size and ellipsoidal.","E":"An Entamoeba cyst is two to three times the size and is not acid-fast."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_3001.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q19","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"A mosquito specimen is shown with an egg that is boat-shaped with lateral floats. Give the genus and its medical importance.","lec":"LAB","sl":4006,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q19 and Q20 — Anopheles vs Aedes, with medical importance and breeding place</span>","o":{"E":"Anopheles spp. — vector of malaria and of lymphatic filariasis","C":"Aedes spp. — vector of dengue virus, chikungunya and Zika virus","B":"Culex spp. — vector of Japanese B encephalitis","D":"Mansonia spp. — vector of Brugia malayi","A":"Anopheles spp. — causes malaria"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both parts. The answer key's own note explains how the two genera are separated on the specimen: Anopheles eggs carry floats and are laid singly, while Aedes eggs are oval/rugby-ball shaped.","C":"Aedes eggs are oval without floats, and the companion question asks where Aedes breeds — the answer is water containers around the house.","B":"Culex lays a raft, not single eggs with floats.","D":"Mansonia lays a sunflower-shaped cluster attached to plant roots.","A":"⚠ Wrong FORM, even though the genus is right. The mosquito does not CAUSE malaria — it is a vector. The lecturer marks this as incorrect."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4006.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q20"]},{"id":"I|LAB|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-tick","t":"Hard tick","s":"A demonstration specimen has four pairs of legs, a dorsal shield and a capitulum visible from above. What is it, and what is its medical importance?","lec":"LAB","sl":4007,"c":"<b>Hard tick</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 lab · BM30 — scutum, visible capitulum, 4 pairs of legs</span>","o":{"B":"Hard tick — vector of Lyme disease and Rocky Mountain spotted fever","C":"Soft tick — vector of tick-borne relapsing fever","D":"Chigger mite — vector of scrub typhus","E":"Sarcoptes scabiei — causes scabiasis","A":"Demodex spp. — causes folliculitis"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Scutum present + capitulum visible from above = Ixodidae, the hard ticks.","C":"Soft ticks have NO scutum and their capitulum is not visible from above.","D":"The chigger mite also has a scutum — but it is the LARVA, with only THREE pairs of legs, and it is 0.1–0.3 mm rather than 2–10 mm.","E":"Sarcoptes is a burrowing mite without a scutum of this kind.","A":"Demodex is cylindrical with bud-like legs and lives inside follicles."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4007.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q38"]},{"id":"I|LAB|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q24","t":"Plasmodium","s":"A thin blood film shows a trophozoite lying as a band across a normal-sized red cell. Which species, and what are the two key points?","lec":"LAB","sl":3001,"c":"<b>Plasmodium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q24 — Plasmodium malariae trophozoite, two key points</span>","o":{"E":"Plasmodium malariae — BAND-form trophozoite, and the infected red cell is NOT enlarged","A":"Plasmodium vivax — amoeboid trophozoite in an enlarged red cell with Schüffner's dots","C":"Plasmodium ovale — comet-shaped, fimbriated red cell","B":"Plasmodium falciparum — ring form with double chromatin, multiple infection of one cell","D":"Plasmodium knowlesi — indistinguishable from P. malariae on morphology"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both counts, and the pairing matters: the band form IS the key point, and 'red cell not enlarged' is what separates it from vivax and ovale.","A":"P. vivax enlarges the cell and shows an amoeboid parasite with pink dots.","C":"P. ovale gives a fimbriated, comet-shaped red cell.","B":"P. falciparum gives double-chromatin rings and banana gametocytes.","D":"P. knowlesi does resemble P. malariae, but the taught answer for a band form is P. malariae — and knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle rather than 72."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_3001.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Scabies","s":"A child bitten between the fingers has a skin biopsy showing a mite with four pairs of legs. What is it?","lec":"LAB","sl":4007,"c":"<b>Scabies</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q26 · BM30 — four-legged-pair mite from a finger-web biopsy</span>","o":{"B":"Sarcoptes scabiei","A":"Pthirus pubis","D":"Pediculus humanus capitis","E":"Pediculus humanus corporis","C":"Demodex spp."},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Four pairs of legs makes it an arachnid, and the finger-web site with burrows is classical scabies.","A":"Lice are INSECTS with three pairs of legs, and the pubic louse is found on pubic hair and eyelashes.","D":"Also an insect, and found on the scalp.","E":"Also an insect, and found in clothing seams.","C":"Demodex has four pairs of legs too — but they are stubby and bud-like, the body is cylindrical, and it lives inside hair follicles rather than burrowing between the fingers."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4007.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM30 Q-scab"]}];
window.META_I={"lecOrder":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","LAB"],"lecName":{"L1":"Intestinal Nematodes","L2":"Blood & Tissue Nematodes","L3":"Cestodes","L4":"Trematodes","L5":"Amoeba, Ciliate, Flagellate","L6":"Intestinal Sporozoa","L7":"Toxoplasma & Malaria","L8":"Medical Entomology","L9":"Antiparasitic Drugs","L10":"Introduction to Virology","L11":"Viral Epidemiology & Pathogenesis","L12":"Viral Exanthems","L13":"Oncogenic Viruses","L14":"Arbovirus","LAB":"Labs 1–5"},"lecShort":{"L1":"L1","L2":"L2","L3":"L3","L4":"L4","L5":"L5","L6":"L6","L7":"L7","L8":"L8","L9":"L9","L10":"L10","L11":"L11","L12":"L12","L13":"L13","L14":"L14","LAB":"Labs"},"yearOrder":["BM32","BM31","BM30","BM29","BM28","LS54"],"yearMeta":{"BM32":["BM32 · 2568","BM32 Infectious summative 1 · SUM2 lecture & lab"],"BM31":["BM31 · 2567","BM31 SUM1/SUM2 lecture & lab, with the BM32-written answer keys"],"BM30":["BM30 · 2566","BM30 Lec+Lab Summative I & II (2566) + the two 2566 formatives"],"BM29":["BM29 · 2565","BM29 summative 1 & 2 with เฉลย"],"BM28":["BM28 · 2564","BM28 Summative I & II (2564) + the Practice paper"],"LS54":["หลักสูตร 54 · 2557–2560","Ax_Infectious question bank, cohorts 57–60"]},"authorMeta":["Authored","Exam-grade items written for this block — one clinical decision per stem, one planted near-miss."],"built":"Infectious Summative I · BM33 L1-L14 + Labs 1-5","lecBlurb":"15 buckets, L1 → L14 plus the labs","authBlurb":"NOT past papers — exam-grade items written for this block","aboutTitle":"Infectious Summative I — question trainer","aboutBody":"General Principles of Infectious Diseases <b>1007105</b>, Summative I. Every past-paper item names the <b>cohort and question number</b> it came from, and the BM33 source slide is shown on the answer card.<br><br>The past-paper layer was re-harvested from <b>all 31 recalled papers</b>: 162 cited items carrying 263 cohort appearances across BM28–BM32 and the หลักสูตร 54 bank. The BM30 เฉลย keys its answers by DOCX <i>highlight</i>, which the plain-text extracts had dropped — recovering it is what took the past-paper count from 72 to 162.<br><br>⚠️ <b>Two circulating student keys are wrong</b>, and the cards say so rather than quietly correcting them. BM30 Q63 keys <i>Rubella</i> for the infant whose rash appears <i>after</i> the fever subsides — it is roseola (HHV-6/7). BM29 Q13 keys <i>trophozoite, schizont, gametocyte</i> for P. falciparum in blood; BM30's key and the BM33 deck both say <b>trophozoite and gametocyte only</b>, because schizonts sequester.<br><br><b>Scope note:</b> Summative I examines <b>L1–L19</b>. L15–L19 (HIV &amp; viral STD, emerging viruses, laboratory investigation, antiviral drugs, clinical correlation) are not here yet — those lectures have no folder in the block. About 40 harvested items belonging to them are held in the coverage ledger of the local dashboard, not lost."};
